# What happened at a meeting, keyed by video id. Kept apart from CIVIC_BOARDS so
# the video lists stay one line per meeting; a meeting with no entry simply
# shows its title, as they all did before.
CIVIC_DESCRIPTIONS = {
 "bbc3cz61R9A":
  "The legislative delegation gives its annual update: Senator Cyr and Representative Moakley on "
  "the transfer fee home rule petition, now filed in the House and named the senator&rsquo;s "
  "highest priority, on an inspector general&rsquo;s report into the Steamship Authority, and on "
  "why energy prices will stay high while offshore wind is stalled. Housing staff report that 64 "
  "rental units at Ticcoma Green have been added to the subsidized housing inventory, lifting "
  "the island to 8 percent of the state&rsquo;s 10 percent requirement. Public comment turns to "
  "childcare costs and to block grant money Nantucket cannot reach because it is counted a "
  "wealthy community.",
 "mzKNkN0SSpU":
  "A workshop on the sewer master plan, and the consultants are blunt about the arithmetic. "
  "Joining the Cape Cod and Islands Water Protection Fund, paid for by a surtax on lodging, would "
  "have Nantucket putting in around $300 million over three decades and drawing back perhaps "
  "two-thirds of it, so they steer the board towards a local surcharge already allowed under "
  "state law instead. One member rules out a home rule petition on the grounds that housing has "
  "been stuck on that road for fifteen years. Only one needs area has been sewered so far &mdash; "
  "Shimmo, the first extension since the late 1980s &mdash; and more than 700 parcels already "
  "fronted by sewer are not connected to it. The argument that occupies the board is the one "
  "underneath all of it: what limits building in these neighborhoods is the bedroom count a "
  "septic system allows, so running sewer removes that limit at the same time as it protects the "
  "ponds. Madaket, voted down at the ballot and now facing $50 million just to raise its road "
  "above the flood line, sits further down the list than ever.",
 "seMYN6zpJKw":
  "A long agenda. The board sends a letter backing changes to the Board of Health&rsquo;s septic "
  "rules for the wellhead protection district, having worked out with the Land and Water Council "
  "that the benefit should attach to a year-round deed restriction rather than to who lives in "
  "the house &mdash; treating residents differently by season would run into the interstate "
  "commerce clause. Then the Town Meeting warrant: the state&rsquo;s accessory dwelling unit law "
  "would have allowed four units on a single lot here, so the tertiary dwelling category is "
  "struck and folded into the state definition to hold density where it was. FEMA language goes "
  "into the flood overlay so islanders can keep buying flood insurance, and another batch of "
  "properties leaves the RC2 district the 2009 master plan set out to retire &mdash; among them "
  "Cisco Brewers, the Galley and Cliffside Beach Club, several after a decade of negotiation. The "
  "Nantucket Center for Elder Affairs asks consent to extend the Saltmarsh Senior Center, where "
  "evening events cap at about 45 people, and a resident uses public comment to push his article "
  "on pyrolysis as an alternative to incineration.",
 "FqYmbFWSGho":
  "The housing department&rsquo;s monthly report, and the numbers are the story. Of roughly 400 "
  "year-round town employees, between a quarter and a third do not know where they will be living "
  "in twelve months&rsquo; time, and 48 are on the list waiting for somewhere. The department "
  "manages 37 units, 20 of them leased from private owners, and ran a month-long advertisement "
  "across every island outlet asking for more: fifteen homeowners answered and every one offered "
  "shared housing, when what is needed is standalone homes for families and single parents. A "
  "three-bedroom was quoted at $10,000. Against that, the Affordable Housing Trust reports a year "
  "that carried the island from 6.55 percent of the state&rsquo;s subsidized housing requirement "
  "to 8.04, with 95 units added and 594 projected by 2028. The board hands the town manager "
  "authority to sign employee leases on three days&rsquo; notice, because waiting for a board "
  "agenda has been costing the town units. In public comment a resident asks whether safe harbor "
  "gives the town any more leverage over the Surfside Crossing development, whose unit count she "
  "calls preposterous during a level three drought.",
 "EDDqzfHFFLM":
  "Two minutes and a roll call, and the shortest meeting of the year. National Grid cannot attend "
  "in the storm, so both of its public hearings are opened and continued to February 9th, and the "
  "board adjourns.",
 "10uYHnZPb9A":
  "A storm debrief opens the meeting: eight to fourteen inches of snow, fifty-mile winds, and "
  "temperatures swinging either side of freezing so the powder set like concrete. Plows were out "
  "twenty-seven hours at a stretch, dispatch fielded 571 calls in thirty-six hours, and of the "
  "two hundred cars moved for the parking ban only five were towed &mdash; the deputy chief "
  "credits islanders for staying off the roads, and the Patriots for playing at the right moment. "
  "The new Steamship Authority general manager is introduced, three weeks into the job. The "
  "claims fund from the GE Vernova blade failure settlement opens, with a June deadline. On the "
  "African Meeting House investigation, a member reports that the investigator has read most of "
  "the documents, has asked for redactions to be lifted, and will not commit to a finishing date. "
  "The town also answers a question put the week before: because Surfside Crossing applied for "
  "its permit in 2018, when the town was not in safe harbor, the current status gives it no "
  "leverage there. The evening ends in joint session with the planning commission, hearing "
  "MassDOT on the replacement of the Sagamore and Bourne bridges.",
 "uyQXf1B8X0I":
  "The Sconset Beach Preservation Fund uses public comment to describe the cuts found in the "
  "geotubes below the bluff as unmistakably deliberate &mdash; reported to police, inconsistent "
  "with any storm &mdash; and warns that until they are repaired the damage widens and Baxter "
  "Road and the way to the lighthouse sit at greater risk. A resident thanks the public works "
  "crews for clearing downtown around the clock, then asks what obligation property owners have "
  "for their own frontage, having walked down the middle of Union Street because the sidewalks "
  "were buried. A board member reads out a hundred-year-old newspaper item in which the highway "
  "superintendent explains a new sidewalk plan to the Rotary Club &mdash; asphalt free, brick or "
  "concrete if the abutter pays the difference &mdash; and observes that the town is still having "
  "the same argument about who pays. The housing trust&rsquo;s quarterly report brings the island "
  "to 8 percent of the subsidized housing requirement with safe harbor now secured to December "
  "2027, testimony delivered at the State House on the transfer fee, and the covenant "
  "program&rsquo;s ceiling raised to $854,171.",
 "W8TK5Xi9YyI":
  "A licensing morning. Fusaro&rsquo;s changes hands: the new owner tells the board he will run "
  "it as it is for the season, keep the staff and the local suppliers, and only then consider "
  "renovating and rebranding, while keeping what he calls the affordable local place it has been. "
  "The Nantucket Performing Arts Center takes out a liquor license in its own name &mdash; "
  "technically a new one, since the old White Heron license lapsed between the theater "
  "surrendering it and the building changing hands &mdash; and the Theatre Workshop moves from a "
  "seasonal license to an annual one so it can hold an off-season benefit for another nonprofit. "
  "Running underneath is the licensing administrator&rsquo;s argument that the process has become "
  "too administrative: one recent applicant never spoke to her and never appeared at his own "
  "hearing. The board also turns over whether the seasonal fee, at around $600, is fair to the "
  "restaurants that stay open all year against those that unlock only for the busy weekends.",
 "fGi2VQEb1Lc":
  "The coastal resilience coordinator lays out what Easy Street costs either way. Do nothing in "
  "that stretch and the projection is $1.2 billion in losses by 2070; the three designs on the "
  "table &mdash; raising the bulkhead, raising the road to six and a half feet, or raising it to "
  "eight &mdash; run from $47 million to $62 million. The arithmetic that lands hardest is what "
  "the money buys over time: eight feet protects against a hundred-year storm today and against a "
  "two-year storm by 2070. Nothing is being built soon either, with permitting alone running "
  "three to seven years and construction split across two because downtown cannot be dug up in "
  "summer. She concedes that the first round of public outreach was not enough, which a board "
  "member seizes on approvingly &mdash; done properly, he says, people are so tired of hearing "
  "about a project by Town Meeting that the vote is easy. Elsewhere the fire chief marks a call "
  "firefighter&rsquo;s retirement after 23 years, the town clerk is certified by the state "
  "association, and residents are warned that scammers are trawling public permit filings and "
  "invoicing applicants in the town&rsquo;s name.",
 "JJ32I_jyEVM":
  "The evening opens as the transit authority, awarding its bus management contract to Keolis "
  "North America from March 1st. The incumbent operator appears in public comment to object: "
  "thirty years of local ownership, the only regional authority in the state running a contented "
  "non-union workforce, against a multinational he says cannot keep the MBTA commuter rail on "
  "time. He calls the two-week deadline the authority&rsquo;s own procurement failure, and cites "
  "electric buses bought without a charging plan and now served by a propane generator running "
  "twice a day. A resident asks the board to move Town Meeting back from 4:30 to five, since "
  "people work until five, Our Island Home is article eleven, and an early start risks both "
  "excluding them and the quorum. Another wants far harder penalties after a demolition at "
  "Smith&rsquo;s Point &mdash; six-figure fines, permits rescinded, the lot frozen for five "
  "years. A third asks how anyone is meant to judge article 78, a change to a town council form "
  "of government set out in five and a half lines. The capital programs committee presents its "
  "FY2027 report, noting two large borrowings it declined to recommend on the economics.",
 "IlVronCGQ6o":
  "The blizzard takes the first half hour. At its height 86 percent of the island was without "
  "power &mdash; some 11,000 customers &mdash; with a warming shelter open around the clock at "
  "the intermediate school, 75 fire and ambulance calls, 121 to the police, and 25 trees down "
  "across the 90 miles of road the crews were still plowing. Senator Cyr, joining from his "
  "parents&rsquo; house, says they lost more trees than in any storm since Hurricane Bob, and "
  "notes that burying the lines would run about $6 million a mile. Brush tipping fees are waived "
  "at the landfill through the end of the month, for residents only, with the license plate the "
  "only way to tell a resident from a contractor. A resident asks the town to pursue "
  "undergrounding the main feeds to Tom Nevers and Surfside, whose neighborhoods are already "
  "buried, and reports that Commercial Street flooded not over the bluff but backwards through a "
  "storm drain from the harbor, asking for a check valve. The board also declines, in a written "
  "answer, to move Town Meeting from 4:30 to five: the time was set in July and posted for months "
  "without objection.",
 "xM2tREL-x_Q":
  "Public comment is dominated by electricity bills. A resident describes being billed some $400 "
  "over on two months of estimated readings, never having been told that a newly installed meter "
  "can take a month or two to produce a real one, and a reporter asks what the town&rsquo;s "
  "statement that the meters are definitively not at fault actually rests on. The evening&rsquo;s "
  "substance is solid waste. Public works brings a rebuilt tipping fee schedule: every "
  "residential fee drops to zero, commercial rates rise to what each waste stream actually costs, "
  "leaf and yard waste is charged for the first time at 9,500 tons a year, and the general "
  "fund&rsquo;s share of the $18.1 million budget falls from 66 percent to 56. A public hearing "
  "is set for April 1st. The board waives permit fees for a family rebuilding after a house fire "
  "but keeps the sewer privilege fee, on the grounds that everyone else pays it. It approves a "
  "$9.3 million bond anticipation note, lifts the summer restriction on six aquaculture leases, "
  "and approves a four-way stop at Surfside and Miacomet Roads, piloted through Town Meeting so "
  "voters can try it before they vote on it.",
 "shP1LvTsKmc":
  "Three boards sit together and walk the Town Meeting warrant article by article. The knot is "
  "article 12, the school athletic fields. Because the debt exclusion question on the ballot "
  "names a synthetic turf field, anyone moving from the floor to substitute grass would leave the "
  "appropriation and the ballot question describing different projects, and bond counsel would "
  "not permit the borrowing; the appropriation is contingent on the exclusion passing, so both "
  "would fall and a fresh election would be needed by September 15th. Town counsel and another "
  "attorney read the scope of the article differently, the wording being improvements to the "
  "athletic facilities including a synthetic turf field. On Our Island Home the Finance Committee "
  "has moved against and the Select Board will move for, which means two-thirds to pass against a "
  "simple majority to reject. Eight linked zoning articles rewrite the island&rsquo;s dwelling "
  "categories around the state&rsquo;s by-right accessory dwelling unit, striking the tertiary "
  "dwelling and folding its protections in to hold density where it is. They get an explanatory "
  "insert at the back of the warrant, the hope being that nobody tries to amend them from the "
  "floor.",
 "Vb6HE5_Wml4":
  "A licensing night. Seasonal liquor licenses come up for their annual renewal, one package "
  "store license treated as abandoned after the holder closed without ever telling the town, "
  "which frees a slot in the quota. The wine festival&rsquo;s charity pouring licenses are held "
  "over: the state requires that a majority of alcohol proceeds reach charity, the accounting the "
  "town finally received shows what was raised but not where it went, and the board wants that "
  "before it signs. The fiftieth Daffodil Festival gets its parade form and its open container "
  "waiver in Sconset, with marked boundaries, food permits, drone rules and a campaign about not "
  "overdoing it. One member says he would like the alcohol waiver dropped in the longer run and "
  "the day returned to the family event it was, and the board agrees to take up festivals and "
  "drinking as its own subject over the summer. A restaurant that ran a bar out of a catering van "
  "on Old South Wharf during Christmas Stroll, outside its licensed premises and with drinks "
  "walking off down the street, draws a two-day suspension held in abeyance for a year, a new "
  "license manager and mandatory staff training.",
 "K6Mq24Qc6Wg":
  "The evening&rsquo;s weight is a consultant&rsquo;s pro forma for the proposed new Our Island "
  "Home, commissioned so that voters can see what the building would cost to run as well as to "
  "build. It assumes forty-five private rooms, an average daily census of forty-two, and daily "
  "rates of $800 for private pay and Medicare against $260 for Medicaid: $7.6 million of revenue "
  "against $11.8 million of expenses, an operating deficit of $4.2 million in the first year "
  "widening to $8.5 million by the tenth as costs outrun rates. Add debt service on $125.5 "
  "million borrowed over twenty years and the town&rsquo;s yearly contribution starts near $14.8 "
  "million. The existing override covers the operating side until roughly year seven, after which "
  "another would be needed. Members are blunt that this is the last plan the board will bring and "
  "that a no vote begins a wind-down of three to five years rather than a return to the drawing "
  "board. The rest of the meeting drafts Select Board comments on the warrant, among them one "
  "against moving the wind settlement money into a stabilization fund, and a dark sky week "
  "proclamation opens it.",
 "tCistMVirUk":
  "National Grid comes to answer for the winter&rsquo;s electricity bills. Its deployment lead "
  "reports ten thousand smart meters installed on Nantucket, about seven in ten residential "
  "customers, and argues that the meters only measure what was always measured: consumption ran "
  "nine percent higher across the island in a winter twelve percent colder, old and new meters "
  "track each other, and tested accuracy is better than 99.9 percent. What went wrong, he says, "
  "was a data backlog that left thousands of accounts on estimated readings for months. The room "
  "is unconvinced. A reporter says he has reviewed bills showing increases of several hundred to "
  "a couple of thousand percent; a caller describes a highly efficient house billed the same for "
  "an empty December as for an August with fourteen people in it; another has gone from four "
  "hundred dollars to two thousand with nobody in residence. Opting out costs $26 a month. "
  "Billing specialists stay behind and return the next day. The board then closes a loophole that "
  "let corporate short-term rental operators register through limited liability companies, and "
  "adopts its warrant comments, including a warning that it will move to take no action on the "
  "Baxter Road license if no agreement is reached.",
 "DkP2gtNPCBE":
  "Most of the evening goes to the draft license that would let the Sconset Beach Preservation "
  "Fund keep its geotubes on town-owned beach below Baxter Road. Twenty-two terms are read out: "
  "roughly $2.4 million held in escrow so the town could remove the tubes itself, $6.7 million "
  "more for a year of mitigation sand, annual review of both figures, financial responsibility "
  "for damage to the roads the sand travels over, a public stairway down to the beach, and a "
  "clause committing the town to support a future application to dredge sand offshore. Opponents "
  "lay out six earlier agreements with the same partner and where each went unhonored, and count "
  "the cost of trucking: three hundred thousand tons of sand, some fourteen thousand round trips, "
  "seventy-five a day through Sconset for four months. Staff answer that those trips come to well "
  "under one percent of the traffic on Milestone Road over the same stretch. One member says he "
  "can support the license for a year and no longer. The board adds an escrow requirement, a road "
  "repair escrow and an annual compliance review, and holds the vote until the 15th.",
 "-z-5T1pipFE":
  "A workshop on housing the town&rsquo;s own workforce. The numbers set the frame: nearly "
  "two-thirds of the island&rsquo;s dwellings are occupied only seasonally, the median house "
  "sells for three and a half million, a two-bedroom rents for $4,200 a month and a single room "
  "with a bathroom for $3,000. Surveys of town staff find middle-income employees spending more "
  "than a third of their pay on housing and almost one in five spending half, a third of them "
  "sharing with people they would not otherwise live with, and over half doubting they could keep "
  "working for the town without something stable. The housing office places thirty-five employees "
  "at present, most of them in units the town rents from private owners, with fifty more on the "
  "waiting list. The plan is twenty-five to fifty units over ten years, built on land the town "
  "already owns, starting with eighteen at Wade Drive. The argument staff want the board to carry "
  "to Town Meeting is a financial one: the half million a year the town pays one landlord in rent "
  "would service the debt on building its own. Parking, and how many cars a unit should assume, "
  "takes up much of the rest.",
 "_VXy8Vr3owk":
  "The finance director&rsquo;s last meeting after twelve years, and the board credits him with "
  "audits that were in disarray when he arrived and a bond rating only fifteen Massachusetts "
  "communities hold. Several residents use public comment on the Board of Health&rsquo;s proposed "
  "turf moratorium, which would require testing to a standard of zero: parents say families do "
  "not know whether their children can practice on Friday, and speakers argue that no such "
  "standard exists in science. The town moderator brings in the chair of Wayland&rsquo;s "
  "electronic voting subcommittee to explain how remote participation in an open town meeting "
  "could work &mdash; listening, speaking and voting from a phone, with a published audit table "
  "so every voter can check their own vote against the count, and a photograph taken at check-in "
  "to deter voting by proxy. It is not legal yet; pilot legislation sits in Senate Ways and "
  "Means. The new bid for Our Island Home comes in at $137.3 million, its contingency four "
  "million lower because tariffs no longer need covering, with the borrowing held at $119 million "
  "by drawing on retained earnings. The Baxter Road license terms pass, and three parking "
  "decisions send the board back to its own curb cut policy.",
 "5fCcb5_pg6I":
  "A licensing afternoon, opening with a new manager&rsquo;s guide that every liquor license "
  "holder will sign each year, written after a run of compliance failures and missed renewals. "
  "Three of the historical association&rsquo;s summer fundraisers have moved to properties in "
  "residential neighborhoods, and the board trims two of them from three hundred fifty guests to "
  "two hundred fifty, requires shuttles from off-site parking rather than valet runs back and "
  "forth through town, and takes the Jetties lot off the table for the third. Members say plainly "
  "that the wider pattern, events pushed into residential streets in August, needs a policy "
  "rather than case-by-case bargaining. The Land Bank&rsquo;s Sconset golf course is upgraded to "
  "a full alcohol license for the narrow purpose of selling canned cocktails, on condition that "
  "private events come back for approval. A restaurant reopening on South Water Street will cross "
  "an occupancy of one hundred, which brings sprinklers, music that cuts out when the fire alarm "
  "sounds and certified crowd managers, and the board adds doors and windows closed during live "
  "music. A Verizon petition is held because the poles to be pulled carry the town&rsquo;s fiber "
  "to the schools.",
 "9iWXlxhA4-o":
  "A suspended supervisor in the 911 communications center uses public comment to accuse the "
  "police chief of retaliating against dispatchers who filed grievances, and the board of "
  "declining to act on them; he says he was offered a resignation to keep him quiet. The "
  "year&rsquo;s audit follows: reserves back near their peak after a deliberate drawdown, a "
  "reserve ratio comfortably above what the rating agencies look for, and the AAA rating saving "
  "real money on a heavy borrowing schedule. The auditor notes that the retiree health liability "
  "has not fallen despite steady funding, because covered payroll has nearly doubled in eight "
  "years, and flags water department inventory controls and close to four million dollars sitting "
  "in special revenue accounts nobody can account for. The airport needs its fuel revolving limit "
  "raised from $8.5 to $11 million on the price of jet fuel. Then two hearings: a hound that "
  "killed a neighbor&rsquo;s cat and two deer is found a dangerous dog, a behaviorist&rsquo;s "
  "assessment ordered and the animal leashed whenever it leaves the house; and an appeal over "
  "fencing and cattle grates is denied four to one, the board unwilling to call the historic "
  "commission&rsquo;s decision arbitrary.",
 "ImhMGNBZsYs":
  "Three dispatchers and one former dispatcher use public comment. The assistant supervisor who "
  "addressed the board two weeks earlier was fired the following day and served his papers in the "
  "police station lobby; a colleague says he consulted a lawyer before deciding whether to speak "
  "at all, and another describes a two-year grievance over flagging training the department "
  "provided to community service officers but not to dispatch. The board reads the chief&rsquo;s "
  "statement that she cannot say more but has a duty to address conduct in her department. The "
  "behaviorist hired to assess the hound found dangerous in April reports back: he is friendly "
  "with people and most dogs, what goes wrong is a predatory sequence that engages only when he "
  "is off leash and something runs, and with reward-based training, a real leash in place of the "
  "electronic collar, and a muzzle in specific situations, she does not consider him a danger. "
  "The board keeps the finding and the leash order and sets four months before reconsidering. The "
  "housing trust reports the island at 8.28 percent of the state&rsquo;s subsidized housing "
  "requirement, and staff demonstrate the new asset management system live from the sewer office.",
 "iwcFDW55BLY":
  "Two restaurants that do most of their business in takeout ask for full liquor licenses, and "
  "the board spends the afternoon working out where the line should fall. At the taco place on "
  "Sanford Road the owner wants to serve the kitchen and hotel staff who finish at half past ten "
  "and would rather not pay a cover charge somewhere for a single beer; he is open every day of "
  "the year, and says that is the point. The board weighs an eleven o&rsquo;clock cutoff, the "
  "condition it put on a comparable downtown spot, against creating a third category for the "
  "police to enforce, and settles on the ordinary one o&rsquo;clock with a review in October. A "
  "second application, on Orange Street, gets the eleven. That brings the island to a hundred and "
  "eleven licensed dispensaries, up from the high eighties ten years ago. The historical "
  "association returns having moved its design party off the pony field to a downtown hotel with "
  "school parking and a shuttle, which the board calls a good solution. Two National Grid "
  "petitions on Somerset Lane go over to September so the cables and the sewer project can open "
  "the road once rather than twice.",
 "3buql-h8eYM":
  "A reconstituted board elects its officers, welcomes two new members, and sits with the "
  "Planning Board to fill a vacancy there. National Grid then lays out the island&rsquo;s "
  "electrical arithmetic. The first submarine cable was laid in 1996 and the second in 2006, each "
  "with a forty-year life, and summer peak demand has climbed from 33 to 54 megawatts in "
  "twenty-two years, winter from 22 to 35 &mdash; growth the utility says it sees in no other "
  "community it serves. On current projections the island stops peaking in summer and starts "
  "peaking in winter around 2038, and by 2050 would need 113 megawatts in January. A study of "
  "eighteen to twenty-four months will weigh replacement cables, new substation capacity away "
  "from the downtown flood plain, and alternatives to poles and wires. A member asks whether the "
  "existing cables are paid off yet, since new debt stacked on old is what worries people. The "
  "Daffodil Festival debrief follows: some eighteen thousand people, a quarter more than last "
  "year, the best-behaved crowd anyone can remember, and a list of what strained &mdash; trash, "
  "restrooms, the VIP parking, and a date that keeps landing inside school vacation week.",
 "70dER4WX94I":
  "The morning after the annual town election, and the shortest meeting on the list. The two "
  "winners take the oath, sign the book, and are congratulated; their first official act as "
  "members of the board is to move that it adjourn.",
 "RZVYt15ZNUU":
  "The evening&rsquo;s substance is two reports. The committee operations coordinator, six months "
  "into a new position, has taken conflict-of-interest training compliance across forty "
  "committees from eighteen percent to ninety-eight and a half, by chasing two hundred volunteers "
  "and sitting down with the ones the website defeated; open meeting law training follows in "
  "September, and she is building a governance audit meant to catch problems before they become "
  "complaints. The police chief then runs through the summer: nearly fifteen thousand parking "
  "tickets last fiscal year, a downtown officer back on the beat, and seventy encounters with "
  "electric bikes and scooters since October, with fewer double riders and more helmets, though "
  "some devices cannot be stopped safely at all. She explains why impaired driving charges "
  "involving cannabis rarely hold up &mdash; there is no roadside test and no legal threshold to "
  "measure against. Six people apply for two Board of Health seats, and two sitting members use "
  "their introductions to deny, point by point, what has been said about that board and "
  "artificial turf. An elm planted on Surfside Road in 1852 is designated a Liberty Tree for the "
  "country&rsquo;s 250th anniversary.",
 "ULCPUUn2ppY":
  "The long item is the Pleasant Street sidewalk between Silver Street and Main. A neighborhood "
  "survey drew 112 responses and put historic character and the shade trees at the top of what "
  "should be preserved and on-street parking at the bottom, which shapes the recommendation: "
  "bring the walk out around the trees rather than take them or cut into front yards, and give up "
  "eight parking spaces to do it, plus seven more further along where cars now park on the "
  "sidewalk. Members ask that the walk wrap back between the big elms so a space or two survives, "
  "and note that the church up the street has already lost parking twice this year. A former "
  "chair of the tree advisory committee objects that her committee was never consulted and that "
  "removing hundred-year-old elms appeared on the list at all; the board adds a motion to bring "
  "them in. The airport wins an amendment for mobile jet fuel storage, having come within five "
  "thousand gallons of running dry one year and rationed general aviation four times last summer. "
  "In public comment, residents bring photographs of construction trucks filling the Cliff Road "
  "sidewalk end to end.",
 "TbuFXMyGOOc":
  "The Fourth of July water fight is off. The water director lays out why: the island averages "
  "forty-two inches of rain a year and the last three brought twenty-four, twenty-seven and "
  "twenty, and the state&rsquo;s monitoring well sits nine inches below the level that would lift "
  "the drought. He says he cannot ask businesses to restrict outdoor water and then hose Main "
  "Street. One member argues hard for a compromise, a single tank and enough water for children "
  "to fill something, and is reminded that the water guns were stopped years ago because older "
  "kids were firing into shops. Bubble machines, a touch-a-truck and a 250th anniversary parade "
  "go in instead. Short-term rental registrations come back at 1,623, four in five of them owned "
  "by people living off island, which prompts the observation that the figure tracks the share of "
  "property tax paid by non-residents almost exactly and so shows no recent shift. The coastal "
  "resilience report draws a blunt response from a new member who does not accept the sea level "
  "projections behind half a billion dollars of planned work. Committee appointments are made by "
  "paper ballot.",
 "ei7OhVosYks":
  "The state has told restaurants they may ask to serve until three in the morning through July "
  "to accommodate World Cup matches, and the board signals early that it is disinclined: nothing "
  "good happens after one, and the season is no time to add pressure on staffing or the police. A "
  "brewery gets a narrower version of the same thing, permission to put up a twelve-foot screen "
  "and run it to ten o&rsquo;clock with abutters notified and a report back after each match, "
  "which covers most of the group stage without the late kickoffs. A hotel is allowed to extend "
  "its liquor license across the back lot into the building behind it, adding a ground-floor "
  "lounge, four rooms and a patio, with recorded music indoors only until eleven and nothing "
  "outdoors. A restaurant on Fairgrounds Road turns its left-hand driveway to grass and adds an "
  "outdoor bar and tables behind the building, the parking rearranged rather than reduced; the "
  "board presses on how three separate service areas will be supervised. Three utility petitions "
  "pass, and a member asks National Grid to stop addressing its paperwork to the Board of "
  "Selectmen.",
 "NvO-J1rKFNE":
  "Two threads dominate. The first is money. Three contracts for the new Our Island Home come to "
  "the table and a member declines to wave them through without a borrowing schedule, drawing out "
  "that the $119 million will be taken in short-term notes as the work proceeds and converted to "
  "long-term debt only once the building is finished. The town manager then reports that the "
  "ten-year capital plan holds more than a billion dollars in projects that have never been "
  "sequenced against one another, and proposes a three-step process for the board to rank them. "
  "Against that, the assessor walks through the residential exemption, now twenty-five percent of "
  "an average residential value near $3.6 million and held by 2,341 qualified owners, and "
  "explains that the whole residential class pays for it. Members divide: one wants relief for "
  "people whose assessments have climbed through no choice of their own, another refuses to raise "
  "it on the grounds that renters get nothing from it and everyone who voted the spending should "
  "feel it. The second thread is the meeting house review, where the board states firmly that the "
  "work group&rsquo;s charge ended when the investigator was hired.",
 "1nLU6Gb7T6Y":
  "Four officers are promoted and sworn, and four long-serving employees retire, two of them from "
  "the nursing home after twenty-six and twenty-eight years. Moody&rsquo;s reaffirms the "
  "island&rsquo;s AAA rating, and the treasurer reports the sale: $19.6 million in bonds at a "
  "total interest cost of 2.65 percent and $50.2 million in short-term notes at 3.28, after which "
  "the chair reads the entire bond counsel vote into the record, which takes some time. The "
  "substance is the Two Fairgrounds parking lot. It holds about 127 spaces, runs past capacity in "
  "summer and at seventy to eighty percent in the off season, and in five months produced nearly "
  "four hundred over-duration violations and sixty vehicles treated as abandoned, people using it "
  "as winter storage. Staff put up four ideas: paid parking, a garage above or below ground, a "
  "town impound lot, and a redesign combining the first with permit spaces. The board sends the "
  "garage away at twenty-one million for the underground version, and asks for a worked-up "
  "overnight paid parking pilot with plate readers and an honest assessment of whether an impound "
  "lot is worth building. The police chief explains why towing here rarely works.",
 "LkOrzs_qkbY":
  "A nine o&rsquo;clock meeting with one item on it: the year-end transfers between the salary "
  "and operating lines that Town Meeting votes separately and that cannot be left in deficit. The "
  "interest is in unwinding a deliberate experiment. Last year&rsquo;s budget cut $2.25 million "
  "across every general fund department and $750,000 from health insurance on the assumption that "
  "some positions would stay unfilled, and about $1.4 million now goes back to the departments "
  "that turned out not to have the vacancies. The departing finance director, back for the "
  "occasion, says he would do it again.",
 "zrr3JWnr0N0":
  "Eight car rental medallions have come free from an agency that closed, and four applicants "
  "want them. The bylaw caps rental vehicles on the island at 700, and 188 medallions already "
  "issued to existing agencies went unused last season, which the board will revisit in December. "
  "A national chain has been on the waiting list for years and wants a foothold; a resident "
  "running five Jeeps through a peer-to-peer app wants to make his business legitimate; two "
  "others argue the real gap is commercial vehicles, a box truck or a pickup a contractor could "
  "rent here instead of shipping his own over, which would take vehicles off the island rather "
  "than add them. The board debates whether to allocate by order on the list or by business plan "
  "and premises, and then an email arrives mid-meeting from the acting airport manager: there is "
  "no counter space out there and no request for proposals until 2031, which undoes most of what "
  "the applicants had been describing. It all goes over to August, with the applicants encouraged "
  "to talk to each other.",
 "t4C7OZZ5Yn8":
  "The independent review of how the 2018 African Meeting House hate crime was investigated is "
  "released, having been read beforehand only by town counsel, and the board sits jointly with "
  "the work group that hired the reviewer. Members of that group say the report covers the police "
  "department and little of what else the Town Meeting motion named, and that the interviews they "
  "were promised never happened. The museum&rsquo;s president, grateful for the report, says this "
  "is probably as far as the matter can be taken. A speaker objects that words painted on a "
  "national historic building have been reframed as vandalism rather than a racial hate crime. "
  "Town counsel sets out what remains: the statute of limitations has passed, a man was found "
  "civilly liable in 2022 and another prosecuted for obstruction, and the district attorney found "
  "no basis to charge anyone else. The vice chair calls the investigation imperfect but within "
  "protocol, and says the real failure was the conversation the community never had afterwards. "
  "Also on the agenda: the audit of the Wiggles Way housing project, and a first look at a "
  "ten-year capital plan carrying $1.19 billion.",
 "fXACTqiLmn0":
  "The town&rsquo;s own chief technology officer comes to public comment as a private resident to "
  "object to an automated license plate reader installed where Broad Street meets Easy Street, "
  "watching traffic to and from the ferry. He says neither he nor his predecessor had any part in "
  "buying or approving it, that the chief told him it had been there a decade while the model "
  "suggests otherwise, and that his worry is not the officers but the data: what it captures, how "
  "long it is kept, who can reach it later. Pending contracts then take an hour. A $30,000 "
  "assessment of a single house is sent back for its scope, a $500,000 climate action plan passes "
  "three to two over an objection that the island has enough plans already, and the design "
  "contracts for the new public works facility go through only once the board establishes that "
  "bid-ready drawings do not go stale if Town Meeting waits a year. The harbor action plan, three "
  "years in the making, is adopted with one correction. The town&rsquo;s financial advisor closes "
  "with the arithmetic of borrowing a billion dollars without losing the AAA rating.",
 "T-2N5x9MRk4":
  "A workshop to rank the thirty-six capital projects over three million dollars, which between "
  "them account for 85 percent of a plan now past a billion. Some agreement comes quickly: "
  "maintaining the buildings the town already owns rates high with everyone, though the ten "
  "million a year attached to it is conceded to be a placeholder, and so do the public works "
  "facility and the seasonal barracks. A new senior center is rated low by nearly all, one member "
  "arguing that the vacated nursing home should serve as interim office space rather than be "
  "designed around a single use. The $105 million downtown flood barrier draws the longest "
  "exchange of the evening, a member recounting the 1990 no-name storm hour by hour and arguing "
  "that raising Easy Street only pushes the water somewhere else, against staff pointing out that "
  "a road now flooding eighty times a year is the island&rsquo;s main route to the ferry. Road "
  "connectors around Old South Road emerge from the session as a new priority. The school, at two "
  "hundred million, has moved out to the 2030s. The meeting runs past its hour unfinished.",
 "3Wol02pUQbo":
  "A short morning hearing that returns to the eight car rental medallions left unallocated in "
  "July. The licensing administrator shows five years of utilization first: of the medallions "
  "already assigned to the existing agencies, roughly 144 go unused at any given time, nearly all "
  "of them held by one national chain &mdash; which has now filed for all eight. The vice chair "
  "asks its attorney more than once why a company sitting on that many unused medallions needs "
  "more, and does not accept the answer that cars cycle on and off the island; the administrator "
  "adds that she walked its lot the week before and found three cars being rented without "
  "medallion stickers. One member votes against any allocation on principle, arguing the town is "
  "handing out something of unknown value with no bidding process and should not be picking "
  "winners. The board votes three to one to allocate four medallions each, preliminarily, to two "
  "of the newer applicants, with final business plans due in September. A longtime operator "
  "disputes that these eight sit inside the island&rsquo;s cap of 700 at all. Three National Grid "
  "conduit petitions close the meeting.",
 "KtfqOnnqDDo":
  "A long meeting opens with the town&rsquo;s written answers on the license plate reader at "
  "Broad and Easy Street &mdash; installed in 2021, pointed at vehicles to and from the ferry, "
  "images deleted after thirty days, access held by a single administrator &mdash; and with "
  "residents unpersuaded. The board then sits jointly with the Planning Board to adopt the 2026 "
  "Housing Production Plan. Much of the public comment turns on a figure of 17,000 units that "
  "several speakers have read as a goal; the planning director explains it comes from a separate "
  "maximum-buildout study reaching to 2050 and appears nowhere in this plan. Historic District "
  "Commission members object to a line suggesting the special act be amended, and fear being "
  "bypassed by pre-approved house plans. Both boards vote to adopt. The island&rsquo;s first "
  "comprehensive human services needs assessment follows, built on responses from 621 residents: "
  "nearly a quarter did not know the services existed, and the grant pool has been level at "
  "$650,000 since 2020 while costs climbed. One member calls that appalling next to what the town "
  "spends on plans and studies. The Muse takes a two-day license suspension for serving minors on "
  "four nights.",
 "l7srNuR0g9M":
  "Four members, and four hours. Two proclamations open the meeting, for suicide prevention "
  "awareness and for climate change awareness, the first noting an island suicide rate more than "
  "twice the state&rsquo;s. Then a public hearing on sewer rates, where the enterprise "
  "fund&rsquo;s savings have fallen to about $400,000 against a $2 million floor. The consultant "
  "offers two paths: two 30 percent increases to the base rate six months apart, or one 20 "
  "percent increase plus a flat $20 monthly surcharge. Residents object to being charged for "
  "water they were told to conserve. One member, calling the surcharge a way of hiding a 60 "
  "percent increase, moves to halve it to $10 and to commit the board to a Town Meeting article "
  "that would refill the fund instead. It passes unanimously, minus the word sustainability, "
  "which another member objects to seeing attached to a fee. The Westmoor Club takes a two-day "
  "suspension for drinks leaving the premises and years of club liquor served aboard its boat. "
  "Homeowners in the Somerset sewer area come in numbers to ask whether a hookup they cannot "
  "afford can be made optional; the easement takings pass anyway. The schools ask for $4.5 "
  "million in office space.",
 "ZOyf_LW2fyA":
  "The transit authority meets first: buses have been deviating to the high school since the "
  "district lost its bus contractor, and a proposal to fit small solar lights with a stop-request "
  "button to the island&rsquo;s bus stops is sent to the Historic District Commission before any "
  "are ordered. The board then approves a long-term lease of town land at Industry Road for the "
  "transit garage, makes its third round of committee appointments, and sells $48 million in "
  "short-term notes. A film company is allowed two November days on the island over objections "
  "from Sconset, where speakers describe visitors walking into houses looking for a bathroom; "
  "several members answer that an off-season film about year-round life is the opposite of that "
  "problem. A request to lift the short-term rental restriction written into the 2015 Richmond "
  "Great Point agreement is denied outright, the board recalling the density bonuses granted in "
  "exchange for it. Our Island Home construction begins, with a tiered approval process set up "
  "for change orders against the project&rsquo;s $6 million contingency. The evening ends with a "
  "unanimous vote to suspend the Flock camera contract and have the cameras taken down.",
 "8IIA8XXM5AE":
  "With the chair and vice chair joining online, a member takes the gavel for a working meeting. "
  "The largest item is a framework for coastal resilience districts, the mechanism Town Meeting "
  "approved in 2024 for charging betterments to the properties a small shoreline project "
  "protects. Staff walk through a six-step process; one member argues at length that people who "
  "buy on a bluff knew what they were buying and that a betterment is simply another tax, another "
  "that the process is burdensome and leans on committees rather than expertise. It passes three "
  "to two, with a requirement added that a neighborhood show sixty percent support before the "
  "town carries a project forward. Public works then reports that the new department facility has "
  "come down 56.5 percent in floor area, having lost its indoor vehicle storage, a garage bay and "
  "the fitness room, and that the Land Bank has offered 54 acres of off-site mitigation that "
  "would free the rest of the Shadbush Road parcel. The new grass field at Nobadeer, rebid after "
  "February&rsquo;s prices came in nearly a million over, will be built without its lights or "
  "scoreboards. The African Meeting House work group is formally disbanded.",
 "yiSbSQRKpS8":
  "A Planning Board session to work through the zoning articles headed for Annual Town Meeting, "
  "taken by roll call. Two carried-over minor modifications go quickly: a small addition to a gas "
  "station&rsquo;s retail space, and a vapor recovery unit on Industry Road. A citizen petition "
  "moving a 7,500-square-foot slice of a Tombs Court lot from R20 to commercial neighborhood is "
  "continued so the owners, who live next door, can list the uses they would rather not see "
  "approved beside them. The longest discussion is a third attempt to stop lots divided under "
  "state law from inheriting pre-existing nonconforming rights automatically; an attorney warns "
  "it would send every such lot back to the appeals board for a variance and asks for "
  "grandfathering, and the article is continued for town counsel to draft that language. Three "
  "more articles move parcels out of the RC2 district the board has spent years phasing out, one "
  "over a member&rsquo;s objection that commercial Mid Island zoning is meant for "
  "transit-oriented development, not land beside the airport. Large restaurants become allowable "
  "in commercial neighborhood by special permit, and the bylaw gains a definition for a community "
  "land trust.",
 "iEgYjomyih4":
  "Two members are away, so alternates are activated item by item. The longest discussion is not "
  "the board&rsquo;s own business: the Board of Health is proposing septic regulation changes for "
  "the Hummock Pond watershed and the Wellhead Protection District that would add inspections and "
  "withdraw the extra bedroom an advanced treatment system currently earns. Staff put such a "
  "system at roughly $40,000 against $25,000, plus about $2,500 a year to maintain, and the board "
  "decides to ask for a joint meeting rather than send an opinion of its own. A run of storage "
  "container permits follows, the first fruits of the town&rsquo;s new full-time zoning "
  "enforcement officer. One contractor, told his eight were never allowed, cut back to five and "
  "then mounted them on registered low trailers, which puts them outside the bylaw&rsquo;s "
  "definition altogether; the board permits them anyway, with conditions, and agrees a definition "
  "is needed. Two subdivision plans are endorsed that everyone agrees will never be built, paper "
  "roads filed only to freeze zoning against the new inclusionary housing bylaw. And a rear lot "
  "subdivision is continued after the year-round restriction the board thought it had agreed to "
  "is no longer offered.",
 "7D5yeN2o--Y":
  "A special meeting on the zoning articles, most of it given to eight intertwined ones that "
  "rebuild the bylaw around the state&rsquo;s new protected accessory dwelling unit. Town counsel "
  "sets out the difficulty: Nantucket allowed second and third dwellings long before the state "
  "required ADUs, so the work is fitting in a use the town cannot condition &mdash; no "
  "owner-occupancy requirement, no bedroom cap &mdash; without handing every lot another kitchen. "
  "The answer is to delete the tertiary dwelling, fold the garage apartment into the secondary, "
  "and carry the old occupancy language across. One member presses to raise the ADU ceiling from "
  "900 square feet to 1,200 so that more existing second dwellings would already qualify and "
  "fewer lots would gain a third dwelling by right; counsel cautions that an owner could still "
  "claim the 900 the statute grants, and it stays at 900. Also recommended: a rear lot amendment "
  "letting the board ask for a year-round restriction, and a fractional ownership definition "
  "borrowed from Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard that is meant to catch eighth-share buyers without "
  "catching families. A technical change to demolition delay notices splits the board and gets no "
  "action at all.",
 "5B_KqNdni9c":
  "A short remote meeting on the two citizen articles held over from January. The Toombs Court "
  "rezoning returns without the list of prohibited uses the board had asked the owners to "
  "consider: staff advised that with the abutting commercial properties carrying no restrictions "
  "of their own, a private deed restriction at the time of sale made more sense than one the town "
  "would have to enforce. It passes. The second article, a third attempt to stop lots divided "
  "under state law from inheriting pre-existing nonconforming rights, comes back with the answer "
  "the board had been waiting for. Town counsel confirms the change cannot reach backwards, so a "
  "plan endorsed before the hearing was noticed is untouched. The remaining confusion, about "
  "ground cover, is settled plainly: the divided lots together could only have what the undivided "
  "lot was allowed. Both articles get positive recommendations. The chair urges the petitioner to "
  "keep an implementation date in reserve as a floor amendment, believing that is what cost the "
  "article its two-thirds last year.",
 "XGg_vqPMgU4":
  "Public comment opens with the Nantucket Civic League, whose vice chair says a letter sent in "
  "November asking why the board sponsored a failed North Water Street rezoning has gone three "
  "months without acknowledgement, and calls that a transparency problem. The board says it will "
  "answer, but not here. Then a long run of articles. The flood hazard overlay district gets a "
  "rewrite that has to pass: without it the island&rsquo;s 800 flood insurance policies are at "
  "risk, and the maps it adopts are FEMA&rsquo;s from 2014, the most recent that exist. The "
  "phase-out of the RC-2 district advances again, several owners asking to be left out another "
  "year while they decide what they want; the district is down from about 1,300 acres to 45. The "
  "rezoning at the brewery end of Bartlett Farm Road gets no recommendation at all: with one "
  "member recused the board splits two to two over extending the town overlay district into the "
  "Hummock Pond watershed, even with the owner offering to surrender uses and cap future lots. A "
  "rear lot subdivision passes three to two.",
 "lku_YphCFNk":
  "The master plan consultants deliver their key issues, and it is a blunt hour. The zoning bylaw "
  "is called old and over-complicated, with so many districts and special permits that it is hard "
  "to administer and harder to amend safely. Permitting is described as burdensome and "
  "unpredictable, enforcement as widely believed to be weak, and the town&rsquo;s own buildings "
  "as in poor enough condition to affect how government works. The consultants also raise, "
  "unprompted, whether the town&rsquo;s relationship with the Land Bank still matches present "
  "needs now that half the island is protected. The chair says he feels scolded. Another member "
  "objects that this is a list of grievances rather than a planning process, and asks for a "
  "schedule and real public dialogue before any goals are adopted. The rest of the evening is "
  "employee housing. A request to house sixteen Waste Options employees, two to a bedroom, in an "
  "eight-bedroom house on a residential loop draws neighbors asking for a management plan and "
  "quiet hours, and is continued. So is Cape Cod Express&rsquo;s plan for eight apartments at its "
  "truck terminal.",
 "FZL2Cy0ntS8":
  "The joint meeting the Planning Board asked for in January: three bodies at one table, "
  "planning, health and the Affordable Housing Trust, over the septic regulations the Board of "
  "Health wants to extend to the Hummock Pond watershed and the Wellhead Protection District. The "
  "health department lays out its case with numbers. The 2014 estuaries study put 65 percent of "
  "the nitrogen reaching Hummock Pond on wastewater, and 81 percent of the nitrogen anyone can "
  "control; the pond is prone to cyanobacteria blooms; a public well has been shut since 2022 for "
  "PFAS at more than twice the state limit; and the island draws from a single aquifer while in "
  "severe drought. Against that sits the cost. Members put an advanced treatment system at "
  "$85,000 to $100,000, with about $2,500 a year to maintain, and argue it will land hardest on "
  "the year-round households closest to the edge of staying. The Select Board&rsquo;s four "
  "requests &mdash; hardship waivers, an extended loan program, help with maintenance, and "
  "nitrogen credits traded for a year-round deed restriction &mdash; are read out one by one, and "
  "the health board says it is willing to write them in. Staff from all three will draft the "
  "revision.",
 "ULdO4xP71tk":
  "The board opens by reading its answer to the Civic League, three months after being asked why "
  "it sponsored a failed rezoning of three North Water Street properties: the corridor has held "
  "inns and guest houses for generations, and the island&rsquo;s stock of guest rooms has been "
  "shrinking. The Civic League&rsquo;s vice chair says that is a fine rationale but still not the "
  "question, which was who came to the board asking for it. Then the meeting turns to employee "
  "housing and stays there. Cape Cod Express wins its eight apartments, conditioned on paving the "
  "full width of the road across its frontage, dark-sky lighting, no short-term rentals and a "
  "named point of contact. The request to house Waste Options employees in an eight-bedroom house "
  "on a residential loop does not: the applicant cuts it from sixteen people to twelve, an "
  "alternate member submits sixteen pages of objections that the attorney calls insulting to the "
  "employees themselves, neighbors describe smoke and noise crossing property lines, and with no "
  "four votes in sight the hearing is reopened rather than lost. The Cliffside Beach Club&rsquo;s "
  "forty new memberships stall over a valet agreement the neighbors want made permanent.",
 "PiI-qwov6MU":
  "An unusually long list of approval-not-required plans, and staff explain why: these are "
  "perimeter plans, filed with the town clerk in the days before Town Meeting to freeze a "
  "property&rsquo;s zoning for three years. Town Meeting had just eliminated the tertiary "
  "dwelling, so owners are protecting the right to build one. Two preliminary plans follow, and "
  "both expose the same discomfort. A six-lot plan on twelve acres between Skyline Drive and "
  "Lovers Lane draws a no vote over a narrow road layout, easements kept outside it, and a list "
  "of waivers the board has not been shown. So does a plan on Williams Lane that deliberately "
  "draws three lots the owners do not want, to show what zoning would allow before they ask for "
  "two rear lots instead. Later in the evening that member moves to reconsider her own vote on "
  "the second one and changes it, saying approving a preliminary plan ought to mean the board "
  "supports the plan, not merely that it heard it. Habitat for Humanity gets four two-bedroom "
  "homes on Nancy Ann Lane, tandem parking left as the applicant&rsquo;s option. A tent that "
  "outstayed its thirty days gets a permit, and an argument about Sunday hours.",
 "eTUuV2uYz-Y":
  "A short meeting, and a changed table: one member is thanked on her way out, two new ones are "
  "welcomed, and the alternate who sat with the board for more than a decade has withdrawn his "
  "application. Most of the business is money coming back. The performance surety for Wiggles Way "
  "is released in full, Sandpiper gets $30,000 of the $40,000 it asked for because the "
  "landscaping is unfinished, and Bittersweet is allowed to release its last lot against a "
  "$10,000 deposit while the easement paperwork grinds on. Two more storage container permits "
  "come through, and the board tightens the pattern it is settling into: tied to the present "
  "owner, a fixed number, some green space at the edges. The item the chair is plainly pleased "
  "about is a double covenant &mdash; Housing Nantucket buying an oversized lot from sellers who "
  "went to the housing office rather than the highest bidder, keeping a shag-carpeted house from "
  "the mid-seventies as year-round housing and adding two covenant homes beside it. Staff believe "
  "several have been approved and none yet built from scratch. The Easy Street restaurant passes "
  "its one-year review with no complaints.",
 "CVelp5erKqo":
  "The housing production plan comes in for review before the board is asked to adopt it in "
  "August. The consultants explain what it is for: the ten percent that Chapter 40B counts, which "
  "on Nantucket now means 618 units against just over 500 on the subsidized inventory today and "
  "594 projected by 2028. Thirty-one units built in a year buys a certified plan and the power to "
  "refuse a comprehensive permit, sixty-two buys two years. What stands out in the telling is how "
  "quiet the public process has been this round next to past ones &mdash; less urgency, or, as "
  "the consultant suggests, plain housing fatigue after years of articles and funding requests. "
  "Asked how the town should handle overcrowded housing, the board finds it cannot agree what "
  "overcrowded means: per bedroom, per house, or per neighborhood. Dave Iverson is re-elected "
  "chair. The White Elephant pulls a residential lot back into the hotel&rsquo;s master plan to "
  "add a plunge pool, which prompts a recounting of the Harbor House redevelopment from 2006 "
  "through the crash that halted it. And staff float amending the bylaw so that not every storage "
  "container needs a permit of its own.",
 "GMWZ-OmuZIo":
  "A short meeting, with an alternate thanked on his way to the Finance Committee. The housing "
  "production plan comes off the agenda, to be adopted jointly with the Select Board on the 19th "
  "instead, and the master plan&rsquo;s draft implementation matrix goes online within days, with "
  "six listening sessions split across natural, community and built systems. Seven secondary "
  "dwellings pass in a block. Egan Lane returns for what amounts to a re-approval: the plan had "
  "been endorsed before anyone noticed the order was wrong, so the Select Board first had to "
  "petition the County Commissioners to lay out part of the road &mdash; a matter more than "
  "twenty years in the making, and someone promises champagne. The gym on Lovers Lane reports "
  "three months of its seasonal tent without a complaint, but its request for longer outdoor "
  "hours cannot be heard at a meeting never advertised for it, so it waits. The golf club widens "
  "a turnaround so its employee vans, and the fire department&rsquo;s coming ladder truck, can "
  "get back out at the gate. And a surety is posted on Old South Road because the drought will "
  "not let the grass be seeded.",
 "ujYlVLeSEzA":
  "The board sits with the planning and economic development commission to receive the finished "
  "draft master plan and open a thirty-day comment period on it. The consultant names five things "
  "she believes the town must do, and two are blunt. One is to put the year-round community first "
  "in every policy and regulation, with the warning that buying deed restrictions is not what "
  "made Vail work &mdash; the zoning requirement behind it was. The other is to establish a civil "
  "discourse program, because across three states of practice she cannot name another town where "
  "the will to create tension runs as strong, and no plan gets implemented until people can "
  "disagree civilly. Scenario planning is offered in place of the carrying capacity study people "
  "keep asking for. Then the hospital&rsquo;s staff housing on Vesper Lane, where its own "
  "employees describe moving twice in four years and a nurse walking across a parking lot at "
  "midnight to deliver a baby; the board, uneasy about the massing against the abutters and a "
  "parking waiver, continues it to October. The long-contested subdivision off Lovers Lane is "
  "approved with conditions, and the chair&rsquo;s parting advice to that neighborhood is equally "
  "blunt: renew your deed restrictions before they expire.",
 "NZ-WevBhoUE":
  "A short afternoon: three continuances and three decisions. The first is a hot tub. A spa "
  "installed at 7 Pleasant Street in 1998, before a 2023 Town Meeting article put outdoor water "
  "features off limits in the old historic district, may be replaced with one four square feet "
  "larger &mdash; granted, on the condition that any further growth comes back here. The second "
  "is a modification the board turns into something else. A site on Ocean Avenue falls fifteen "
  "feet from the parking area to the front door, and the owners have given up an approved shed "
  "and a wheelchair ramp in favor of a wooden platform to drop bicycles on; because it sits four "
  "feet from the road, closer than what was approved, members decide a variance rather than a "
  "special permit is the honest instrument and grant that instead. The third is erosion. A house "
  "on Madequecham Valley Road may move forward to as little as zero feet from its front lot line "
  "&mdash; latitude the board itself proposed at the November hearing, rather than see the owners "
  "back again in a year or two as the bank comes closer.",
 "uH92aN0Bv7Q":
  "Half the board cannot open the packet: the document portal starts returning errors partway "
  "through the afternoon, so members argue over drawings they can only see on their phones, while "
  "music from the gym downstairs comes up through the floor. The substantial matter is a "
  "foundation at 2 Green Lane, where a house moved to the site a century ago stands a foot from "
  "the Maria Mitchell Association&rsquo;s line, beside a 1920s terracotta-and-steel library the "
  "association spent five years and some $750,000 conserving. The work will be hand dug beneath "
  "the lifted structure with seismic monitors on the neighbor&rsquo;s buildings, and the board "
  "adds a condition putting one small projecting section on a pier rather than a poured wall to "
  "keep the digging further off. A garage on Cliff Road is continued because nobody can establish "
  "whether its new half-story is finished storage or an artist&rsquo;s studio, which would be a "
  "change of use. And a house on West Miacomet Road is allowed to retreat across the road from "
  "the eroding side, on condition that the lot it leaves behind stays vacant and unbuildable.",
 "SlzNgOtnDNE":
  "The Cliff Road garage returns with its answer &mdash; unfinished, unconditioned, storage only "
  "&mdash; and passes with conditions to keep it that way. The container appeal at 9 Hinsdale "
  "Road is withdrawn, the violation behind it having been resolved at the Planning Board instead. "
  "Then two swimming pools in Madaket, and a question the board does not really answer. A "
  "neighbor explains that when she bought in 2020 the town required an advanced septic system at "
  "her own expense and refused her a modest reconfiguration of a 950-square-foot house, both in "
  "the name of protecting a fragile aquifer, and asks how pools square with that. Both pools "
  "proceed, one by special permit after its variance request is dropped. A shed on a triangular, "
  "almost entirely wet lot is continued so its surveyor can try again to get it clear of a "
  "setback measured from a road that exists only on paper. The afternoon ends on the citizen "
  "article that would stop lots divided under state law from inheriting nonconforming rights, and "
  "one member&rsquo;s unresolved question: once that status is gone, what is the new lot?",
 "boJbm7bmMbY":
  "A single hour, and nearly all of it on an article the board declines to vote on. The citizen "
  "petition headed for Town Meeting would stop lots divided under state law from automatically "
  "inheriting pre-existing nonconforming rights, and the board agrees with the intent. What it "
  "cannot settle is what such a lot then becomes. If the status is gone, a member argues, owners "
  "will come here to have buildability confirmed, and the only instrument for that is a variance "
  "&mdash; resting on a hardship they created by dividing the lot. Town counsel and the Planning "
  "Board hold that the article closes the gap; one of the attorneys who practices before this "
  "board says plainly that she reads it the other way, that she has no idea what it does, and "
  "that this is why it frightens her. Two readings of one text, and a board reluctant to grant "
  "variances caught between them. A suggestion that they send a written comment is raised and "
  "then set aside, on the ground that the petitioner has heard the concerns anyway. They take no "
  "position, thank her, and adjourn.",
 "0bImfpWAC3E":
  "A house on the bluff side of Baxter Road may be lifted and its porches filled in, and the "
  "board offers something extra: with the structure coming up anyway, members suggest the owners "
  "would find a sympathetic hearing if they came back to move it further from the edge while the "
  "equipment is still there. The longer matter is a swimming pool. An owner on a "
  "6,500-square-foot lot obtained a certificate of appropriateness for a fourteen-by-sixteen "
  "pool, had the shell custom built in Canada and shipped as far as New Bedford &mdash; $22,000, "
  "plus $8,500 in freight &mdash; and learned only when his surveyor staked it out that the bylaw "
  "allows him a hundred square feet, not a hundred and sixty. Two neighbors in the same ten-lot "
  "subdivision have larger pools on larger lots. Most of the board is sympathetic to the loss and "
  "unconvinced it amounts to a variance, so rather than refuse they continue it to July to let "
  "him look for another way out. Nearly everyone has to invoke the rule of necessity first, the "
  "pool contractor being that widely known.",
 "mDeJjTMIzWc":
  "Three matters, and the board decides not to decide the first. Surfside Crossing asks for a "
  "ruling that swapping concrete curbing for granite on its sixty-unit comprehensive permit is "
  "insubstantial &mdash; a permit this board denied, the state housing appeals committee "
  "restored, and the town is appealing in superior court. Town counsel calls the posture unusual: "
  "the regulations offer only substantial or insubstantial, and twenty days of silence counts as "
  "the latter. One member calls the request bad faith; the applicant&rsquo;s attorney, ready to "
  "order materials at risk, finds that offensive. The board votes to decline to act, and to say "
  "why in writing. Next, a net-zero exchange of land between 11 and 12 Jonathan Way so a septic "
  "system can clear wetlands by a hundred feet, granted despite a cottage already framed under a "
  "permit for composting toilets nobody had worked out how to plumb. Last, the Fawn Lane pool: "
  "its water surface, which is what the bylaw measures, is thirty-nine square feet over, not "
  "sixty, and with the New Bedford yard now wanting it gone, the variance passes &mdash; traded "
  "for a promise to return before adding any ground cover, shed included.",
 "HVZ8kDaO14c":
  "The first variance under the new state standard, and it lands on a house retreating from the "
  "sea. Chapter 40A&rsquo;s hardship test was struck out in July and rewritten as one of "
  "practical difficulty; the board tries it on a dwelling at 124 Tom Nevers Road that will move "
  "back to fifteen feet from its front lot line, away from a bank now fifty feet off. The "
  "town&rsquo;s sustainability manager speaks in favor, holding it up as the sort of retreat the "
  "2021 coastal resilience plan asks for, and it passes. The Cliff Road garage returns a third "
  "time, its condition rewritten to permit insulation and painted drywall &mdash; the owner has "
  "no basement or attic and wants somewhere dry for photo albums &mdash; and a cottage at 15 Old "
  "North Wharf may be raised three feet toward the flood elevation and given a third story, a "
  "corrected drawing having shown its ridge height was measured from sea level, not from grade. A "
  "new house on Orange Street, meant to replicate a gambrel lost in the 1846 fire, is continued: "
  "its front stairs overhang town land, which the Select Board has not licensed.",
 "Z2Gv5C6dbjc":
  "A training session, opened with the announcement that no business would be transacted and no "
  "votes taken. Two staff from the Massachusetts Association of Health Boards spend the afternoon "
  "on what a board of health may do and how it must do it. First the authority: the legislature "
  "created these boards and handed them its police power, a board&rsquo;s regulation stands on "
  "the same footing as a statute, and a court will not substitute its own judgment unless the "
  "board acted arbitrarily. The illustrations stay with you &mdash; a flavored tobacco case "
  "decided by an inspector&rsquo;s nose, a property presented as a free-range piggery that proved "
  "to be a dump the pigs lived in, complete with a bus that had a tree growing through it, and a "
  "town&rsquo;s attempt to ban cell phones in cars, a real hazard it had no way to enforce. Hence "
  "the standing advice: just because you can does not mean you should, and enforcement is about "
  "compliance, not punishment. Then the open meeting law, serial conversations, public records "
  "down to texts sent from the table, and the new state standards the island already meets "
  "through its shared-services arrangement.",
 "5lzOajcn2gk":
  "Three hours, and the hardest stretch comes first. A woman who has lived at 49 Polpis Road for "
  "fifty-three years is under an order to connect to town sewer; the estimate runs past $125,000, "
  "her earlier two-year variance has expired, and the regulation allows no variance at all in an "
  "environmentally sensitive area. A neighbor, a contractor of fifty years, says he will go to "
  "court when it reaches him. The board is plainly uncomfortable and finds a middle course: "
  "ninety days to price a basement pump, a tie-in to a neighbor&rsquo;s line, or any second "
  "opinion, with the understanding that otherwise it denies. Then the Maria Mitchell Association "
  "wins a temporary housing variance for a circus at Tom Nevers Field in July &mdash; twenty-five "
  "performers in self-contained trailers, no animals, nothing dumped. A nineteen-bedroom plan for "
  "housing on Woodland Drive is put off a month because it never reached the meeting packet. And "
  "the Hummock Pond and wellhead amendments open for comment, the housing department and the Land "
  "and Water Council arriving together to ask that hardship stay possible.",
 "3RMHDy940ZY":
  "The last bedroom variance in the wellhead district, and it passes three to one with an "
  "abstention. Woodland Drive gets its nineteen bedrooms &mdash; capped at twelve dwellings, "
  "restricted to housing for people over fifty-five, the best available treatment technology "
  "required, a walk-through before any certificate of compliance &mdash; over one member&rsquo;s "
  "objection that the board is about to write a regulation forbidding exactly this kind of "
  "credit. The chair&rsquo;s closing line: there will be no more variance applications. Those "
  "amendments are themselves continued again, now carrying a hardship waiver, and the chair reads "
  "aloud a legal summary of the board&rsquo;s own authority and the deference courts give it, "
  "adding that the decision must be made without a great deal of pressure from outside forces. "
  "Elsewhere: December sampling on Fulling Mill Road returns 347 parts per trillion at the house "
  "that started that investigation, three times its earlier result; the wastewater dashboard is "
  "extended from respiratory illness to opioids and other high-risk substances, a $30,000-a-year "
  "program the health director, Roque Miramontes, measures against nineteen overdose deaths since "
  "2016; and a hearing is set on closing a loophole in the short-term rental rules.",
 "rFfa8GLBGVE":
  "Four hours on one question, with no vote at the end: a round table on the synthetic turf and "
  "track proposed for Vito Capizzo Stadium, framed point by point around a Mount Sinai position "
  "paper and argued by experts the school and the Nantucket Land and Water Council each brought. "
  "A psychiatrist opens on what team sports do for adolescent mental health. A professor of "
  "environmental medicine follows on what is not settled &mdash; no prospective study has "
  "followed children who play on turf, surfaces run forty to sixty degrees above the air, and "
  "shed plastic is not confined to the infill. The school&rsquo;s consultants lay out the testing "
  "they would require before any material ships, and agree there is no such thing as PFAS-free. A "
  "scientist from Public Employees for Environmental Responsibility explains how to read the "
  "resulting report: a detection limit set too high returns nothing, the way a can of Coke tests "
  "sugar-free at a hundred grams. Underneath sits one figure nobody disputes &mdash; the field "
  "takes some 1,200 hours of play a year, on grass rated for a few hundred.",
 "j-JMiiYCWA4":
  "The turf discussion collapses on procedure. The chair had emailed the board two draft motions "
  "that afternoon so members could read them in advance; town counsel weighed in beforehand, a "
  "Select Board member calls the agenda item too vague to support any motion at all, and after "
  "several points of order it is deferred to a properly noticed special meeting. What survives is "
  "the public comment before it &mdash; a coach on a track team practicing in a school hallway, a "
  "parent on eighty athletes sharing one field, and a co-founder of the island&rsquo;s PFAS "
  "action group on the distance between &ldquo;PFAS free&rdquo; and &ldquo;no intentionally added "
  "long-chain PFAS.&rdquo; Business does get done. The short-term rental regulations are amended "
  "to close a loophole that let a company qualify by naming an officer with no island connection "
  "as its only member. Five septic requests pass, among them a $153,000 loan on Hummock Pond Road "
  "where a clay layer must be dug out and replaced. And a new fact sheet on home gardening draws "
  "the sharpest comment of the afternoon: a resident asks why soil that warrants washing your "
  "vegetables is fine for children to play on.",
 "i3M-McV8fXc":
  "Twelve minutes, and a single item: two open meeting law complaints filed against the board "
  "over its March 19th meeting. Town counsel walks members through both. The allegations are the "
  "same in each &mdash; that the meeting notice was too vague to cover what the board intended to "
  "discuss, and that the chair&rsquo;s emails to members that afternoon amounted to deliberation "
  "outside a posted meeting. The draft responses concede ground on three fronts: the emails will "
  "be released publicly and filed with these minutes, the turf discussion will be taken up again "
  "on April 16th with proper notice and a vote to ratify whatever happened on the 19th, and the "
  "board will review its noticing practices generally. One member asks that the response letters "
  "go into the public packet and that the April date be announced so people can plan to attend. "
  "The vote authorizing town counsel to file with the complainants and the state&rsquo;s division "
  "of open government is unanimous, taken by roll call. Nothing else is discussed, by design. "
  "Both complaints came from residents, one of them dated the day of the meeting it concerns.",
 "2_A8Mngj4hs":
  "Three hours, most of it on the stadium, and the moratorium never comes to a vote. The vice "
  "chair asks for an emergency pause until testing is done; town counsel John Giorgio explains "
  "what that would take &mdash; a health regulation, a public hearing, twenty-one days&rsquo; "
  "notice, none of which can precede Town Meeting &mdash; and says of the language as drafted "
  "that he would be hard-pressed to figure out how to write the damn thing. Consultants call a "
  "threshold of zero scientifically impossible. Public comment runs forty minutes and mostly "
  "against, from coaches, parents, and one who telephoned the testing laboratory herself. What "
  "passes instead, unanimously, is authority for staff, the school and the Land and Water Council "
  "to keep negotiating the pre-installation protocol &mdash; eight samples of each material, "
  "three analyses apiece. Earlier: a variance for a fourth bedroom at 48 Lovers Lane squeaks "
  "through three to two while the board drafts a rule to forbid exactly such credits, 49 Polpis "
  "Road gets another month, and the public health director, Roque Miramontes, sits his last "
  "meeting. One member closes by apologizing to the room for how the board has handled the whole "
  "business.",
 "pHSrLWlXu5k":
  "The watershed amendments finally pass. After a year of drafting, four boards&rsquo; worth of "
  "comment and continuance after continuance, the Hummock Pond and wellhead protection district "
  "regulations are adopted four to one apiece &mdash; treatment systems required on new "
  "construction, transfer or failure, no nitrogen credits beyond the limit, inspections every "
  "five years, and one word added from the floor at the deputy housing director&rsquo;s "
  "suggestion, so the hardship waiver reaches attainable housing as well as affordable and "
  "year-round. The dissent both times is the member who has argued throughout that a variance "
  "clause undoes the point of the rule. Then 49 Polpis Road, where an attorney newly engaged asks "
  "to be heard in July, and the member who offered to help reports back from half a day at the "
  "property: the system works, the house sits seven hundred feet from the road, and running a "
  "line to it could reach half a million dollars. Also: a new member seated from the Select "
  "Board, and a template letter approved for short-term rentals advertising without a "
  "registration number, of which there are now more than sixteen hundred registered.",
 "MkIA4R5839Y":
  "The chair&rsquo;s last meeting, and she reads a statement into the record. Ann Smith says she "
  "is disappointed not to have been reappointed by the Select Board and troubled by the "
  "implication that she and her colleagues lacked objectivity on the turf field. She notes that "
  "Massachusetts created boards of health in 1797 as independent bodies, meant to be free of "
  "pressure from other boards, and warns that removing members and appointing others who have "
  "publicly backed a particular outcome sets a concerning precedent. The vice chair leaves with "
  "her. Before that, the island&rsquo;s ordinary business: the lone star tick appears to have "
  "spread well past its old foothold, drought suiting it, and a summer intern will drag "
  "volunteers&rsquo; yards to find out how far; a well on Quidnet Road tests just above the state "
  "standard, which brings no letters to the neighbors, since only an imminent hazard triggers "
  "those; and a surveyor asks why an irrigation well nobody drinks from requires certified mail "
  "to everyone within four hundred feet. The health director also cautions the board against "
  "repeating that the island has the state&rsquo;s highest breast cancer rate, the numbers being "
  "too small to carry that weight.",
 "cOQGm-nPDCs":
  "A nearly new board, one seat still vacant, settles the case that occupied its predecessor all "
  "year. 49 Polpis Road has come before the board four times since October. The regulation bars a "
  "variance outright in an environmentally sensitive area, so the owner&rsquo;s attorney asks for "
  "something else: not a variance but a stay of enforcement, lasting as long as she lives there. "
  "The board grants it on conditions &mdash; the existing system pumped and inspected yearly, no "
  "new construction beyond emergency repairs, and any change of ownership, family included, "
  "ending the stay at once &mdash; and on a finding of demonstrated financial hardship, with a "
  "letter from her trust officer to follow. A Madaket resident objects that she and her neighbors "
  "paid to connect; the sewer director lays out the notices sent since 2017 and the ninety-five "
  "percent who complied. Also: the turf pre-testing agreement is reached, the adhesive dropped "
  "for want of a workable test; the Woodland Drive developer is released from three conditions, "
  "including the one restricting the site to housing for people over fifty-five; a new health "
  "director is named, Dr. Joanna Watson, formerly of the CDC; and a member proposes bringing back "
  "guinea hens to eat the ticks.",
 "k8iFr3QMnbg":
  "The summer intern presents the island&rsquo;s first tick survey, and it is the most "
  "interesting stretch of the afternoon. Fifty yards dragged with a standardized cloth, ticks "
  "found on about sixty percent of them, and a map that shows the island divided: the lone star "
  "established in the west, the Asian longhorn in the east, the two never once turning up "
  "together, and the native deer tick the most common overall. The lone star does not wait to be "
  "brushed past. It pursues a host from yards away, and its larvae travel in clusters of up to a "
  "thousand, which is why alpha-gal &mdash; carried in the tick&rsquo;s saliva at every stage of "
  "its life, so that even a larval bite counts &mdash; is the risk it brings. The American dog "
  "tick turned up not at all. Elsewhere: five town houses on South Cambridge Street, sharing one "
  "failed septic system and obliged to notify each other as abutters, are finally granted "
  "separate systems; a well that tested above the state standard last June comes back non-detect, "
  "both results valid; and the state&rsquo;s ban on kratom takes effect next week, enforceable by "
  "this board.",
 "PHNa9pkgGfg":
  "A new board settles in, and takes one thing back. 49 Polpis Road won a stay of enforcement in "
  "July on a finding of financial hardship, conditional on documentation that never arrived; "
  "after three unanswered emails to the owner&rsquo;s attorney, the board rescinds the stay, five "
  "to nothing, and will notify her by certified mail. The rest of the afternoon is a beginning. "
  "Officers are elected at last, Bob DeCosta as chair and Kate Garrette as vice chair, the board "
  "having run on an acting chair since June. Each member introduces themselves, four of the five "
  "new this year: two nurses from the hospital, a retired special educator, an excavation "
  "contractor born on the island, and a charter captain. Several make the same point, that public "
  "health here gets read too narrowly as septic systems. Then the new director, Dr. Joanna "
  "Watson, four weeks into the job and an epidemiologist by training. And a market on Amelia "
  "Drive is told that a fifth instance of cutting and repackaging meat its permit does not cover "
  "will cost it the permit; the nurses are the ones who press that point.",
 "bo2cVXljSV8":
  "The rates hearing is the meeting. A five percent increase across the board is adopted after "
  "Delta&rsquo;s representative asks for three and a half, pointing out that what the carriers "
  "pay here has roughly doubled in five years, from about $900,000 to $1.8 million. Nobody "
  "disputes the figure. A second argument runs longer: common-use terminal charges are billed on "
  "a three-year average, which means an airline that shrinks keeps paying for flights it no "
  "longer operates while one that grows pays less than its share. Several commissioners want "
  "actual monthly usage instead, and staff is asked to bring that back. Smaller items carry the "
  "same logic. A boarding ramp for general aviation comes at the fire department&rsquo;s "
  "suggestion, after crews were called out to help passengers off small aircraft. A bus for the "
  "fixed-base operator draws a rule of thumb from one commissioner: you buy what appreciates and "
  "you lease what depreciates. Terminal optimization is now put at roughly $27.5 million, with "
  "about $15 million of it sought from the FAA. And starting February 1, a traveler who arrives "
  "without acceptable identification pays the TSA $45 to be verified.",
 "FtKPQnfcIzM":
  "One subject, most of the night: how big the terminal should be. The architects put numbers on "
  "it. The hold room and the seasonal tent together take 313 people; the design on the table "
  "would take 361; five aircraft boarding at once at full load would put 578 in the building. "
  "Commissioners circle that gap for two hours. Some want the design to solve it now, on the "
  "grounds that nobody gets a second chance at a terminal. Others want to know what 578 would "
  "cost before anyone commits, and one sums up his objection as ready, fire, aim rather than "
  "ready, aim, fire. What actually gets voted is narrower than the argument: a $1.5 million "
  "letter of authorization so design work can continue while the scope is still open. Even that "
  "draws a careful accounting of what the commission is and is not agreeing to. Staff expects the "
  "full design fee to land near $3 million and offers Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard, which paid $4.6 "
  "million, as the comparison. The motion carries. How many people the building should hold is "
  "left for another meeting.",
 "rBG3O8Mw0v0":
  "The meeting opens with a moment of silence for a former member of the commission. Then a "
  "sublease at the airport gas station is held over, and the reason is stated plainly: the tenant "
  "is subletting space at about forty percent of what it pays the airport, and the agreement "
  "carries no escalation until its tenth year. Commissioners want that looked at before anyone "
  "signs. The terminal design comes back as a choice between two schemes. Option A adds a "
  "seasonal terrace on the roof; Option B is a permanent addition that would replace the tent "
  "altogether. The commission picks B, the reasoning being that a canvas structure put up and "
  "taken down every year was never a solution and should stop being treated as one. The fuel "
  "subcommittee reports on expanding the fuel farm. The tanks and parts themselves run about "
  "$700,000, but with sitework, permitting and everything around them the budget is put at two to "
  "three million. The subcommittee also wants more fuel moving: seventeen truckloads a week off "
  "the ferry instead of fourteen, booked early, so the tanks are full before July.",
 "F7e0-TU06eU":
  "A special meeting, called mostly for housing. Two items come first. A ten-year TSA lease at "
  "$55 a square foot is held over because it contains no escalation, the same objection the "
  "commission has been raising all winter. The concrete hard stand contract is awarded at "
  "$348,460. Then housing, and it runs long. The airport has seven employees who need somewhere "
  "to live now, and seven dorm rooms that sit empty seven months of the year. The average island "
  "home sells for $2.97 million. Commissioners work through what other resort airports have done, "
  "Sun Valley, Eagle County, Telluride, Jackson Hole, and find the same pattern in each: the "
  "airport that can house its own people can staff itself, and the one that cannot, cannot. What "
  "passes is a number rather than a plan. Three million dollars is added to the fiscal 2027 "
  "capital request, drawn from the fuel revolver, and $50,000 is moved out of professional "
  "services to pay for design now, so the appropriation has something to build when it arrives. "
  "The design is what the commission wants to see first.",
 "GLu6tgD7Uvo":
  "Two long arguments, and a policy that comes out of them. The first is a $788,448 environmental "
  "permitting order covering fifteen capital projects, put in front of the commission two weeks "
  "before a grant deadline it cannot move, by a consultant whose own contract with the airport "
  "expires in May. One commissioner says plainly that this looks like a back door to an "
  "extension; the consultant answers that the task order would survive the contract but could not "
  "be amended. It passes. The second is a sublease inside a sublease, FedEx proposing to put a "
  "local shipping and printing business into counter space it no longer uses. The master lease "
  "permits a freight facility, and a print shop is not one, so the item is held while counsel "
  "works on it. Out of that comes the night&rsquo;s durable decision: sublease guidelines setting "
  "a $1,000 application fee, legal costs charged to the tenant, four percent of gross sales, and "
  "three quarters of any rent a tenant collects above what it pays the airport. Fuel pricing "
  "moves to a fixed margin, reset with each delivery, and the fuel revolver rises to $11 million.",
 "BzUK_foRAe4":
  "The PFAS cleanup finally has a shape. The licensed site professional walks the commission "
  "through what will be done and where: natural attenuation for the plumes inside the area the "
  "state has already written off as a source of drinking water, capping where pavement already "
  "covers the contamination, and active work in the two areas that sit inside the wellhead "
  "protection zone. Rather than digging the soil out and washing it, the recommendation now is to "
  "till a carbon or clay additive into the top foot and a half, at about a third the cost, and to "
  "pump and treat the groundwater there. A consent order gives the airport until June 2028 to "
  "have the remedy built and running. Two households are still on bottled water; one more "
  "connection should end that. Then the idling rule is rewritten. The 15-minute limit stays, the "
  "threat to withhold catering is dropped, and fines of $200 and $300 go in, alongside a "
  "requirement to use a ground power unit whenever one is available, one commissioner arguing the "
  "better fix is to stop charging for them. At the end, chairman Arthur Gasbarro says he did not "
  "put his name in for another term.",
 "OE92W06P88g":
  "The grant did not come, so the terminal question gets asked again from the beginning. A "
  "scoping meeting with the FAA and MassDOT produced no discretionary money, and the consultant "
  "offers his reading of why: the airport received about $34 million for the South Apron five "
  "years ago and is about to ask for a runway costing well over $100 million, and the FAA would "
  "rather fund pavement than buildings. That leaves three choices, laid out on a single page. "
  "Keep the tent, which has cost roughly $1.6 million across its life and needs structural "
  "members replaced in 2028. Finish the design and stop there, at about $3.3 million, which "
  "leaves a project ready to bid whenever money appears. Or build it, at $33.4 million, which on "
  "a 30-year bond at four and a half percent works out to about $2 million a year. The commission "
  "takes the middle course without needing a vote, on the shared view that a canvas tent put up "
  "every spring was never a terminal. Also adopted: minutes to be circulated within ten days, "
  "after a commissioner asked for seven.",
 "o-5hRIFqtYA":
  "A subcommittee meeting held in the middle of a supply problem. The fuel terminal in East "
  "Boston is down with a contamination issue, so trucks are running to Albany and back; one of "
  "the temporary storage trucks is out of service, cutting capacity by 7,000 gallons; and the "
  "inventory line came within about a hundred gallons of the level at which the airport starts "
  "pulling alarms. Eight ferry reservations went unused in June, one of them lost to a broken "
  "trailer axle, and a reservation that goes unused cannot be made up. No hazardous materials "
  "boat ran on the Fourth of July at all. The other subject is what one commissioner calls the "
  "freeloaders. Fifty letters went to operators who almost never buy fuel here, and the result is "
  "reported plainly as disappointing: one recipient came back 21 times and bought nothing. A few "
  "moved, one of them from 16 percent of visits to 66. The conclusion is that persuasion has now "
  "been tried, and that the answer belongs in the rates and charges this winter, where fees can "
  "be waived for those who do fuel up.",
 "jjit3yjqolQ":
  "A new chair, a new commissioner and, for the first time, penalties. The public hearing on the "
  "idling rules draws no comment from anyone, and the amendment passes: shut down within 15 "
  "minutes of parking, use a ground power unit when one is available, park with the engines "
  "pointed toward the runway, and, for repeat offenders, $200 and then $300, assessed against the "
  "operator rather than the crew. The architects present two interior schemes, a circulation axis "
  "and a single open volume, and the commission takes the second on their recommendation. Fuel is "
  "the emergency. The farm started the previous morning with 3,000 gallons in it, and general "
  "aviation is capped at 350 gallons per aircraft until the next delivery lands. And the terminal "
  "is over capacity: 2,000 passengers through the checkpoint in a day, roughly 600 of them "
  "between 10:30 and noon, against a hold room built for 313. A commissioner tells the story of a "
  "friend who could not get out of that hold room during a ground stop, and finally followed a "
  "screaming toddler through the door.",
 "5bf1sluyQeE":
  "Housekeeping first: a vice chair is elected at last, and the appointment of Preston Harimon as "
  "acting airport manager is ratified, his salary set at $200,000 retroactive to when he took the "
  "job. Then the capital plan, which a commissioner works through line by line against last "
  "month&rsquo;s version, asking what changed and why. PFAS fell from $22 million to $10 million, "
  "because the larger figure had been a 30-year operating cost mistaken for a construction "
  "number. The parking lot rose from $1 million to $2.2 million once 62 light poles were counted. "
  "The terminal gained $6 million by absorbing the geothermal treatment system, which means the "
  "project now starts with a four rather than a three. And employee housing was cut from $6.1 "
  "million to $3.4 million after a consultant came back recommending a duplex. That last one "
  "draws the sharpest response: the commission has already decided it will not build housing, and "
  "directs that no more be spent studying it. The year closes with fuel revenue up $4.9 million "
  "and operating spending up 2.1 percent.",
 "Jii4LYbNkbQ":
  "The fuel subcommittee cannot meet. Acting airport manager Preston Harimon opens at the "
  "appointed hour, asks everyone present to state their name for the record &mdash; one "
  "commissioner, three staff, the airport&rsquo;s consulting engineer and a reporter &mdash; and "
  "then says what the roll call has already made plain: there is no quorum, so the meeting has to "
  "be rescheduled. It is adjourned inside of two minutes.",
 "GKSxXmhuBC0":
  "A resident of Squam Road opens the meeting by telling the commission this was the worst summer "
  "for aircraft noise in the 17 years he has lived there: flights from half past five in the "
  "morning until as late as ten at night, and days when planes pass over his house every two or "
  "three minutes for hours at a stretch. He asks only whether relief is possible. The long "
  "argument that follows is about a different kind of accounting. A $6 million treatment system "
  "for the geothermal wells has been folded into the terminal article, and commissioners say "
  "plainly that this is a budget game, one that inflates the terminal and hides what the PFAS "
  "cleanup actually costs. One of them reports that the company that built the town&rsquo;s own "
  "system quoted about $225,000; the project manager allows that a container outside, or a unit "
  "fitted into the existing basement, could come in at a fraction of $6 million. The direction is "
  "to separate the two and bring back real numbers. The preliminary fiscal 2028 budget is up less "
  "than one percent.",
 "38A_urEC-_k":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: new dwellings at 53 Nobadeer Avenue, 1 Pawguvet Lane and 7 Orange Street. Nobadeer "
  "took the longest, about twenty-three minutes. A two-story house on a stretch of one-story "
  "cottages drew letters from abutters. One neighbor spoke of erosion, of moving his own house "
  "back twice, and of a precedent that would remake that shoreline. Pawguvet came next. A 1986 "
  "house there became a tear-down once the flood plain required raising it, so the owner asked to "
  "move it off, holding demolition in reserve through a six-month notification. Its replacement "
  "stands four feet up to clear the flood plain, which a neighbor said pushes the ridge to "
  "twenty-nine feet. The Orange Street gambrel would sit beside an older brick gambrel, near the "
  "Unitarian church. Its three-over-five bay came from a hand sketch of a house demolished around "
  "1870. Most commissioners found it too large for its neighbors. All three were held for "
  "revisions, as were 78 Cliff Road and a cottage at 13 Williams Street meant for hospital staff.",
 "N6OzW5cIYEo":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 85 Vestal Street, 17 Hooper Farm Road, and 37 Squam Road. Vestal took about thirty "
  "minutes, its seventh appearance before the commission. A main house, guest house and pool sit "
  "on a steep lot. The owner&rsquo;s attorney argued that substantive changes had been made. "
  "Commissioners answered that the overall volume had not come down. One said that if the board "
  "were consistent, the application would have been denied four rounds ago. The chair set out the "
  "three-strike policy: the project is two hearings into three, and a denial can carry prejudice. "
  "By the end the applicant was asking whether a motion to hold for revisions might be had in "
  "place of a denial. The Squam Road application is a sport barn, enclosing a padel court and a "
  "gym on a rural road. Asked why it could not be sunk, the architect cited groundwater. One "
  "commissioner said it reads less like a barn than a ballroom. The commission also voted to "
  "appoint a member to the Planning and Economic Development Commission.",
 "2mbczar2Z_c":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 74 Monomoy Road, window and door revisions at 23 Orange Street, and "
  "a historic determination the preservation planner declined to put on consent. This was a short "
  "meeting, under an hour. Monomoy took the longest, about ten minutes. The Orange Street "
  "application replaces four rotting windows from the 1950s and shifts a set of doors. The "
  "determination was held back because photographs suggested the foundation may have been raised "
  "from its historic elevation, and the planner wanted the board to decide rather than staff. The "
  "commission settled it by changing the figure in the determination from 96 percent to 75. "
  "Twelve Pine Street was called, passed over, and finally dropped when nobody appeared; the "
  "chair allowed six minutes before rescheduling it. Old Business hours move to 4:00 to 7:00 p.m.",
 "mDDZP6jzyOw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a 1985 house on Eel Point Road that cannot be raised to meet FEMA, an unpermitted "
  "tent at 85 Pleasant Street, and Land Bank buildings to be moved off. The Eel Point house is "
  "not a contributing structure, so it cannot be held to anything less than FEMA requires. Staff "
  "was blunt. Without an elevation certificate or a letter from an engineer, raising it is not "
  "going to happen. The Pleasant Street application is after the fact. A hoop tent for equipment "
  "storage went up without approval or a building permit and drew a violation letter from the "
  "zoning inspector, though a surveyor has since confirmed it is out of the setback. The Land "
  "Bank item posed the opposite problem. Commissioners were asked to approve moving buildings off "
  "without being told where they are going, which procurement rules have delayed. Smaller matters "
  "filled the rest: a shed at 57 Somerset Road, a board fence inside a privet hedge, and a "
  "vehicle gate on Monomoy Road.",
 "XlM3j12-4Zg":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new hotel building at 30 North Beach Street, window replacement at 14 New Mill "
  "Street, and a chimney at 126R Main Street. New Mill Street ran longest at about twenty-five "
  "minutes, on an application first heard in August 2024. The hotel took twenty-two. Its "
  "architect noted that the new building sits roughly eight feet back from the structure it "
  "replaces, which is already approved for demolition. The chimney took twenty minutes on its "
  "own, and concerned the chimney alone, with no change to the house. Its representative showed "
  "photographs of neighboring chimneys that run the full height of an exterior wall. The "
  "commission approved it only after stripping the bottom three quarters of the corbelling from "
  "the cap and pulling in its projection. An addition at 83 Washington Street opened the meeting. "
  "The architect offered three schemes and the board took the third, which drops the full-height "
  "columns, on the grounds that the site is very visible. Doors and shutters in Essex Green "
  "passed four to one.",
 "_zsCVlxFAhE":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 9 New Jersey Avenue, a group of sign applications, revisions at 22 Bassett Street, "
  "and a sports court. At four and a half hours, this was the longest meeting of the winter. New "
  "Jersey Avenue took the most time. An approved renovation had evolved until every side of the "
  "old structure was changing, at which point there was little left to keep. The representative "
  "apologized for not returning to the commission before the work was done. The Bassett Street "
  "revisions were pulled off consent by the preservation planner, who thought a second roof walk "
  "central enough to deserve a hearing. The sports court is surfaced in rubberized interlocking "
  "tiles, chosen to kill the noise of hard pack, set at the back of more than an acre in a "
  "subdued gray. The chair headed off a discussion of pickleball. A structure already approved "
  "for Dukes Road was also allowed to move to Vestal Street, carrying its approved changes with "
  "it.",
 "Z20CrXoI_mI":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a house move at 8 North Mill Street, a public hearing on the commission&rsquo;s fee "
  "schedule, and a fence and mechanical equipment at 18 Fair Street. North Mill took about thirty "
  "minutes, the longest of the night. The application is to move the house five feet on its own "
  "lot. It had not been back in a year, held up by matters in the applicant&rsquo;s family, and "
  "the agent pressed to finish so the owner could get a certificate of occupancy. The fee hearing "
  "ran twenty-six minutes. Staff explained that the schedule has not been updated in roughly a "
  "decade, and that the Planning Board and Zoning Board of Appeals schedules have already been "
  "revised, leaving the commission last. Fair Street is St. Paul&rsquo;s Episcopal. The argument "
  "was less about the fence than the machinery. National Grid dictates where the switch gear "
  "sits. The condensers were cut from five units to two, then tucked behind the church sign and a "
  "four-foot privet hedge.",
 "dqOPRbyou5A":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new building at 159 Orange Street, and painting a roof black at 79 Orange Street to "
  "allow rooftop solar. The new building took about thirty-seven minutes, the longest item. "
  "Commissioners wanted the taller mass held away from the street and buffered with trees. The "
  "roof question took twenty-six. The owner needs the shingles dark for the solar array to be "
  "approved, and it was not clear whether the roof should be repainted or replaced. A "
  "commissioner settled it from the table by texting a builder he knew, who reported that paint "
  "on asphalt shingles had held up for six years. The commissioner noted that six years is not "
  "very long. The commission also adopted its meeting and deadline schedule through August 11, "
  "and introduced a lightning round, complete with a sound effect, to move the shorter "
  "applications along.",
 "iJxE4twtAbs":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 7 Orange Street, a new dwelling at 85 Vestal Street, and pools at 8 "
  "and 10 Cato Lane. The meeting opened with two policy votes, on the definition of demolition "
  "and on unapproved demolitions and the fines for them. Orange Street then took the better part "
  "of thirty-five minutes. The chimneys have been moved to the central ridge, and commissioners "
  "compared that against illustrations of the house that stood there, which show a chimney "
  "forward near the front. Vestal took twenty-eight. The chair tried to narrow it, asking the "
  "board to opine on the design of the main house alone. Cato Lane brought the two adjacent lots "
  "onto a single plan at the commission&rsquo;s request, and the applicant reported that the "
  "zoning there requires an extra ten-foot setback nobody had accounted for. The preservation "
  "planner reported that Nantucket is one of about four communities in the Commonwealth receiving "
  "Certified Local Government funding this year.",
 "PtyiiPF9zew":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: housing units being moved off 41 Jefferson Avenue, a church at 42 Monohansett Road, "
  "and a new dwelling at 118 Eel Point Road. The Jefferson move-ons took about thirty minutes "
  "across two applications. The Land Bank and the Housing Authority are relocating the units to "
  "159 Hummock Pond Road and 158 Madaket Road. One is the only two-story structure in the group. "
  "Another shares a wall with a unit that stays behind, which leaves a blank elevation to be "
  "dealt with once it is exposed. The church took twenty-two minutes. It revises a building "
  "approved on the site in 2004 and is smaller than what was permitted then. Eel Point ran "
  "thirteen. Its architect said the street carries a range of architectural language, some of it "
  "ornate, and that the design aims between. The commission also ratified its votes from the week "
  "before on the definition of demolition and on unapproved demolitions and fines.",
 "3iV2LhPm4gQ":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a 400-square-foot addition at 4 Westmoor Lane, and a new dwelling at 7 Orange Street. "
  "Westmoor Lane ran about thirty minutes. The longest item of the night, at thirty-seven, was an "
  "application the chair took unusual care to frame. He set out the order of business before "
  "anyone spoke. Presentation, then questions, then comments confined to the commission&rsquo;s "
  "purview of shape, massing and architectural features, then deliberation. He noted that this "
  "was a public meeting and not a public hearing. Orange Street took twenty-two minutes and ended "
  "in a condition about planting. Screening on the exposed side must have a columnar habit, which "
  "rules out arborvitae and leyland cypress. The commission took that in place of a trellis to "
  "soften the gable facing the neighbor.",
 "5tDKuVEgfoI":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: an addition at 55 Baxter Road, a new dwelling and guest house at 44 Nonantum Avenue, "
  "and alterations on Tuckernuck. Baxter Road took about thirty minutes, the longest of the "
  "night. The architect described the house, called Flagship, as one of the oldest on that road, "
  "if not the oldest. Nonantum took twenty. The existing guest house is to be donated and moved "
  "away, with a new main house and guest house in their place. The commission held it for a view, "
  "asking for stakes set at the outside corners so the footprint can be read on the ground. "
  "Tuckernuck took nineteen, and the problem was not design. The project was approved last "
  "summer. Afterward the applicant found a conservation restriction from 1974. It limits the site "
  "to a single story and a footprint of 1,800 square feet. He has been negotiating with the "
  "conservancy that holds it.",
 "Hc6UxWDKHHQ":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the church at 42 Monohansett Road, the gym structure at 37 Squam Road, and two "
  "applicants who asked to be held. The meeting opened with the held requests and ran half an "
  "hour. One came in the afternoon before, the other arrived just before the previous meeting, "
  "once it was clear there would be no quorum. The chair pressed staff on when each had been "
  "received and whether abutters had been in touch. Monohansett took twenty-four minutes. Little "
  "was left to settle beyond the treatment of the exterior stair and kick panels on the doors "
  "facing the parking. Squam Road took nineteen, and is the structure the commission has been "
  "calling a sport barn since January. Another applicant spent nearly half an hour on a dormer. "
  "Before the vote the chair paused to ask whether the condition being proposed would actually "
  "suit him, or whether revisions would serve better.",
 "11PVvcMAN_0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a citizen article on demolition permits, hardscape at 22 Nonantum Avenue, and a house "
  "whose move fell through. The article came first and ran nearly half an hour. Article 72 on the "
  "Annual Town Meeting warrant would change the quantum of votes required for a demolition "
  "permit. The chair asked for it on the agenda after it surfaced at a meeting of the local "
  "government program. Nonantum drew a long line of public comment. The hardscape plan filed in "
  "2023 was scanned into the database in a form nobody can read. The move case has a longer "
  "history. The board found the house significant and contributing in January 2025 and allowed a "
  "move but not a demolition. A destination at 4 Pocomo Road was approved, then fell through when "
  "moving it in sections proved impractical. A new owner has now come forward. A cattle grate at "
  "190 Cliff Road took twelve minutes.",
 "Afy59fFqR0I":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a fence at the pocket park at 106 Main Street, a new dwelling at 44 Easton Street, "
  "and a garage apartment at 28 Miacomet Road. No single item dominated this meeting, which ran "
  "past four hours on a long list of small ones. Main Street is a Land Bank park. Vegetation was "
  "cleared and planters added last year, which brought noticeably more people in, and neighbors "
  "then asked for a fence along one side. Easton Street takes its cues from the house next door, "
  "historic and moved to the site from town years ago, borrowing its hipped roof and the handling "
  "of its dormers. It sits lower to the ground because it is in the flood zone rather than the "
  "velocity zone. Miacomet Road is a garage studio joined to the house by a roof carried off the "
  "existing porch. It passed, but not unanimously, with one vote against and another cast "
  "reluctantly.",
 "owKQTyFaGYc":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 118 Eel Point Road, several applications at 7 Maxey Pond Road, and "
  "85 Vestal Street. Eel Point took about forty minutes, by far the longest. The revisions answer "
  "earlier objections. The center gable still carries the height, but the rest of the structure "
  "now sits between twenty-five and twenty-seven feet, the chimney owls are gone, and the front "
  "porch has been cut back eighteen inches. Maxey Pond took twenty-three. The applicant asked to "
  "begin with the garage rather than the pool house, since the garage had been the "
  "commission&rsquo;s main concern. Vestal took twenty-two. An abutter from 34 Dukes Road thanked "
  "the team for downsizing but warned that without meaningful screening the buildings will be "
  "plainly visible from his road in winter.",
 "PBJ6erEf4aA":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the new harbormaster building at 34 Washington Street, a new dwelling at 123 Orange "
  "Street, and a driveway wall at 126 Main Street. Washington Street took the longest, about "
  "twenty minutes, without ever being opened. No representative appeared. The commission used the "
  "time to look at what staff had done with the filing, which ran to 124 pages before it was "
  "pared back. Orange Street took seventeen. The new dwelling sits behind a duplex already "
  "approved, reached down the side from Bear Way. Main Street drew the sharpest comment of the "
  "night. A speaker pointed out that the applicant had subdivided the lot voluntarily. The "
  "driveway and garage had served 126 Main Street for well over a century, and the side yard is "
  "already crowded. An earlier vote had limited the driveway to a width no greater than that of a "
  "typical car.",
 "t3tm4p6EUhk":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 9 New Jersey Street, and a gambrel whose eaves and dormers the "
  "commission worked over at length. New Jersey took about sixty-eight minutes. That is the "
  "longest any single application has held this commission so far this year, and it ran so long "
  "the chair called a two-minute break in the middle of it. The gambrel took twenty-three. "
  "Commissioners wanted the eave dropped a foot or so to distinguish the wings from the main "
  "mass, and said the pergolas out front were not the way to achieve it. They also found the "
  "reverse gambrels running perpendicular to the main mass too close to the dormers at the ends. "
  "A later item turned on whether a motion was, as someone put it, essentially a ratification of "
  "the previous approvals. The commission decided it was not that simple.",
 "o8zL755X_68":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a renovation at 5 New Street, and a new dwelling at 6 Berkeley Avenue. New Street "
  "took about fifty minutes. The building is the old warehouse of Murray&rsquo;s Toggery Shop, "
  "and the applicant had worked with staff to compile its history before coming in. Berkeley "
  "Avenue took forty-three. The house it replaces was approved for demolition and is effectively "
  "invisible, reached by an easement across another property and screened entirely by the house "
  "in front. The architect could only show it to the commission in a photograph taken from a "
  "drone. The new house is proposed within four feet of where the old one stands, on the flat "
  "ground that is the only sensible place for it. The meeting also spent its first forty-eight "
  "minutes on consents, signs and staff comments, with one consent item pulled onto the regular "
  "agenda.",
 "9bzB9fAjaak":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a driveway gate at 18 Gladlands Avenue, and the new dwelling at 118 Eel Point Road. "
  "Gladlands took about fifty-seven minutes, the longest item of the night. It covered a gate, a "
  "deer fence, and an area of Belgian block. Parts of it had already been approved through staff. "
  "The commission worked through the plan and the neighbors&rsquo; situation in detail before "
  "settling it. Eel Point took forty-six. The commission has seen it more than once, and the "
  "comments have centered on height, which the applicant has now dropped. The meeting opened with "
  "a long stretch of routine business, running more than forty minutes through consents, five "
  "signs carrying recommendations from the sign advisory council, and questions for the "
  "preservation planner.",
 "iUP58CygDXc":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 2 Sea Street, a cedar fence at 27 Ridge Lane, and a new dwelling at "
  "56 Vesper Lane. Sea Street took about thirty minutes. Ridge Lane took twenty-five, and the "
  "application is after the fact. The fence is in Fishers Landing and is already built. The "
  "applicant opened by apologizing for not having asked first. Vesper Lane took twenty-two. The "
  "owners want a second dwelling beside the one they live in, kept deliberately plain. It is a "
  "simple single-family house with an accessory unit above a garage off the side. The trim is "
  "natural cedar, the sash black, the roof black asphalt. This was a shorter meeting than most, "
  "at two and a half hours.",
 "P3GAdWxF1sw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new cottage and garage at 35 Morey Lane, and three applications to move houses onto "
  "112 Wauwinet Road. Morey Lane took about thirty-four minutes, the longest of the night. Much "
  "of it went to a new hedge meant to screen a porch and tie it into the lawn, and to photographs "
  "taken from the lane in both directions. Wauwinet took twenty-four. The house being moved there "
  "comes from 81 Pocomo Road. Its agent recapped the history. More than a year ago the commission "
  "found the house historically significant and approved a move but not a demolition. It has "
  "taken until now to find it somewhere to go. Another new dwelling took thirty-one minutes and "
  "came back visibly altered. The roof walk is narrower, a projecting gable over the front door "
  "is gone, and the continuous eave at the dormer is now broken by a shingle course. The "
  "commission also discussed holding an organizational meeting.",
 "PvM6e314m8w":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the airport terminal at 14 Airport Road, town buildings to be demolished or moved at "
  "39 Washington Street, and a fence at 38 Lovers Lane. The airport took about eighteen minutes. "
  "Its architect appeared for both the town and the airport, and described the purpose plainly: "
  "take down the seasonal hold room tent and build in its place. Washington Street took "
  "seventeen. The town is asking to demolish or move two buildings there, with a third "
  "application on Okorwaw Avenue alongside it. Lovers Lane took fourteen and was the simplest "
  "thing on the agenda. The owner wanted a fence and gate to keep deer off her hydrangeas, said "
  "so in about a sentence, and the commission approved it four to one.",
 "MEmLXqb3Pkw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: window replacement at 14 New Mill Street, and fenestration changes at 4 South Mill "
  "Street. This was a short meeting, under an hour and a half, and no item ran past twenty "
  "minutes. New Mill Street took sixteen. The application has been in front of the commission on "
  "and off since 2024, and the board had to sort out who was seated on it before the presentation "
  "could begin. South Mill Street opened old business. The preservation planner noted that she "
  "had written comments on every application on the agenda. Some ran long, because several were "
  "appearing yet again.",
 "RWeo6WkWh8E":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a Superior Court remand concerning 43A Union Street, hardscape at 50 Starbuck Road, "
  "and a carport. The remand came first, though only a preliminary discussion was held. Town "
  "counsel George Pucci laid out the history. The commission denied permission to demolish a "
  "garage at 43A Union Street. The owner appealed to the Select Board, which upheld the denial, "
  "then appealed again to Superior Court, where the case was tried and the preservation planner "
  "testified for the commission. The judge has sent it back. Commissioners were urged to read the "
  "decision themselves, and the formal remand hearing the court ordered was set for August 4. "
  "Elsewhere a carport passed four to one, over an objection about what would be visible from a "
  "public way.",
 "FB47DL6Qxz4":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the new harbormaster building at 34 Washington Street, and a pool at 3 Pops Lane. "
  "Washington Street took about forty-five minutes, by far the longest. Two firms presented for "
  "the town. They came with a supplemental document a commissioner had asked for at the previous "
  "hearing. It sets out, point by point, how the current revisions differ from what was submitted "
  "and approved in 2020. Pops Lane opened old business. A fence at 11 Bluet Court, held for a "
  "view at the last meeting, was passed over again because nobody appeared to represent it.",
 "uBLfKHX_Jho":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the Superior Court remand hearing on 43A Union Street. It took roughly three quarters "
  "of an hour and opened the meeting. Town counsel explained the posture. The court has ordered "
  "the commission to rehear the application to demolish a garage and to issue a new decision. The "
  "required public notice had been given, so the rehearing could proceed properly. Commissioners "
  "had been told the week before to read the judge&rsquo;s decision for themselves. The "
  "preservation planner reported separately on a Community Preservation Committee project with "
  "the Preservation Institute Nantucket, a study of Main Street streetscapes, and asked "
  "commissioners to circulate its survey before it closes at Labor Day. Later, on a historic "
  "determination, she asked that any approval carry an explicit finding that the structure will "
  "keep its historic character once the work is done. A commissioner stopped the vote to confirm "
  "that the motion included her language.",
 "CMf5ux4kuJw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the house move at 8 North Mill Street, and the new building at 1 Cherry Street. This "
  "was a short meeting, a little over an hour. North Mill Street took twenty-six minutes. Cherry "
  "Street took twenty-two and was the last application of the day, once the others in the queue "
  "had asked to be held. It passed four to one. Earlier the commission approved work at the Salt "
  "Marsh Center and added language of its own to the motion. The building is not owned by the "
  "town, but the land under it is, and the property is public.",
 "a-Y1_klc8sw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: as-built revisions at 34 Bosworth Road, and the Affordable Housing Trust&rsquo;s "
  "production plan. Bosworth Road took about thirty-two minutes. Its agent called the project old "
  "and cold. He had drawn it in 2016, and it failed its final inspection then over trim color, "
  "which is why it is back. The fine was waived. The housing discussion came early and ran about "
  "fifteen minutes. A representative of the housing office walked the commission through the "
  "production plan and what it might mean for this board. The Superior Court remand on 43A Union "
  "Street was on the agenda again. The commission also declined to take up one application at "
  "all, the chair saying he was not inclined to hear it without anyone there to explain it.",
 "LXMbFjX7OLY":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: an addition to the barn at 39 Orange Street, the new building at 1 Cherry Street, and "
  "the harbormaster building at 34 Washington Street. Orange Street and Cherry Street together "
  "filled fifty-five minutes. Washington Street took thirty-three. Its architects put three "
  "elevations on one page: the original approved in 2020, the revision brought on July 28, and "
  "what they proposed now in answer to the comments they received then. A new dwelling at 19R "
  "Hooper Farm Road took twenty-eight minutes. A commissioner opened the meeting with a question "
  "he had not been able to get a plain answer to. What percentage of a structure has to go before "
  "the work counts as a demolition?",
 "U3vWtAKmR_w":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the commission&rsquo;s own rules. This was a special organizational meeting, held on "
  "a Friday and running a little over an hour, with no applications on the agenda at all. The "
  "commission worked through a list of its own making. Whether to hold applications for a view "
  "only. How as-built reviews should be handled and what a motion on one should say. A tiered "
  "structure of fines for as-builts. The definition of demolition, including how other "
  "commissions set their fines. Guidelines for fences, gates and cattle grates, which have come "
  "before the board repeatedly this year. And updates to both the approved roof shingle colors "
  "and the approved paint colors. Scheduling was deferred, since not every commissioner was "
  "present to settle it.",
 "h9rOTfDcaFs":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: brickwork at 51 Main Street, and a report from the preservation planner. Main Street "
  "took about thirty-nine minutes, the longest item. The application is to take brick off a "
  "historic facade and put it back. It came to the full board rather than staff for a reason. On "
  "a facade like that, the choice of brick and of mortar is the whole question. The "
  "planner&rsquo;s report ran eighteen minutes. She told the commission she had written to the "
  "island&rsquo;s representatives about proposed changes to Section 106 of the National Historic "
  "Preservation Act, and that she considers them a serious mistake. She also flagged an "
  "application on Derry Lane that had not been highlighted, because the site abuts Land Bank open "
  "space.",
 "HD3JdSUxHik":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a cottage and duplex at 4 Rose Bud Lane, and a shed at 37 Cliff Road. Rose Bud Lane "
  "took about thirty-three minutes, the longest of the night, and most of it went to screening. "
  "Liquid amber trees go at the front on either side, Swedish aspen every ten feet down the east "
  "side. The dormers on the cottage were stepped back and the trim around the door made heavier. "
  "Cliff Road returned for a second look. The decking had been shown wrapping the pool in the "
  "earlier drawings, and the applicant explained that the landscape architect will come "
  "separately rather than assume permission. As-built deer fences, gates and cattle grates at 6 "
  "and 8 Clifford Street also came back.",
 "ZM3pyBusX7Q":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a pool, a building held over a conflict, and a new pump station at 77 Bartlett Road. "
  "The pool took about twenty-five minutes. Its designer argued the size down carefully. Fourteen "
  "feet by fourteen is 196 square feet, above the 150 that would make it small by definition. But "
  "on his own survey of the seventeen pools in that neighborhood, it would still be by far the "
  "smallest. It carried, with one nay. Another application stalled before it began. The presenter "
  "had scanned the building inside and out, as he does with historic structures, but a "
  "commissioner turned out to have drawn the previous plans for the same owner, could not sit, "
  "and the item was held.",
 "pnZoUjplfHY":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: four applications at 5 Nonantum Avenue, and a garage apartment at 1 Mothball Way. "
  "This was a short meeting, an hour and a half. Nonantum took about twenty minutes for all four. "
  "The commission worked through them in sequence. It approved the new dwelling, then the cabana, "
  "an open structure at the back of the site with a small bathroom attached, then the shed and "
  "the pool together as revised. It went quickly enough that a commissioner proposed cutting to "
  "the chase and taking the last two in one motion.",
 "i1EG5Xr1Ong":
  "The Land Bank comes to relocate part of Falmouth Avenue, the dirt road that reaches Cisco "
  "Beach from the east. Its southwest end has been compromised by erosion, so the plan is to pull "
  "that stretch north, away from the resource area, and tie it back into the existing road. Its "
  "executive director and attorney both attend. The hearing itself is not long. The order of "
  "conditions takes half an hour. No fertilizer or agricultural chemicals. Every planting a "
  "native species, with no cultivars. A yearly report with photographs showing what has survived. "
  "If the commission later decides more planting is needed, tonight&rsquo;s discussion authorizes "
  "it. Also heard: a Sconset Trust application on Baxter Road, two Pocomo Road parcels, 67 Squam "
  "Road, 16 North Beach Street, 8 Brant Point Road and 21R Allens Lane. The Baxter Road "
  "alternative access and 54 Washington Street were continued to the next meeting.",
 "NR4b9nR8Na4":
  "Most of the evening goes to 94 Hulbert Avenue. The owners want to enclose an existing porch, "
  "put living space and a second floor above it, and add to the west side of the house. The "
  "complication is what surrounds it. A coastal dune runs east from the property, the one that "
  "holds the tennis court and carries on to Jetties Beach. The commission also counts two "
  "separate stretches of coastal bank on the site, by its own policy rather than by the state "
  "map. That hearing takes thirty-seven minutes. Thirteen Commercial Wharf takes thirteen. The "
  "Land Bank is back with 22 Cathcart Road, and the Baxter Road alternative access is continued "
  "again, to February. A request for delineation at 15 Meadow Lane and an extension for the "
  "Sconset Beach Preservation Trust round out the list. Under enforcement the commission notes "
  "34A Grove Lane, which will appear on its agenda for months.",
 "m6I9kcuPnOY":
  "Two hearings take more than an hour between them. The first is 2 Shore Road, which the "
  "surveyor describes as looking more like a driveway than a road, running off Francis Street. "
  "What makes it awkward is the company it keeps: commercial, municipal and residential uses on "
  "every side, with the town&rsquo;s Salt Marsh Center and its rear parking lot immediately "
  "north. That takes thirty-six minutes. The second is 113 Eel Point Road, which takes "
  "thirty-two. The Land Bank returns with 41 Jefferson Avenue, a property that will occupy "
  "several boards this year. Also before the commission: 1 Allens Lane, a minor modification at 3 "
  "Pops Lane, and an extension covering 34 West Chester Street and 3 Westco Place. Baxter Road "
  "alternative access, 22 Cathcart Road and 94 Hulbert Avenue are all continued to the "
  "nineteenth. Enforcement at 34A Grove Lane remains open.",
 "hDDASgQn3i0":
  "A remote meeting, and a long agenda moved briskly. The town brings 130 Tom Nevers Road, "
  "presented by a wetland scientist working for it, and the commission holds presenters to five "
  "minutes. That is the longest hearing at twenty-one minutes. Nine other notices of intent are "
  "taken up. Among them are 1 Pawguvet Lane, 74 Monomoy Road, 3 Sherburne Way, and an application "
  "from the Nantucket Center for Elder Affairs at 81 Washington Street. Four minor modifications "
  "are handled together. The evening ends with a discussion of the Sconset Beach Preservation "
  "Trust&rsquo;s sand plan, and then enforcement: the Baxter Road geotubes, and 34A Grove Lane "
  "again.",
 "kVu7-F5GN78":
  "The enforcement discussion is the meeting. The order that authorized the geotubes along Baxter "
  "Road has expired, and the commission had planned to talk about amending an enforcement order "
  "so more sand could be placed. Events overtook that. Recent activity on the site has punctured "
  "the geotubes and left them deflated, which changes what is being asked and what can be "
  "allowed. A commissioner raises a plainer objection to the document in front of them. He is not "
  "comfortable calling the memo a plan, because he cannot see the steps in it. The conversation "
  "runs from before six until past seven and takes in 34A Grove Lane as well. The rest of the "
  "agenda is ordinary by comparison. A notice of intent for 53 Wanoma Way. Orders of conditions "
  "for 94 Hulbert Avenue, 74 Monomoy Road, 130 Tom Nevers Road, 275 Polpis Road and 39 Monomoy "
  "Road. And the Baxter Road alternative access, continued once more.",
 "58ciN3pF8xM":
  "The Land Bank spends nearly an hour on two adjoining parcels at East Creek Road. It bought 16 "
  "East Creek in 2010, and that one is already public: a parking lot, a mown area and benches "
  "looking over the creek. It bought 19 East Creek in 2022, after the previous owner had started "
  "renovating. The house was left gutted and the dock is dilapidated. The proposal is to take the "
  "structure down and put in a scenic boardwalk and a rebuilt dock, both accessible. Engineers "
  "present the design, again inside the commission&rsquo;s five-minute limit. A second long "
  "stretch, thirty-three minutes, goes to requests for determination, including 11 H Street and "
  "two certificates of compliance. The Baxter Road alternative access is heard again. And once "
  "more the meeting closes on the Sconset Beach Preservation Trust&rsquo;s sand plan and on "
  "enforcement at the Baxter Road geotubes and 34A Grove Lane.",
 "sEPr8AoUZoQ":
  "A short meeting, under an hour, and it opens with a warning about telephones. Anyone using a "
  "cell phone in the room, the chair says, even to send a text, makes it part of the record under "
  "the open meeting law. If you need to use it, step outside. Public comment is capped at three "
  "minutes and taken without deliberation, following the procedure the Select Board uses. Nobody "
  "speaks. The hearing then turns to 62 Wanoma Way, and to the Land Bank&rsquo;s East Creek Road "
  "project, which returns for its order of conditions and takes twenty-six minutes. Hoicks Hollow "
  "Road is continued yet again. Enforcement on the Baxter Road geotubes is continued to the "
  "sixteenth.",
 "uS0rcVBo0tE":
  "Forty-five minutes, and one substantial item. The town brings its dredging plan for Polpis "
  "Harbor and Hither Creek, presented by the Natural Resources Department with its consultants. "
  "That hearing runs nineteen minutes and is most of the meeting. The other notice of intent, 14 "
  "Bassett Road, is closed without a single commissioner asking to open it. Four certificates of "
  "compliance are issued, covering 30 Willard Street, 10 New Whale Street, 1 Pawguvet Lane and 6 "
  "Harbor Terrace. The commission also takes up the planting list and sedimentation control plan "
  "for East Creek, the project it spent an hour on in March. Enforcement now carries three "
  "entries: 21R Madaket Road, the Baxter Road geotubes, and 34A Grove Lane.",
 "KHvc6Z_dzWA":
  "One item, an hour, and no applications at all. When the commission rewrote the town&rsquo;s "
  "wetlands protection regulations, effective at the start of 2025, it promised to hold hearings "
  "twice a year on further changes. This is one of them. A member of the public had submitted two "
  "proposed definitions, and the commission works through them. A commissioner raises something "
  "else he had emailed about beforehand: applications that get continued almost in perpetuity, "
  "month after month, without ever being decided. He says plainly that he has no good solution to "
  "it. Staff is asked whether any logistical revisions are needed on its side as well.",
 "IpQOZn9JNE4":
  "A short evening, chaired by the vice chair, and nobody comes to speak. The commissioners "
  "decline to open the notices of intent that require no waiver, which is their way of saying the "
  "files are in order. The longest stretch, twenty-eight minutes, covers 6 New Hummock Circle and "
  "240 Polpis Road, and the town&rsquo;s dredging plan for Polpis Harbor and Hither Creek returns "
  "for its order of conditions. Two items of other business follow. The East Creek planting list "
  "and sedimentation control plan come back for review. And the Land Bank brings a beach "
  "management plan for 41 Jefferson Avenue. Enforcement on 21R Madaket Road and the Baxter Road "
  "geotubes stays open.",
 "ority6IKFlM":
  "Most of the meeting is spent closing things out rather than opening them. Forty-seven minutes "
  "go to certificates of compliance, and to the conditions that stay in force after a project is "
  "finished. On one pool the commission reads out seven continuing conditions. The pool may never "
  "be drained or discharged into land under its jurisdiction. It may not be chemically treated "
  "between November 1 and May 1. Treatment must stop three weeks before any draining, and the "
  "water has to be pumped out by truck to the treatment plant. Exterior lighting must point away "
  "from the resource area. The Land Bank appears four times, on 2 Milestone Road and 174 Orange "
  "Street, 231 Polpis Road, 50-56 Madaket Road and 1 Osprey Way. Also heard: 28 Lily Street, 11 "
  "Jonathan Way and 100 Low Beach Road. The town&rsquo;s dredging plan for Polpis Harbor and "
  "Hither Creek is continued again, as is 240 Polpis Road.",
 "MBGzvmDF2BE":
  "Twenty-five minutes, start to finish, and nothing contested. One notice of intent is heard, "
  "for 268 Polpis Road. The dredging plan for Polpis Harbor and Hither Creek is continued, as is "
  "240 Polpis Road. So is the Hoicks Hollow Road application, which has been rolling forward "
  "since January. There are no certificates of compliance and no extension requests. A monitoring "
  "report notes upcoming maintenance at 42 Shimmo Pond Road. One commissioner joins from his "
  "boat. Enforcement on 21R Madaket Road is continued to the eighteenth, and the Baxter Road "
  "geotubes stay on the list.",
 "h6z1q8jKrxg":
  "The evening turns on how to take the geotubes out. More than an hour and a half goes to the "
  "removal along Baxter Road. The tubes were installed to stabilize the toe of the bluff and stop "
  "it eroding, and by the applicant&rsquo;s account that worked. Pulling them out could "
  "destabilize what they were holding. A geotechnical engineer has recommended shoring in front "
  "of 93 Baxter Road, and possibly 97 as well if the damage there requires removal. Those houses "
  "sit very close to the top of the bluff. The commission works through the demolition sheet by "
  "sheet. The president of the Nantucket Coastal Conservancy speaks during public comment, with a "
  "question about the agenda itself. Elsewhere the Land Bank brings 25 New Lane and 10 Grove "
  "Lane, and the dredging plan and 240 Polpis Road are heard again.",
 "ASfJm_7eUYo":
  "Seventy-nine minutes, mostly on septic. A lot beside Hummock Pond has a system that failed on "
  "separation from high groundwater, and the whole of it sits inside the hundred-foot buffer to "
  "bordering vegetated wetland and coastal bank. The applicant asks for a waiver to replace it "
  "with an innovative and alternative system, arguing that it improves on what is there. No work "
  "is proposed in the flood zone or within twenty-five feet. The leach field has to sit inside "
  "the hundred, because the lot offers nowhere else. The Board of Health has already asked for a "
  "monitoring well, which may change the design. The town brings the Sparks Avenue roadway. The "
  "Land Bank reopens the Falmouth Avenue relocation. The Conservation Foundation brings South "
  "Pastures. Four certificates of compliance are issued for 73 Hulbert Avenue alone. Enforcement "
  "adds 16 Meadow Lane to the list.",
 "HV7z5bbxQec":
  "Near the end, the commission is told the harbor plan is going to the Select Board for "
  "approval, after four years. A commissioner notes the statute allowed twenty-four months. Staff "
  "then warns the commission to buckle up, because he expects to set off what he calls a "
  "firestorm of snow fencing enforcements before long. Nantucket Island Resort brings 19 North "
  "Water Street and 3 White Elephant Way. The Land Bank brings 101 Miacomet Avenue. There are "
  "applications at 27 Nonantum Avenue, 73 Hulbert Avenue and 83 Squam Road, and a delineation at "
  "55 Baxter Road. Enforcement carries 21R Madaket Road, 16 Meadow Lane and the Baxter Road "
  "geotubes.",
 "hmRcTWVL8sc":
  "Sixteen minutes, the shortest meeting of the year. Everyone is present, so the chair dispenses "
  "with roll call votes and asks for a show of hands. Two notices of intent are heard, 23 Dukes "
  "Road and 14 Almanack Pond Road. The Osprey Way and Falmouth Avenue applications are carried "
  "forward. Seven certificates of compliance are issued in a block. They cover 5 Gingy Lane, 2 "
  "Wanoma Way, 15 North Water Street, 59 Polpis Road, 10 Westmoor Lane, 231 Polpis Road and the "
  "Conservation Foundation&rsquo;s Windswept Bog. Enforcement on 21R Madaket Road and the Baxter "
  "Road geotubes remains open, and the commission adjourns.",
 "0LNsvAHjmV8":
  "An hour and a quarter on one application, and it is the Cisco Beach driveway. The Land Bank "
  "wants to shift part of the access drive that runs from the beach parking lot east to Falmouth "
  "Avenue, moving it further from the dune. The commission had reopened this on July 2 and "
  "continued it to tonight. The Land Bank&rsquo;s executive director attends with its attorney, "
  "who says he will not belabor a project the commission has now seen across several meetings. A "
  "second stretch of twenty-three minutes goes to the order of conditions. Also heard: 53 "
  "Nobadeer Avenue, 145 Wauwinet Road, 27 Osprey Way, and two certificates of compliance at 174 "
  "Cliff Road. Enforcement stands at three items.",
 "GnujJeipits":
  "A museum wants people to be able to use its grounds. The Egan foundation brings 156 and 158 "
  "Polpis Road, proposing at-grade decking to connect the parking area to the building and stone "
  "terrace seating cut into the hillside on the west side. That is part of a forty-two minute "
  "opening stretch. The rest of the meeting takes in the Procaccianti Group, Nantucket Golf Club "
  "at 250 Milestone Road, 10 Straight Wharf, 9 Wauwinet Road and 7 Kelley Road all heard. "
  "Committee reports bring word that the state hearing on the harbor plan falls on September 8, "
  "and that the commission&rsquo;s role there is mainly to listen. Enforcement occupies a good "
  "deal of what follows. Several actions are listed and several more are described as potential.",
 "Vtnlm7kaL8Y":
  "The last forty-eight minutes are about the calendar. The commission needs a special meeting "
  "for the Sconset Beach Preservation Fund, and it has already scheduled one on October 29 to "
  "take up revisions to the wetlands regulations. Staff says plainly that he has a significant "
  "amount of legwork to do for the regulations meeting, all of it falling in October. He proposes "
  "pushing the regulations to November so the beach fund can have the earlier slot. The hearings "
  "before that include the Nantucket Golf Club at 250 Milestone Road, 124 Tom Nevers Road, 198 "
  "Hummock Pond Road and 43A Massachusetts Avenue. The Sconset Beach Preservation Fund also "
  "appears on a certificate of compliance for 79-81 Baxter Road. Enforcement adds 27 Washington "
  "Street to a list that already runs to several addresses.",
 "F1XLJtgXvc8":
  "A special meeting, called for one question, and it takes an hour and a quarter. The question "
  "is the coir array at 79-81 Baxter Road, and whether the commission should issue an enforcement "
  "order allowing the array to be repaired or maintained and sand to be placed. Staff sets out "
  "the reasoning against it. An enforcement order exists to restore compliance, not to authorize "
  "new work, so it cannot be used to permit a project that already has a permit. That leaves two "
  "possibilities. The work proceeds under the order of conditions issued in January. Or it "
  "proceeds under a certificate of compliance, which was never issued because there is no "
  "structure there to be in compliance. The commission ends by rescinding a vote it had taken and "
  "referring the matter to town counsel.",
 "5Aq158gLpf8":
  "Two citizen articles take the meeting. Article D would change the Planning and Economic "
  "Development Commission. Members debate whether appointment by the Select Board already puts a "
  "candidate under enough public scrutiny, or whether more is needed. Article F would create an "
  "offshore wind stabilization fund. The discussion covers money now arriving as a result of "
  "project failures, and the good neighbor agreement that covers two large lease areas. Town "
  "counsel is not present, and the chair steers the committee back to what it can decide without "
  "him. Attendance is briefly interrupted while a participant is identified by name for the "
  "record.",
 "ZU_dFJn8Kes":
  "Two meetings in one sitting. The Nantucket County Review Committee goes first, on the fiscal "
  "2027 county budget. The Registry of Deeds, which houses three full-time employees, comes to "
  "$589,000 with administrative expenses and any special projects. The public safety facility "
  "debt service is offset by a $250,000 receipt. Because the police side of that building was a "
  "debt exclusion, the transfer reduces what the town raises above the levy limit. The Finance "
  "Committee then takes four citizen articles. On the home rule petition to change to a town "
  "council form of government, a member objects that Town Meeting will not have the charter text "
  "in front of it to read and study. He says the process was not the best way to approach it. On "
  "the Boynton Lane acquisition, the petitioner explains that the objective is to make the road "
  "public rather than private, so it can be maintained.",
 "o3Vwp_f7Awc":
  "The committee begins reviewing fiscal 2027 operating budgets. The housing department asks for "
  "a second rental property manager, to work alongside the current one on maintenance and "
  "management of municipal employee housing, and for increases to the employee housing line. "
  "Another department explains how it paces its work. It runs one survey every two to two and a "
  "half years, depending on how long field data takes, and relies on four or five summer interns. "
  "Committee reports follow, including an account of a working group and who sits on it.",
 "HnBBVXZtrgw":
  "Most of two hours goes to departmental budgets. Visitor services describes steady demand for "
  "merchandise, and its practice of keeping a daily running list of availability for the "
  "independent walking and ghost tours, with bus tours leaving from outside. Public works "
  "describes new treatment for the liquid that comes off catch basin debris. Oils and volatile "
  "organic compounds would be removed before it enters the plant, rather than the material being "
  "dried and returned to road base. The ambulance and nursing accounts follow. The private rate "
  "has risen to $560 and will increase 2.8 percent a year going forward, admissions are up to "
  "forty-seven, and the department has begun marketing where it previously did almost none.",
 "e86jaKN0jZk":
  "A public hearing on the warrant, and one article draws most of it. Walter Flaherty explains "
  "his citizen article for stop signs where Miacomet Road meets Surfside Road, a proposal he "
  "began about four years ago through the traffic study work group. The alternatives considered "
  "then were stop signs or straightening the intersection. The committee settles on recommending "
  "no action, with a comment noting that a pilot is already in place, and the petitioner accepts "
  "that. In public comment a seasonal resident speaks from notes to keep inside the three-minute "
  "limit. He discloses that he sits on the Coastal Resilience Advisory Committee and has recused "
  "himself from its Baxter Road and geotube discussions.",
 "XsYH_4F2-T0":
  "The Planning and Economic Development Commission comes to explain its own article. It wants "
  "the word planning out of its name, because people take it for the Planning Board, and it wants "
  "its mission restated to make clear that it is advisory rather than leading. The conversation "
  "widens. A member asks how anyone is supposed to join an advisory board, learn the ropes and "
  "work up to the Planning Board or the Select Board. Serving, he says, should not cost them "
  "money. A later appropriation is discussed in terms of keeping a place in the queue, so that "
  "the town can continue monitoring a technology as it develops.",
 "ajUYmgrubCI":
  "Zoning, article after article. The committee works through amendments 38 to 61, all sponsored "
  "by the Planning Board. On the use chart for large restaurants, members try to name which "
  "existing places would fall in the range and conclude there are few. They note the change would "
  "open the commercial neighborhood district in Mid Island to more of them. The proposal came "
  "from staff rather than from any applicant. On accessory dwelling units, the discussion turns "
  "to state law, under which the building official has to decide what is reasonable when a lot is "
  "already at its ground cover limit. Conveying units into separate ownership is taken up as "
  "well, along with what happens to pre-existing non-conforming height, ground cover and setbacks "
  "once an owner starts changing them.",
 "VIGcsrhx35c":
  "The Community Preservation Committee walks the committee through six months of work. An "
  "information session was held for potential applicants to explain changes in the grant process. "
  "Applications were due in October, interviews ran into November, deliberations followed in "
  "December, and the report now in front of the Finance Committee came out of that. Article 69, "
  "on membership of the Council for Human Services, draws a motion to take no action. A member "
  "notes its purpose was to get the Planning and Economic Development Commission to make an "
  "appointment, and that if the town cannot compel that, the article does not accomplish it. "
  "Later the committee takes up an $8 million appropriation, to be spent by the town manager with "
  "Select Board approval, contingent on a successful ballot vote at the annual town election. The "
  "chair reminds members that it is a debt exclusion.",
 "6wxoWropazI":
  "Two long articles. Article 71 would establish a general bylaw for stormwater management. The "
  "committee works through the definitions and confirms that areas with higher potential for "
  "pollutants are mapped through the town&rsquo;s GIS. A member asks whether the utility will "
  "grow as sea level rise and water quality push more activity through it. The answer is that it "
  "allows for stormwater master plans covering pumping for resiliency as well as quality. Article "
  "73 would license Baxter Road property for an erosion control structure. The Natural Resources "
  "Department explains that a town bylaw requires license agreements to come before Town Meeting "
  "before the Select Board can issue them. This one has been on the warrant in two previous years "
  "and was set aside both times.",
 "qyUKtjKh838":
  "A long list of appropriations. Design money for public works facility improvements is "
  "discussed alongside the working group now assembled around it. A member suggests looking at "
  "what another project did before repeating last year&rsquo;s objections. The Board of Health "
  "septic betterment loan program comes back for renewal. It is the third round the town has "
  "taken to the state, each one for $2 million, and every penny of the previous rounds has been "
  "spent. Revolving account spending limits, personnel compensation plans, the waterways "
  "improvement fund, the ambulance reserve fund and the ferry embarkation fee are all taken up. "
  "Near the end a resident asks to put her questions as part of the public hearing rather than "
  "after it. The chair, watching the clock with one article still to go, allows it.",
 "-QolwI8sNtc":
  "Housing, sewer and the Community Preservation Committee. Article 13 would fund design, an "
  "owner&rsquo;s project manager and construction for town employee housing. The capital "
  "committee had not recommended it outright. Its view is reported as more a case of maybe not "
  "this year, since three different schematics had been offered and the proposal seemed "
  "premature. Bringing in a private developer is raised and described as difficult. Articles 65 "
  "and 66 concern an affordable housing property tax exemption and an amendment to the housing "
  "trust&rsquo;s declaration of trust. A member warns that the exemption will read at Town "
  "Meeting as subsidizing someone to live in a unit for less than the private market. He says "
  "that is the wrong message to send. On Article 15, the Somerset sewer extension, the committee "
  "works through the sewer capacity fee of $20.32 per gallon per day, and whether nitrogen from a "
  "farm and a brewery in the area has been accounted for.",
 "dZw0nIcUgJI":
  "Two hours on appropriations, and the longest stretch is Our Island Home. The figures are set "
  "out in full. Revenue is $2.1 million from Medicaid, $2.6 million from Medicare and $2.9 "
  "million from private pay. Against that are non-labor costs of $2.7 million and labor costs of "
  "$9.1 million. Total town investment stands at $14.8 million and rises with debt service. A "
  "member says voters may look at the number of people it serves and decide they do not want to "
  "invest in it. Another asks which projects on the capital list he would drop instead. On "
  "Community Preservation, the committee is told the town is not responsible for funding the "
  "program directly. When Nantucket joined in 2001 the state match was almost 100 percent, and it "
  "has fallen as more than 130 other towns have joined. Enterprise fund operations, capital "
  "expenditures and two accrued liability stabilization funds are also taken up.",
 "Px8HsqBNiyE":
  "The school budget leads. The superintendent&rsquo;s office reports that the district budget "
  "has grown by almost $20 million since 2015, a period that includes opening Nantucket "
  "Intermediate School in 2017 and staffing it. This year&rsquo;s increase is 5 percent, which a "
  "member notes is well below the last three years. Article 12 would fund school athletic "
  "facility improvements, and the questions turn to what happens at the end of a turf "
  "field&rsquo;s life. The answer is that at year twelve or fifteen, machines vacuum up the "
  "infill, which here would be a southern yellow pine product. The surface is replaced rather "
  "than the whole facility. The committee also reviews the general fund operating budget, capital "
  "expenditures, the property tax base, a noise bylaw and appropriations to the stabilization "
  "fund and from free cash. On capital requests a member observes that the inflexibility of the "
  "process pushes departments to ask high, and that the oldest project still on the list dates "
  "from 2019.",
 "AGTruDPTZM0":
  "Nineteen minutes, and the airport comes first. Jet A has risen 45 percent and 100 low lead 23 "
  "percent, and the airport asks to raise its fuel revolving fund spending limit by another $2.5 "
  "million, to $11 million. Anything spent above the limit would have to come out of the "
  "operations budget. The rest is an information session on the Annual Town Meeting warrant. "
  "Technical amendments are previewed. One would change Article 10, after bids for the Marine "
  "Department replacement patrol boat came in at $627,090 rather than $603,900, a difference of "
  "about $23,000. Another would restore a Select Board comment that had been left out.",
 "VjhQYeTiemk":
  "A short meeting devoted to technical amendments to the Town Meeting warrant. The committee "
  "works down a numbered list. Article 11 is left alone because the Planning Board has not "
  "changed its motion and the committee&rsquo;s role there is only to comment. Amendment six "
  "corrects a chapter reference in Article 15, from 398 to 296. Amendment seven adds $850,000 to "
  "the sewer department capital request, an item the committee is told fell through the cracks "
  "and has since been traced, and which is needed for a replacement. The amendments had been "
  "reviewed with the capital program committee the previous week.",
 "_xJpLawtXI4":
  "Transfers back to the departments the money came from. At the start of the year the general "
  "fund was reduced by $2.25 million and the health insurance budget by $750,000 to account for "
  "expected vacancies. The committee now approves roughly $1.6 million in transfers returning to "
  "those departments. Staff is explicit that this is not the result of over-expending, but of "
  "always having intended to return the money. Asked whether it will happen again, she says it "
  "will in fiscal 2027. The sewer enterprise fund brings a related request, $175,000 from "
  "salaries into operating, because vacancies have left it relying on on-call services. The "
  "committee also elects a chair and vice chair and makes committee appointments.",
 "FBXq89pdKDE":
  "An organizational meeting. The committee elects a chair and vice chair and works through which "
  "town committees still need a representative from it. The capital program committee is covered. "
  "The solid waste committee is described as no longer active, its work having been tied to "
  "redoing the waste services agreement. One member is corrected on the rule that a Finance "
  "Committee member may sit on an advisory body but not a regulatory one. The conversation then "
  "turns to what the committee is able to say about capital projects. A member gives the example "
  "of a bridge carrying a $40 million request and asks whether the committee may comment on "
  "whether a state grant exists for it. The reply notes that the town had to identify money up "
  "front in order to qualify for a grant it was pursuing.",
 "HHuTC6SmKAA":
  "A facilitated discussion of the commission&rsquo;s own enabling legislation, and of the "
  "competing warrant articles that would rewrite it. An attorney runs the session. The central "
  "disagreement is membership. The commission would keep three Planning Board members on it; the "
  "citizen petition would cut that to two. Members work through what the existing statute "
  "actually says, which is that each body appoints a representative rather than a member, "
  "language that has always allowed someone who does not sit on the appointing board. A member "
  "recalls that a term limit article was voted down at Town Meeting in 2017, and offers that as "
  "evidence of what voters think. Another argues the change would make the membership more "
  "confusing without accomplishing anything. Staff is asked to prepare something showing how the "
  "membership has turned over across recent years.",
 "t4rrREedZV0":
  "The enabling legislation again, with votes expected. The planning director briefs two new "
  "insertions: a definition of resident, which the statute requires, and a line stating that "
  "decisions of the commission are made by a majority of those present and voting. A member says "
  "he is confused about what is being decided. Late in the meeting a commissioner objects to "
  "something introduced in the last few minutes of the article. After months of work, a "
  "facilitator, and an effort to narrow the gap between the two proposals, he says it is not "
  "close to the mark. He calls it sad and says the two sides are not on the same page.",
 "Gr40GnQMlj4":
  "Three hours, and two very different halves. The first is technical. The state&rsquo;s "
  "integrated land use strategy is presented. MassDOT&rsquo;s safety performance targets are then "
  "taken up, among them a serious injury rate of 4.03 per hundred million vehicle miles traveled, "
  "with non-motorized deaths and serious injuries counted together. The commission authorizes a "
  "letter of support for a transit authority grant application and a technical assistance "
  "contract. The second half returns to Articles 74 and 75. The commission agrees ground rules "
  "for a compromise and then works through it in the open. The chair takes an informal poll, "
  "member by member, on whether the commission can accept two Planning Board seats on a "
  "thirteen-member body. A motion follows on how the at-large seats would be divided between "
  "elected and appointed members, including one drawn from the business community.",
 "Qc3JVsFlKaE":
  "The Land Bank&rsquo;s executive director, Rachael Freeman, is the guest speaker, and she "
  "starts a long way back. As whaling declined Nantucket became a tourist destination, large "
  "tracts were subdivided and sold, and regular visitors grew concerned. The Nantucket "
  "Conservation Foundation was created in 1963, building and zoning codes followed, and the "
  "sequence continues into the 1970s. The commission then considers a letter of support to "
  "MassDOT District 5 for the Land Bank&rsquo;s highway access permit onto Milestone Road for a "
  "proposed bike park. Transportation business follows, including an adjustment to the current "
  "transportation improvement program and a first look at funding scenarios for the next one. A "
  "member asks how the public can join the recreation facilities work group and is told it is not "
  "a public body, but a town manager&rsquo;s working group.",
 "LHwnG-breEM":
  "A short meeting with one substantial item. The commission authorizes its director to sign a "
  "contract with the University of Massachusetts Donahue Institute for a population study, at "
  "about $45,000, which also needs Select Board approval. The point of it is to count the "
  "population the census does not: seasonal residents and day trippers, as well as year-round "
  "residents. Staff describes the value for economic planning, resource allocation and "
  "infrastructure. On transportation, the commission is told there are more needs than there are "
  "state or federal dollars. It hears what other regions do with their program money, including "
  "bike rack programs, shared micromobility, and the permanent traffic counter program on "
  "Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard. Twenty-one day public review periods are authorized for two "
  "transportation improvement programs.",
 "UIsN49K9-y4":
  "Twenty-five minutes. The commission hears that the bike path signs on Milestone Road have been "
  "changed over, with slow-down signs replacing stop signs away from the major intersections. A "
  "member who drives it daily reports they appear to be working, and credits the bicycle and "
  "pedestrian advisory committee and public works. A vote on the transportation improvement "
  "program passes without a single nay, which the chair notes he has not seen before. The draft "
  "program for federal fiscal years 2027 to 2031 follows. Highway funding will be flexed to "
  "transit in 2027 and 2028, and no regional projects are programmed for 2029 through 2031. A "
  "grant application for safe streets planning and demonstration is also discussed.",
 "hfA1D-4OwGg":
  "Two and a half hours, ending in staff contracts. Jesse Bell is appointed to a three-year "
  "at-large term. Staff gives an overview of what the commission and its office are carrying, and "
  "a member observes that projects like the Tom Nevers path are not a matter of getting equipment "
  "out and building, but represent a large amount of staff time. The travel demand forecast model "
  "is presented for the first time with base year outputs. The unified planning work program is "
  "broken down by salary, overhead and direct costs. Time is allocated to traffic model "
  "scenarios, the traffic safety action plan, bike paths and intersections, and assistance to "
  "local boards. Public forums are announced for the Tom Nevers and Wauwinet Road multi-use "
  "paths. After an executive session the commission returns to authorize an employment agreement "
  "for Leslie Snell as Director of Planning and a contract amendment for Megan Trudel as Deputy "
  "Director.",
 "9TxKvhjZMzY":
  "The commission elects officers, makes its committee appointments, and authorizes a response to "
  "an Open Meeting Law complaint filed against it on July 16 by Hillary Rayport. One newly "
  "appointed member abstains on a set of minutes, since his term began on July 1 and he was not "
  "present for most of the meetings they cover. The travel demand forecast model returns for its "
  "second presentation. Staff walks through which zones exchange the most trips, and notes that "
  "where population is highest is where travel exchanges concentrate, which matters for "
  "projecting future traffic. Asked about electric bicycles, staff explains the model can show "
  "what a modest increase in e-bike use would do to congestion, but cannot account for parking "
  "availability. A member raises the growth of the school system as another pressure. The "
  "commission will not meet in August.",
 "9yemr6bKs5Q":
  "A nine-minute meeting with one item on the agenda. The commission votes to join the "
  "Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard Commission and the Nantucket Affordable Housing Trust in commissioning "
  "an economic study of the real estate transfer fee from the UMass Donahue Institute. The "
  "Vineyard Commission would pay half, the Affordable Housing Trust has committed $10,000, and "
  "Nantucket&rsquo;s share is about $25,000, drawn from district local technical assistance funds "
  "or professional services. Staff explain that the Vineyard Commission proposed the partnership "
  "and that the three bodies would share decision-making under a memorandum of understanding. "
  "Members note that Nantucket and the Vineyard are the only Massachusetts communities with a "
  "land bank transfer fee, and that the study answers questions the legislature has raised in "
  "recent sessions. The vote is unanimous.",
 "HRp8jpJpA70":
  "The board takes final action on car rental medallion allocations, continued from July 20 and "
  "August 17. Eight medallions are formalized, four each to Compass Point Rentals and Grey Lady "
  "Jeeps. Discussion covers parking, airport advertising, and peer-to-peer rental platforms. "
  "Members differ on whether the allocation fits the island&rsquo;s 700-medallion cap, and on the "
  "roughly 140 medallions that go unused. A bylaw amendment on peer-to-peer rentals is drafted "
  "but awaiting legal review. The vote is unanimous. The Theatre Workshop of Nantucket&rsquo;s "
  "liquor license is then reissued under the name Theatre Nantucket, Inc., with one member "
  "recused. The board also adopts a policy letting licensing staff settle minor compliance "
  "violations by written warning instead of a hearing. The longest segment, about forty-five "
  "minutes, is a joint public hearing on Verizon and National Grid petitions to remove two poles "
  "on Sparks Avenue. Staff report a sewer main and a force main under the sidewalk, and no "
  "survey-quality plan showing where new conduit would run. Both petitions pass contingent on "
  "that plan and department sign-off. Two National Grid petitions on Somerset Lane are continued "
  "to November 16.",
 "HwPVp7rFUMk":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: as-built fines, 4 Rose Bud Lane, 115 Cliff Road and 5 Columbus Avenue. The longest "
  "discussion, about thirty-nine minutes, is the commission&rsquo;s own policy on as-built fines. "
  "One commissioner proposes a graduated system with defined categories, a scoring worksheet and "
  "repeat-offender status. Members debate whether the owner, the architect or the builder should "
  "carry the penalty, and settle on the owner, who holds the certificate of appropriateness. "
  "Several observe that a flat fine falls unevenly given the range of incomes on the island. "
  "Salem&rsquo;s scale of violations and the town code&rsquo;s three-year building delay after an "
  "unlawful demolition are both raised, and a work group is to be formed. The duplex at 4 Rose "
  "Bud Lane runs about thirty-two minutes, turning on the dormers and on the building&rsquo;s "
  "height relative to its neighbors, and is held for revisions. 115 Cliff Road is approved three "
  "votes to two, with conditions on hedge screening and cedar detailing beneath the dormer sills. "
  "The pool at 5 Columbus Avenue is held for revisions after neighbors object that it would be "
  "visible from the road. 1 Mothball Way is approved as submitted.",
 "UWfC1SmQUVM":
  "The commission works through its hearing list and issues an order on each filing it closes. "
  "The longest item, about twelve minutes, is Sankaty Head Golf Club&rsquo;s proposal to move a "
  "2,750-square-foot isolated wetland from the edge of the 17th fairway into a former stockpile "
  "area, replicated at one and a half times its size. The Nantucket Land and Water Council "
  "objects that the wetland has functioned since at least 1938 and asks the commission not to "
  "trade established functions for anticipated ones. The order passes with monitoring conditions. "
  "A second hearing on 156 and 158 Polpis Road returns with revised plans for the Egan "
  "Foundation&rsquo;s museum property. The mulch path is shifted onto already-cleared ground, a "
  "rain garden is added, the decking is narrowed, and a river-rock seating area and a swing set "
  "are removed. At 15 Old North Wharf a cottage is to be raised about three feet on a new "
  "foundation with flood vents, with a third floor added. The two Massachusetts Avenue filings "
  "are split: 43A is approved with a seasonal restriction for nesting plovers, and 43B is "
  "continued for a state determination.",
 "Wvf78YK0HGc":
  "The board&rsquo;s annual strategic plan retreat, facilitated by Michelle Ferguson of Raftelis "
  "and running a little over four hours. Housing takes close to half of it: whether the town "
  "keeps building rental housing for its own employees or shifts toward ownership, whether the "
  "next phase of a municipal housing project goes to next year&rsquo;s town meeting or yields to "
  "a public works project, ground leases that would let employees build on town land, and an "
  "argument that the island should stop treating a friendly 40B as something to avoid. Members "
  "press as hard on structure, naming departments that work in silos and the habit of committing "
  "to a capital project before knowing how it will be paid for. Traffic and parking take about "
  "an hour and circle one conflict: on-street parking against accessible sidewalks, curb cuts "
  "onto private property, satellite lots and the NRTA, and a parking structure staff call too "
  "costly to be near-term. Natural resources reports that the watershed and estuary plans are "
  "some twenty years old and that a funded update is under way. The last two topics are "
  "previewed rather than taken up; staff say unapproved benches and signs have cluttered "
  "downtown and promise a policy the restored downtown officer can enforce.",
 "_fXxWq8k53o":
  "The recording covers two meetings. The County Commissioners meet first and select a preferred "
  "alignment for the Wauwinet Road multi-use path, a federal and local aid project developed with "
  "the Eastern Federal Lands Division and the US Fish and Wildlife Service. The Select Board is "
  "called to order at 6:04. Its longest item, about seventy-five minutes, is the town&rsquo;s "
  "beach concessions at Jetties, Surfside and Children&rsquo;s Beach. The board discusses floor "
  "repairs at Jetties, whether leases should run for longer terms, how much building maintenance "
  "should fall to the concessionaires, and how renovations at the three sites would be sequenced. "
  "Before that the deputy town manager continues the review of the capital improvement plan for "
  "fiscal years 2028 through 2037, about fifty minutes. Projects have been rephased since the "
  "previous week, including the coastal resilience work, and the vehicle replacement schedule is "
  "explained. The board then approves additional compensation to Sherburne Commons for increased "
  "use of the property leased for Our Island Home, four votes to one, following a member&rsquo;s "
  "statement that the project is unlikely to come in on budget. It adjourns to executive session.",
 "aael7N_tBhA":
  "The board takes up three housing updates, none of which requires a vote. Staff present the "
  "Affordable Housing Trust sites on Orange Street, Bartlett Road and Vesper Lane, where income "
  "levels were lowered to qualify the project for low-income housing tax credits, and report that "
  "the market study covering the workforce units needs updating. The attainable homeownership "
  "project at 158 Madaket Road is shown to the board for the first time, with ownership "
  "restricted to households up to 250 percent of area median income. A request to waive a sewer "
  "connection fee is referred to the Affordable Housing Trust and housing staff, who are asked to "
  "review the history of waivers and recommend a policy. The longest item, about thirty-four "
  "minutes, is an overview of the update to the local hazard mitigation plan. The board then "
  "continues its review of the capital improvement plan for fiscal years 2028 through 2037. A "
  "research vessel is removed from the plan, having been funded from outside sources, and the "
  "Brant Point shellfish hatchery dock is deferred. The board closes on the spending caps of the "
  "town&rsquo;s revolving accounts and adjourns to executive session.",
 "bF3BU9jbD0k":
  "Most of the meeting, about forty-five minutes, is the third presentation of the town&rsquo;s "
  "travel demand forecast model. The consultants model two scenarios for 2050: the long-range "
  "transportation plan as a lower bound and full buildout as an upper bound, each at average and "
  "peak-season conditions. Delays concentrate at the Milestone Rotary and along Milestone, "
  "Surfside and Old South Roads. Reliable travel on the island&rsquo;s major roads falls from "
  "near 100 percent off-season to about 60 percent at peak. A first test of travel demand "
  "management, small village centers modeled on Sconset, produces a slight improvement. Transit "
  "frequency, parking pricing, shorter trips and bicycle routes are to be tested next, along with "
  "capital projects including the rotary and two road connectors. A fourth presentation is set "
  "for December 7, jointly with the Select Board. The commission then approves four items. One is "
  "a letter of support for the Steamship Authority&rsquo;s grant application for a terminal "
  "feasibility study. The others are its annual town report, a comment letter on the "
  "state&rsquo;s draft priority habitat map, and a comment letter on accessory dwelling unit "
  "regulations. The meeting adjourns to executive session.",
 "nEw70DaNOWE":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 2 Wingspread Road, 4 South Mill Street, 3 Sea Street and 9 Swift Rock Road. The "
  "longest stretch, about twenty-eight minutes, is three applications at 2 Wingspread Road: a new "
  "dwelling, a garage and a pool house. The commission asks for variation in the roof line across "
  "the flanking wings, which run at the same height for some eighty-eight feet, and for site "
  "sections showing existing and proposed grades. All three are held for revisions. 4 South Mill "
  "Street takes about nineteen minutes. The questions there are the driveway and foundation "
  "plantings carried over from the earlier certificate, a new stair down to the basement, and "
  "four French doors facing the street. It is held for minor revisions. At 3 Sea Street the "
  "demolition of the existing dwelling is approved with a condition added to the certificate. The "
  "new inn proposed for the site is held for revisions. The commission asks for material placing "
  "it in context with its surroundings. The sports court at 9 Swift Rock Road turns on fence "
  "height and on screening that holds through the winter.",
 "1Ht3AokwDcI":
  "No Description.",
 "4QS0LIrV-ZM":
  "No Description.",
 "4QcPzOs6Xr4":
  "No Description.",
 "58Lz4nFrfr0":
  "No Description.",
 "6Fy8x4rLTHY":
  "No Description.",
 "7yK7UwvhMwM":
  "No Description.",
 "80CWYjYMu3E":
  "An information session on Article 71, a stormwater management bylaw on the warrant for the May "
  "4 Annual Town Meeting. The bylaw would not itself set rules. It authorizes a stormwater "
  "utility and the framework for regulations, permitting and fees, on the model of the sewer "
  "bylaw and funded through an enterprise fund. The consultant presents flooding at Nobadeer Way "
  "and Airport Road as the pattern the town wants to end: infiltrators clogged with sediment for "
  "want of routine cleaning, then replaced at far greater cost. Drafting the regulations and "
  "putting them in place is expected to take about two years. Most of the questions concern "
  "sequence. One attendee asks whether voters will adopt a bylaw creating permits and fees before "
  "seeing the regulations those will contain. Staff answer that drafts will be vetted with a "
  "stakeholder group before public hearings. Several speakers press on enforcement of stormwater "
  "conditions in existing special permits, which staff acknowledge is not happening. The "
  "stormwater division currently has one employee.",
 "89c1KoB0-q0":
  "No Description.",
 "AzxRlxtvZGs":
  "No Description.",
 "Cg9bRqACDvU":
  "No Description.",
 "CwTYstsLk0w":
  "A public meeting on where the Wauwinet Road multi-use path should run. The transportation "
  "program manager and the federal and consultant team present four alignments across six "
  "sections of the two-and-a-half-mile route. Alternative one follows the road behind a "
  "three-foot buffer and a steel-backed timber guardrail, and the others meander away from it. "
  "The study finds alternative one carries the least property and cultural impact and the lowest "
  "cost, with slightly more wetland impact. The constraints are set out at length: five "
  "delineated wetlands, potential habitat for fourteen protected species including two bats, the "
  "American burying beetle and the northern harrier, and twelve historic properties, with Squam "
  "Swamp contributing to the historic district. Funding is a $2.4 million federal grant alongside "
  "$4.6 million approved by 68 percent of voters in 2023. Construction might begin in 2029, "
  "depending on permitting. Public comment divides. One Wauwinet resident reports landowners "
  "roughly 60 to 40 against on security grounds. Several ask why the path cannot sit farther from "
  "the road as it does elsewhere on the island, and staff cite a tight right-of-way and the cost "
  "of easements. A request to bury utilities where poles must move draws an estimate of years and "
  "millions.",
 "EV7Svj4Si_o":
  "No Description.",
 "F7UYtgrBjm0":
  "No Description.",
 "If3sbz1vMco":
  "A training session on private drinking water wells, presented by a specialist from RCAP "
  "Solutions under EPA funding and hosted by the health department. It opens with the "
  "island&rsquo;s PFAS map, which the environmental contamination administrator brings to the "
  "Board of Health each month, and with the reminder that private wells are not regulated as "
  "public systems are. Testing is the owner&rsquo;s responsibility. Most of the session is "
  "practical. Nantucket&rsquo;s wells draw from sand and gravel rather than bedrock, and the "
  "sandy soil carries whatever enters the ground straight to the aquifer, which makes septic "
  "maintenance a drinking water question. The presenter works through well types, the hazards of "
  "old dug wells, what a sound wellhead looks like, how to find a well log, and setbacks from "
  "sources of contamination. The recommended testing is bacteria and nitrate each year, nitrate "
  "being the early signal of septic influence, with PFAS every few years. Questions cover whether "
  "help exists with testing costs, where state well legislation stands, and whether irrigation "
  "wells should be tested. A follow-up session on septic systems is announced for July.",
 "L-FucT4lbZE":
  "The second of three information sessions on the Somerset Needs Area sewer extension, three "
  "weeks before the vote on Article 15. The engineers cover the familiar ground: the 2004 and "
  "2014 wastewater plans, nitrogen from septic systems reaching Hummock and Miacomet Ponds, and "
  "$44.8 million in construction on top of $6.4 million already appropriated for design. What "
  "distinguishes this session is the questioning on cost recovery. The Finance Committee&rsquo;s "
  "motion would assess up to 25 percent of the sewer cost on the properties served, through "
  "betterments or an adjusted capacity fee. The finance director corrects the consultant on the "
  "point, noting that the language is already in the article. This would be the first Nantucket "
  "needs area to recover connection costs that way: Town Meeting struck the equivalent provision "
  "from the Shimmo article in 2016, and one attendee is weighing an amendment to remove it again. "
  "A second question goes unresolved. Asked whether a property with two buildings on one septic "
  "system needs two connections, and two betterments, staff say they believe so, and that two "
  "stubs will be set at every property, but promise a written answer.",
 "MtYeA_dt6Ds":
  "No Description.",
 "N3ziUkKcRlM":
  "No Description.",
 "OWYJJeg7NBs":
  "No Description.",
 "P01TaV7axO4":
  "No Description.",
 "PE9qy3KOrsM":
  "No Description.",
 "QSWJ8eCFZhc":
  "No Description.",
 "SrtQ3EDthA4":
  "No Description.",
 "W9SuVSN3KuE":
  "No Description.",
 "Wcgd7v_Kvsk":
  "The second information session on the stadium project, now before voters as Article 12, "
  "following the one held on February 19. The design is unchanged &mdash; a regulation six-lane "
  "track, a synthetic multisport field, an accessible grandstand, two support buildings and new "
  "lighting &mdash; and construction would begin after the last game in December and run sixteen "
  "to eighteen months. Two subjects get the detail this session adds. The first is drainage. The "
  "design aims to discharge no stormwater from the site at all. Rain passes through the permeable "
  "turf backing and shock pad into a stone base, then to underdrains, collector pipes and roughly "
  "fifty infiltration galleries. A trench drain rings the track, and a filtration unit is meant "
  "to catch sediment, microfibers and nanoplastics before water reaches the galleries. The second "
  "is PFAS. Consultants set out the testing the school department proposes for each turf "
  "material: cryomilling with solvent extraction, a total oxidizable precursor assay, and a "
  "synthetic precipitation leaching procedure. Results would be compared against the "
  "state&rsquo;s soil standards, with detections below the reporting limit treated as real rather "
  "than estimated.",
 "ZWIg7OHI69k":
  "No Description.",
 "aGUiEc-M0iM":
  "The third information session on the Somerset Needs Area sewer extension, tied to Article 15 "
  "and ballot question five. The engineers trace the project to the town&rsquo;s 2004 wastewater "
  "plan and the Massachusetts Estuaries Project studies, which identified septic systems as a "
  "major source of the nitrogen impairing Hummock and Miacomet Ponds, and show recent photographs "
  "of algal blooms. Besides sewer, the project would add water main on streets without town "
  "water, drainage on Raceway Drive, a softened curve at Bartlett Road, and multi-use paths on "
  "Raceway Drive and Somerset Lane. Construction is put at $44.8 million, on top of $6.4 million "
  "already appropriated for design. Article 15 requires two-thirds on May 4, and the debt "
  "exclusion is a simple majority on May 19. The town is again seeking a zero-interest state "
  "revolving fund loan after an unsuccessful 2024 application. Most questions concern what "
  "connection will cost a household. Staff describe a $4,000 permit fee, an estimated $20,000 to "
  "$25,000 for a three-bedroom house, a Board of Health loan at 2 percent, and a capacity-fee "
  "lien at 4.4 percent over twenty years. Another asks whether island labor will build it; staff "
  "answer that state procurement law bars the town from requiring it.",
 "aPJeJoz7h-M":
  "No Description.",
 "cgXVnW3avu4":
  "No Description.",
 "h6ULgsN063g":
  "No Description.",
 "h8TeUrMGBp4":
  "No Description.",
 "hCObaU3VYys":
  "No Description.",
 "q04DZk1ojbA":
  "The third in a series of public presentations on PFAS, this one reporting a summer study of "
  "Nantucket&rsquo;s beaches, harbors and ponds. Dr. Rebecca DeVries, an epidemiologist, walks "
  "through the method: a sampling plan written in June, two rounds of samples in July and August, "
  "and 27 locations, most of them sites the town already visits for weekly bacterial beach "
  "monitoring. PFAS were detected in surface water at 13 of 21 locations, all at concentrations "
  "below the state&rsquo;s health-based guidelines for recreational waters. Inland ponds showed "
  "more frequent detections and slightly higher concentrations than coastal sites, which the "
  "presenter attributes to less mixing and dilution. One unusually high result at Sconset Beach "
  "in July was not reproduced in two later samples. Shoreline foam is the study&rsquo;s other "
  "subject, and only two places held enough to sample: Sesachacha Pond and Madaket Harbor. "
  "Twenty-one compounds were found in foam, often orders of magnitude above the water beside it. "
  "No health guidance exists for foam, so the health department issued a precautionary advisory "
  "to avoid contact, rinse with fresh water, and keep children and pets away. Questions cover the "
  "five-year resampling interval, the landfill, and what the foam leaves behind in the sand.",
 "rr8dnFrxNCY":
  "No Description.",
 "sqRH2lYIcio":
  "An information session narrowed to two upcoming steps in the Somerset Needs Area sewer "
  "extension: easements on private roads, and the third phase of subsurface investigation. A "
  "fuller session on the project is promised for late October. The engineers recap the design: "
  "about 15,200 feet of gravity sewer and 8,100 feet of low-pressure sewer, all routed to a "
  "proposed pump station at 77 Bartlett Road and on to the Surfside treatment facility. They note "
  "that the new water main will now stop at 8 Highbrush Path. Roadway work would soften the curve "
  "at Bartlett and Raceway, add a guardrail, and move the sidewalk to the side that needs no "
  "crossing. The easements are limited to the roadway corridor and take no residential property. "
  "The Select Board hearing is set for September 9, and soil borings run September 21 to October "
  "2. Residents ask what becomes of existing septic systems, whether a grinder pump is needed on "
  "the gravity line, and what connection will cost, which staff cannot yet estimate. One asks "
  "about hardship relief after a recent $100,000 septic installation.",
 "tVb7tVM5qy8":
  "No Description.",
 "tpFhAYBx3nk":
  "An information session on the Vito Capizzo Stadium renovation, presented by the school "
  "district and its designers. The case rests on use. About 70 percent of students play a sport, "
  "and the grass field carries close to 1,200 hours of play a year against the 400 to 600 a "
  "natural field can take. The cinder track is not regulation size, so teams cannot host meets, "
  "and the grandstand and press box do not meet accessibility requirements. The proposal is a "
  "400-meter six-lane track, a synthetic multisport field, a 980-seat accessible grandstand, a "
  "team room, a boosters and concessions building, and dark-sky lighting. Readings in the current "
  "end zones run as low as four to six foot-candles against a recommended fifty. Questions turn "
  "on the turf. The designers describe a southern yellow pine infill rather than crumb rubber, a "
  "shock pad, an eight-to-twelve-year life, and recycling plants in Rockland and Baton Rouge. "
  "Asked about PFAS, they say none is added, that tests from late 2024 and early 2025 found none "
  "at detection limits, and that the contractor would have to test the lots produced for this "
  "project. A Board of Health special meeting on the question is set for March 2.",
 "xBS_jyqsXa8":
  "No Description.",
 "yWZZ7sXBi3U":
  "No Description.",
 "ymII7Vgyx04":
  "No Description.",
 "zHTyqftCnHg":
  "An information session on Article 13 and ballot question three, a $7 million debt exclusion "
  "for municipal workforce housing at 15 Waitt Drive. Phase one would build two apartment "
  "buildings on town-owned land between the Habitat for Humanity houses and the seasonal dorm: "
  "six units, nine bedrooms, and the site work for all three lots. The full build-out would reach "
  "six buildings, 18 apartments and 24 bedrooms, two of them ADA-compliant. Staff place the "
  "project within a housing department strategic plan adopted in April, which targets 25 to 50 "
  "units over ten years, and cite a 25 percent annual turnover rate in the public schools. The "
  "designs have been bid, so the figures are firm. The stated tax impact is about $10.38 a year "
  "for 25 years on a year-round property valued at $1,878,950 with the residential exemption. "
  "Questions cover the basement apartments&rsquo; light and egress, and the cost per bedroom, "
  "which speakers revise downward as the site work is spread across later phases.",
 "zVTHrsdChZw":
  "An abutters&rsquo; session on the Tom Nevers Road multi-use path, presented at final design "
  "rather than as a choice between options. The engineers walk the corridor section by section "
  "from Milestone Road, describing an alignment that avoids wetland resources, threads around "
  "utility poles, and crosses a low area on Land Bank land by raised boardwalk. Bidding is set "
  "for September 2026, construction for late fall, and opening for summer 2027, with no work "
  "between Memorial Day and Labor Day. The questions come mostly from property owners. Several "
  "press on burying the utilities, which staff say would turn a path project into a far costlier "
  "utility project. One speaker argues at length for the west side of the road, which carries no "
  "poles or guy wires, and for two one-way lanes rather than a two-way path on safety grounds. "
  "Staff reply that the alternatives analysis is complete and the alignment is final. Town "
  "counsel explains that the easements are eminent domain takings under state law and the county "
  "charter, not voluntary, and sets out the notice and award process. Screening is the recurring "
  "concern, the civic association president calling it the neighborhood&rsquo;s main feedback.",
 "z_OVE0B9XUs":
  "No Description.",
 "zjNVBKUMNtc":
  "No Description.",
 "UZwCMPfgenU":
  "Most of the meeting goes to the fuel revolving fund, and the commission ends up arguing "
  "itself out of it. The immediate question is raising the FY2027 spending limit from $8.5 "
  "million to $13 million so fuel can be bought through the rest of the year, and that passes; "
  "the larger question is whether the fund should exist. It was set up when the airport was in "
  "financial difficulty, members are told, and the airport now has revenues enough without it. "
  "Worse, the way it was written does not return interest to the airport, which the FAA could "
  "read as revenue diversion. Once replenished it cannot revolve again, and permission has to be "
  "sought at town meeting every year. Nobody offers a reason to keep it. Two FAA grants are "
  "ratified, $4,287,587 for the commuter apron and $475,671 for the MEPA and NEPA work, and the "
  "FY28 budget adds $100,000 of contingency against rising costs. The manager reports PFAS phase "
  "three closed out and phase four coming, a water treatment system being sized for the "
  "geothermal plant, and a fire suppression test that found a shorted notification card and an "
  "untested backflow device.",
 "H2eZEov95CM":
  "A short meeting with one long question at the center of it: whether the board needs a written "
  "policy on how long a property owner has to connect to town sewer. The Somerset needs area "
  "prompts it, and the board is told the real constraint is that there are only so many "
  "engineers and installers on the island to do the work. Members look back at what was done for "
  "Mattapoisett and at the existing five-year test, and ask whether a policy should cover one "
  "project or any project, how fiscal hardship would be judged, and whether an annual inspection "
  "ought to be the condition of any extra time. Staff caution that they are not forensic "
  "accountants and cannot examine anyone&rsquo;s finances in depth; they are directed to gather "
  "the underlying numbers on the Somerset systems and come back. Elsewhere the public health "
  "director is appointed an alternate voting member of the Inter-Island Public Health Excellence "
  "Collaborative, and the Maria Mitchell Association&rsquo;s temporary housing for next "
  "summer&rsquo;s circus is reviewed, the inspector reporting lidded dumpsters and no "
  "violations. Influenza A has turned up in the wastewater, opening the respiratory virus "
  "season.",
 "jLy8OuOSIHY":
  "The Land Bank&rsquo;s Falmouth Avenue relocation takes most of the meeting and is the first "
  "real test of an authority the commission gave itself. A stretch of access road said to be "
  "unsafe would be moved, with rare plants translocated and the abandoned roadbed restored to "
  "dune by replanting sod plants. The commission expanded its jurisdiction over rare species in "
  "January 2025 and is told plainly that this is the first time it has used it, and that the "
  "record before it will not support a waiver. The state&rsquo;s own permit is not in hand, "
  "which leaves a chicken-and-egg the commission cannot resolve from its side. A long line of "
  "Cisco residents, several of them there for decades, ask that the road be left where it is, "
  "pointing to flags marking rare species that the new alignment would run through and arguing "
  "the parking lot can be reached another way. The applicant answers that the route has been "
  "travelled and maintained for more than twenty years and that only the locations of listed "
  "species are confidential. Certificates of compliance are issued in a block, and the Baxter "
  "Road geotubes are carried to a special meeting the following week.",
 "jQngdY2OANo":
  "A special meeting called for one subject: what to do about the geotubes on the beach below "
  "Baxter Road. The Siasconset Beach Preservation Fund proposes placing roughly 10,000 cubic "
  "yards of sand, more than the six or seven thousand placed earlier in the year, trucked to the "
  "south end of the site and carried north by conveyor. It is careful to say this is not a "
  "repair: the sand would go on the bluff to cover areas now bare. The obstacle is procedural. "
  "The 2023 order for 106,000 yards was appealed, so no mechanism exists to place sand at all, "
  "which is why the work would come through an enforcement order instead. Commissioners ask that "
  "any sand placed count against the accumulated deficit and that sieve and compatibility "
  "testing be spelled out in the order, and note that the opinion they asked town counsel for on "
  "the licensing question has still not arrived. One speaker reminds the commission that the "
  "array was described as nasty as far back as March. Monitoring reports are now filed twice a "
  "month. The matter is continued to November 5.",
 "UWKCbpltIDk":
  "The 2026 Master Plan is adopted, unanimously, after a last pass through the written comments. "
  "Staff work down the list &mdash; the Land and Water Council wants an accounting of what the "
  "previous plan did and did not achieve, and a proposed plan coordinator post is judged outside "
  "the plan&rsquo;s scope &mdash; and confirm that the sub-plans listed on page 11 are "
  "incorporated by reference and stay open to public scrutiny. The chair thanks the consultants "
  "and the many residents who turned out for the surveys and kitchen conversations. The rest of "
  "the evening belongs to the Hensdale Club, a private club proposed for three lots on Old South "
  "Road, presented with its entry sequence, cottages, staff housing and clubhouse, a kids&rsquo; "
  "club with a maker space, and a gravity sewer collection running to an abutting pump station. "
  "Neighbours press on buffers, lighting and traffic; the board authorises staff to engage a "
  "peer review consultant on the traffic report and continues the hearing to November. The "
  "hospital&rsquo;s Vesper Lane housing is approved with financing conditions, and the board "
  "agrees it is time to put a formula business article before town meeting.",
 "7F25QxA6Ngg":
  "Seven minutes. The two licence hearings for One Harbor, a seasonal all-alcoholic restaurant "
  "licence and a live entertainment licence, are opened and continued to November 16 while "
  "further information is sought from the applicant. The utility petition that follows is "
  "granted: about twenty-five feet of conduit from a pole on Fairgrounds Road to serve 6 Rugged "
  "Road. The sewer department has flagged a twelve-inch force main near the bike path, and the "
  "board attaches its own condition &mdash; bore under the path rather than cut it, unless the "
  "force main makes that impossible, and compact the underlayment properly if it is cut. The "
  "chair is blunt about why: sunken utility patches on the bike paths.",
 "EGzgmcRU2zY":
  "The deer take up the last third of the meeting. A member who hunts describes taking fifty "
  "animals out of the Middle Moors in the first week of shotgun season and argues the herd is "
  "far smaller there than people assume; the problem is the posted private land where the deer "
  "shelter and the animals now wandering into town. Alpha-gal is the public health thread "
  "running through it, with the Board of Health having already written to restaurants, and the "
  "board looks for a standing arrangement &mdash; a monthly update, or someone from health or "
  "natural resources sitting in on the hunters&rsquo; group. Hunters have been given bags and a "
  "dumpster open around the clock for remains, which has changed what gets left in the fields. "
  "Earlier, the board returns to as-built fines, several members now thinking the process rather "
  "than the schedule is what needs fixing, and objects to borrowing money to pay for a software "
  "licence. The planning director walks through the draft 2027 warrant, including lingering "
  "references to tertiary dwellings that town meeting already removed. The state election "
  "warrant is approved.",
 "6p7jYuqnryc":
  "Two houses are retreating from the sea and the board is still working out what its new "
  "standard allows. At 12 Madequecham Valley Road the existing dwelling would move forward from "
  "about sixty feet off the eroding bank to more than a hundred, which the board has no trouble "
  "with; the difficulty is the secondary dwelling that would sit four feet from the front lot "
  "line, because it is new. Members say repeatedly that relocating an old house into a setback "
  "is one thing and building two stories there from scratch another, and that the practical "
  "difficulty test makes the arithmetic harder, not easier. Smooth Hummocks is simpler: a "
  "dwelling moved 175 feet north, with the board reminded that the seasonal work restriction it "
  "is asked to keep exists for spawning season, not for the neighbors. The reconstruction at 5 "
  "Cudweed Road passes with a landscape plan and the pool equipment pushed behind the garage. "
  "The longest argument is over Beach Plum Village, where abandoning two easement areas would "
  "quietly take away a neighboring lot&rsquo;s driveway access, and no written easement exists "
  "to say what the rights ever were.",
 "s2F1Kc0n1Co":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 100 Main Street, 5 Bank Street and 6 Gay Street. The three applications at 100 Main "
  "Street &mdash; a guest house, a greenhouse and a fence &mdash; run about forty-five minutes, "
  "the longest stretch of the meeting. The guest house is faulted for reading as a meeting house "
  "rather than a cottage, on account of its doors and the roof above them; the greenhouse is "
  "glass and aluminium painted green; and the fence, wanted against deer, is settled only after "
  "the commission works out which run is to be tall and which low, with the site plan to be "
  "annotated accordingly. One member would rather see thick planting than fencing where the aim "
  "is to block sound. It carries on a roll call. The deck alteration at 5 Bank Street takes "
  "about half an hour, turning on a viewing porch with a natural-to-weather railing and a "
  "removable ship&rsquo;s ladder, on how much of the little Codfish Park cottage disappears "
  "behind the addition, and on a roofed patio never authorised; a site viewing is scheduled. 6 "
  "Gay Street, a historic determination and an addition, is approved with a shingled rail in "
  "place of balusters.",
 "II5dIr_p4JI":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 126 Main Street, 2 Wingspread Lane and 19R Hooper Farm Road. The driveway and wall "
  "at 126 Main Street, back before the commission since the spring, take some forty-five "
  "minutes. The applicant argues for consistent treatment and points to photographs of nearby "
  "properties; an abutter asks the commission to hold to what it said in May about how much of a "
  "Main Street side yard is being given up. Members worry that the rendering reads as a runway "
  "running from the sidewalk to the back gate, and option two is approved on the condition that "
  "the brick between sidewalk and driveway, the two wheel strips and the grass between them all "
  "become cobble, leaving a brick sidewalk, a cobble drive and a brick landing, with a gate to "
  "be shown on an exhibit. 2 Wingspread Lane, heard as a dwelling, a garage and a pool house, "
  "runs nearly as long over the gambrel wings, a balustrade above the front door and an "
  "inconsistency between the site plan and the elevations. 19R Hooper Farm Road is pushed to "
  "simplify and to screen, with vegetative screening only acceptable if its maintenance is "
  "conditioned.",
 "A1JOWEBGgLA":
  "Charter captains open public comment by telling the board that no marine diesel is available "
  "on the island, and ask the town to act before next season. The board fills a seat on the "
  "Coastal Resiliency Advisory Committee by paper ballot from four applicants. The airport "
  "manager, Preston Harimon, asks to raise the FY27 fuel revolving fund limit from $8.5 million "
  "to $13 million, reporting jet fuel up 66 percent and avgas 41 percent over the year; it "
  "passes unanimously. Poseidon Development then asks the board to waive the four-times penalty "
  "fee assessed when steel went up at 20 Sparks Avenue before the building permit issued. "
  "Building commissioner Paul Murphy says the Select Board sets the fee and that he has no "
  "discretion over it. The board reduces the charge to double the permit fee, with two members "
  "opposed. A consultant from Barrett Planning Group presents the draft master plan, whose "
  "priorities include centralizing island-wide planning and putting the year-round community "
  "first. Finance director Aleesha Nunley Benjamin reports three straight years of revenue "
  "shortfalls in the sewer enterprise fund. The capital review continues, with the ten-year plan "
  "down $563 million. Assessor Robert Ranney presents on the residential exemption and on "
  "payments in lieu of taxes. Preservation planner Holly Backus describes proposed amendments to "
  "Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act, and the board votes to oppose them.",
 "vOryhJ6nd38":
  "The committee welcomes new finance director Aleesha Nunley Benjamin at her first meeting. It "
  "then takes up the airport&rsquo;s request to raise its FY27 fuel revolving fund spending "
  "limit from $8.5 million to $13 million. The airport manager reports that purchases had "
  "reached $8.3 million by September 16, against a cap set in January, and projects $12.4 "
  "million by the end of the fiscal year, leaving a $544,000 buffer. Much of the discussion "
  "turns on why a revolving fund is sized to a full year of purchases rather than to working "
  "capital. The answer given is that the limit is a cap on departmental spending required by "
  "state law, not a cash-flow instrument. The committee approves the increase by roll call. PLUS "
  "director Leslie Snell then previews the zoning and general bylaw articles the Planning Board "
  "may bring to the 2027 Annual Town Meeting. Her list is conceptual and unvoted. It includes a "
  "year-round requirement for newly permitted secondary dwellings, changes to accessory dwelling "
  "unit and duplex rules driven by recent state legislation, the storage container bylaw, and "
  "the formula business bylaw. Members also discuss a tracking tool for town-funded studies and "
  "the town&rsquo;s Moody&rsquo;s rating, and agree to meet monthly until Town Meeting work "
  "begins.",
 "-MOTHwnB630":
  "The board reads an Arbor Day proclamation, approves renewal contracts for the fire chief, "
  "finance director and town manager negotiated earlier in the month, and grants a legacy "
  "transfer of the Highland Taxi license to the late licensee&rsquo;s daughter. National Grid "
  "then presents its advanced metering program: new meters would report usage in fifteen-minute "
  "intervals to an online dashboard, a network of pole-mounted devices has to be built first, "
  "customers may opt out, and each installation interrupts power for a few minutes, enough to "
  "trip an alarm or start a generator. The housing office wins a letter supporting local "
  "preference for seventy percent of the income- restricted units at the sixty-four-unit Ticcoma "
  "Green project on Fairgrounds Road. Most of the meeting is a first look at license terms for "
  "the Siasconset Beach Preservation Fund&rsquo;s erosion control project at Baxter Road, now "
  "permitted by the Conservation Commission. The draft would require removal funding that rises "
  "with construction costs, removal and makeup sand plans filed with the board as well as the "
  "commission, and failure criteria matching the ones the commission set. Members disagree over "
  "the license term, weighing a five-year limit against a consultant&rsquo;s testimony that the "
  "walkable beach standard may hold for fifteen to twenty-five years. The board tables the "
  "related town meeting article and will draft a work plan.",
 "0DtvYACuH_M":
  "At the county meeting a resident uses food dye and a siphon to show how a contaminant spreads "
  "as wells draw on an aquifer, and questions whether drilling more wells answers the "
  "island&rsquo;s PFAS problem when the aquifer has not been mapped and saltwater intrusion "
  "could follow. The Steamship Authority representative reads a statement against a Senate bill "
  "that would impose term limits on the authority&rsquo;s board, arguing the choice should stay "
  "with the elected county commissioners. The Select Board sends a preservation restriction at "
  "Old North Wharf to town counsel, then takes up a Siasconset Civic Association proposal to "
  "amend the noise bylaw: construction hours of 7:30 to 6, reached after talking with about "
  "twenty builders and landscapers, with discussion of an earlier window in winter and of one "
  "rule islandwide because hours that change by neighborhood could not be enforced. The longest "
  "item is a blanket request from the producers of Five Star Weekend to film on town property "
  "for three weeks in September, with street closings and noise and lighting waivers. Members "
  "complain the plans came too late and are still unsettled, note a date moved to avoid a "
  "wedding and a bluff walk location the town would not allow, and ask for a review process for "
  "large productions and an after-action report; the requests pass with one member opposed and "
  "another recused. The finance director closes with first projections for the fiscal 2027 "
  "budget.",
 "1DuIs_bR_mw":
  "A licensing meeting that spends half its time on policy. Staff ask for direction on one-day "
  "pouring permits, which now run well over a hundred a year: the regulations treat them as "
  "one-off licenses for functions held no more than annually, not a substitute for a seasonal "
  "license, and would not cover the art gallery pouring the department is being asked to allow. "
  "The board gives consensus to build a program and asks that any fee or limit change go to a "
  "public hearing with past applicants invited. A pedicab company&rsquo;s request to carry "
  "removable advertising boards and a sponsoring hotel&rsquo;s logo is denied; taxis, tours and "
  "limousines carry none, and members call it a precedent they do not want to set. In public "
  "hearings the board approves a manager change at the seasonal package store Fresh Nantucket "
  "and a new live entertainment license for the Nantucket Performing Arts Center at 5 North "
  "Water Street, the former White Heron Theater, bought by several of that theater&rsquo;s "
  "former board members with Drew Kowalkowski as executive director; the license is written as "
  "new because the old one lapsed. Public assembly permits follow for the Land Bank family "
  "picnic, the Maria Mitchell Association&rsquo;s Stargazer Gala at Tom Nevers Field, where "
  "staff press for the earlier tent and parking layout, and Theatre Workshop&rsquo;s Come "
  "Together at Bartlett&rsquo;s Farm, where tent occupancy and parking are reviewed.",
 "34NHpSKIMcE":
  "A compliance seminar for Nantucket license holders, the first the town has held since before "
  "the pandemic, with staff from the state Alcoholic Beverages Control Commission and the "
  "Department of Labor. The town&rsquo;s licensing administrator opens by explaining how annual "
  "licenses are treated when holders close for the winter: closures longer than two weeks must "
  "be reported, and sixty consecutive days closed brings the seasonal fee. State investigators "
  "then walk through the rules most often broken. Any change to an application needs approval, "
  "including new stockholders, a new LLC member or manager, and alterations to the premises, "
  "with a carve-out for adding a patio; outdoor seating no longer requires the full process. "
  "Hours are whatever the license states, and service stops then. A social media post "
  "advertising a happy hour is itself a violation, and competitors routinely forward them. "
  "Buying from a package store to resell is illegal. Drinks to go must be sealed. Eight forms of "
  "identification can be relied on, two of them added in April. The investigators explain the "
  "24J notice, filed when someone convicted of drunk driving names their last place to drink, "
  "and warn that several will bring an undercover visit. They also cover the duty to protect "
  "patrons, a statewide rise in drink tampering, and the fact that a lapsed workers compensation "
  "policy can close a business the same day.",
 "48jBwvd0xls":
  "The County Commissioners open with a House of Representatives citation honoring twenty-five "
  "years of service on the Planning Board and the planning and economic development commission, "
  "then hear from the Steamship Authority: three newer vessels will all be in service by the end "
  "of the year, and the general manager asks drivers to slow down after two employees were "
  "hospitalized in collisions, reminding people of the four-foot passing law. In public comment "
  "a landlord asks the town to write to the Department of Revenue confirming that the community "
  "impact fee was deferred for leases signed before October 2024, and another resident asks the "
  "town to put listing platforms on notice of the registration and advertising rules. The "
  "housing office gives the trust&rsquo;s quarterly update: three more units on the subsidized "
  "inventory, now 6.55 percent against a ten percent goal, closing cost help for six families, "
  "the seasonal communities designation accepted at town meeting, and a year-round deed "
  "restriction program in preparation. Speed humps on Gold Star Drive will return in August if "
  "enough neighbors sign on. The board sets the 2026 annual town meeting for May 4 at 4:30 and "
  "the election for May 19, and expects to call a special town meeting in the fall. Most of the "
  "evening goes to whether to hire a mediator on short-term rental zoning before that meeting; "
  "speakers question the closed-door format, the short timeline, and the absence of enforcement "
  "while the Land Court case is on appeal.",
 "5hnX1rhcWOI":
  "A short licensing meeting with three members present. Surfside on Broad Street has its liquor "
  "service hours extended from nine to eleven o&rsquo;clock. Staff explain this is a technical "
  "correction: state law bars a licensing board from preventing service between eleven in the "
  "morning and eleven at night, so the nine o&rsquo;clock limit written into the license in 2023 "
  "cannot stand. Compliance and neighbor relations have been good, and members confirm that "
  "conditions could still be placed on outdoor service if late crowds became a problem. "
  "Queequeg&rsquo;s on Oak Street is allowed to add up to twenty-four seats on an adjacent "
  "patio, a change the planning board cleared in May and one the board can now approve on its "
  "own under revised state policy; the patio must be fenced and visible from the main premises, "
  "and the existing entertainment license, limited to quiet background music, is unchanged. "
  "Staff report that a revised special events policy goes to the town&rsquo;s cabinet in July "
  "and should reach the board in August, and that applicants are being asked for longer lead "
  "times. Six National Grid petitions for conduit and handholes are then approved, among them "
  "one on Orange Street that can be done inside an existing road closure for sewer work.",
 "7YAHXL_aPX0":
  "September is proclaimed both Suicide Prevention Awareness Month and Climate Change Awareness "
  "Month. The state&rsquo;s deer biologist explains the extended hunting season and offers to "
  "work directly with farmers suffering crop damage, noting that deer management is driven by "
  "forest and habitat conservation. The board accepts an eelgrass management plan it calls one "
  "of the best staff reports it has seen, then opens a hearing on revised shellfishing "
  "regulations: a year of work with the Harbor and Shellfish Advisory Board, matching state "
  "terminology, clarifying catch reports and dredge inspections, and renaming the retired "
  "commercial license a recreational scallop dredge license. The advisory board&rsquo;s chair "
  "objects that rules settled over roughly thirty public meetings are being reopened at the "
  "table. Overnight noise is allowed for National Grid&rsquo;s downtown work into October. Staff "
  "give a first look at fiscal 2027 capital requests, explaining that nothing has been ranked or "
  "recommended yet, and report on the harbor master building, which is largely a public restroom "
  "and shower block with offices for the staff who hire seventy-five lifeguards. The housing "
  "director makes the case for building municipal housing on Waitt Drive rather than renting "
  "from private owners at five to seven thousand dollars a month or buying houses in competition "
  "with year-round residents. The board lets the African Meeting House workgroup follow the "
  "independent investigation it procured, and authorizes an administrator for the Vineyard Wind "
  "blade failure claims fund.",
 "9A_0zif-EuI":
  "The County Commissioners meet first and hear that the Sheep Pond Road alternative access, "
  "needed because of erosion, is advancing: an agreement with the Nantucket Conservation "
  "Foundation would let the new road cross its land in exchange for a conveyance, a temporary "
  "easement on Head of the Plains Road is being extended, and state natural heritage approval is "
  "still outstanding. A speaker asks the town to revisit the roundabout proposed at Old South "
  "and Fairgrounds roads, describing morning backups toward the airport. Sitting as the Select "
  "Board, the members convey six town-owned yard sale parcels on Folger Road in a single "
  "transaction; the six abutters hired one attorney to prepare all the deeds and the easements "
  "between them, which staff describe as far quicker than separate conveyances. Two drainage "
  "easements for the Surfside area transportation project are accepted, each granted in exchange "
  "for something from the town, released paper road easements in one case and driveway and "
  "planting work in the other. The town manager reports that the Town Meeting warrant goes to "
  "the printer this week, with ten informational sessions, a voters guide and a video with NCTV "
  "and the moderator planned. The board adopts one comment, on the digital signs article, and "
  "decides against commenting on the short-term rental articles.",
 "BxW4raQMYJk":
  "Announcements cover an information session on the proposed public works facility and shooting "
  "range at 1 Shadbush Road, a virtual open house on three design options for Easy Street flood "
  "mitigation, and soil test borings for the Somerset needs area sewer extension. In public "
  "comment a Rhode Island property owner raises health and marine impacts of offshore wind "
  "beyond the one blade failure. The board makes appointments to the Cultural Council, the "
  "Scholarship Committee and a Zoning Board of Appeals alternate seat. It extends the commercial "
  "scallop season to April 11, conditioned on the season ending if the water reaches fifty "
  "degrees, and grants noise bylaw waivers for National Grid&rsquo;s conduit work and for the "
  "Sea Street sewer force main project. Sitting as the Sewer Commission, the board holds a long "
  "public hearing on five sewer district articles headed for Annual Town Meeting. The sewer "
  "director reports that two parcels on Woodland Drive score below the checklist threshold "
  "because the treatment plant&rsquo;s capacity never included them. The developer disputes the "
  "scoring and neighbors object on density grounds. The board declines to recommend either "
  "article, noting that Town Meeting will still vote on them. Health and Human Services then "
  "report on gatekeeper training and lone star ticks, and Town Administration on public records "
  "requests, up sixfold since 2017.",
 "CMVjQRfBI2U":
  "A two-minute ceremony the morning after the annual town election. The chair congratulates "
  "Brooke Mohr and Matt Fee on winning their seats, both of them returning members, and the two "
  "sign the oath book and are sworn to carry out their duties under the constitutions of the "
  "United States and the Commonwealth and the laws of the town. Members note that choosing a "
  "chair and making committee assignments will wait until the following week.",
 "Ems45MuBQ7I":
  "With applications for two dozen boards and committees now closed, the board reads the list "
  "seat by seat: most have as many applicants as openings, the Finance Committee has eight for "
  "four, and two committees drew none. One application nearly went missing after a fax machine "
  "jammed. In public comment a resident argues the Civil War monument should come out of the "
  "roadway, saying box trucks cannot get past it. The board reappoints a constable and a "
  "registrar of voters at the town clerk&rsquo;s request. Three traffic safety recommendations "
  "follow. One on-street space goes at Gay Street, where a narrow sidewalk and wider vehicles "
  "make it hard for a resident to get out of the house; two more go on Liberty Street, where "
  "staff and the fire chief describe a stretch too narrow for two-way traffic and a turn onto "
  "India Street that fire apparatus cannot make when cars park tight to the corner. A member "
  "warns that sidewalk parking, once allowed on three streets and only where a baby carriage "
  "could still pass, has become general, and asks whether the town will keep giving its "
  "sidewalks over to wider cars. The third request, from a church at Church Court, adds two "
  "accessible spaces and removes an awkward one that blocks fire access behind the building; the "
  "chair recuses.",
 "G0qAK66ebd0":
  "Water restrictions dominate the opening announcements: the island is in a level one drought, "
  "the water department has heard from at least one customer who would rather pay a fine than "
  "comply, and a level two declaration would bring tighter rules. The board makes its annual "
  "committee appointments, most by acclamation, and settles the contested Finance Committee "
  "seats by written ballot after what one member calls the hardest vote of their time on the "
  "board. The sewer rate hearing opens with a revised tiered proposal from the finance director "
  "and the town&rsquo;s consultants, which would raise sewer revenue by about a quarter by "
  "charging according to use rather than one rate for every gallon; the board continues the "
  "hearing a week and asks for a further refinement, for the treatment of customers who have "
  "sewer but not town water, and for a courtesy meeting with the water commissioners. Two "
  "amended bulk fuel storage licenses are taken up for a gas station and a fuel dealer. Human "
  "resources presents its monthly report on wellness programs, internships and recruitment, "
  "noting vacancies down from about two hundred to a hundred and twenty-five and crediting new "
  "employee housing. The longest discussion is a proposal to put short-term rental zoning to a "
  "professional mediator over the summer, with past article sponsors as parties, so that "
  "something agreed can reach the 2026 warrant; members weigh that against a failed subcommittee "
  "and a pending court ruling.",
 "HaZLYdqBLqk":
  "The board opens with a moment of silence for a former member who had also served on the "
  "Historic District Commission, the historical commission and the Planning Board. Announcements "
  "cover an information session on the proposed 45-bed Our Island Home, two open houses on the "
  "public works facility, and the two-part Master Plan visioning program in April. A resident "
  "uses public comment to say he cannot get his vehicles inspected because the island&rsquo;s "
  "inspection station keeps turning cars away. The board fills two citizen seats on the African "
  "Meeting House Investigation Procurement Workgroup by paper ballot, and the chair states that "
  "the group&rsquo;s charge is limited to selecting a vendor to review how the investigation was "
  "conducted, not to reinvestigate the crime. The school district&rsquo;s finance officer then "
  "asks to draw on the special education reserve fund for out-of-district tuition, reporting "
  "eleven students placed off island, annual tuition increases of seven to eight percent, and a "
  "deficit near $747,000 that staff vacancies no longer offset. The board votes unanimously to "
  "end the Pleasant Street one-way pilot immediately, after residents describe wrong-way drivers "
  "and congestion, and will return to choosing between a sidewalk and a bike lane. It closes by "
  "reviewing which Town Meeting articles warrant board comments.",
 "IGCPhbwG9JI":
  "A short meeting. The fire chief, whose department took over emergency management in June, "
  "gives the Fourth of July debrief: four state troopers on island, police calls mostly about "
  "parking, crowded beaches and noise, one arrest for driving under the influence, 33 ambulance "
  "calls and 50 fire calls that were largely false alarms, and more than 37,000 visitors counted "
  "at the guarded beaches through July 6. The hospital is granted a noise waiver for two nights "
  "of line painting in September. The board approves the sale of two series of bond anticipation "
  "notes, just over $30 million for municipal purposes and $6.8 million for airport projects, at "
  "interest costs the town&rsquo;s adviser calls among the best rates seen in the sale. It "
  "accepts the deed for 9 Goldfinch Drive, a three-bedroom house bought for $1.9 million under a "
  "town meeting article to house municipal employees. The continued sewer rate hearing closes "
  "and the tiered increases are adopted unanimously, reworked so that every customer contributes "
  "rather than some seeing a decrease, and split so the first takes effect for August usage and "
  "the second in January, leaving time for public outreach. A member urges residents to follow "
  "the Affordable Housing Trust, where programs are expanding.",
 "J2C8L_ojR8c":
  "The meeting opens with a moment of silence for a longtime Siasconset water superintendent. In "
  "public comment a resident thanks the town manager for writing to the online platforms about "
  "short-term rental rules, and another asks hard questions about the Loran station, where town "
  "employees live: what the four and a half million dollars voted at town meeting actually "
  "bought, who maintains the building, and why a septic system pumped twice a month in summer is "
  "not treated as failed, arguing the town cannot ask for money for Our Island Home while its "
  "own buildings decay. The board authorizes a letter asking the state to certify the housing "
  "production plan, which keeps the town in safe harbor under Chapter 40B on the strength of "
  "thirty-one new units, and accepts a deed from the Coast Guard for land off Milestone Road "
  "that Congress transferred to the town two decades ago but which took four federal real estate "
  "officers to finish. The long item is encroachment: stakes, rocks, hedges and lawns that have "
  "crept onto public ways. Staff, helped by summer interns, review the existing policy, the "
  "three-foot sight line rule near corners, and the letters sent to owners, and members ask why "
  "past enforcement drives lose momentum, how parking pressure has changed the Cliff, and "
  "whether hedges planted on lot lines should be caught during permitting. Procurement reports "
  "that the restroom and harbor master projects will not start this season.",
 "L21toDeoCAI":
  "The longest meeting of the year. The deputy housing director reports that the state has added "
  "twenty-eight units at Meadows I to the subsidized housing inventory, lifting the town to 7 "
  "percent and buying another year of safe harbor under Chapter 40B. Culture and tourism "
  "debriefs the summer&rsquo;s feature film shoot, laying out the months of contact with "
  "location scouts that preceded it, and the board adopts amendments to the town film policy, "
  "among them ninety days&rsquo; notice; staff note they still turn down requests that would "
  "close Main Street in July and August. The coastal resilience committee gives its quarterly "
  "report. At the tax classification hearing the assessor shows how value and levy share have "
  "moved among the property classes since 2010, and the board again keeps the 25 percent "
  "residential exemption and the shift onto commercial property, with a member arguing that the "
  "question deserves a meeting of its own rather than a rush before town meeting. An hour and a "
  "half goes to Our Island Home. Survey results show little appetite for a smaller building, for "
  "rebuilding on the current site, or for doing nothing, and support falls further when the cost "
  "of Sherburne Commons is used as a comparison. The design under discussion would drop one of "
  "the three residential wings so that a single nursing station could serve the rest. "
  "Pre-qualification is being advertised now, bids are expected in February, and the warrant "
  "must be set in January. Traffic safety brings recommendations for Easton Street, Waitt Drive "
  "and Hussey Street, one of them continued. The board also agrees to remake the roads and "
  "rights of way committee as a public access and transportation planning committee, taking up "
  "the work of the bicycle and pedestrian committee.",
 "Mi8-afkOBwg":
  "The County Commissioners grant Ticcoma Green the use of thirty-two of the fifty-one spaces in "
  "a town parking area on Waitt Drive for its tenants, with the police department issuing "
  "placards against a lease and a plate; the vote is three to two. In public comment a resident "
  "asks whether the town meeting warrant and the agendas could be translated into Spanish and "
  "Portuguese so that more registered voters can take part, and another describes years of "
  "trying to build affordable housing on a site of their own. National Grid reports on the smart "
  "meter rollout and the translation services offered with it. The housing office gives the "
  "trust&rsquo;s quarterly report, most of it on the transfer fee: testimony in July on the "
  "statewide enabling bill and in September on the island&rsquo;s home rule petition, where "
  "twelve people spoke, among them the school superintendent, the police chief and the housing "
  "director. Planning and land use services presents its monthly report, with 2,110 historic "
  "district certificates reviewed over the year, a grant application for more than $240,000 to "
  "replace oil furnaces with heat pumps, and the savings returned by the municipal electricity "
  "program. The finance director sets out where the fiscal 2027 budget stands, including school "
  "requests driven by out-of-district special education costs. The board continues sorting its "
  "own 2026 articles on electric bikes, noise and home rule petitions.",
 "OdBMypR4MC4":
  "The chair reads out answers to Vineyard Wind questions asked at earlier meetings, covering "
  "when the town learned of the revised construction and operations plan, water quality testing "
  "after the blade collapse, and a marine life assessment whose samples are still at the lab. In "
  "public comment a resident raises PFAS in the town water supply. At the finance "
  "director&rsquo;s request the board then declares a state of emergency over PFAS contamination "
  "at Water Department Well 15. The declaration allows spending beyond appropriations to design "
  "and install a filtration system and waives certain procurement advertising requirements; the "
  "same mechanism was used for the 2018 sewer force main break, and the cost will come to Town "
  "Meeting as a capital article. Department heads present the fiscal year 2026 enterprise fund "
  "budgets for Our Island Home, sewer and stormwater, and water. The administrator of Our Island "
  "Home reports higher costs for food, medical supplies, pharmacy and fuel, and an effort to "
  "replace travel nursing with permanent hires. The last hour goes to a new public comment "
  "policy brought forward by town counsel in response to Barron v. Kolenda, which struck down a "
  "civility rule. The board adopts it with three minutes per speaker and thirty minutes overall, "
  "dropping a proposed ten-minute provision.",
 "Pl4BIM6-s8o":
  "A licensing meeting of four items. The board takes up a change of hours for the seasonal wine "
  "and malt license at the Straight Wharf Fish Market, whose 2023 approval had been conditioned "
  "on a 9 p.m. last call under an agreement with abutters. Staff report no complaints in the "
  "year since. On advice that state law allows service until 11 p.m. and that the board cannot "
  "set an earlier hour except by mutual agreement, the motion is amended to extend the license "
  "to 11 p.m. subject to the private agreement between the licensee and the abutters. The board "
  "then approves the transfer of the all-alcoholic restaurant license at 20 Broad Street from "
  "Dune Nantucket to Billie&rsquo;s on Broad, which will seat about 149, forty of them outside. "
  "It denies the accompanying application for a live entertainment license, preferring that the "
  "new operator apply for individual event permits through the season and return next year. It "
  "closes by approving the National Grid conduit petition at Still Dock, continued from January "
  "13, after the company resolved abutters&rsquo; concerns about delivery access during "
  "construction.",
 "PuRzAin84kw":
  "The longest item is Our Island Home, where staff lay out what closing the existing nursing "
  "home would involve: a letter of closure starts a 120-day clock for residents to leave, with "
  "notification, counseling, placement, family meetings and an admissions freeze all required, "
  "and only a possible extension for the island&rsquo;s distance. A set of design changes might "
  "save about nineteen million dollars but needs agreement from Sherburne Commons residents, and "
  "a November special town meeting is no longer realistic, so any new article waits for 2026. "
  "Members and residents weigh fewer beds against the larger operating subsidy that lower "
  "revenue would force in perpetuity, the five million a year an override already covers because "
  "Medicaid and MassHealth fall short, whether the hospital could take on long-term care, which "
  "licensing rules largely bar, and whether to sell the current site or let the Land Bank buy "
  "it, which several are reluctant to do. The African Meeting House investigation procurement "
  "workgroup reports that its request for proposals had an omission, now being corrected, with "
  "responses due July 2; speakers question the wording of the advertisement and who approved it. "
  "The board approves the transfer of the Academy Hill housing management contract to a new "
  "company formed by its managers, reviews preservation restrictions proposed for two "
  "properties, and lets the film festival hang banners for its thirtieth year. A second round of "
  "committee applicants introduce themselves.",
 "QqHAaZeXR4Y":
  "The board signs off on the annual renewal of seasonal liquor licenses, which run April 1 to "
  "January 15. Staff note that renewal is by right but that each licensee must still clear "
  "inspections, and report two non-renewals, one of them a quota license whose holder has "
  "decided not to reopen at Straight Wharf. The board approves the state event notification form "
  "for the Daffodil Parade and Sconset tailgate picnic on April 26, the annual waiver allowing "
  "alcohol at the picnic, and charity wine permits for Nantucket Wine and Food Festival events "
  "in May. It grants a new seasonal all-alcoholic restaurant license to the Easy Street "
  "Restaurant at 31 Easy Street, contingent on the other boards&rsquo; approvals, after the "
  "applicant&rsquo;s attorney notes that a restaurant of up to seventy seats is allowed by "
  "right. A non-live entertainment license follows, limited to background music with speakers "
  "facing inward and outdoor sound ending at 10 p.m., matching the restaurant next door. The "
  "board closes by approving the transfer of the seasonal package store license from Current "
  "Vintage to Mor Wine at 16 Federal Street and changing its category to all-alcoholic "
  "beverages; with the package store quota full, a transfer is the only route to a license.",
 "RSJBPr64ddQ":
  "Announcements include a fourth round of committee vacancies, state hearings on proposed deer "
  "regulations, and the transfer of film, photo shoot and special event permitting from the "
  "police department to the Culture and Tourism Department. In public comment a resident asks "
  "why a benefit approved at Town Meeting has not reached town employees. The board meets "
  "jointly with the Capital Program Committee and the Finance Committee, receives the fiscal "
  "year 2026 capital report and endorses it. A public hearing follows on amendments to Chapter "
  "375 of the town regulations, covering traffic and parking. Staff describe new definitions for "
  "municipal paid parking lots and for a third-party parking vendor, revised maximum parking "
  "times, and an enforcement plan for abandoned vehicles. The effective date is left to town "
  "administration, with March 1 suggested. The board then spends an hour and forty minutes "
  "adopting the May 3 Annual Town Meeting warrant. Most of that time goes to Chapter 58 and car "
  "rental agencies, and to whether a single peer-to-peer vehicle at one address should sit "
  "outside the medallion system; counsel for two rental companies argues it should not. The "
  "board leaves the e-bike and motorized device article alone, and senior planner Megan Trudel "
  "presents a cottage communities zoning article.",
 "U5CTd0W5ZuY":
  "With annual town meeting three days away, the board previews informational videos on two "
  "appropriation articles headed to the ballot, among them new town employee housing on Waitt "
  "Drive. A resident asks again for a detailed legal explanation of why small dwellings already "
  "permitted on island cannot count under the new by-right accessory dwelling unit law, and "
  "warns about neighborhood crowding. A team from the National Science Foundation, the "
  "international ocean drilling program and the British Geological Survey presents Expedition "
  "501, an offshore freshwater drilling project in the Northwest Atlantic, and answers questions "
  "about contamination risk, citing 2019 imaging that still showed clear separation between "
  "fresh and salt water at earlier bore holes. In a public hearing the board renews ten-year "
  "private aquaculture licenses for five growers in the head of the harbor, all found in "
  "compliance, and expands one of them to its full ten acres; another holder&rsquo;s licenses "
  "are left to expire and will go to the waiting list. The DPW director then walks through the "
  "island&rsquo;s road reconstruction schedule, including sewer work, the state project at "
  "Milestone and Polpis, National Grid&rsquo;s downtown work, and water main and airport "
  "projects, and members remark on how much has come due at once. The board approves the "
  "purchase of the composter and transfer station and a new waste services agreement.",
 "UMYA1cpUwEY":
  "A six-minute special meeting with one purpose. The board opens the warrant for the November 4 "
  "special town meeting, confirms that no further articles have been submitted, and closes and "
  "adopts it. Both articles concern short-term rentals, and members set the order: the full "
  "codification article first, the planning board&rsquo;s article second. A member warns of "
  "stepping away from the table if the discussion turns to the merits, and the chair stresses "
  "that positions on the articles will be taken at a later meeting; this session was only about "
  "fitting a special town meeting into a timetable set by a citizen petition, and the planning "
  "board and the finance committee will each hold public hearings where there is more time to "
  "speak. The timeline in the packet is endorsed.",
 "XbeuBbug6SU":
  "Much of the meeting is taken up with written answers to questions asked earlier. Staff "
  "explain that deer management belongs to the state rather than the board; that the layer about "
  "eighty feet down in the aquifer is semi-confining rather than impermeable, confirmed by "
  "transducers placed above and below it during pump tests, with the true clay barrier below "
  "roughly 250 feet and recharge passing slowly through; and that a records search covering "
  "twenty-eight names turned up no correspondence between town staff and a subdivision&rsquo;s "
  "representatives beyond a single voicemail. On short-term rentals, renewal reminders went out "
  "on September 12 after a week&rsquo;s delay in the registration system, 1,354 permits have "
  "been issued since the program began in 2024, and the most common reasons for rejection are "
  "insurance documents and unfinished applications. The board approves a preservation "
  "restriction on Vestal Street and hears the coastal resilience committee describe raising a "
  "roadway with a shared path, a living levee beside a parking lot, and lifting a park above the "
  "marsh. It then clears up the ownership of Tom Nevers Road, accepting a release deed from the "
  "state for parcels nobody meant to hold and conveying the full layout to the county, which "
  "lets the bike path design move ahead. The town manager reports on the parking lot assessment, "
  "code problems in town buildings, and the timetable for a special town meeting warrant.",
 "Ziz2DpWZFts":
  "The film production request for Five Star Weekend is held to August 20 because the logistics "
  "are not settled. Announcements cover the continuing level one drought restrictions, an "
  "information session on the Surfside roadway, drainage and bike path project due to start "
  "after Labor Day, and weekly pond testing for harmful algal blooms. Staff read answers to "
  "earlier questions about offshore wind, among them why the town wrote to the Interior "
  "Secretary about South Coast Wind. A resident praises the response to an incident on Main "
  "Street the night before but notes that it took three hours to get a state police dog to the "
  "island, and asks whether local officers could be trained for such events. The board appoints "
  "election workers, designates them special municipal employees, and fills three seats on the "
  "Historical Commission by ballot from seven applicants, with members calling it the strongest "
  "field they have seen. Gifts accepted include money for bike path improvements and five "
  "granite way markers. The information technology department reports on monthly cybersecurity "
  "training for employees, its work with state and federal agencies, and the rise of phishing "
  "written with artificial intelligence. On short-term rentals, town counsel says no one has "
  "told him they intend to petition for a special town meeting and advises waiting until the "
  "proposals come together, with the planning board likely to bring an article to the 2026 "
  "annual town meeting.",
 "_i_jkrFJFs4":
  "Mandatory outdoor water restrictions are in effect under a level one drought, limiting "
  "irrigation to two days a week by street address and none from Friday through Sunday, and "
  "staff explain that the town&rsquo;s new agenda system requires everyone to resubscribe for "
  "meeting notices. National Grid presents a non-wires alternative program launching in July, "
  "which tries to meet growing demand through peak shifting, home batteries and local generation "
  "rather than new substations and cable: island peak load has risen from about forty megawatts "
  "in 2004 to around fifty-five last year, with higher forecasts ahead, and all of it still "
  "arrives on two undersea cables. The board then meets jointly with the Board of Health. Most "
  "of that discussion is about regulating large gatherings: whether health regulations are the "
  "right instrument, who would be liable, how anything could be enforced on private roads, and "
  "whether rules should cover the whole island rather than short-term rentals alone. Members and "
  "callers press for real enforcement to be costed out instead of treated as impossible. The "
  "health board also explains its temporary moratorium on septic variances in nitrogen sensitive "
  "areas, meant to buy time for expert advice on the risks and benefits of alternative systems "
  "rather than to deny them outright. The board signs documents transferring the open space at "
  "Wiggles Way to the Land Bank, supports a land trust&rsquo;s tax exemption application, and "
  "approves a late-night noise waiver for Bash in the Bog and new parking restrictions on "
  "Ticcoma Way.",
 "_tyihhs5Sgc":
  "The board&rsquo;s first meeting after the election begins with the election of officers: Dawn "
  "Holdgate is chosen chair and Matt Fee vice chair, and the outgoing chair hands over the seat. "
  "Staff read the committee applicant list again and summarize the master plan update&rsquo;s "
  "outreach so far, including an islandwide survey with more than ten thousand participants. In "
  "public comment a resident asks what the board will treat as a favorable vote on Our Island "
  "Home, noting the ballot question passed by 185 votes while the town meeting article failed "
  "its two-thirds, and reports that the unmarked lanes at the state&rsquo;s Milestone and Polpis "
  "project nearly caused a head-on collision. Two utility easements to National Grid are "
  "granted, one at Town Hall for a switchgear module and conduit and one off Amelia and Waitt "
  "drives, both authorized at town meeting. The town manager reviews ballot questions whose "
  "companion articles failed, starting with the public works facility, where the design funding "
  "lost both votes; the recommendation is to work with the architect toward a smaller building "
  "and a stronger outreach effort for 2026. On Our Island Home, members say the site cannot take "
  "a renovation that would meet current code without moving residents out. Committee assignments "
  "are set for the coming year, and the County Commissioners name their representative to the "
  "planning and economic development commission.",
 "adnBwqrL2h4":
  "The annual licensing meeting. The board begins the renewal of the general business "
  "categories, common victualler, entertainment, motor vehicle sales and rentals, mobile food "
  "units and flower and produce vendors, subject to inspections and fees, and sets a date early "
  "in the new year to take up the rental car medallions that are assigned but never used. "
  "Fifty-five annual liquor licenses are renewed, two of them with conditions: one holder must "
  "file a change of manager, and another, closed for more than a year, must say how and when it "
  "intends to reopen. Several that shut from January to April are told they may be moved to "
  "seasonal licenses. The board keeps the seasonal population estimate at 45,000 while asking "
  "staff for better data next year, and agrees the island has enough package stores for now. A "
  "new restaurant at 22 Federal Street receives a restaurant liquor license and a live "
  "entertainment license, with service allowed on the patio but no music there. A consultant for "
  "Verizon explains how utility poles are chosen for small cell antennas and wins approval for "
  "six attachments, and a long run of National Grid conduit petitions is approved, several of "
  "them reaching private roads the board has no say over.",
 "afgIlnNLOZg":
  "Announcements cover the Master Plan community survey, open until January 24, and two state "
  "hearings on proposed deer regulations that would create a January primitive-firearms season "
  "on Nantucket, Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard and the Elizabeth Islands. The town manager reports "
  "that the Bureau of Safety and Environmental Enforcement session has been postponed again, "
  "with no new date. The board accepts a corrected gift to the Saltmarsh Senior Center and a "
  "recurring $11,000 foundation gift for fire department training. The coastal resilience "
  "coordinator delivers the advisory committee&rsquo;s third and fourth quarter reports, "
  "covering the Francis Street Beach project, now moving into schematic design, and five concept "
  "alternatives for flood mitigation on Easy Street. Consultants then present the completed "
  "design for the Baxter Road alternative access. The board endorses the design plan while "
  "stating that it is not endorsing the cost, and the town manager notes that not every affected "
  "property owner has offered an easement. The remaining two hours continue the review of the "
  "2025 Annual Town Meeting warrant. Planning staff take the board through twenty-five Planning "
  "Board articles, most of them zoning map changes implementing the 2009 master plan. The "
  "longest debate is again peer-to-peer car rental and whether it belongs under the medallion "
  "cap.",
 "auTA4YHo_FU":
  "Culture and Tourism closes out the season: 273 special event permit applications this year, "
  "all but eight finished, with 170 handled over the summer and 66 in August alone against "
  "eleven in September. Two compliance hearings follow. More than a dozen noise complaints "
  "around a downtown restaurant bring its licensee and a neighboring one before the board, and "
  "the discussion turns to the town having no entertainment regulations at all and having "
  "applied conditions unevenly in the downtown core; staff will convene businesses and the "
  "chamber to write rules, with sound measured at the source so that officers and operators both "
  "know when a limit is passed. The hearing on a noodle bar&rsquo;s violations, outdoor dining "
  "with alcohol past ten o&rsquo;clock without a signed permit and alcohol carried off the "
  "premises, is tabled to October after the licensee does not respond, though the cease and "
  "desist stands. The board denies the Hussey Street pole relocation on learning that the "
  "driveway was widened and landscaping removed without historic district permits, now the "
  "subject of a violation notice, and that a neighbor has been recommended for an accessible "
  "parking space the move could displace; members say they will not reward work done before "
  "permission is sought. Several new service petitions are approved, one conditioned on "
  "restoring the road so an abutter&rsquo;s runoff problem does not return, and the last takes "
  "down a stretch of poles and overhead wires along Willard Street and Hulbert Avenue.",
 "co5rNr2KWoo":
  "The chair reads out answers to Vineyard Wind questions, including that cost recovery "
  "negotiations with the developer contemplate damages for future blade failures. A member holds "
  "back a pending contract for a traffic study of the Pleasant Street one-way pilot, arguing the "
  "board should first decide whether the pilot continues; that review is added to the following "
  "week. Members of the Nantucket Historical Commission, short of a quorum and so speaking "
  "individually, join the board for an hour on the Easy Street flood mitigation study. The "
  "coastal resilience coordinator and consultants describe drainage work, runoff controls, "
  "stormwater pumping and an elevated roadway concept that would sit above the floors of "
  "adjacent buildings, and report that Easy Street could flood regularly as soon as 2030. The "
  "preservation planner raises the effect on the character of the National Historic Landmark "
  "district, including the view from the ferry. The board approves the sale of $32.9 million in "
  "general obligation bonds and $5.79 million in bond anticipation notes. It reconsiders its "
  "December denial of a curb cut at 126 Main Street, after the applicant&rsquo;s representative "
  "says she was never notified of that hearing, and unanimously upholds the denial. It raises "
  "Our Island Home private pay rates by about seven percent, the first increase since 2019, and "
  "supports legislation placing a surcharge on streaming services to fund community media.",
 "cp7CkYDFRno":
  "Announcements cover the February 7 deadline for committee vacancies, the Master Plan "
  "visioning kick-off with Charles Marohn of Strong Towns on February 13, and the transportation "
  "planner&rsquo;s update on the Pleasant Street one-way pilot. In public comment a resident "
  "argues that the bylaw requiring property owners to clear abutting sidewalks of snow should be "
  "amended or repealed, and raises the question of liability when a cleared sidewalk refreezes. "
  "The board accepts gifts of $6,600 for the Brant Point shellfish hatchery and $250 for the "
  "Saltmarsh Senior Center. It then approves a request from Nantucket Moorings to move from "
  "static to dynamic pricing on rental moorings, with a $70 base rate that could rise toward "
  "$100 in the six busiest weeks of summer; the last fee change was approved in 2020. Staff "
  "report on Nantucket&rsquo;s PFAS presentation at the New England Water Environment "
  "Association conference, and the new environmental contamination administrator, Andrew "
  "Shapiro, gives his takeaways. The board closes by establishing a working group to oversee the "
  "review of the investigation into the hate crime at the African Meeting House, called for by "
  "Article 36 of the 2024 Annual Town Meeting, after an earlier procurement process drew "
  "objections from the article&rsquo;s proponents.",
 "dMgKJpI2UOY":
  "The County Commissioners meet first and approve a memorandum of understanding with the town, "
  "the Nantucket Conservation Foundation and the eleven Sheep Pond Lane homeowners, resolving an "
  "access question that needed repeated temporary easements and a three-year home rule petition; "
  "the Select Board approves the same agreement. The police chief reads a National Police Week "
  "proclamation and presents appreciation awards. A South Water Street household wins a waiver "
  "of the one-commercial- vehicle limit on residential parking permits, on condition it keep two "
  "permits instead of four, matching a 2019 case; staff caution against one-off exceptions. The "
  "board raises the airport fuel revolving fund limit from six to eight and a half million "
  "dollars against projected costs near 7.9 million, and buys 77 Bartlett Road for a pumping "
  "station in the Somerset area sewer, drainage and roadway project. An extension of the Ticcoma "
  "Green land development agreement passes four to one; the sixty-four rental units, reaching "
  "down to thirty percent of area median income, missed a June closing and will add two years of "
  "safe harbor. The auditor reviews fiscal 2024, noting steadily rising reserves and about 1.3 "
  "million dollars in unclaimed Chapter 90 road reimbursements now being recovered. In a long "
  "public hearing the board unanimously upholds the Historic District Commission&rsquo;s "
  "approval of a 140-square-foot addition at 23 Ocean Avenue, denying a neighbor&rsquo;s appeal.",
 "e6OhPyLpB2s":
  "The recording covers two meetings. The County Commissioners meet first and hold a public "
  "hearing on an order of taking by eminent domain, for the temporary construction, drainage and "
  "slope easements and the permanent utility easements needed for the second phase of the "
  "Surfside area transportation project around Lovers Lane, Okorwaw Avenue and Monohansett Road. "
  "A Webster Road abutter asks what will happen to a corner marked by boulders and scotch broom. "
  "The public works director explains that a dirt surface is preferred to gravel for "
  "conservation permitting. The commissioners report that a bill improving access to the Housing "
  "Court has been filed by the island&rsquo;s legislators. The Select Board convenes at 6:07. A "
  "member marks the death of a former Select Board member, and in public comment a local "
  "reporter asks the board to urge the Land Bank to record and stream its meetings. The board "
  "accepts a sewer easement on Jones Street in Siasconset and approves two National Grid "
  "licenses, one at 16 Broad Street and one serving the seasonal workforce housing dormitory at "
  "19 Waitt Drive; both still require Town Meeting approval. It then selects Waste Options "
  "Nantucket to operate the solid waste facility, unanimously, and delegates the contract "
  "negotiation to the town manager.",
 "eNdi1S_NTWk":
  "The board takes the legislative update first so the island&rsquo;s delegation can leave "
  "early, then recesses to meet as County Commissioners. Senator Cyr and Representative Moakley "
  "describe the new seasonal communities designation, which Nantucket qualifies for "
  "automatically and which bundles housing tools the island had previously sought one home rule "
  "petition at a time. They report that a Senate bill raising the Steamship Authority "
  "embarkation fee from fifty cents to a dollar did not advance in the House. The town manager "
  "adds that twenty-one turbines still carry blades of the type that failed, that four complete "
  "sets have been removed, and that a vessel dedicated to blade removal is expected. As County "
  "Commissioners the board approves the Registry of Deeds budget. Real estate items follow: an "
  "easement and license for a relocated sidewalk and drop-off area on North Beach Street, a "
  "condition of a 2022 special permit, and permission to convey a lot on Masaquet Avenue under "
  "yard sale deed restrictions, with one member recused. The last hour is the quarterly PFAS "
  "update, covering landfill concentrations, sampling of reclaimed soil stockpiles and "
  "bench-scale treatment of wastewater centrate, followed by the airport and solid waste "
  "enterprise fund budgets. The board adjourns to executive session.",
 "eOkTxbzzwdk":
  "Four utility petitions, two of them straightforward. National Grid is approved for a handhole "
  "and conduit serving a house on Madaket Road, and for a junction box, transformer and "
  "underground conduits at Brewster Road after confirming the work will stay out of a gravel "
  "road the public works department maintains, where sewer, water and two force mains already "
  "run. The two pole relocations, tied to driveway changes at Hussey and Pleasant streets, are "
  "both continued to September. Members say the applications arrived incomplete: they cannot "
  "tell what the driveway work involves, one site sits at what a member calls one of the worst "
  "bottlenecks on the island, and neighbors may be seeking an accessible parking space across "
  "the street. One member argues for denying the weaker application outright so that applicants "
  "get the message, but the board settles on delay rather than treating two similar cases "
  "differently. Staff report that a new pole has already been set at the Pleasant Street "
  "location before any hearing, and in the wrong place, out past the curb line; the utility "
  "believes it is temporary. The chair warns that the arguments will have to be strong in "
  "September, citing a dislike of yards turned into parking lots, and asks that utilities come "
  "to the town before the work rather than after.",
 "f_jJidq8b4s":
  "The board meets remotely in observance of Yom Kippur and finishes in about five minutes. "
  "Announcements cover the Affordable Housing Trust&rsquo;s new year-round deed restriction "
  "pilot, which offers financial incentives to owners and buyers who commit a property to "
  "year-round use; a survey on the future of Our Island Home open until the end of October; and "
  "the opening of the May 2026 annual town meeting warrant to citizen articles until November "
  "17, with two sessions of consultation with town counsel. A member remarks on never having "
  "faced a town meeting where they have a conflict on both of the articles before it. The appeal "
  "of the Historic District Commission&rsquo;s approval to demolish a building on New Whale "
  "Street is continued to November 5. Nobody speaks in public comment.",
 "gJq-IQjjqNo":
  "A day-long facilitated retreat on the board&rsquo;s strategic plan. Members start with the "
  "forces behind the work: wealth and competition for real estate, the town buying property to "
  "house essential workers, the subsidized housing inventory goals that keep unfriendly "
  "comprehensive permits and lawsuits at bay, and the traffic that follows when people cannot "
  "live near where they work. One member says the island&rsquo;s value is a community people "
  "join rather than a place to hide from the world, and asks how the town can say that in its "
  "communications. Staff report that the plan is about seven years old: fewer than one in ten "
  "goals are complete, because goals span several budget years and cost real money, while "
  "roughly half the initiatives the board has approved are finished. Members note how much work "
  "falls outside the plan altogether, including public health programs and some eight hundred "
  "thousand dollars in grants to local nonprofits. The rest of the day goes line by line, "
  "confirming what is complete, moving a sentence about energy-efficient building from housing "
  "to the environmental goal, clarifying that the Sparks Avenue project is a shared-use path, "
  "reviving a lapsed education program for septic applicants, adding a goal on recruiting more "
  "people to boards and committees, and asking for an analysis of the Somerset sewer needs area "
  "as a whole.",
 "iRKh-W_WdM8":
  "Announcements include public sessions by the Town Council Study Committee on a possible shift "
  "from Town Meeting to a town council form of government. The team for the proposed Our Island "
  "Home takes most of the first hour. The architect reports Planning Board and Historic District "
  "Commission approvals in hand and the public health review nearly complete, and sets out a "
  "total project budget of $134 million, of which $125.9 million would be sought at Town Meeting "
  "after an earlier appropriation. That figure includes about $13.5 million of housing costs "
  "tied to Sherburne Commons, two contingencies above $10 million, and insurance and medical "
  "equipment. The town manager notes a separate fundraising campaign by the Friends of Our "
  "Island Home, which the town cannot run itself under campaign finance law. Staff then report "
  "that the 2 Fairgrounds Road parking lot reopens April 15, having been closed since November "
  "for a state PFAS well investigation, dormitory staging and the reclamation of a third of an "
  "acre of pavement. The board reviews the terms of the waste services agreement and the "
  "purchase of the composter and transfer station; the request for proposals drew a single bid, "
  "and consultants say the higher price buys the town control it did not have before. A comment "
  "letter on an offshore freshwater drilling expedition is approved.",
 "ik2NgPIF--4":
  "The finance director presents the fiscal 2027 general fund budget, with debt service at $16 "
  "million, retirement up 8.5 percent, insurance and enterprise fund subsidies, and free cash "
  "expected near last year&rsquo;s $15.2 million, $8.2 million of it recommended for town and "
  "school capital. The capital review follows, and most of the public is there for the high "
  "school track and field. Students describe losing the end of their seasons to rescheduled "
  "games when rain and boat cancellations close the grass fields, the superintendent describes a "
  "summer spent trying to grow grass, and the sustainability coordinator says the chemical "
  "questions raised about artificial turf were put to experts and resolved. Other capital items "
  "include town employee housing, a fourth phase of seasonal housing for public safety, and "
  "rebuilding the roller hockey rink in post-tensioned concrete striped for tennis, pickleball "
  "and basketball, with a member noting that the town inherits privately built facilities like "
  "it along with their repair bills. The board works through the Somerset sewer question, "
  "including how betterments would be assessed and how long the owners of new septic systems "
  "could wait to connect. The housing office asks to modify the housing production plan to hold "
  "40B safe harbor and to spend $550,000 to continue the Lease to Locals program. The airport "
  "manager wins an increase in the fuel revolving fund cap from $6 million to $8.5 million, the "
  "fund that has carried more than $10 million of PFAS work. Sitting as County Commissioners, "
  "the board then hears a homeowners association&rsquo;s request to take an easement over Egan "
  "Lane, a fix twenty years in the making.",
 "lx1v8DTlxL8":
  "The board confirms three non-renewals of seasonal liquor licenses, then spends most of the "
  "meeting on policy. Culture and Tourism presents its first report on event permitting since "
  "taking the function over, with a full list of permitted events and statistics going back to "
  "2015. Staff raise the strain of days carrying several events at once, since police and fire "
  "still have to answer emergency calls, and describe late-night crowd problems at the larger "
  "festivals. A proposed rental car medallion waiting list draws similar concern: staff say an "
  "opening could bring three hundred applicants in May with no system in place to process them. "
  "The licensing administrator then reviews the 2025 sidewalk dining conditions. Pedestrian "
  "passage is the main issue, along with trash, inconsistent furniture and lighting, and host "
  "stands and statues appearing on sidewalks outside the permitted areas; tables that cannot "
  "leave adequate clearance would be reduced in size. The board approves extended entertainment "
  "hours for a June event at Cisco Brewers, a change of officers at the Sankaty Head Beach Club, "
  "and a new seasonal package store license for Provisions at Harbor Square, which became "
  "possible when a non-renewal freed a seasonal slot. It also approves management transfers for "
  "two hotel licenses and two utility petitions.",
 "mKhiAtxKLpI":
  "A short licensing meeting. Culture and tourism reports twenty-two event permit applications "
  "for October and November, twelve of them serving alcohol, and asks anyone planning a 2026 "
  "event to wait for the new application form in January. The licensing administrator notes that "
  "restaurants may request one-day entertainment permits for the holiday stroll, with outdoor "
  "music ending at 10 p.m. and fire code conditions attached. The board then holds two public "
  "hearings on 27 Fairgrounds Road, where the restaurant is changing hands: the transfer of the "
  "annual all-alcoholic common victualler license to Nantucket Fairgrounds, with no change in "
  "the type of license or the operation and the spelling of the name brought into line with the "
  "road, and a new live entertainment license on the same terms as the old one, with "
  "entertainment ending by midnight. Both pass unanimously by roll call.",
 "oRcnzLPo-So":
  "The board hears the Nantucket Preservation Trust&rsquo;s appeal of the Historic District "
  "Commission&rsquo;s approval to demolish the brick building at 10 New Whale Street, sitting "
  "with the commission for an hour of argument. The trust says the commissioners relied on an "
  "engineer&rsquo;s report that never concluded the brick was beyond saving, and that a second "
  "engineer described how the building could be stabilized; counsel for the owner answers that "
  "two engineers inspected the building in person, that the fire department recommends "
  "demolition, and that a rebuild would produce a replication rather than a restored building. "
  "Commissioners defend their deliberations, and the chair casts the deciding vote to let the "
  "approval stand. Earlier the board waives sewer connection fees for covenant homes on Cato "
  "Lane and Toombs Court and approves a regulatory agreement for the rental project at 18-26 "
  "Sparks Avenue. Questions from prior meetings are answered, among them a step-by-step account "
  "of how the Affordable Housing Trust evaluates proposals. Public works and solid waste gives "
  "the monthly departmental report: budgets across seven accounts, a fresh assessment of town "
  "buildings in three phases, outdoor lighting work, and training that taught crews to crown and "
  "compact dirt roads. Much of the evening&rsquo;s testimony concerns the proposed noise bylaw, "
  "where contractors ask for a 7:30 start, a golf course asks that routine mowing be exempt, and "
  "landscapers describe the quiet work that could be done outside the hours. The police chief "
  "explains what state law counts as an electric bike.",
 "p51Vcd1ZQB4":
  "Four boards sit together for the first time, the Select Board with the Planning Board, the "
  "Affordable Housing Trust and the Board of Health, for a two-hour facilitated conversation "
  "about housing with no votes taken. Members begin with values, and most return to the same "
  "gap: public money has done reasonably well for households at eighty percent of area median "
  "income and below, while people earning far more still cannot find a place, the missing middle "
  "that one speaker puts as high as 240 percent of median. The scale of the money needed comes "
  "up early and nobody has an answer for it. Permitting takes much of the first hour. Members "
  "describe a process grown too complex for an owner to navigate alone, the arrival of accessory "
  "dwelling units, and applicants who go first to whichever board is likeliest to say yes, "
  "leaving the others to inherit the decision. A visiting speaker offers two examples from the "
  "mainland: a town that holds a state of the town session each fall so that officials reach "
  "town meeting already briefed, and a campaign that showed residents what municipal employees "
  "and restaurant workers actually earn and what they could afford. Later discussion covers the "
  "price points in the trust&rsquo;s current proposals, tying a septic variance for an extra "
  "bedroom to a year-round restriction, thin planning staff capacity, and mapping infill and "
  "unused ground cover in and around town.",
 "r1gSQdNnDZc":
  "The transit authority meets first. Its administrator reports ridership up seventy-three "
  "percent in the year since fares were dropped, from about 190,000 riders to 328,000 with two "
  "months still left in the fiscal year, and says fare-free funding runs through December. He "
  "reviews capital spending, two grants worth roughly nine million dollars toward electric and "
  "hybrid buses, and a decision to lease five small buses for the summer so the oldest diesel "
  "vehicles can come off the road; the Jetties Beach stop moves beside the tennis courts for "
  "safety. That board approves its budget and a long-term contract for the administrator. The "
  "Select Board then takes up questions left from the previous meeting, among them the Baxter "
  "Road erosion control license: the 2022 memorandum of understanding still stands, the town "
  "meeting article was amended to take no action, and the license terms remain under review "
  "while the beach preservation fund seeks permission to place sand. Members approve noise bylaw "
  "waivers for early morning work on the airport runway centerline and for overnight National "
  "Grid conduit work downtown. The town manager describes the paperwork that follows town "
  "meeting and explains that debt exclusion questions stay on the printed ballot even where the "
  "matching article failed. Applications for committee seats are due May 16.",
 "rSFmzFIWAU0":
  "The town manager reports that the Bureau of Safety and Environmental Enforcement has "
  "postponed its public webinar on the Vineyard Wind turbine blade failure to February 3, after "
  "receiving about 500 written questions. In public comment a coalition member asks for an "
  "in-person conversation with the bureau rather than a pre-submitted question format, and a "
  "retired deputy fire chief raises security at the island&rsquo;s large public events. The "
  "board appoints applicants to the Capital Program Committee, the Real Estate Assessment "
  "Committee and the Zoning Board of Appeals by acclamation. It opens the annual public hearing "
  "on the proposed fiscal year 2026 general fund budget, receives no comment, and endorses the "
  "budget. The remaining two and a quarter hours are the town manager&rsquo;s article-by-article "
  "review of the draft 2025 Annual Town Meeting warrant. It covers a revolving fund renamed from "
  "lifeguard housing to seasonal employee housing, a new fund for year-round employee housing, "
  "amendments to the streets and sidewalks bylaw, and coastal erosion articles tied to the "
  "Sconset Beach Preservation Fund. The longest exchange concerns a proposed limit on "
  "peer-to-peer car rental, which a representative for Turo and local hosts opposes.",
 "rurUOIXQiu8":
  "The finance director opens with the year-end transfers the law allows through July 15, among "
  "them $950,000 at Our Island Home for travel nurses and interim nursing leadership now filled "
  "permanently, $130,000 toward sending new firefighters to the academy, and $110,000 for "
  "election and special town meeting costs. Culture and Tourism reports 69 special event permit "
  "applications for the month, averaging 122 attendees, with alcohol service requested in 51 of "
  "them. The Boston Pops concert at Jetties Beach is granted its usual half-hour extension past "
  "the ten o&rsquo;clock noise limit. Most of the meeting goes to rental car medallions. The "
  "licensing administrator, calling it the most confusing issue of a long career, proposes "
  "opening the eight available medallions to new agencies only, for vehicles rented commercially "
  "rather than personal cars let out occasionally, working down a waiting list kept since 2021. "
  "Members push back: one says the point was to bring peer-to-peer renters within the law, "
  "another warns that issuing a few medallions to them would cause confusion while enforcement "
  "is unsettled, and a third will not support anything until the unused medallions held by "
  "existing agencies are recovered, which would make a far larger pool. Staff note the "
  "medallions have moved by transfer for decades and that the rental platform itself may be the "
  "better target. A new bagel shop on Straight Wharf is licensed for beer and wine and recorded "
  "background music.",
 "ryQKJhuk7iE":
  "The board signs the closing documents for the Ticcoma Green workforce rental project on town "
  "land at 6 Fairgrounds Road and 4 Waitt Drive: an assignment of the development agreement, a "
  "ground lease and its notice, landlord estoppels and consents for the two condominium units "
  "created for the tax credit program, affordable housing restrictions, regulatory agreements "
  "and a right of first offer. More than two hours then go to Our Island Home. Residents fill "
  "the room to describe the care their parents and grandparents received there and what happened "
  "to relatives who had to take beds on the Cape; one speaker who owns no home on the island "
  "offers to pay the $516 the project would add to a tax bill, and another recounts how a fund "
  "set up to repay the town for part of the construction had to be closed after town meeting "
  "voted the project down. Members say plainly that going back to the drawing board has never "
  "made a project cheaper here, and that a second failed vote would amount to a decision to "
  "leave the nursing home business. The board votes unanimously to pursue bids and return the "
  "project to the 2026 warrant and ballot at its full size, with the wing that had been taken "
  "out put back. Engineers report on the Somerset sewer extension, where state revolving fund "
  "money is scarcer and more competitive than at any point in thirty-five years. Town "
  "administration presents its capital recommendations for fiscal 2027. The budget presentation "
  "and the rest of the warrant review are held to the following week because of the hour.",
 "twPXMQgQRc8":
  "The County Commissioners meet first and choose a preferred alignment for the Tom Nevers Road "
  "multi-use path, now funded at town meeting and on the ballot, taking both staff "
  "recommendations unanimously; members say the cheaper alignments would not be safe enough to "
  "justify five million dollars, and two residents urge them to put public safety ahead of "
  "abutters&rsquo; property interests. The district attorney then reports on a new child "
  "emergency response team that brings prosecutors, police, child services and forensic "
  "interviewers together from the start of a case; a million-dollar grant to expand an overdose "
  "data system already used by most Cape departments; and a push on elder fraud, citing one "
  "department with 393 cases over about eighteen months, reports completed in 253 and charges "
  "brought in 15. He is seeking $580,000 for forensic examiners, prosecutors and victim "
  "advocates, and has letters of support from nearly every police chief in the district. The "
  "board grants an overnight noise waiver for line painting on the Sea Street sewer force main "
  "project and hears the first round of committee applicants introduce themselves. The finance "
  "director and a consultant present proposed sewer rate increases, which would raise about 10.5 "
  "million dollars of the sewer budget; a public hearing is set for July 9 and any change would "
  "take effect with the August 1 bill. Monthly reports from the finance and fire departments "
  "close the meeting.",
 "ugUqZjUeMd4":
  "Nantucket Community Television gives its annual report, including a filmmaking class that "
  "drew more than twenty-five people one Sunday morning, taught by two 2021 high school "
  "graduates now working at the station, with a dozen of those who attended planning shorts for "
  "next year&rsquo;s festival. The board approves a preservation restriction on Sherburne "
  "Turnpike. Staff then review the Baxter Road alternative access filing made to the "
  "Conservation Commission in August after years of design work: owners whose land is affected "
  "are being notified, a two-part application keeps the project moving, and members discuss "
  "tying the town&rsquo;s help with the beach preservation fund&rsquo;s license to its securing "
  "the permissions the town needs. A transportation update covers Polpis Road paving and a "
  "cold-in- place recycling trial, Main Street cobblestone repairs, and the Surfside project, "
  "which rebuilds drainage and roadway and adds 2.4 miles of bike path with four raised "
  "intersections, phased so that access from Old South Road stays open. The PFAS update runs "
  "longest: sampling around the landfill meets the drinking water standard upstream but exceeds "
  "it downstream, with some results above a thousand parts per trillion and Long Pond above the "
  "state limit, while a separate study concludes that stockpiled soil is not a significant "
  "ongoing source. The board previews its own 2026 articles on electric bikes, noise and rental "
  "cars, and decides to offer no comment of its own on the two special town meeting articles.",
 "umTtPA7J0_8":
  "The transit authority meets first and approves a new on-demand service to cover the areas "
  "whose bus routes are dropped for the winter, among them Madaket, Polpis, Surfside, the "
  "Jetties and Brant Point; rides are free until January 5 and $3 after that. Its administrator "
  "reports 301,000 fare-free rides through October, more than all of fiscal 2024, and says the "
  "state money behind the program runs through the end of 2026 while the regional authorities "
  "press for a larger share of it. The County Commissioners meet briefly and adjourn. The Select "
  "Board hears public comment asking for the minutes of the chair&rsquo;s meetings with the "
  "African Meeting House investigators and returning to the idea of translating town meeting "
  "material, then grants the Nantucket Education Trust a temporary right of entry to town land "
  "at 48 Sparks Avenue so it can run the surveys and engineering studies needed to plan three "
  "duplexes for school staff housing. Solid waste reports that the final cap on the "
  "landfill&rsquo;s first phase still waits on state authorization and that test pits have "
  "sampled where mining soils and compost were handled. The new waste services agreement is "
  "explained: eight service areas, each with its own scope and fee, so costs can be assigned "
  "service by service. Commercial disposal fees will be reviewed before any hearing, and a "
  "member warns that charging more for certain items once pushed people to dump them on the "
  "moors. The e-bike and scooter article is dropped from the 2026 warrant because state "
  "legislation is pending.",
 "uxSkMzzKaNk":
  "Most of the hour goes to two subjects. In public comment the dispute over the African Meeting "
  "House investigation continues: the chair reads the town&rsquo;s answers to questions raised "
  "the week before, that preparatory work on the investigation began in October, that an "
  "addendum to the request for proposals was emailed in July, and that the work group&rsquo;s "
  "chair will call a meeting. The questioner replies that the revised proposal still has not "
  "arrived and asks for a date, and a second speaker, a civil rights advocate from off-island, "
  "presses the same matter at length and objects to the board&rsquo;s practice of not letting "
  "one speaker hand time to another. Later the board takes an overview of the community claims "
  "fund created by the settlement that followed the Vineyard Wind turbine blade failure. The "
  "firm hired in September to administer it explains how claims will be documented and paid, and "
  "a member asks whether the names of people who receive money would be public under a records "
  "request; town counsel will advise. The board endorses its 2026 meeting schedule, with the "
  "licensing meetings shifted so that applications reach the planning board first and so that "
  "they avoid holidays and school vacation week. The noise bylaw is held over because the "
  "material was not in the packet, and members ask that town counsel attend future historic "
  "district appeals in person.",
 "vBqXh1De_jA":
  "The board takes up how to allocate eight surplus rental vehicle medallions. Staff present two "
  "models drawn from existing town practice: a lottery, as used for town pier slips, or a "
  "waiting list, as used for taxi, limousine and charter tour licenses. Staff recommend the "
  "waiting list, reasoning that a medallion supports an ongoing business rather than a one-time "
  "award, and note the process is needed whatever becomes of the proposed rental car bylaw "
  "amendments. The rest of the meeting is five National Grid utility petitions, all filed under "
  "one plan number, for conduit work in the downtown streets. The board approves four of them: "
  "Broad and Federal streets, Easy Street, North Union Street behind St Mary&rsquo;s, and India "
  "Street through Coleman Court to Center Street. On the first it asks that the conduit run "
  "under the sidewalk rather than the roadway where that is possible, because a sidewalk is "
  "easier to repair. The petition running from Old North Wharf to 20 Straight Wharf is continued "
  "to February 10, after a representative of the property owner raises delivery access to Still "
  "Dock during construction and the transformer&rsquo;s exact location is left unresolved.",
 "vHxrPsgSVYI":
  "The Select Board, the Finance Committee and the Planning Board each call themselves to order "
  "for a joint read-through of the final 2025 Annual Town Meeting warrant with motions. Staff "
  "work down the list article by article, and members stop the reading wherever they want an "
  "explanation. One article will have to be re-advertised in April because the contract "
  "purchaser of a Rosemary Way property prefers a different zoning district from the one the "
  "Planning Board recommended. The Planning Board has voted no action on the open space parcels "
  "article, after a conservation agency raised concerns about several of its parcels too late to "
  "change the printed warrant. Staff explain that most of the map changes continue the master "
  "plan&rsquo;s long-running phase-out of the RC-2 district. A temporary moratorium on tertiary "
  "dwellings is described as a pause while the town assesses how the state&rsquo;s new by-right "
  "accessory dwelling unit allowance interacts with properties that already hold three units. An "
  "attorney attending argues that the warrant&rsquo;s definition of year-round housing should "
  "require twelve months rather than ten. One article, number ten, remains to be reopened the "
  "following week.",
 "zdT_yoFwx-M":
  "Announcements cover the two public works facility open houses, the Master Plan visioning "
  "weekend, and the reopening of the 2 Fairgrounds parking lot on April 15 after a winter "
  "closure, with ferry connector service resuming from downtown. In public comment a resident "
  "asks for clearer definitions of residency in the short-term rental articles, saying the "
  "community is confused about what distinguishes a year-round resident. The board accepts a "
  "gift for the Saltmarsh Senior Center and approves the name Jones Way for a new road between "
  "Ticcoma Way and Young&rsquo;s Way; Jones Road was ruled out as too close to Paul Jones Road "
  "for the emergency dispatch system. It then approves a purchase and sale agreement for 9 "
  "Goldfinch Drive, a three-bedroom house in Naushop, for municipal employee housing. Staff "
  "explain that leasing has given the town short-term access but is not sustainable, and a "
  "member notes that the Affordable Housing Trust could not buy the property for this use. "
  "Members say the town cannot deliver services without housing its employees, and that the "
  "management and maintenance arrangements still need tightening. The sewer director and the "
  "stormwater manager close with the department&rsquo;s monthly report, covering a new strategic "
  "plan and draft stormwater regulations.",
 "-Pj8sfSUebY":
  "A short special meeting, board discussion only with no public comment, on whether to keep a "
  "draft zoning article about private pickleball courts on the 2025 Annual Town Meeting warrant. "
  "As drafted the article inserts the courts into the use chart as prohibited in every zoning "
  "district, and staff explain that the board could soften that to a yes or to a special permit "
  "through the hearing process, but that writing a broad setback requirement into it would "
  "probably fall outside the article&rsquo;s scope. Almost nobody wants a flat ban. Members "
  "argue the problem is the noise rather than the use, the sound being compared to a "
  "truck&rsquo;s backup alarm and to a swimming pool, which can at least be used quietly, and "
  "that setbacks, construction and sound mitigation details, and a special permit in the smaller "
  "districts would do more than a prohibition. They note the complaints arriving at the planning "
  "office and at the Historic District Commission, which can regulate how a court looks but not "
  "how it sounds. A moratorium is raised and judged unworkable. A straw poll comes out in favor "
  "of keeping an article in play for the spring rather than holding the whole subject for a "
  "special town meeting.",
 "-y49H7ynAqk":
  "Nearly two hours go to one special permit: an orthopedic surgeon&rsquo;s plan to move his "
  "practice out of the hospital into a 960-square-foot office on Goldfinch Drive, in a building "
  "put up years ago as the construction headquarters for the development around it. He describes "
  "a low-volume practice, twenty patients a day at most, no procedures on site, appointments "
  "timed by text so that nobody waits. neighbors fill the room. The driveway and parking are "
  "shared with the unit next door under an easement written for two homes; one speaks of "
  "children playing in a front yard beside what would become a steady flow of cars, another has "
  "walked the neighborhood and counted 116 parked cars on streets that are private. The board "
  "asks for a parking plan that works, fourteen spaces having been requested where six are "
  "needed, says it wants to walk the site itself, and urges the applicant and his neighbors to "
  "talk. Earlier a developer asks to keep nine storage containers and a construction office "
  "already the subject of a zoning enforcement action, the containers serving modular houses "
  "that arrive by barge. Second dwellings, garage apartments and division plans are approved, a "
  "performance surety is partly released, two permits are modified, one application is withdrawn "
  "without prejudice and two are continued to September. Late in the evening the master plan "
  "vision statement comes back with a few wording changes and is adopted. The board then sets "
  "its list of articles for the 2026 town meeting without arguing their substance, to be worked "
  "through in special meetings over the next two months, and puts a measure that failed last "
  "spring back on the list to be rewritten. A member notes that splitting one question across "
  "five or six articles splits the vote when two-thirds is needed to carry it.",
 "10Q7TnXcvaU":
  "A Zoom-only special meeting on one question: whether the board will sponsor a short-term "
  "rental article at the special town meeting. The draft would define hosted stay, operator, "
  "owner and short-term rental, add the use to the chart as accessory everywhere but the "
  "commercial industrial district, and cap both the number of nights a property may be let and "
  "the months in which it may happen. Members work through the definitions, asking whether "
  "renting the cottage you are not sleeping in counts as a hosted stay, what the fourteen-day "
  "threshold does to weekly rentals, and why the language on subleases is lifted from state law "
  "to stop a three-month tenant re-letting the house. One member argues this is not really a "
  "planning board article and that renting ought to be made lawful before it is regulated; "
  "counsel answers that it is a legitimate planning concern and that whether this is the right "
  "article is the board&rsquo;s own call. Others warn that the coalition behind it is fragile "
  "and that the last attempt failed on amendments. The lodging tax take is laid out, from just "
  "under eight million dollars in 2021 to about 13.7 million last year. With the warrant closing "
  "at the end of the week, the board votes unanimously to put the article on it and leaves the "
  "question of whether to recommend it to its October meeting.",
 "5SXNJMQMUcA":
  "A full regular meeting of applications. The second dwellings go through as a group, with one "
  "tertiary dwelling allowed to proceed despite the moratorium article pending at town meeting "
  "because a perimeter plan filed in 2022 gives that property a zoning freeze. A batch of "
  "division plans is endorsed once staff correct two copy-and-paste errors in their own report. "
  "The preliminary plan for 25 and 27 Fairgrounds Road proposes nine lots, eight of them "
  "buildable, covering the restaurant property and the vacant parcel beside it; a member takes a "
  "moment over what the restaurant has meant to the island while allowing that buyers have "
  "rights too. A waiver of restriction is endorsed for 46 Essex Road, one application is "
  "withdrawn without prejudice and another continued to April. Storage takes much of the "
  "afternoon: a major commercial development permit for two one-and-a-half-story buildings on "
  "Spearhead Road and Arrowhead Drive to hold cars and boats, where drainage, a curb cut and the "
  "case for a homeowners association are all discussed, and fourteen vehicle storage units "
  "inside the existing Davkim Lane building, now owned by a national operator. A definitive "
  "subdivision is heard for two Trotts Hill Road lots that front a proprietors&rsquo; road, and "
  "the industrial park permit on Lovers Lane is amended to allow a recreational use, a jiu-jitsu "
  "and fitness tenant already in a building that has been a warehouse, a function hall and a "
  "restaurant in its time. A convenience store proposed at 21 Amelia Drive draws staff concern "
  "about shared parking and a recommendation to look at it again in six months.",
 "A1iNflXtoWI":
  "A short special meeting to settle the final motions and comments on every zoning article "
  "before town meeting. Three articles are reopened over wording caught after the fact: the "
  "Surfside Road rezoning, now sent forward as a change to Commercial Neighborhood alone, which "
  "drops the vote needed from two-thirds to a simple majority; the year-round housing "
  "definition; and the 81L amendment, where members want the language to make clear that the "
  "zoning board is to weigh an unusual situation rather than an ordinary request for more ground "
  "cover, and that an owner still gets a market-rate lot but not what the old provision allowed. "
  "A parking article left off an earlier agenda by mistake is heard for the first time and "
  "recommended. Staff read through the motions on the Residential Commercial-2 phase-out "
  "articles and are authorized to attach a short comment to each article arising from the "
  "state&rsquo;s Affordable Homes Act, on the grounds that more people read the comments than "
  "read the report. The longest exchange is over the article the board supported three votes to "
  "two, and how to word a comment recording that the support rested on the sponsor working with "
  "staff on deed restrictions; staff remind the board that the hearings are closed and the "
  "motions can no longer be reopened.",
 "Dd8DWGm--6o":
  "A long regular meeting, most of it covenant lots and subdivisions. Four public hearings are "
  "continued to February at the start, among them Habitat for Humanity&rsquo;s two Waitt Drive "
  "lots. The second dwelling and garage apartment approvals go through as a group, with 44 "
  "Skyline Drive and 24 Essex Road pulled out for discussion of their own. Staff are authorized "
  "to write to the owner of 30 and 34 Sparks Avenue clarifying which of the two buildings the "
  "housing occupancy condition in an earlier special permit attaches to. The Affordable Housing "
  "Trust&rsquo;s 14 Somerset Road comes forward as a two-lot division, the existing house and "
  "garage staying as town employee housing on one lot and the other becoming the covenant lot, "
  "with setback relief sought between them. A second covenant application divides 12 Ipswich "
  "Street, recognizing the two driveways already there and spreading the ground cover across "
  "both lots. At 20 Shell Street the argument is about where a parking space sits in relation to "
  "the corner, settled by shifting it ten feet and capping the height of future planting. The "
  "rear lot subdivision at 62 and 64 Sankaty Road produces the awkward moment of the evening: "
  "approval carries three to two, which is not enough for a special permit, so the board "
  "reconsiders and the motion passes the second time with one member switching their vote. The "
  "meeting ends on the Old South Road commercial lots, where parking, drainage and compliance "
  "with the water resource district are gone through before approval.",
 "EYiCMFEguwI":
  "A special meeting with one item on it: the updated draft vision statement for the master "
  "plan. The consultants have merged the two earlier versions, keeping the short prose paragraph "
  "and setting the detail out as bullets beside it. The board settles on the prose as the vision "
  "and moves the bullets into the goals, where each can be tied to something specific, then "
  "works the wording line by line. Quality schools, healthy activities for young people and "
  "support for elders are added. &ldquo;Build what we needed&rdquo; gives way to leaving the "
  "island better than it was found, with the investment in physical and social infrastructure "
  "that implies. &ldquo;Reducing our dependence on private vehicles&rdquo; becomes "
  "&ldquo;reducing discretionary vehicle trips,&rdquo; so that nobody reads it as a plan to take "
  "their car away. And the word diversify is questioned for implying growth when what people "
  "asked for was a working year-round economy, the scallop fishery being the example. The "
  "statement goes to a public hearing on July 14.",
 "FapI2Z3CIDo":
  "The kick-off session for the town&rsquo;s master plan visioning process. The keynote comes "
  "from a planner and engineer from central Minnesota speaking for the nonprofit Strong Towns, "
  "who compares a town&rsquo;s growth to a child&rsquo;s, each stage trading one set of problems "
  "for the next, and sets a historic photograph of a main street beside the same street today, "
  "parking lots and half-empty buildings, to argue that the post-war American pattern of "
  "development spends more on infrastructure than the land it serves can return. The arithmetic "
  "comes from a study of one Louisiana city, where the median family pays $1,500 a year in city "
  "taxes and $9,200 would be needed to keep every promise made about roads, pipes and drainage. "
  "Examples follow of places that did better, among them a resort town with its own architect "
  "and a pattern book of pre-approved designs, and the advice is to start where people are "
  "visibly struggling and do something small rather than wait on a grand plan; grant-chasing "
  "gets a sceptical word. A panel then takes questions: whether the island should dream big or "
  "sequence small actions, why the recommendations of earlier plans were shelved, a recent "
  "building that could have been dropped into any town, and the warning that spreading pipes and "
  "roads thinly leaves plenty of wealth to tax but arithmetic that still does not work. The "
  "closing exchange turns to the fear of becoming a place staffed by transient workers.",
 "KKNpyIhvw0Q":
  "A housekeeping vote opens the meeting, striking from a 2017 permit the requirement that the "
  "market rate flats at Ticcoma Green be let by a state-approved lottery, which is not how "
  "market rate units are rented. A pair of Bayberry Lane plans is approved together, replacing "
  "an endorsed division plan with a rear lot subdivision, and the board notes that the ground "
  "cover problem it has been circling for years is finally solved for buildings that already "
  "exist. A boat house and garage is allowed as the only structure on a vacant lot opposite its "
  "owner&rsquo;s house. Then most of an hour on 17 North Water Street, where the guest house "
  "permitted in the spring asks for six more rooms, sixteen instead of ten. neighbors come in "
  "numbers: parking lost from their streets, garbage trucks idling, laundry coming and going, "
  "uncertainty about where staff would sleep, the noise of twelve more guests on a summer night, "
  "the Veranda House fire still close, and what one speaker calls the slow chipping away of a "
  "residential neighborhood. The owner talks about his hospitality background and an "
  "unprofitable first season, and staff sets out the conditions the board has attached to such "
  "permits before. Two applications are withdrawn, several continued to January and a surety "
  "released. The last hour is 2026 town meeting work. The inclusionary housing bylaw adopted "
  "last spring is still waiting on the attorney general, with an answer due within days, and the "
  "board wants year-round deed restrictions on rear lot subdivisions written into it plainly, "
  "required above five units and discretionary below. The accessory dwelling unit article is "
  "harder: how large a protected unit should be against the state&rsquo;s 900 square feet or "
  "half the principal dwelling, whether to adopt the regional model wording, and and the trap "
  "that setting the figure below what is commonly built already would hand some lots a third "
  "dwelling nobody could refuse.",
 "NT25aWBuHwY":
  "A special meeting on the warrant articles still outstanding. The board&rsquo;s own solar "
  "energy amendment is explained first: it would keep ground-mounted solar out of the ground "
  "cover calculation while making it a structure, so setbacks still apply, define roof-mounted, "
  "ground-mounted, grid-connected and photovoltaic systems, and list ground-mounted systems as "
  "an accessory use in every district. Staff say the changes come out of a consultant&rsquo;s "
  "review of the bylaw and largely write down existing practice. A technical fix to the "
  "secondary dwelling definition follows, striking language that belongs to ground cover rather "
  "than to secondary dwellings. The four Woodland Drive articles are continued to February 6, "
  "the chair explaining for anyone watching that the postponement is to let the proponent and "
  "the neighbors try to reach agreement. The author of the short-term rental article returns to "
  "argue it, allowing that some people would rather the use were not regulated at all. The map "
  "changes take the rest of the meeting, and spot zoning is the difficulty the board keeps "
  "circling: members warn that rezoning a single property could carry town meeting and still be "
  "struck down by the Attorney General, and suggest the proponent at 25 Monohansett Road bring "
  "neighbors in and return at a special town meeting with deed restrictions attached. The owners "
  "of 32 Appleton Road argue their case is different, two families in two houses for forty years "
  "with nothing new to be built. A member closes on the covenant programme failing to keep pace "
  "with interest rates, building costs and the shortage of land.",
 "O_1CYOCCM3k":
  "A brisk regular meeting. The citizen article to rezone Rachel Drive and South Shore Road is "
  "dropped: staff report that the sponsor, having talked to the neighbors and the other affected "
  "owners, no longer wishes to pursue it, so the board takes no action and records the reason, "
  "one member recused. Secondary dwellings and garage apartments are approved as a block, from "
  "North Swift Rock Road to Honeysuckle Drive, with the Waydale Road application pulled, and "
  "three division plans are endorsed, among them the Sankaty Road lots and 88 Vestal Street. A "
  "preliminary plan for 4 Nancy Ann Lane is filed because subdividing a commercial property "
  "requires one before a definitive plan reaches the board; the surveyor notes the "
  "parcel&rsquo;s thirty thousand square feet and its place in a wellhead protection area. The "
  "Van Gilder Avenue lots on Old South Road are endorsed now that staff comments have been "
  "resolved. Two applications are withdrawn without prejudice and two more continued to March. "
  "The hearing of substance is Habitat for Humanity&rsquo;s three lots on Waitt Drive, which it "
  "took title to from the town to build affordable housing for local families: counsel presents "
  "a secondary lot special permit for each, with one division plan covering all three.",
 "QlCKELfYUHI":
  "A long special meeting on the town meeting warrant. Staff open with Article 82, acceptance of "
  "the state&rsquo;s seasonal community designation, which is not a zoning article though zoning "
  "obligations come with it; the advisory council has issued no guidance yet, and the benefits "
  "named are for attainable and year-round housing, for year-round owners&rsquo; taxes and for "
  "town financing. A run of definitions follows, all required by the new state accessory "
  "dwelling unit regulations: dwelling unit replaced with the state&rsquo;s wording, principal "
  "dwelling, accessory dwelling unit, protected use ADU and year-round housing, each heard and "
  "recommended in turn. Asked from the floor whether an accessory unit can be sold off "
  "separately, staff say the question is still open, is not answered in the state guidance and "
  "cannot be settled through zoning. The companion article is a moratorium on tertiary dwellings "
  "to the end of the year: the state&rsquo;s accessory unit is essentially the island&rsquo;s "
  "tertiary dwelling with fewer strings attached, so the town wants time to decide what to do "
  "with its own provision. The two long discussions are the cottage community article and the "
  "Woodland Drive rezonings, where the proponent shows that 13 and 13A account for 4.8 of the "
  "5.9 acres fronting the street and asks that the land be treated like the rest of it, offering "
  "year-round occupancy restrictions, attainable pricing and a twenty-four-foot height limit; "
  "members weigh that against zoning creep. The open space parcels and Rosemary Way are heard as "
  "well.",
 "SNxyu_zUFdc":
  "A Thursday session added to the calendar for six hearings. The Easy Street restaurant "
  "application, on its third appearance, is settled table by table: ten patio seats come off the "
  "plan, a gate will screen the trash, gas tanks and ice machine from the waiting area, the "
  "broken junction of driveway and sidewalk is to be repaired, and the board asks to see next "
  "spring how it has all worked. A patio at East Chestnut and Oak Street is approved with "
  "customer access only through a host station, no roof and no umbrellas. The long item is an "
  "apartment building special permit at 1 Toombs Court, where the chair steps aside and an "
  "alternate is seated: access would come off Toombs Court rather than Old South Road, "
  "short-term rentals would be barred and a management company required, and the "
  "applicant&rsquo;s counsel observes that a commercial use would be more intense as of right. "
  "neighbors speak against it, one family having bought their land more than seventy years ago "
  "and feeling pushed out by what has grown up around it, and the board is unified in finding "
  "the unit count too high. A member argues that density, affordability and year-round occupancy "
  "belong in a joint strategy with the affordable housing trust and the select board rather than "
  "in one application at a time. The Summer House&rsquo;s voluntarily filed major commercial "
  "development permit is paused for town counsel, the staff report having concluded the board "
  "has no jurisdiction over it. A change of use is approved at 36 Tennessee Avenue, where "
  "wetlands and an incorrectly drawn assessor&rsquo;s map constrain where a garage can go. The "
  "meeting closes with thanks to a departing member.",
 "T9FeueEXqzU":
  "Second dwellings and a garage apartment are approved as submitted and the division plans "
  "endorsed with one held back. Preliminary plans follow: eight lots off a new cul-de-sac on a "
  "Pine Crest Drive parcel, where the board asks for off-street parking and a neighborhood "
  "sidewalk, and two plans filed only to freeze the zoning while their owners work out what the "
  "land allows. Four of five lots are released under a Form J. The golf club at Sankaty Head "
  "seeks a major commercial development permit for a twelve-unit employee dormitory and is asked "
  "whether the new building will ease the intensity of use at the single-family houses it owns "
  "inside the neighborhood; its counsel answers that the club&rsquo;s housing need is nearly "
  "met, its membership finite, and its building program closer to its end than its beginning. "
  "The permit is approved. The Easy Street restaurant application returns for most of an hour, "
  "on the fence or planters that would separate diners from the sidewalk, a revised seating "
  "plan, deliveries and trash in one of the busiest corners on the island, and the absence of a "
  "ramp or lift, which one member cannot get past; it is continued to Thursday. A builder "
  "presents five apartments over commercial space on Old South Road, redrawn after the "
  "board&rsquo;s earlier comments on quality of life, and the sidewalk gaps either side of it "
  "are worked through with the public works department. Two landscape contractors&rsquo; yards "
  "on Little Isle Lane are heard together and voted separately. The meeting ends with a rear lot "
  "special permit on Fairgrounds Road falling one vote short of the four it needed, and the "
  "release of lots that went with it denied.",
 "WbFADUgVLs8":
  "Four hours, with a theme running through most of it. The water department director opens on "
  "the drought: the monitoring well is read by radio, the island sits about seven inches below "
  "the drought mark, and since only half of any rain reaches the water table it needs fourteen "
  "or fifteen inches of rain or snow before March, then sixty days at that level before the "
  "state lifts the restriction. While it stands he cannot turn the pumps up whatever the demand. "
  "Staff then walks through the state&rsquo;s draft seasonal communities regulations before the "
  "board&rsquo;s comment letter goes back: the tiny house definitions conflict with the "
  "town&rsquo;s own and with the building code, which is why none have been built, and the rules "
  "on undersized lots set an income threshold without defining density or even what makes a lot "
  "undersized. Three of the applications are the same maneuver, an approval-required plan with "
  "waivers filed to prove how many lots could be made, with a rear lot subdivision to follow. "
  "The eight-lot plan on Pine Crest Drive returns as five, fully conforming and asking for no "
  "relief; a three-lot plan off Nobska Way would hold the same six dwellings two lots could "
  "carry anyway, the smaller lots being likelier to reach someone who lives here; and a two-lot "
  "plan on Fairgrounds Road takes its frontage from an easement, which starts an argument about "
  "precedent. The board treats the rear lot step as its moment to bargain, for one dwelling and "
  "an accessory unit, or a year-round deed restriction where an applicant will discuss one, "
  "while the land council argues the route is a loophole for waivers the board would otherwise "
  "refuse and reads the bylaw&rsquo;s limit on lot counts into the record. A building on North "
  "Beach Street is approved for demolition and a larger replacement, the only changes from the "
  "earlier permit being its size and that nothing is now being renovated, with planting along "
  "the lot line required in perpetuity.",
 "Z2LaloeQaT4":
  "A special meeting on the board&rsquo;s own zoning articles for the 2025 Annual Town Meeting. "
  "Two items advertised earlier are dropped from the agenda at the start, having been left off "
  "the warrant after all. The board then works down a list of map changes, most of them "
  "continuing the phase-out of the Residential Commercial-2 district: properties on Surfside "
  "Road and Miacomet Avenue, Rosemary Way, Appleton Road, Old South Road, Nobadeer Farm Road, "
  "Washington Street and Airport Road, each moved to a residential or a trade and craft district "
  "according to how it is actually used. Staff report that the owners of the Surfside Road "
  "parcels never answered their letters and that the lots are in residential use, so a change to "
  "Residential-10 is recommended. Three North Water Street properties, two of them run as guest "
  "houses, move from the old historic district into commercial downtown, a change neighbors "
  "opposed when it was last proposed and did not object to this time. Housekeeping follows: "
  "references in the Moorlands Management District section are corrected to name the zoning "
  "board as the permit granting authority, and the spent moratorium on recreational marijuana "
  "establishments is struck. The positive recommendations go forward with the two-thirds vote "
  "noted. Most of the meeting, though, goes to housing: a definition of attainable housing, a "
  "cottage community option by special permit aimed at households at or below 240 percent of the "
  "county median income, and a new inclusionary housing section. Those are continued to February "
  "6, members asking first for work on what the density would mean on the ground and for a "
  "traffic study.",
 "_6as-MJkoCc":
  "The public hearing on the two short-term rental articles for the November special town "
  "meeting, and close to four hours of it. One article, by citizen petition, would write renting "
  "back into the bylaw as an allowed use. The board&rsquo;s own would allow it as an accessory "
  "use, with definitions for hosted stays and operators, a cap on the number of days and limits "
  "in the busiest months. Public comment runs the length of the argument. A laundry owner "
  "describes what happened downstream, to cleaners and to her own business, when rental "
  "inventory left the market. The registry figure of eleven per cent is pulled apart, since a "
  "house let for two weeks never has to register at all. Owners who have rented the same cottage "
  "for decades ask to be grandfathered, and hear that the other side of grandfathering is that "
  "their children never get the same start. Enforcement comes up again and again: who checks "
  "whether a stay ran twenty-nine days or thirty-one, what happens when the town starts asking "
  "who counts as family, and the prospect of neighbors policing neighbors. Town counsel explains "
  "the court decision that put renting itself in question, advises settling it locally rather "
  "than trusting an appeal, and says either article would likely make the present litigation "
  "moot. Against the fear that restricting supply will push rental buying into year-round "
  "neighborhoods sits the revenue arithmetic: about 7.8 million dollars in short-term rental tax "
  "last year, down from 8.6 the year before, and perhaps thirty per cent lower again if limits "
  "pass. The board closes by drafting its warrant comments line by line, concluding that both "
  "articles would resolve the zoning question behind the lawsuits and that only its own adds "
  "further limits, which it recommends by three votes to two.",
 "dpFOqsIzojI":
  "The vision statement for the master plan comes to a public hearing. The consultant repeats "
  "June&rsquo;s presentation for anyone new to it and reads out the priorities that follow from "
  "it: coastal ecosystems and water, housing for a year-round population, safer and more "
  "efficient transport, development that respects the island&rsquo;s character, a broader "
  "economy, schools and recreation for young people, services for older adults. The talk turns "
  "to the economy, and a member pulls it back from forecasting to what has actually changed in "
  "thirty years, the shoulder seasons that now carry the island, restaurants that no longer shut "
  "in September, chefs who live here year round, and how much permitting it takes to start "
  "anything at all. Comment stays open through August 4. Second dwellings and division plans "
  "pass, and a preliminary plan near the airport would make commercial lots of about five "
  "thousand square feet to match what is already around them. Releases, endorsements, an "
  "as-built clarification and a surety refund are worked through together. A four-lot "
  "subdivision on Nancy Ann Lane is approved with the town engineer asked to look again at storm "
  "water for a larger building, an extra tree added at a corner and the land council consulted "
  "on what to screen it with. Nantucket Safe Harbor for Animals has its permit amended for a "
  "bigger building, about 7,100 square feet in place of 5,750, with the uses unchanged. The long "
  "hearing is a Wyers Way division that has been to court and back: the conditions hold it to "
  "two lots, convey the roadway to a homeowners association and set a payment per dwelling "
  "toward pedestrian improvements. A wider worry about how land gets reshaped in plans like it "
  "is set aside for a conversation with staff rather than taken out on this applicant.",
 "fS0SBNjeIKg":
  "The longest meeting of the year, six and a half hours of it. It opens on the master plan "
  "visioning weekend just past: members praise the turnout and the facilitation, then note which "
  "parts of the island were missing from the room and ask anyone with ideas for reaching them to "
  "speak up. Two re-advertised town meeting zoning articles follow. The map amendment at "
  "Woodland Drive takes an hour, the owner offering permanent deed restrictions that would hold "
  "the site to nine buildable lots with one dwelling each, down from fifteen, while counsel for "
  "an abutter argues the split zoning has another remedy and the board should not lend its "
  "support. The Rosemary Way article, part of the phase-out of the RC-2 district, is settled "
  "quickly: one lot to R-5, the other to CTEC at its owner&rsquo;s request, with a positive "
  "recommendation. Second dwellings and division plans pass as blocks, a covenant release and a "
  "Form J swap are taken separately, and escrow is released on a subdivision changing hands. The "
  "hearings run late. A ten-room guest house is proposed at 17 North Water Street, where one "
  "operated for about 130 years until the pandemic. A boarded-up restaurant at 31 Easy Street "
  "would reopen with no change to the building and seventeen more seats inside; the board likes "
  "the direction but wants answers on parking, sight lines and whether the building department "
  "will allow the old use back. Fourteen vehicle storage units at Davkim Lane return with the "
  "town engineer&rsquo;s conditions attached, the industrial park permit on Lovers Lane is "
  "amended for a recreational use, and in another storage case the board weighs asking employee "
  "housing of a project that has no employees. A convenience store at 21 Amelia Drive is argued "
  "over parking and left to prove itself over six months. The Fairgrounds Road subdivision comes "
  "back as a definitive plan of eight buildable lots and a roadway, and the master plan "
  "consultant&rsquo;s contract is amended to cover added public engagement and travel.",
 "gWneTAPrZmE":
  "A special meeting given over to the citizen zoning articles filed for the 2025 Annual Town "
  "Meeting, after staff relay the state housing office&rsquo;s comments on the town&rsquo;s "
  "draft accessory dwelling unit regulations. The board works through a run of map changes: 25 "
  "Monohansett Road and 44 Skyline Drive, both asking to move out of limited use general "
  "districts into Residential-20 with the town overlay; 32 Appleton Road, from Residential-20 to "
  "Residential-10; and several articles covering 13 and 13A Woodland Drive, where the board "
  "settles on recommending no action and staff explain, on counsel&rsquo;s advice, that bundling "
  "an overlay change with a map change forces a two-thirds vote rather than a simple majority. "
  "The Rachel Drive and South Shore Road article is continued to the end of the month at the "
  "proponent&rsquo;s request, the chair stepping out of the room for it. Most of the meeting "
  "goes to three bylaw amendments. The first would strip pre-existing nonconforming status from "
  "lots created under the state&rsquo;s 81L subdivision provision and require a zoning board "
  "special permit instead; members like the way it borrows the ground cover arithmetic used for "
  "covenant lots. The other two are competing attempts to write short-term rentals into the "
  "bylaw: one creates a Nantucket vacation rental category meant to mirror the zoning "
  "board&rsquo;s finding that renting is a long-standing customary use here, the other defines "
  "short-term and accessory short-term rentals and attaches requirements to them. Residents "
  "press on how either would fall on an owner who rents a few weeks a year. Several articles are "
  "continued to January 30.",
 "j79cQL4wzKo":
  "The master plan&rsquo;s inventory and assessment is adopted, a stocktaking of facts rather "
  "than a policy document, already circulated to the town&rsquo;s departments and open to "
  "additions if anything is found missing. A clerical error from 1994 comes back to be fixed: "
  "when the Fintry Lane lots were released from their performance covenant they were released "
  "from the subdivision approval along with it, which terminated the subdivision, and the lot "
  "owners want the board to join them in confirming that so their titles can be put in order. "
  "The convenience store at 21 Amelia Drive reports back at six months. It had drawn no "
  "complaints until the afternoon of the meeting, when one arrives about the delivery truck: "
  "rather than have several purveyors coming to the site, the owner bought a truck and loads it "
  "off site, and it has sometimes been left on the street, which its counsel points out is "
  "public. A sketch plan brings part of Woodland Drive back as elder housing, eight two-bedroom "
  "dwellings inside the septic capacity already approved and within ground cover and setbacks, "
  "the number of dwellings to be set by special permit; the board likes it. The Cliffside Beach "
  "Club asks to raise its membership from 110 to 150 on the strength of the valet and courtesy "
  "car it already runs. The Greydon House would fold the building across the street into its "
  "permit, four guest rooms and employee space inside an existing footprint, work that would "
  "need no permission at all but for the hotel&rsquo;s own. A frontage exception is sought on "
  "Old South Road, twenty feet where fifty is required, under a power the board took over from "
  "the zoning board. A contractor&rsquo;s shop for a caretaking business is heard, and a second "
  "dwelling is argued on the ground that most lots on that street already have one.",
 "m4Itd5hMMwE":
  "A nine-minute remote meeting to cure a procedural defect. The notice of the October 9 hearing "
  "ran in the newspaper on time but was not filed with the town clerk the required fourteen days "
  "ahead, so the hearing was re-advertised and is held a second time. Nobody comes forward to "
  "speak. The public comment from the earlier hearing is adopted into this record, and the board "
  "re-adopts the same motions and the same warrant comments, which are already printed in the "
  "booklet going out to voters. An item on seasonal communities is taken off the agenda and held "
  "for the regular meeting on Monday.",
 "vG_pIkvOl6I":
  "The medical office on Goldfinch Drive comes back and is denied, unanimously. Counsel for the "
  "neighbors who hold the easement over its parking lays out the history: the building&rsquo;s "
  "1996 occupancy permit rested on a parking plan that the house built next door in 2006, and "
  "the cross easements that came with it, left impossible to honor, and no relief from the "
  "parking requirement has been asked for. The homeowners association describes streets that are "
  "private, curb cuts everywhere, and the one two-way block where parking is forbidden and "
  "unenforceable against anyone but an owner. The applicant&rsquo;s counsel answers that the "
  "owner inherited the arrangement rather than creating it. Ticcoma Green&rsquo;s 2017 permit is "
  "then tidied up: it counted on 32 on-street spaces along a road that did not exist at the "
  "time, and the licence going before the county commissioners will issue 32 placards good for "
  "any space on a first-come basis rather than assign numbered spots. The board sends a letter "
  "of support. The storage container cleanups continue, one of them on Greglen Avenue, where a "
  "condition against stacking things on top of the containers draws the observation that such "
  "cases can neither be waved through nor held to the standard of a project built from nothing; "
  "the airport&rsquo;s container yard is allowed to expand, two units tall and every one of them "
  "beige. A subdivision off Nobska Way would show a right of way only to replace it with a rear "
  "lot plan. The eight-lot plan on Pine Crest Drive runs long on registered land, an easement "
  "that cannot supply frontage, and the width and parking of the street it would feed into, and "
  "is continued to October. Escrows are partly released, and a year-round deed restriction is "
  "weighed against the limits of what the state&rsquo;s accessory dwelling law lets a board "
  "require.",
 "ycar_kWDDhI":
  "The first ninety minutes belong to the master plan. The consultants walk through the draft "
  "vision statements and the input behind them: seventy stakeholder interviews, a survey with "
  "more than 1,600 responses, eleven kitchen-table conversations, the April workshops and a "
  "session with an eighth grade class. Members argue over how broad a vision can be and still be "
  "useful, one warning that a statement broad enough to please everyone ends up in a drawer and "
  "asking for pillars underneath it of the kind the select board&rsquo;s strategic plan has, and "
  "over who has still not been heard from. A resident says the two things to measure everything "
  "else against are year-round housing and coastal resiliency, and points out how late the plan "
  "already is. Officers are elected and committee seats filled. Second dwellings and division "
  "plans pass as blocks; the apartment building at 1 Toombs Court is withdrawn without prejudice "
  "and its division plan endorsed instead. Form J releases, performance deposits and an "
  "endorsement are handled together. The board is asked to sign off on a charitable event at the "
  "Cliffside Beach Club, a hundred-guest fundraiser for Nourish Nantucket&rsquo;s food security "
  "work, which the club&rsquo;s permit leaves to the board case by case. A boat storage permit "
  "on Bartlett Farm Road is amended to drop a washdown pad whose runoff counts as industrial "
  "waste, every other condition staying in force. Old South Road draws the evening&rsquo;s "
  "public comment: the traffic, the buses held up in it, and the want of a sidewalk on a street "
  "about to take more cars. A Nancy Ann Lane subdivision inside the wellhead recharge district "
  "is laid out, and the Summer House is continued again.",
 "-c_dFqHMLww":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the garage apartment at 6 Fargo Way, hardscape at 32 Hulbert Avenue, and hardscape "
  "at 8 Macy Road. Fargo Way ran close to twenty minutes. The dormer was simplified and brought "
  "to the center of the elevation, the second floor exit moved out onto the deck rather than "
  "through the side of the dormer, and the header heights made consistent across the dwelling. "
  "Hulbert Avenue returned with options for three arbors and gates, drawn after members asked at "
  "the last hearing for the lattice to come off and the upper portion to be simplified, and "
  "after one called the arbor over elaborate while allowing that the granite border might pass. "
  "Each option was offered for all three locations: the curved beams cut straight, the lattice "
  "filled in with wood to match the lower half of the gate, or the top arched to follow the "
  "curve of the door below. Members disagreed about how many of the gates can be seen from the "
  "road. Macy Road is gates as well, two of them salvaged, one at the driveway off Macy Lane and "
  "one at the rear of the property where it cannot be seen. A new boathouse and garage in "
  "Madaket drew a neighbor who said the twenty-two foot building will loom over a street of "
  "single story houses, and who asked whether anything provides for drainage or spillage from "
  "the boat and fuel stored there. The commission answered that twenty-four feet is permissible "
  "there.",
 "-l-ANxcql9Q":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 7 White Street, an as-built chimney cap at 1 Farmer Street, and "
  "alterations at 100 Low Beach Road. White Street had been approved before. The property then "
  "changed hands, and the new owner redesigned the house almost entirely, at the same size as "
  "the one approved. Members found it approvable on a large and well set back lot, but returned "
  "to how it meets the two story structure already standing there. The two speak different "
  "languages, one with two over two sash and the other six over six, and an earlier cupola and "
  "barnboard siding had been stripped away so that the windows would match. Farmer Street came "
  "to the commission on a neighbor&rsquo;s complaint. The mason, who said he has worked on the "
  "island&rsquo;s fireplaces for thirty years, had replaced a bluestone cap with a powder coated "
  "black guard, arguing that spark and wind guards keep rain and animals out while a five "
  "hundred pound stone cap leaves no way to service the flue. The owner had not paid the "
  "as-built fee and asked the commission to waive it. Low Beach Road is a brick driveway widened "
  "to the foundation of the house, and the advice ran to a three foot planter to soften that "
  "edge, running bond rather than herringbone, a gate lowered a few inches from seven feet, and "
  "the air conditioning units moved inside the fence.",
 "0QfAtbb48MA":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a re-siting at 16 Walsh Street, a second floor addition at 8 Federal Street, and "
  "main house revisions at 85 Vestal Street. Walsh Street ran about twenty-five minutes. The "
  "house has been in one family since 1978, and the owners spoke to that history before the "
  "architect took up the drawings. He had pulled the structure south and rotated it so that one "
  "gabled end stands more than five feet off the setback line, and he extended the porch across "
  "the East Lincoln elevation to cut a tall double gable in half. Members still objected to two "
  "tall gables close to the street, asked for the lower masses to step down, and suggested "
  "marrying the shed roof to the porch roof so that the eave line between the gables disappears. "
  "Rotating the house toward East Lincoln would mean cutting off the kitchen and the upstairs "
  "bedrooms, the architect said. Federal Street is the Artists Association&rsquo;s building, "
  "bought for its location and last used as a real estate office. Ornamentation was removed and "
  "the building was lowered. Members missed the steep roof pitch found elsewhere on the street "
  "and questioned the cupola and the window panes, while the applicant said wall height is "
  "needed to hang work. Vestal Street returned for at least the fifth time, now with the center "
  "mass tallest and the sides stepping down. Two neighbors opposed it, and the engineered "
  "section showing cut and fill was missing.",
 "3Xy_ZdElclM":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: an addition at 47 Quidnet Road, a main house addition at 35 Meadow View Drive, and a "
  "rebuild at 12 Oak Hollow Lane. Each ran about twelve minutes. Quidnet Road brought massing "
  "changes. The mass at the far left of one elevation moved out about three feet and was widened "
  "so that the rear facade sits flush, two dormers now peak out of the main gable, and a bay "
  "pops off the first floor. A member said the new dormers carry extra height above their "
  "windows, which the commission does not usually see. Abutter comments had been filed, but no "
  "abutter was present to speak. Meadow View Drive is the main house, the guest house and the "
  "garage having been held two weeks earlier. The chair stopped the presentation because the "
  "previous drawings were not laid beside the proposal at the same scale with the changes "
  "bubbled. Members then objected to a flush dormer that joins the entry gable to the gable "
  "beside it with no setback, and to two large skylights on one front roof plane. Oak Hollow "
  "Lane is a rebuild of a house already approved for demolition. The footprint changes little, "
  "the trim will weather naturally, and the windows will be platinum gray casements. A member "
  "asked for muntins somewhere, since clear glass on new construction is not ideal.",
 "4fGYPwRlNtM":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: three applications at 15 Pilgrim Road, a new dwelling at 27 Willard Street, and work "
  "at 11 Pine Street. Pilgrim Road took the longest, about half an hour. The architect described "
  "a main house just over 2,500 square feet on the ground, its highest ridge at twenty-eight "
  "feet telescoping down in two steps, with wood windows and doors rather than clad, and a wood "
  "roof. Commissioners objected to the height and to a grade change of up to a foot, and "
  "questioned whether the guest house should present an entrance to Pilgrim Road rather than sit "
  "behind a vegetated screen. The dwelling and guest house were held for revisions and added to "
  "the site view. A garage with a studio above was heard after them. Willard Street is the rear "
  "lot of a parcel split into three, behind a gambrel the same designer built facing Hulbert "
  "Avenue. The new house is a gable design, meant to read as an older separate house rather than "
  "part of a compound. With no photographs of the surrounding houses in front of them, "
  "commissioners held it for minor revisions. At Pine Street several members asked why the "
  "existing garage should be demolished rather than reused or moved. The commission also voted "
  "to appoint Cathy Flynn, the town&rsquo;s land use specialist, as an HDC enforcement officer.",
 "4pUTceoPra0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a garage apartment and a duplex at 10 Marble Way, a cattle grate at 6 Hinckley, and "
  "a shed and driveway revision at 22 Coffin Street. Marble Way ran about fifteen minutes and "
  "drew the neighborhood. A resident said three original lots have been subdivided by right into "
  "six, that four of them already carry eleven dwellings and four commercial spaces, and that "
  "three more dwellings and another commercial space were now sought. He asked the commission to "
  "view the site before reviewing any design, said conditions on distance from the front "
  "property lines had not been met, and described a neighborhood of one and a half story single "
  "family houses on lots of about eight thousand square feet. A member replied that this side of "
  "the street is commercial residential and the other side is not. Hinckley is a grate set in a "
  "driveway to keep deer off the plantings. Members who dislike grates accepted this one once it "
  "was established that the drive is an easement and not a public way, although the chair found "
  "that the locus plan and the site plan disagree on how far the access sits from the street. "
  "Coffin Street was heard late, after the agent&rsquo;s slot had passed. The lot is small and "
  "the house sits at the rear, so the shed was designed small, set into the hill and screened. A "
  "rooftop array at 41 Macy Lane was held for a screening plan.",
 "7xxl1l0Z1sw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: an as-built fence at 15 Allens Lane, fenestration revisions at 47 Hulbert Avenue, "
  "and a fence, gate and hardscape plan at 27 Baxter Road. Allens Lane ran about fifteen minutes "
  "and came in answer to a violation letter. The fence was never approved, and neither was the "
  "one that replaced it. Members accepted that it is there, but raised sight lines at the "
  "intersection and asked that the end approaching the corner be lowered and sloped back up as "
  "the older fence had been. A solid board fence does not meet the guidelines, so any approval "
  "would be written to cover this fence and not its replacements. Hulbert Avenue has been "
  "approved twice already, and the architect returned at the owner&rsquo;s request to the "
  "oceanside elevation, with minor fenestration shuffling on the east. Two skylights were "
  "proposed where the rule allows one to a roof plane. A gable blocks any view of both at once, "
  "but the one on the west was struck, and the motion carried three to two. Baxter Road is a "
  "pool, gates and fencing behind a privet hedge about five feet wide. Only two short sections "
  "of fence read from the road, each about seven feet long, and members were satisfied as long "
  "as the deer fencing and the plank fence stay hidden by the hedge. An apron was proposed at "
  "the garage, not at the road.",
 "868iamx9s0w":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the new dwellings at 8 and 10 Cato Lane, hardscape at 12 Angola Street, and the main "
  "house at 35 Meadow View Drive. Cato Lane ran about thirteen minutes. The architect brought "
  "the pairing the commission had asked for over several hearings, the two houses drawn together "
  "as seen from the street with site plans showing the driveways and the layout. He said the "
  "house at number 8 meets the front, side and rear setbacks, and described red cedar trim, "
  "white cedar shingles and a Quaker gray stain. Angola Street came down to one retaining wall "
  "with about thirty inches exposed, supported by engineer plans and a letter from a neighbor. "
  "Members wanted the wall to read as natural rock rather than the poured concrete the "
  "engineering calls for, and a rounded sawn veneer was offered in answer. The street side fence "
  "divided them. It had been changed from a board fence to a painted white type to match what "
  "was approved next door, and one member said it now reads as part of the federal style "
  "buildings beside it rather than the more rural setting the design suggests. Another asked to "
  "hold it for a view. Meadow View Drive kept its chimney, lost the horizontal railing leading "
  "to the basement bulkhead, changed the dining room windows to a two over two pattern and "
  "steepened the shed dormer.",
 "9ZFYwKmo8uQ":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: St. Mary&rsquo;s rectory at 6 Orange Street, hardscape at 18 to 26 Sparks Avenue, "
  "and 1 North Swift Rock Road. Orange Street took the longest, about half an hour over two "
  "applications. The rectory was built in 1800 for Captain James Easton, and the contractor "
  "described work already done to preserve it: every existing window reconditioned with new "
  "pulleys and old-fashioned metal weatherstripping, and an Andersen window replaced with an "
  "arched one to match the rest. At issue was a second-floor sun room at the rear, never "
  "properly insulated and not original to the house. The commission settled on sliding sash "
  "rather than awning or casement. The hardscape covers the north side of the lot, the open lawn "
  "below the rectory as Orange Street rises out of town, where a masonry wall is heaving. That "
  "was held for revisions because a walk appeared to run under a tree. Sparks Avenue is a "
  "commercial hardscape: brick to match the existing sidewalks, granite pavers rather than "
  "cobblestones, the recessed granite curb extended flush, and a crosswalk the applicant will "
  "petition the town to build across to the Cumberland Farms opening. An atypical fence becomes "
  "a five-in-one. It was held a week so the commission can see the wording, with members invited "
  "to name plant preferences. North Swift Rock Road came back with revisions to a dwelling "
  "approved seven or eight months ago.",
 "AsmokYdoXJ0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the rehabilitation of the brick building at 5 Candle Street, a painted wall sign at "
  "57 Main Street, and an as-built at 13A Willard Street. Candle Street took the longest, about "
  "half an hour. It is the former Nantucket Electric building, and the commission split the "
  "question in two, approving the scope of work and leaving the materials to come back. The vote "
  "was unanimous, on conditions: the glass block on the east and west elevations to be restored "
  "rather than removed, the Secretary of the Interior&rsquo;s standards and industry practice "
  "for period brick buildings to be followed, and the brick selections returned to the "
  "commission for approval. One member had pointed out that the panel of glass block proposed "
  "for removal on the east elevation looks original to the building. Main Street is a painted "
  "wall sign, a ghost sign most of which has worn away over the years. The Sign Advisory "
  "Committee recommended holding it: only three of its five members were present that morning, "
  "and the application carries images but no exact paint colors and no finished design. Willard "
  "Street is work already done, a skirt around the house of the kind on 13B, 13C and the house "
  "opposite, and a condenser pad that was not on the approved elevations. The condensers cannot "
  "be seen from the street.",
 "BT5BikyGS-M":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a re-used structure allowance, amendments to Chapter 124 of the town code, and the "
  "cottage colony article. This was a work session on Annual Town Meeting warrant articles, not "
  "a hearing, and no applications were taken up. The re-used structure allowance ran longest, "
  "about thirty-five minutes. Applicants secure a move off and then find the receiving lot "
  "cannot absorb the ground cover, so the sponsor floated tiered relief: a letter approval for a "
  "small overage, a planning board special permit for a large one, with language written to keep "
  "the system from being gamed. Members asked where zoning ends and the commission&rsquo;s "
  "review of massing and character begins. The Chapter 124 article would move the demolition "
  "delay out from under a heading about signs, satellite dishes and roof lines, where the "
  "sponsor said nobody would think to look for it, and rewrite the minimum maintenance "
  "standards, which he called too vague to follow. It would go to the Select Board rather than "
  "through zoning. Planning staff explained the cottage colony article: small owner-occupied "
  "clusters for year-round buyers at or below 240 percent of area median income, by planning "
  "board special permit, with no added ground cover and a 24-foot height limit.",
 "Cdjp1zb7n_4":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: retaining walls at 12 Angola Street and 8 North Mill Street, the reconsidered "
  "demolition of 10 New Whale Street, and a report from the preservation planner. The two walls "
  "took nearly fifty minutes between them. The properties adjoin, and both had their walls "
  "staked for the commission to look at. Photographs showed the top of the second tier falling "
  "below the sightline of the house. Angola Street was held for revisions, the commission asking "
  "to see one wall built as a sample first. Members observed that three properties on that slope "
  "are tied together, and that an outside party may be needed to find one solution for all of "
  "them. New Whale Street came back on a motion to reconsider the tie vote, after a letter from "
  "a preservation architectural conservator said restoration remains possible and has been done "
  "elsewhere, taking time and money rather than being futile. The fire chief&rsquo;s view is "
  "that the building must come down. The owner&rsquo;s counsel said they cannot commit to a "
  "replica on a timetable, because they may not own the property by the time it is developed. "
  "The chair accepted that the commission cannot require a particular building in advance, but "
  "said future development could be conditioned to pay homage to the character of what stands "
  "there. The preservation planner also reported on Certified Local Government business and on "
  "education webinars.",
 "GIZIdVejy1k":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the new dwelling at 8 Cato Lane, four new structures at 7 Maxey Pond Road, and "
  "hardscape at 32 Hulbert Avenue. Cato Lane ran longest, better than twenty minutes. The "
  "application had been withdrawn and resubmitted because the members who heard it last were not "
  "all present, and the commission found itself working without the streetscape drawing that "
  "showed the house beside its approved neighbor at number 10. Members said the design itself is "
  "fine, but that two near identical houses pressed together will read as an anomaly on a rural "
  "lane. They settled on an approval conditioned on number 10 standing at least five feet "
  "further back, with different trim and window colors. Maxey Pond Road is a pool house, a "
  "garage, an open cabana and a shed at the rear of the property, their trim matching the main "
  "house. A bridge is proposed between the second floor decks of the pool house and the main "
  "house because the living space is upstairs. Members found the pool house too vertical to "
  "screen, and the chair noted that the site plan clears vegetation for parking to the edge of a "
  "right of way where the planting does not belong to the owner. Hulbert Avenue returned from a "
  "site visit with photographs of arbors, granite curbing and gates from the surrounding "
  "streets, offered as precedent for the as-built gate and a proposed Belgian block border.",
 "GORcAO6XAzI":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the nine applications at 1 Toombs Court, an as-built retaining wall at 42 Chuck "
  "Hollow Road, and the guest house at 3 Pops Lane. Toombs Court took the longest, about "
  "twenty-five minutes. Each of the three lots carries a duplex, a mixed-use building and "
  "hardscape, and the two buildings on a lot stand at the same height. Members argued over "
  "whether a gable or an eave should face the street, a gable forward being the more aggressive "
  "approach to the streetscape and an eave forward softening it, and over whether that amounts "
  "to asking for a redesign. The chair put a hypothetical about a subdivision whose lots come in "
  "one at a time under separate owners, and whether the commission can object to the whole of "
  "it. Chuck Hollow Road is a wall already built, presented by the owner himself. Pops Lane "
  "returned once more on the guest house, with the owner and his attorney on Zoom. The architect "
  "said the architecture has never been in question, only where the building sits, and brought a "
  "page of site plan options. The commission also looked at 14 Silver Street, a house of about "
  "1834, where a window survey taken with the addition&rsquo;s approval showed that many windows "
  "in the main structure had already been replaced with vinyl clad while the originals on the "
  "second floor are wood.",
 "HypNhF-HON0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the town&rsquo;s new Our Island Home at 43 Sherburne Commons, fenestration changes "
  "at 8 Jefferson Avenue, and alterations at 36B Sesachacha Road. Our Island Home took most of "
  "the first hour. An architect for the town walked the commission through three applications, a "
  "nursing home building, staff housing and hardscape, on the east side of the Sherburne Commons "
  "campus. The project moves the home off its East Creek Road site. Commissioners said one "
  "building reads as a dormitory and asked for the long dark roof to be broken up, the ganged "
  "windows separated, and details carried over from the main building. Another is single story "
  "yet thirty-two and a half feet tall. All three were held for revisions. The Jefferson Avenue "
  "house, built in 1983, came in for fenestration changes, shingling on a second-floor porch and "
  "a front door that would read from the road. Much of that hearing turned on where a railing "
  "should stop. At Sesachacha Road a door flanked by windows becomes a sun room, with several "
  "roof changes; it was approved on an exhibit that set the window spacing and corner boards. A "
  "pool at 64 and 66 Baxter Road was approved after the commission asked whether the paper road "
  "beside it will be needed for the Baxter Road relocation.",
 "J43peZ0XjPI":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a violation at 8 North Mill Street, four applications at 3 Pops Lane, and a new "
  "dwelling at 36 Tennessee Avenue. North Mill Street took about an hour, the longest of the "
  "night. The chair opened by reading the commission&rsquo;s best practices on the conduct of "
  "application hearings. He then noted that the project carries a cease and desist order from "
  "the commission and a stop work order from the Building Commissioner, so nothing is being "
  "built there. He set out the options: approve subject to an as-built fee, a daily fine, or "
  "modifications, or else deny. Rather than choose, the commission agreed to walk Angola Street, "
  "North Mill Street and the blocks around them to see what comparable elements exist, and to "
  "take the case up again at a later old business meeting. No new drawings had been submitted "
  "that day. Pops Lane returned with its main house, guest house, garage and pool, and was held "
  "again for revisions. Tennessee Avenue came back with photographs taken from the creek in "
  "summer, which the commission had asked for. Most of the comment was on the rear elevation "
  "facing the water. It was held for revisions and for options.",
 "Jldf_sGzna4":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the demolition of the garage at 25 Hussey Street, an addition at 11 Pleasant Street, "
  "and hardscape at 89 Easton Street. The meeting opened with the chair reporting what Town "
  "Counsel advised about the tie vote taken two weeks earlier: a motion to deny must carry a "
  "stated reason, and a motion to approve that fails simply leaves nothing decided. Hussey "
  "Street followed, the longest item of the afternoon. The applicant&rsquo;s agent, himself a "
  "commissioner, brought the additional information the commission had asked for: photographs of "
  "the interior framing, and a statement of how much of the structure would be reconstructed and "
  "how much is actually coming down. Pleasant Street is an addition on Starbuck&rsquo;s Court, a "
  "private way. The designer removed a balcony that had sat over the addition, dropped a "
  "replacement bay window in favor of three windows under a shed roof after studying four "
  "options, and changed the doors. Easton Street, known as Captain&rsquo;s Corner and next to "
  "the Nantucket Hotel, came back with the wall along North Avenue cut from twenty-four inches "
  "to fifteen and run on another five or six feet with planting to screen the drive-down, vines "
  "added on its uphill side to soften the brick, and the outdoor shower and pergola dropped "
  "altogether.",
 "KVKFNeKcQy0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: three new lots at 1 Tombs Court, four applications at 3 Pops Lane, and the sign "
  "recommendations that opened the meeting. Tombs Court took the longest, about three quarters "
  "of an hour. The property is where Espresso To Go sits now. It has been sold and divided into "
  "three lots by an approval-not-required subdivision, fronting Tombs Court across from the "
  "dentist&rsquo;s office and the new Thai House development, with another subdivision going in "
  "off Young&rsquo;s Way. The commission held the buildings for revisions and asked for poles "
  "set along the street for the two buildings nearest it on each lot, so their proximity can be "
  "judged, and for a site plan showing the footprints of the existing buildings in context. Pops "
  "Lane ran about forty minutes over four applications. The main house was approved through "
  "staff on an exhibit that keeps the sill in the screened porch continuous and gives the "
  "chimney a more traditional cap. The three-car garage has become a single bay, 456 square feet "
  "smaller and four feet lower at the ridge, with out-swinging doors. A view was arranged with "
  "ridge poles staked, which may make further revisions unnecessary. The pool was questioned for "
  "listing no materials beyond bluestone. The meeting opened by approving the signs on the Sign "
  "Advisory Committee&rsquo;s recommendations.",
 "M4qgbxJ0Mls":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 4 East Lincoln Avenue, three applications at 2 L Street, and an "
  "enclosed deck at 41 Orange Street. East Lincoln took the longest, about twenty-two minutes. "
  "Its architect said the house would stand where an earlier one stood before another firm moved "
  "it back and made it a garage studio. He argued the scale matches 6, 10, 12 and 14 East "
  "Lincoln, and that the neighbors&rsquo; stone walls and flood plain raises already set the "
  "height of the street. Commissioners asked for the front elevation to be simplified. A "
  "suggestion to push the house back another six feet drew little support. It was held for "
  "revisions. At 2 L Street the commission allowed a 1966 structure to be moved off but not "
  "demolished, on the preservation planner&rsquo;s recommendation. The replacement dwelling was "
  "held for revisions, the chair noting that an applicant has three meetings to make material "
  "changes before a denial. A garage there was taken up after it. The Orange Street application "
  "would close in a second-floor deck as a sleeping porch. Commissioners wanted it brought in, "
  "with wider divisions between the banks of windows, and held it for revisions.",
 "OIjvZ3Uj7mY":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new educational research center at 220 Milestone Road, the former Harbor House at "
  "19 North Water Street, and an outdoor shower at 5 Holders Court. Milestone Road took the "
  "longest. The Nantucket Conservation Foundation&rsquo;s executive director set out the goals "
  "for the project and the architect leading the design team presented the building. "
  "Commissioners discussed the gray standing-seam roof and the solar panels proposed as a field "
  "across the south elevation, comparing them with the array at the ice rink, which sits close "
  "to the street and reads harshly. The chair said a ground-mounted array is worth considering, "
  "and warned that roof color will matter to some members when the solar application returns. "
  "North Water Street is the North Water side of what was the Harbor House and is now part of "
  "the White Elephant Resorts. The architects plan to rehabilitate it from the inside out, "
  "taking the roof off and rebuilding it while salvaging, reframing and sistering what is "
  "underneath. It was approved on an exhibit, with a one-foot planting bed along the driveway "
  "foundation and a freestanding lattice panel beside the generator if more than four square "
  "feet of it shows. Holders Court is an outdoor shower built by a local contractor and "
  "presented by the owner&rsquo;s father, with questions about where it sits and the wall it "
  "stands against.",
 "Q8aJM392-0w":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: four applications at 3 Pops Lane, a minimum maintenance hearing on 10 New Whale "
  "Street, and an as-built review of 21 Pleasant Street. Pops Lane took the longest, about "
  "thirty-six minutes. Its architect returned with a main house, guest house, garage and pool, "
  "revised since October. The pool had been moved off the water side to a plunge pool between "
  "the buildings, and Conservation Commission and septic approvals were in hand. Commissioners "
  "pressed on raised grade, siding and trim, and asked for more separation between the "
  "structures. It was held for revisions, the chair repeating that three meetings of material "
  "changes is the limit before a denial. The New Whale Street hearing came from a citizen "
  "complaint under the minimum maintenance bylaw. Counsel and an engineer for National Grid "
  "called the 1927 building unsafe and said the fire department recommends demolition. A "
  "demolition application had been filed the day before. The chair said the bylaw sets no "
  "well-defined terms, referred questions about the commission&rsquo;s authority to Town "
  "Counsel, and asked members to submit further questions through staff. Pleasant Street is a "
  "1920s foursquare with three applications left unfinished and violation fees already paid. Its "
  "main house and hardscape were held for revisions.",
 "QiUISaS_7K8":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: parking revisions at 126 Main Street, the demolition of the dwelling at 12 Atlantic "
  "Avenue, and an as-built deer fence at 111 Surfside Road. Main Street ran about twenty-six "
  "minutes. The agent moved an approved parking space about eighteen inches to stay clear of a "
  "tree, extended the approved rock wall further around it, and proposed a second space surfaced "
  "with a membrane that sits under the grass so that nothing shows. Members said the plans and "
  "the narrative did not agree, that a car parked on a lawn would set a poor example, and that "
  "reinforced lawn grids turn brown. The space is visible from the Main Street sidewalk, and the "
  "planning board had required it. The chair opened the hearing by asking members for short "
  "declarative statements of what an approval requires, so that applications are not heard three "
  "and four times. Atlantic Avenue came as commissioner discussion and a vote on an unsafe or "
  "dangerous condition. The front wall and the ceiling had imploded, and because the structure "
  "stood at the sidewalk the building commissioner gave one week to take it down, which is one "
  "of the few instances in which demolition may precede a certificate of appropriateness. "
  "Surfside Road is a fence already built, presented by the owner, routed between vegetation "
  "where the yard backs onto the road and the bike path.",
 "Qvs7pHsUxoU":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: four applications at 8 Sandwich Road, a move and alterations at 12 Baltimore Street, "
  "and a garage at 25 Hussey Street. Sandwich Road took the longest, close to half an hour "
  "across a dwelling, a garage apartment, a zoning shed and a pool. The architect said the road "
  "itself reads more like a driveway, and that the lot has a second access on Chatham. "
  "Commissioners asked for the same reductions on the garage apartment they had asked for on the "
  "house, dropping the ridge and the eave, and were satisfied that its intersecting shed dormers "
  "sit on an inside corner. The shed was held for revisions. The Baltimore Street house dates "
  "from 1880. The plan is to lift it, move it clear of the setbacks, set it on a full foundation "
  "and renovate inside while leaving the exterior largely intact. It was held for revisions. At "
  "25 Hussey Street the applicant asked to demolish a garage that stands almost on the property "
  "line and has been taking on water, to make room for a guest house with a garage component "
  "behind a house of about 1730. He offered deconstruction and rebuilding as an alternative, "
  "citing a recent approval the preservation planner found in which a structure was taken down "
  "in panels and set on a new foundation. The commission does not meet on February 25.",
 "SBhY2q3GjTk":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the demolition and replacement cottage at 10R Academy Lane, an as-built retaining "
  "wall at 42 Chuck Hollow Road, and an as-built mini-split at 31 Easy Street. Academy Lane took "
  "the longest, a little over twenty minutes across the two applications. A member of the "
  "Historic Structures Advisory Group reported for that body. The documentation disagrees about "
  "the building&rsquo;s age, one account treating it as rather old and the applicant&rsquo;s as "
  "perhaps 1950, and the replacement is so close to the existing cottage in size and proportion "
  "that the group asked whether the cottage could be kept and added to instead. The "
  "owner&rsquo;s attorney answered that the proposal rests on a medical need. The vote drew "
  "three in favor and two abstentions, one of them recorded late, and a member of the public "
  "raised a point of order asking whether two members had been talked out of voting no. The "
  "chair said he had heard no vote against. Chuck Hollow Road is a wall and gate already built "
  "behind the house and not visible from the street. Staff noted that the as-built fee had not "
  "been collected, and the commission discussed adding it. Easy Street is a mini-split a tenant "
  "installed, which reached the commission on a citizen complaint. The co-trustee offered to "
  "paint the covering over the piping, and the fence screening one condenser, white to match a "
  "fence already approved.",
 "Z_heI4IuD1I":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 8 North Mill Street, three applications at 11 Osprey Way, and alterations at 14 "
  "Silver Street. North Mill Street took almost eighty minutes, by far the longest item. The "
  "owner, Robert Sarkisian, opened by apologizing. He had misunderstood the status of his "
  "approvals and believed a change was already allowed. He said the house is his year-round home "
  "rather than a speculative build, and that it was moved five feet forward of the approved plan "
  "without altering the first floor height or its angle to Angola Street. The commission asked "
  "for more material before it decides, including sections and streetscapes, and a full-scale "
  "mockup of the addition. The motion also tracks the hardscape application with the house, in "
  "case modifications follow. Osprey Way returned after more than a year with a main dwelling, a "
  "second dwelling and a pool. Its architect recounted a pond cottage renovation and a second "
  "cottage moved onto a foundation from an adjacent property. Discussion centered on the view "
  "from across the pond, where a flat roof makes the two buildings read as disconnected. The "
  "front door, two hundred feet from Osprey Way, is not visible from the road at all. Silver "
  "Street is a historic house in poor repair that the applicant would rather demolish. It was "
  "held for revisions.",
 "_55oCTbJhlg":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the duplex at 63 Hinsdale Road, a question period with the preservation planner, and "
  "two applications at 2 L Street. Hinsdale Road took the longest, about forty minutes. Its "
  "designer listed the revisions made since the last hearing: the two ridge heights separated by "
  "eight or nine inches, smaller windows, a front porch added to the right side, that side "
  "reduced and pushed three feet back, and the dormers removed on the Hinsdale Street elevation. "
  "Commissioners returned to the continuous eave running the length of the building and to how "
  "the roof planes and pitches on the small gables meet the central element. One warned the "
  "house would read as an oversized suburban home if the height were cut. It was held for "
  "revisions. The preservation planner took questions early in the meeting. She described a Land "
  "Bank project and the town&rsquo;s coastal resiliency plan, including a state-funded "
  "feasibility study of a flood barrier at Easy Street with three alternatives and public "
  "sessions underway. The chair also explained an administrative change: her historic reference "
  "materials now reach commissioners before a meeting, since she no longer sits through all four "
  "and a half hours. The 2 L Street applications are an infill dwelling in a FEMA flood zone and "
  "a garage. Both were held for revisions, over height and over the garage doors.",
 "bTwA3hkWJa8":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the four applications at 85 Vestal Street, the addition and move at 6 Lily Street, "
  "and a four hundred square foot addition at 4 Westmore Lane. Vestal Street took better than "
  "half an hour. The architect had lowered the first floor by about a foot, switched the massing "
  "so that the house reads lower from the street, and reworked the grading, the pool patio and "
  "the retaining walls. The landscape designer described terracing from the road down the back "
  "slope, with the tallest wall at six feet and evergreen and deciduous planting cascading down "
  "it. A commissioner who had recused spoke as a neighbor. Members accepted the architecture of "
  "the main house and returned to the amount of program on the site, then held the garage and "
  "studio as a two story building dressed as a story and a half, with oversized flush dormers "
  "and a basement that shows toward Dukes Road. Lily Street is an Underhill cottage. Its two "
  "parcels have now been merged, which the agent said removes the density that separate "
  "development would bring. Surveys of every house on the street were produced, the street was "
  "scanned in three dimensions, and the proposal was superimposed to argue that it sits within "
  "inches of the average setback. Westmore Lane came back compressed in height and simplified, "
  "with a trellis and a larger window on the north, although a neighbor said the building is "
  "growing taller beside a well traveled corner.",
 "cyUHf_gFXX4":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: four applications at 3 Pops Lane, three at 85 Orange Street, and outbuildings at 2 "
  "Hamblin Road. Pops Lane took the longest, more than forty minutes, and the conversation again "
  "settled on the rear elevation facing the water. The architect said the heights had come down "
  "across the board. The chair had asked for two feet, which would have left no room for a "
  "full-height door, so the plates dropped as far as they could, one wing from seventeen feet "
  "eight inches to sixteen feet ten. The main house was held for revisions, and the guest house, "
  "garage and pool held with it. Orange Street came back with sills added so the windows no "
  "longer read as picture frames, the window manufacturer named in the schedule, and the stoop "
  "detailed to match a historic photograph in the plans. Its front and rear hardscape were voted "
  "on together at the end. Hamblin Road is two outbuildings standing close together. They were "
  "reviewed as one and approved as submitted, one commissioner noting that their doors all face "
  "into the site rather than the road. The commission also took 36 Tennessee Avenue for a third "
  "time, with the two secondary masses dropped a foot to separate them from the primary one.",
 "espbghXX8Ec":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: new buildings on Marble Way, a new dwelling at 36 Tennessee Avenue, and the main "
  "house and hardscape at 21 Pleasant Street. Marble Way took the longest, about thirty-six "
  "minutes. The applications cover a dwelling on one lot and garages on two others, served by "
  "three shared driveways. The agent said a larger proposal for a duplex is being withdrawn "
  "after neighbors objected to the parking, and that landscaping equipment is being moved off so "
  "the site can be employee housing for the landscape company that owns it. Commissioners "
  "counted nine gables facing the street and suggested rotating the secondary buildings ninety "
  "degrees so the primary ones face front. The applications were held for architectural "
  "revisions and for site plan information. Tennessee Avenue replaces a c.1938 Colonial Revival "
  "the commission allowed to be moved across the street. The architect described gray trim, "
  "two-over-two sash, and pent roofs instead of posts to keep work back from the wetlands. It "
  "was held for revisions after a discussion of the flood zone on the survey. Pleasant Street "
  "has been before the commission for more than two years. The applicant agreed to score the "
  "foundation so it reads as block and to cap the picket fence. The height of the hedge on "
  "Candle House Lane was left open.",
 "geZ4KqBZB-w":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new dwelling at 8 Cannonbury Lane, a barn at 4 Dukes Road, and a relocation at 12 "
  "Madequecham Valley. Cannonbury Lane took the longest, more than half an hour. It is a new "
  "house on an empty site. Commissioners thought one column capital too slender to look as "
  "though it carries the beam, asked where tapered posts would be visible, and spent some time "
  "on chimney pots, wanting more character than plain three-foot terracotta. Dukes Road is a "
  "barn beside a house of 1920 that has stood on cribbing for months while a demolition was "
  "sought. The proposal keeps the box shape of the existing structure and extends its roof "
  "upward into a new addition, with new openings cut for doors. One commissioner noted how "
  "little of the old building survives once the plan opens into the addition. A member of the "
  "commission presented it as the applicant&rsquo;s agent, a capacity the agenda records. "
  "Madequecham Valley moves an existing house back from an eroding shoreline, as close to the "
  "road as the lot allows, with two additions. A variance will be sought, as others in that area "
  "have been granted because of the erosion and an adjacent paper road. The commission also "
  "heard 20 Cliff Road, where shutters would be added to two elevations of a house of 1747, "
  "their earlier presence documented from several sources.",
 "gxGYQD2HfxQ":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the five applications at 115 Cliff Road, an as-built retaining wall at 42 Chuck "
  "Hollow, and rooftop solar at 41 Macy&rsquo;s Lane. Cliff Road ran longest, about twenty "
  "minutes across the dwelling, the garage and guest house, the pool house, the pool and a "
  "re-sited shed. The agent had simplified the trim throughout, removed the pilasters and "
  "replaced them with shingle, reduced the frieze boards, added double hung windows to the "
  "kitchen bay and taken the double columns off the side portico. Members called the central "
  "gambrel well proportioned but questioned the flanking gambrels, which face in several "
  "directions at once, and asked that a dormer stop where it meets the main roof rather than cut "
  "into the plane. Two said the design is handsome but out of place on the last property leaving "
  "town, and the garage doors drew objections for being too tall for their width. Chuck Hollow "
  "is a wall already built, presented by the owner, who offered to lower the stone columns about "
  "a foot and to let rhododendron and viburnum cover the wall rather than screen it with "
  "lattice. Macy&rsquo;s Lane sits close to the street, and the panels face the front of the "
  "main structure, which the guidelines discourage. The agent said the lot leaves nowhere to "
  "plant, and the chair asked whether island organizations might lend at low interest for ground "
  "arrays instead.",
 "jHsNIxQlPlA":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: garage apartments at 2A and 2B Forrest Avenue, alterations at 4 Spruce Street, and a "
  "new dwelling at 68 Shawkemo Road. The Forrest Avenue pair took the longest, about eighteen "
  "minutes. Each is a three-bay garage with a two-bedroom apartment above, a cedar roof and "
  "cedar garage doors, and the designer described them as being as simple as possible. The "
  "commissioner who kept them off consent asked to see them in the context of every other "
  "structure planned for the lot. One on its own might be approvable as it stands, she said, but "
  "a row of identical buildings would need variation. The second was taken as a repeat of the "
  "first. Spruce Street was approved on an exhibit that changed fifteen-light French doors to "
  "six-light. Shawkemo Road is a new house on a site of just over five acres, where an existing "
  "dwelling remains and a garage had already been approved on consent. Earlier the commission "
  "heard four applications at 4 Van Fleet, to demolish a main house and a guest house and build "
  "their replacements. The new house would sit more than four hundred feet back on a densely "
  "vegetated lot that falls about seven feet, with no change of grade asked for beyond what "
  "steps require.",
 "n-dMwINLBsY":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the minimum maintenance hearing on 10 New Whale Street, a pergola addition at 23 "
  "Ocean Avenue, and a new dwelling at 7A Clifford Street. New Whale Street took more than an "
  "hour. The chair ran it to a clock, three minutes a speaker, with the owner&rsquo;s attorney, "
  "an engineer and the fire department presenting in turn. The attorney argued that the brick is "
  "splitting where an earlier window was filled in, and that the waterfront has been rebuilt "
  "again and again: the Steamship Authority buildings, the shipyard where Great Harbor now "
  "stands and the old fuel tanks were all approved for removal, and most of downtown was rebuilt "
  "after the fire of 1846. The commission voted to require a further report before deciding. The "
  "chair observed that the building has stood behind fences for the better part of four decades "
  "and that its owner is a large company. Members said that if it has to come down brick by "
  "brick, it might be rebuilt elsewhere on the lot at a later date. Ocean Avenue was approved "
  "four to one, after a reminder to speak only to matters within the commission&rsquo;s purview. "
  "Clifford Street was approved on an exhibit cutting the internal turnaround to the minimum "
  "needed, to leave room for planting between the driveway and the property line.",
 "pAZccLQrGqs":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the garage apartments at 2A and 2B Forrest Avenue, alterations at 4 Easy Street, and "
  "14 Silver Street. Forrest Avenue took the longest, about half an hour across the two lots. "
  "The chair cut the clock to two minutes for the applicant&rsquo;s agent and one minute each "
  "for the first two abutters, since four elevations to a building leaves little new to add "
  "after that. Both were held for revisions. The motion leaves the building closest to the "
  "street as already approved and asks for the one behind it to come back as a smaller "
  "structure, closer to what was first approved than to a two-bay garage apartment. The "
  "commission then repeated the vote for the second lot, with two different members sitting. "
  "Easy Street began as a train barn and became retail space. Its owner asked to clean it up and "
  "make changes that help pedestrians pass while keeping the historic character. The red clouds "
  "marking her revisions had failed to display on some copies of the drawings. It was held for "
  "revisions. Silver Street, seen several times already, passed four to one on an exhibit. The "
  "fence at 62 Wanoma Way came back as well, with the as-built fee paid and an exhibit drawn up "
  "for moving it.",
 "pZ9RNzm9i_E":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: revised parking at 126 Main Street, modifications at 10 and 8 Cato Lane, and the "
  "addition at 8 Federal Street. Main Street ran about seventeen minutes and returned from the "
  "previous meeting with the approved site plan laid over the house plan, the drive moved "
  "eighteen inches, and the reinforced membrane marked. A representative of the neighbors read a "
  "long objection. Shared driveways are common on Main Street, he said, a car parked on a lawn "
  "is not, the lot is overburdened, and the proposal would amend two earlier decisions reached "
  "after extensive deliberation. Every member who spoke remained against parking on the lawn, on "
  "the grounds that a space which can be used will be. One would accept it only with cobblestone "
  "sprinkled through the grass. Cato Lane came as modifications to prior approvals, the two "
  "houses staggered because the property line runs at an angle. Members had no objection to the "
  "bubbled changes, but one said the application is incomplete without a site plan showing the "
  "driveway, since approving the building approves where it sits. The lots carry an unusual "
  "setback, ten feet at the front and five at the rear. Federal Street returned with the cupola "
  "and the Greek ornamentation removed and the roof pitch back to the original, though the "
  "streetscape drawing was not finished and a three-dimensional model was filed instead.",
 "qQR0-qd4utg":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the demolition application for 10 New Whale Street, hardscape at 89 Easton Street, "
  "and an as-built deer fence at 18 Gladlands Avenue. New Whale Street took about half an hour, "
  "its first hearing since March. The chair warned at the outset that contentious applications "
  "need strict time management. The owner&rsquo;s attorney said the report the commission had "
  "asked for was now in hand, and argued that the building would hold no historical significance "
  "after the work a rehabilitation would require. A commissioner pressed the engineer on the two "
  "shades of pink in the condition drawings and what the lighter one means for the rebuild. A "
  "motion to deny the demolition then drew two in favor, two against and one abstention. The "
  "commission could not agree what a tie produces and left the question for the start of the "
  "following Tuesday, rather than ask the applicant&rsquo;s attorney to settle it. Easton Street "
  "was held over from the previous week. The discussion turned on an outdoor shower painted "
  "white, a pergola screening the hotel property behind the patio, and the material of the "
  "drive-down garage. Gladlands Avenue is an as-built landscape fence and cattle grate, "
  "presented on a color-coded plan: the grate in orange, the perimeter fence in red, cedar posts "
  "in green and Belgian block in blue, the block still sitting on the street rather than where "
  "it is proposed.",
 "rCAzDKxxfA0":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: two applications from the public schools at 24 Surfside Road, rooftop solar at 3 "
  "Evergreen Way, and gates at 8 and 16 Stone Post Way. Surfside Road took the longest, close to "
  "twenty minutes. The school department asked for an office building and the hardscape around "
  "it, with gravel parking, concrete that ties into the school, a cedar fence separating the "
  "housing, and a new curb cut off First Way. The commissioner who kept it off consent said she "
  "passes the site every day and wants every tree taken down to be replaced, the trees being an "
  "integral part of the approach to the school. On the building itself, members said it does not "
  "read as one story because of its width, and that a hip roof over that much mass does not look "
  "residential. Evergreen Way is a modular house with a flat black roof. The owner proposed "
  "rooftop panels and offered screening beyond the Leyland cypress already planned. "
  "Commissioners regretted that the panels have to run in two directions. The Stone Post Way "
  "applications replace a winter deer fence with a gate set between two existing granite posts. "
  "One commissioner had kept them off consent because the only comparable gate on the street "
  "sits well back at the corner of a building.",
 "rd3NVlzGfvw":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a retaining wall at 12 Angola Street, the front door and windows at 34 Liberty "
  "Street, and a pool at 20 Main Street. Angola Street took the longest. Its landscape designer "
  "had moved the secondary dwelling back to match approved structural plans, and reworked the "
  "wall so it reads as more natural and ties into the back foundation of the house, which the "
  "contractor had asked for. Liberty Street came back with the detailed window survey the "
  "commission asked for at the previous meeting: close-up photographs of every window and of the "
  "front door, with the condition of each recorded. The agent reported that two more windows had "
  "been damaged in the storm since. Main Street is a pool pushed further over, with a fence "
  "added to screen it, set about a hundred feet off Main Street behind the house. The commercial "
  "hardscape at 18 to 26 Sparks Avenue, held over from the previous week, came back and was "
  "approved with conditions: materials signed off by the chair or vice chair before "
  "installation, five-inch caliber trees anchoring the Sparks Avenue side, and alternating "
  "sections of board fence. The commission also took up a deer gate at 14 Dukes Road, where the "
  "applicant offered two designs, and a door replacement at 31 India Street that the health "
  "department had asked for.",
 "tVl3fUZLVxg":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the cattle grate and Belgian block at 18 Gladlands Avenue, revisions at 11 Davis "
  "Lane, and 34 Tennessee Avenue. This was a short meeting, under fifty minutes, and the chair "
  "ran it as a lightning round. Gladlands Avenue took the longest. The commission had told the "
  "applicant at an earlier hearing that it did not want the cattle grate and was cool on the "
  "Belgian block. The agent returned with precedent photographs showing Belgian block at nearly "
  "every driveway on the street, including both sides of the one with the bike path, and with a "
  "pedestrian gate of the kind members had suggested. The grate comes out. The motion approves "
  "the gate as photographed, with a Belgian block apron about ten feet deep from the road, "
  "vegetation carried back around to the gate, and an exhibit drawn so there is no confusion "
  "later. Davis Lane came back with an altered second-floor deck detail. Tennessee Avenue "
  "follows from the new dwelling approved across the street at number 36: the main body of the "
  "structure that stood there, a gambrel, was approved to move onto lots 49 and 50. The "
  "commission also took questions for the preservation planner, who had nothing new on Certified "
  "Local Government business but expects minutes for coming meetings.",
 "tcoNs24fe58":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the town&rsquo;s new DPW facility at 1 Shadbush Road, a platform lift at 4 East "
  "Chestnut Street, and a garage at 7 Brewster Road. Shadbush Road took the longest, close to "
  "forty minutes over three applications: the facility, a salt shed and hardscape. The public "
  "works director presented with his architect and the town&rsquo;s engineers. One commissioner "
  "asked to view the site once he understood the scale of it. Most of the discussion was "
  "exterior materials and screening, the site being ringed by conservation land that is open to "
  "the public, with views from the beach, the moors and the creeks. Approval carried a condition "
  "that a wall of about four and a half feet be planted with marsh grasses, subject to what the "
  "Conservation Commission allows. East Chestnut Street has a platform elevator at the back of "
  "the property that fails within weeks of every service call. Replacing it would mean an "
  "enclosed glass case of the kind at the rear of the Pearl, which the applicant said would be "
  "visible from the Dreamland and the park opposite. It was held for more information and a "
  "mockup. The Brewster Road garage is a gambrel matching a main house the commission has "
  "already approved. Both sit well back from Brewster Road, behind the houses that front it, "
  "reached by a private easement rather than a road.",
 "umtVcL7cZFU":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the guest house, garage and pool at 3 Pops Lane, a relocation at 12 Madequecham "
  "Valley Road, and four applications at 4 Wood Hollow Road. Pops Lane took the longest, about "
  "twenty minutes. The guest house is an existing structure of about 1980 standing on the east "
  "side of the lot, and the architect said the discussion a week earlier had turned on where it "
  "goes rather than how it looks. Ridge poles had been set for the commission to view, and the "
  "owners&rsquo; counsel had filed a letter answering abutters&rsquo; concerns. Madequecham "
  "Valley Road moves a house back on its lot and adds to it. One member observed that it is not "
  "the only big box out there and moved to approve it as submitted; it passed with one opposed. "
  "The applicant&rsquo;s own remark that the house will be in the ocean in ten years drew the "
  "reply that his clients will take it down first. Wood Hollow Road is a move-off and "
  "demolition, a new dwelling, a cabana and a pool. The commission held the cabana and pool so "
  "they can be seen together with the house, the layout being likely to telescope back, and set "
  "the applications to track one another. Walsh Street followed, and 43 Orange Street returned "
  "on an as-built color change after the colors painted there were not favorably received last "
  "fall.",
 "v85oWlC89fQ":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: 3 Pops Lane, a garage addition at 20 Liberty Street, and the main house addition at "
  "85 Orange Street. Pops Lane took the longest, about thirty-five minutes. Its architect said "
  "the ridge heights had been flipped since the last meeting, raising the eave-forward mass with "
  "the chimney and dropping the flanking gable, after members questioned which was meant to read "
  "as the primary mass. It was held again for revisions, with the other applications tracking. "
  "One commissioner said he would move at the next meeting to act if the height, the "
  "fenestration and the ridge are not materially changed to meet the concerns already raised. "
  "Liberty Street is a garage with a half story above it, eighty-one feet back from the street. "
  "Its designer argued the addition stays subordinate: the ridge on the existing house is "
  "thirty-five feet and the addition twenty-six, with a small gable dormer whose trim and pitch "
  "follow the steep roof already there. It was held for revisions at the end of the night. "
  "Orange Street returned with a board along the base of the siding, the long stoop replaced by "
  "a mirror of the other door, and the roofwalk cut twelve inches, all from corrections against "
  "a historic photograph. Earlier the commission heard the sign advisory committee chair on "
  "Article 71, a Town Meeting amendment covering LED traffic control devices.",
 "wXxhAADBkSg":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: an as-built fence, gate and cattle grate at 11 and 21 Nonantum Avenue, a change of "
  "use at 54R Union Street, and a new dwelling at 5 Gray Avenue. Nonantum Avenue came to the "
  "commission as a violation. The landscape designer, brought in after the work was done, "
  "described a long cattle grate with a large apron that blends in for now, and a perimeter "
  "fence set about eight feet inside the property line except for a few stretches along Nonantum "
  "where it juts out just beyond the existing split rail. Vines and other planting are going in "
  "to hide those sections. Union Street would turn a garage into a dwelling on a lot subdivided "
  "off a bungalow at the front that is falling down. The designer said the state had refused to "
  "treat it as an accessory unit. The existing hip roof line is kept, with a dormer added in the "
  "manner of the house in front, and the commission was invited to view it. Gray Avenue is lot "
  "one of a three-lot subdivision sharing a driveway, with the front door and porch on the south "
  "elevation facing the road and little visibility from the other three sides. The sign portion "
  "of the agenda was dropped because the Sign Advisory Council had no quorum that day. The "
  "preservation planner recommended the National Alliance of Preservation Commissions summer "
  "course, which the commission attends at a discount and which carries continuing education "
  "credit.",
 "www-PoW6N9k":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the five applications at 10 Marble Way, a new dwelling at 16 Squam Road, and a new "
  "hotel building at 30 North Beach Street. Marble Way came back at the commission&rsquo;s "
  "request and took about half an hour, covering a garage apartment and a duplex on one lot and "
  "a dwelling, a garage and a cottage on the other. The garage stands twenty-five feet eight "
  "inches, with natural to weather trim and black windows and roof. One member opened by noting "
  "that this side of the street is zoned for mixed commercial and residential use while the "
  "other side is residential only, that the area was created for businesses and homes together, "
  "and that the garages with apartments above on the street behind are far larger. Squam Road is "
  "one parcel, with a main house set furthest from the road, a guest house of a story and a half "
  "nearer to it, and a garage with a studio above to come. A member who had walked the site "
  "warned that the ground climbs quickly, and did not want a thirty foot house reading like "
  "crenellations on the hilltop, as happened on Eel Point Road. North Beach Street drew praise "
  "as a handsome building that does not sit well with the old hotel beside it. The cupola was "
  "rejected, and one member suggested breaking the plan into an L so that it presses less on the "
  "street.",
 "zF_W2_ifEEM":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: a new house at 5 Weetamo Road, a shed at 16 Deer Run Road, and a fence at 62 Wanoma "
  "Way. Weetamo Road took the longest, about eighteen minutes. The architect placed the house in "
  "a Polpis subdivision whose zoning overlay caps ridge height at twenty-five feet and allows "
  "one primary structure to a lot. The site sits at the end of a cul-de-sac, well back from the "
  "entrance to Polpis Harbor, visible from the water only at a distance or from a kayak, and the "
  "building dips about three feet below the high point of the lot. Letters of support from every "
  "abutter were in the file. It passed with one member opposed, the motion treating it as a test "
  "case for how that area reads from the harbor. A shed there went through as submitted. The "
  "Deer Run shed was held for revisions, members asking for a deeper roof pitch and a sharp "
  "reduction in size rather than set a precedent. Wanoma Way is a fence already built. The owner "
  "said she learned only afterward that the commission&rsquo;s approval was needed, and that the "
  "Conservation Commission cannot act on her application until the fence is approved here. It "
  "runs along the edge of a public path at the back of the property and a short way up one side.",
 "zKatDsO1M5c":
  "This summary skips many topics because of the quantity discussed; some principal topics "
  "include: the paired dwellings at 8 and 10 Cato Lane, a renovation at 17 Eat Fire Spring, and "
  "hardscaping at 14 Silver Street. Cato Lane ran about twenty minutes. The architect said he "
  "had followed the last direction given, to mirror number 10 and move both houses back five "
  "feet, which leaves number 8 about fifteen feet in front of its neighbor. He listed the "
  "differences between them: two over two sash and three dormers at number 10 against six over "
  "six and two dormers at number 8, gray trim with natural cedar at one and all natural cedar at "
  "the other, black windows against Quaker gray. A member said the drawings were hard to follow "
  "because nothing was clouded. Eat Fire Spring is an older house in a rural setting, presented "
  "by the architect in place of the agent. The windows are to be replaced, the dark storms "
  "removed, the siding and roof renewed, and the porch roof rebuilt so that it sheds rain rather "
  "than passing it through open slats. Members called the work a net gain but pressed against "
  "the white trim, several preferring the existing red as a character defining feature or a gray "
  "that works with the landscape, and against double columns with capitals and bases that belong "
  "to another aesthetic. Silver Street is a casual plan of brick apron, grass strip, stepping "
  "stones and a low hedge toward the town parking lot.",
 "5Yxg9cqZeuk":
  "Two public hearings open and are left open another month, on the Hummock Pond watershed and "
  "wellhead protection amendments. A former board member warns that the wellhead zone takes in a "
  "great deal of property still on septic that will likely never see sewer, the town&rsquo;s own "
  "land and the housing authority&rsquo;s among it, and asks that people be given time enough to "
  "come and speak. The phrase extraordinary circumstances, the test a variance would have to "
  "meet, troubles several speakers, and the Land and Water Council endorses the effort while "
  "asking to read the new language closely before giving an opinion. At 12 Nonantum Avenue the "
  "well setback turns out not to need a variance at all, the lot having been created before "
  "1990, and the septic variance passes on its own. A monitoring revision follows at 6 and 6A "
  "Washington Avenue. The sharpest comments of the night come from the public: one resident asks "
  "why a single property should get septic capacity nobody else in the neighborhood has, and a "
  "town housing employee, speaking for herself, observes that it is far easier here to permit a "
  "summer estate than anything denser, then asks whether this board can tie an approval to year "
  "round occupancy.",
 "D0xZSsXmUBo":
  "Three hours. Town counsel brings a draft: a new section in the short-term rental regulations "
  "reaching gatherings and activities at a rented house that are not sleeping and habitation, "
  "offered as a working document under the bylaw that lets this board, with the select board, "
  "regulate rentals against noise and parking nuisance. PFAS data sharing changes outright. "
  "Staff met the state, learned that the island-wide investigation carries a release tracking "
  "number under the Massachusetts contingency plan, and found a hundred sampling results already "
  "posted unredacted. After talking to town counsel, the department will publish sampling "
  "results and property files. A rewrite of Chapter 223 cuts every definition Title 5 already "
  "carries and expands the one for bedroom, because a sleeping loft called storage still sends "
  "water to the leach field. Watershed variances pass at 7 Arkansas Avenue and 16 Ridge Lane, "
  "approved on the septic design, with the building application still to be checked against a "
  "three bedroom count. Nantucket Community Sailing asks for relief from the state camp "
  "regulation, 6 Tautemo Way for a nitrogen loading credit, and 40R Essex Road brings a lift "
  "condemnation.",
 "GzyySjTigig":
  "An appeal from 49 Polpis Road takes the longest. The owner has lived there since 1978, was "
  "permitted to reopen a commercial kitchen on condition that she connect within two years, "
  "broke her hip and let the date pass, and now holds an order to join the sewer. She turned "
  "eighty the Monday before the meeting, is on a tight budget, and the electrical service for "
  "several neighboring houses runs buried the length of a drive seven or eight hundred feet "
  "long, so the cost would not stop at her own pipe. She offers instead to close the kitchen and "
  "return the space to residential use. The board is not comfortable with that and gives her a "
  "month or two to come back with real figures, alternative routes, and what the loan program "
  "would add to her monthly payment. The consultants report the second round of beach and foam "
  "sampling. Draft amendments to the Hummock Pond and wellhead regulations are circulated for "
  "comment, with every mention of an enhanced nitrogen removing system now defined as one "
  "reaching 19 milligrams per liter, and the public hearing set for November 20. The board also "
  "weighs endorsing two state bills on private well water quality.",
 "Hjs0jFGB2Fc":
  "An hour and more on PFAS, told from the beginning: the airport&rsquo;s 2019 notice of "
  "responsibility over firefighting foam, the working group formed in January 2020, and since "
  "then $15 million appropriated across departments, twenty-three contracts, and an $8.3 million "
  "zero-interest state loan for the water main west of the airport. Wells 15 and 16 sit in the "
  "upper aquifer. Number 15 is off, the wells in that area are now sampled monthly, and one "
  "result came back at 55.5 parts per trillion. Town water tests non-detect across the wider "
  "list of compounds, and samples for the federal unregulated contaminant round are going out. "
  "The engineers will not promise that a source is ever identified &mdash; the goal is reducing "
  "exposure &mdash; and one member says a contaminated well raises giant red flags against "
  "everything being planned. Then a public hearing opens, and is left open, on large events at "
  "private homes: the board strikes the option that would reach a family wedding in its own "
  "backyard and aims instead at houses rented commercially for them. Septic loans follow at 28 "
  "Tennessee and 48 Hummock Pond Road, the second replacing a May loan after the old tank proved "
  "unusable.",
 "Kt7GHDFxjmc":
  "A consultant reports the first round of beach water sampling for PFAS: sixteen coastal "
  "beaches, three inland ponds and two points a mile offshore for comparison, with foam "
  "collected where it appears because the compounds concentrate in it. A second round follows "
  "next week and a summary report comes in October. Short-term rental registrations have gone "
  "from roughly 150 last autumn to 1,347, and the department is starting to collect the "
  "quarterly reports the bylaw allows, among them the listing, the nights occupied and the "
  "operator&rsquo;s contact, with an audit to follow. Wicked Island Bakery asks to be let off "
  "hair restraints for its front of house staff, arguing that the food sits two feet away behind "
  "the tallest sneeze guard on the island, that the staff wear their hair back, and that a "
  "server carrying trays past their head takes the greater risk. At 113 Hummock Pond Road the "
  "board returns to a variance it half granted in 2020. The design uses nitrogen reducing "
  "technology and drip dispersal, but the soil log shows a bluish gray that reads as constantly "
  "saturated, and the health official says he is surprised no groundwater was found.",
 "PgN8F0hqT5k":
  "Next Level Watersports returns for its camp variances. The operation teaches kite boarding "
  "and wake boarding, runs four days a week through July and August, and is treated as a "
  "primitive or outpost camp under the state regulation. The change this year is that children "
  "are picked up and dropped off at the town pier slip. The hearing on large events at private "
  "homes draws the culture and tourism department, which took over special events from the "
  "police and finds private property the tricky part. The board still cannot settle how to "
  "measure a house. Bedrooms would be simple, square footage is harder to pin down, and no "
  "single number gives a building capacity, so it goes over another month. The environmental "
  "contamination administrator reports that town counsel cleared the department on February 20 "
  "to upload PFAS results into the property files, and apologizes for not crediting the public "
  "records request and the residents who spent more than a year seeking that data. Private well "
  "rules go before the full membership of the real estate brokers in May. The state sampled the "
  "geothermal well at the fairgrounds and three addresses west of town. Nitrogen loading credits "
  "pass at 57 Madaket Road and 38.5 Lovers Lane.",
 "Qz9HVhG5hys":
  "A special meeting on the moratorium, and most of it is about how old the rules are. The "
  "Nantucket Harbor regulations date from 2015 and Madaket from 2020, Hummock Pond has not been "
  "examined since 2010, and the wellhead district not since 1995, while population, technology "
  "and what is known about harmful substances have all moved on. An engineer offers an interim "
  "course, which is to keep entertaining bedroom credits but only against a ten milligram per "
  "liter standard, and reports that Falmouth is proposing exactly that for all new construction "
  "with Bourne taking it up the same night. The nitrogen sensitive areas the state added to "
  "Title 5 were nearly statewide before being narrowed to the Cape, each town left to write its "
  "own plan. The rest is calendar work. The moratorium declared in June expires on September 18, "
  "so it is extended to the November 20 meeting, and that fixes the rest: the draft back from "
  "town counsel by October 22, then advertised three consecutive weeks from October 30.",
 "RUvr1-4LOMw":
  "A discussion of artificial athletic fields fills most of the meeting, framed by the chair as "
  "a listening session rather than a decision. Supporters cite rising maintenance costs, "
  "overused fields and growing athletic programs, and describe the proposed footprint as "
  "negligible. Opponents raise PFAS, microplastics and the site position inside the wellhead "
  "protection district above the island sole source aquifer, and point to turf bans in other "
  "Massachusetts communities. The board votes to schedule a special meeting in January, then "
  "retakes the vote to satisfy Robert&rsquo;s Rules. Earlier, the finance director explains the "
  "$250 short-term rental registration fee, which pays for an $84,000 software platform and a "
  "compliance position out of a revolving fund approaching $250,000. Neighboring towns charge "
  "between $50 and $500. The Hummock Pond watershed and wellhead protection hearings are "
  "continued to the January meeting. The board releases a betterment lien at 58 Arkansas Avenue, "
  "approves a septic loan for a sewer connection at 39 Bartlett Road, and joins the Inter-Island "
  "Public Health Excellence Collaborative under an agreement carrying a 90-day exit clause. The "
  "PFAS update adds two detections above the state standard of 20 parts per trillion and a 106 "
  "reading near Fulling Mill, with more than 500 wells now tested.",
 "SzmkPI6HaFo":
  "Five minutes of an August meeting that never begins. One member is in the room and another is "
  "attending remotely, which does not count toward a quorum, so the chair declines to call the "
  "meeting to order and the board agrees to wait five minutes before making another plan. A "
  "second member arrives just as the recording ends. The meeting itself was recorded separately.",
 "U4bvpWGjWu8":
  "One subject for ninety minutes: whether to keep granting nitrogen loading credits in "
  "sensitive areas, and what the alternative would look like. The island will never be fully "
  "sewered, not practically and not affordably, so the question becomes whether advanced "
  "treatment should simply be the baseline everywhere, paid for the way Barnstable County does "
  "it, with loans or grants covering the gap between a conventional system and an I/A one. A "
  "member keeps returning to the same ground: one aquifer, one water supply, and waste that "
  "carries pathogens and PFAS as well as nitrogen. Staff set out what is already regulated, the "
  "wellhead protection district, Hummock Pond, Madaket and Sesachacha among them, with a three "
  "hundred foot buffer to ponds for phosphorus, and note that the harbor districts cannot seek "
  "variances at all, which is why the requests arrive from everywhere else. The board asks for a "
  "legal opinion on how Title 5 and its own regulations actually fit together, in plain words "
  "rather than gobbledegook, and agrees it will be more useful once the data arrives.",
 "dxwBOCQi5q8":
  "The board adopts a PFAS vision and mission statement at the town&rsquo;s request: a community "
  "informed, engaged and empowered through science-based environmental leadership, with clean "
  "water and reduced exposure for this generation and the next. The long argument is 113 Hummock "
  "Pond Road, back from August. The water beneath it is perched on a clay layer, with the "
  "aquifer some twenty feet further down. The design already mounds the front yard eighteen "
  "inches, and two feet more would bring retaining walls between the house and the road for no "
  "environmental gain. The existing system has not failed in any practical sense, only on the "
  "measurement to that perched water, and the plan now specifies a test hole dug in front of "
  "staff before anything is installed. At 12 Nonantum Avenue the applicant offers to rebuild a "
  "catch basin across the street as a solid one with its own leaching pit to clear the hundred "
  "foot well setback, subject to written approval from the drainage side of the sewer "
  "department. The administrator explains fingerprinting: every source leaves its own mixture of "
  "compounds, which is how properties near the airport have been ruled out of its investigation.",
 "hVumBiuAmwA":
  "Most of three hours goes to bedrooms and nitrogen. One proposal would move a house from 6 "
  "Ames Avenue onto 339 Madaket Road, putting five bedrooms there and dropping 337 Madaket from "
  "five to four, which needs relief from the Madaket limit of one bedroom per ten thousand "
  "square feet. Credits are asked for three lots at 38 Lovers Lane and six at 13 Woodland Drive. "
  "The board&rsquo;s patience is visibly thinning. Members say an advanced septic system does "
  "not automatically buy extra bedrooms, that nitrogen is not the only pollutant in waste and "
  "that a system can be pushed only so far, and they want firmer guidelines rather than deciding "
  "one application at a time. Sewering the island, one answers, is the purely environmental "
  "solution and is not going to happen. On large events, the chair reports a joint meeting with "
  "the select board set for June 11, the point of it being one island-wide approach rather than "
  "one per department. The PFAS update carries a detection above the imminent hazard threshold "
  "near Hummock Pond and Burnt Swamp, found by the state on April 9, and another above the state "
  "standard on Nobska.",
 "h_AjengMEz0":
  "The numbers come in the middle. The state&rsquo;s source discovery investigation has now "
  "evaluated 86 properties, found PFAS at 63 percent of them, 23 above the Massachusetts maximum "
  "contaminant level, and nine on Tom&rsquo;s Way and the streets beside it above the imminent "
  "hazard threshold of 90 parts per trillion. The highest reading is 787. Earlier, the rewrite "
  "of Chapter 223 brings an architect to the microphone on the definition of a bedroom: cabanas "
  "that lose their walls in order to stay cabanas, a windowless exercise room counted as a "
  "bedroom, wine cellars trimmed to 69 square feet, and a seven room trigger where Title 5 says "
  "eight. Septic setback variances follow at 35 Warren&rsquo;s Landing, 3 Millbrook Road and 8 "
  "Ames Avenue. At 72 Arkansas Avenue an owner asks not to upgrade after a 2010 inspection "
  "failed for hydraulic failure and for groundwater separation. Owners out there signed consent "
  "orders and put down deposits years ago against the day sewer reached Madaket, and it has not. "
  "A member who lives on the street recuses himself. The director closes on measles, with 640 "
  "cases nationwide and none here against school vaccination of 96 to 97 percent, and on federal "
  "cuts and fall vaccine supply.",
 "lpT6dwLAzRM":
  "A long tutorial from the state alternative septic test center sets up everything the board "
  "has been arguing about. Title 5 turned from a disposal code into a treatment code in 1995, "
  "when loading rates came down to deal with pathogens and nitrogen was barely addressed. Ninety "
  "percent of a Cape board of health&rsquo;s work, the presenter says, is Title 5. A board may "
  "require better than the usual nitrogen standard, or fewer bedrooms to the acre, if it writes "
  "the regulation and gives its reasons, and rising nitrogen in a public supply well would be "
  "reason enough. It can never go below Title 5, and the island has yet to finish a watershed "
  "management plan. Enforcement when a system misses its number runs through the operator: "
  "whether the air is on, whether the system runs at all, whether nitrogen that looks high is "
  "really ammonia that never nitrified. The PFAS update brings two more imminent hazard "
  "detections beside the one found in April, one at 171 parts per trillion and one at 87, with a "
  "reading of two parts per trillion a hundred feet away. Officers are elected, and septic items "
  "pass at 259 Madaket Road and 24 Arkansas Avenue.",
 "xKJAotyhutY":
  "The board opens the question of a moratorium on septic variances in nitrogen sensitive areas. "
  "The argument for it is that nobody can say what the island gains or loses by granting an "
  "extra bedroom for every advanced system, that the decisions are being made house by house, "
  "and that there is only one drinking water supply. Barnstable County is expected in July with "
  "data, and the only hesitation is whether six months is longer than a reasonable limit. The "
  "credits keep coming even so, at 13 Woodland Drive and 38 Lovers Lane, one of them a variance "
  "for as many as twelve bedrooms shown as a single building. The engineer explains that a gym "
  "and other rooms count as bedrooms whatever the occupancy, and a member answers that twelve "
  "sounds like a dormitory. Nantucket By Water wins relief from the rule requiring a base of "
  "operation: commercially prepackaged frozen desserts, triple wrapped, kept in a freezer on the "
  "dock and discarded at the end of the day, with the food protection manager certification "
  "still required. The state sampled eleven properties near the imminent hazard detection, "
  "reaching the island on the slow ferry with a health inspector driving.",
 "yrj6yeqeJKc":
  "A special meeting for one subject: camp variances for the water sports programs. Four come "
  "through in turn, two surf schools, a watersports operator and Nantucket Community Sailing, "
  "and the board settles into a pattern, granting three year variances rather than annual ones, "
  "with periodic updates from staff and a standing check-in on the agenda once camp inspections "
  "are done. The operators report a quiet season. One, in its twenty-sixth year, had small "
  "waves, no medications to store and no incidents, and says the department has made the "
  "paperwork easier every year. The longest argument is Community Sailing&rsquo;s case against "
  "the lifeguard requirement. Its instructors hold US Sailing certifications running from "
  "fifty-five to ninety hours, with first aid, CPR, safe sport and concussion training on top, "
  "and the national body has written in support while the school&rsquo;s insurer recommends the "
  "same courses. Those trainings cover person in the water and capsized boat recovery, towing, "
  "and powerboat handling around swimmers, which is the rescue the school actually performs. "
  "Nobody there, instructor or student, is ever on the water without a flotation device.",
 "TBEb-_SsbVc":
  "The first meeting under the commission&rsquo;s rewritten wetlands regulations, which took "
  "effect on January 1. The chair explains which rules apply to what. Anything filed for the "
  "next meeting falls under the new regulations, while applications submitted before the first "
  "of the year, including those already continued, stay under the old ones. The agenda is "
  "printed in a new form to match. Most of the evening goes to 62 Wanoma Way, where a deer fence "
  "was installed through bordering vegetated wetland associated with Tom Nevers Pond and drew an "
  "enforcement order. The notice of intent proposes relocating it. A property manager for an "
  "abutting owner asks that the wetland and buffer lines be shown across the neighboring lot. "
  "That hearing runs about thirty-seven minutes. Two waiver requests follow. At 36 Bassett Road "
  "the question is whether a buffer already altered by a semi-public accessway qualifies. At 3 "
  "Wyers Way the commission turns to whether patios and other hardscaping count as structures, "
  "which the new regulations say they do and the old ones largely did not. Also heard: 9 Cow "
  "Pond Lane, 46 West Miacomet Road, 60 Squam Road and 99 Low Beach Road.",
 "I5p8cz_3pGM":
  "A special meeting called for one application, the Sconset Bluff Preservation Trust at 41-119 "
  "Baxter Road, continued from 2024 so the remaining questions could be settled. Staff opens by "
  "explaining that two nearly identical orders are in circulation, one carrying the state file "
  "number and one the town&rsquo;s. Much of the two hours goes to the sand the project owes. A "
  "speaker tells the commission the volume is arithmetic rather than negotiation, and that there "
  "is no point quibbling over five thousand cubic yards either way. He asks that any deadline "
  "for placing mitigation sand fall inside the term of the order about to be issued, since an "
  "obligation running ten years against a three or five year order would be legally doubtful. "
  "The commission works through the draft condition by condition, including how the project "
  "moves from phase one to phase two and from coir to geotubes. It ends on the existing "
  "structure, to be maintained until phase one is built and then replaced as part of it. Because "
  "the filing has two applicants, the commission asks for a letter confirming the other party "
  "agrees before going further.",
 "9dTfNn7EoL4":
  "The longest hearing is 76 Eel Point Road, a large property whose plan staff sums up as a "
  "whole lot of structures. A member of the public, seeing the diagram of where those structures "
  "would sit for the first time, asks why it was not available sooner, and is told to send "
  "comments to staff the week before the next meeting so that they reach the packet. The "
  "application is continued to February 6. The commission then takes up 80 Madaket Road, a "
  "residential development on a thirty-one acre lot. The engineer says every proposed structure "
  "sits outside the fifty-foot buffer and all landscaping outside the twenty-five-foot buffer, "
  "and that the filing predates the new regulations. An existing dirt driveway inside both "
  "buffers is to be kept and used for access. A one-year extension is granted to the Linda "
  "Loring Nature Foundation for its Eel Point Road parcels. Staff closes by asking commissioners "
  "to read the new regulations and the summary of changes before the next meeting, when the "
  "first applications filed under them arrive.",
 "3Jqr45Wf0wc":
  "Twenty-five minutes, the longest stretch of the evening, go to a second public hearing on "
  "coastal stabilization at 72 and 78 Pocomo Road. The question is whether the array proposed "
  "there is a coastal engineering structure. The applicant&rsquo;s representative says it is "
  "not, calling it a soft solution of the same template the commission has approved before, and "
  "has had a legal study prepared to answer the point. Earlier the commission closes 49 Meadow "
  "View Drive and opens 29A Old North Wharf, whose state file number was issued only that day. "
  "Committee reports follow, including word that a final draft of the harbor plan is expected "
  "within about two months. A commissioner mentions a site visit to an Eel Point Road property "
  "and is reminded that applications are not discussed outside their hearings. The meeting ends "
  "on enforcement. The commission can fine a property owner fifty dollars a day to a maximum of "
  "three hundred, a cap it tried and failed to raise during the regulatory update. It decides to "
  "wait until March 6 rather than issue fines now.",
 "1-9kc9IdH2I":
  "A second special meeting on the Sconset Bluff Preservation Trust at 41-119 Baxter Road, and "
  "most of it is spent on an expired order. The earlier order has lapsed, and the structure "
  "built under it would otherwise have to come out. The commission&rsquo;s purpose in drafting "
  "the new order is to remove that requirement and to settle the sand mitigation volumes owed "
  "under the old one. The applicant argues that the two are separate matters, and that "
  "compliance cannot be demanded under an order that no longer stands. The last hour of the "
  "meeting is given to that question alone. Earlier the commission works through the draft "
  "conditions. The protection for a walkable beach is rewritten as unobstructed beach access, "
  "and the commission debates whether ten or fifteen feet is required, and whether it should "
  "apply in all four quarters or only in the second and third, when the beach is used. The "
  "town&rsquo;s representative asks for time to take the remaining issues back to counsel and to "
  "town administration before the order is final.",
 "lVxU-v4Y9k0":
  "Twenty-seven minutes, the longest hearing of the evening, go to 110 Wauwinet Road, with two "
  "commissioners recused. Before it the commission takes up 14 Tautemo Way, where a pool, patio, "
  "shed, water closet and pergola are proposed near an existing septic system. The filing has "
  "been continued twice for more information, and the applicant asks that drawings for the pool "
  "and shed be made a condition of the order rather than the reason for a third continuance. An "
  "access path has been dropped from the application, because it lies in priority habitat and "
  "would need a separate state filing. Orders of conditions follow, beginning with 72 and 78 "
  "Pocomo Road, taken together as one project and carrying a long list of conditions. The "
  "evening ends on enforcement. Asked whether the commission can make a violator appear before "
  "it, the answer is that it always has ways to compel attendance or impose consequences, and "
  "that negotiations among a violator, abutters and other parties often go on where the "
  "commission cannot see them. Also heard: 7 Folger Lane, 10 Smooth Hummocks Way, 2 Ames Avenue "
  "and 34 Straight Wharf.",
 "Qc9NuBDXOMg":
  "Nearly an hour goes to enforcement at 14 Plover Lane. An enforcement order was issued for "
  "widespread mastication and removal of vegetation, and the owner&rsquo;s representatives "
  "attend to describe what happened. A commissioner calls it one of the more significant "
  "violations of six years on the commission, covering close to the whole of the area. The "
  "discussion turns on sequence and on money. The commission wants a remediation plan in time to "
  "use this growing season. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney proposes a two-step approach, with a "
  "remediation plan approved under the enforcement order first. Commissioners note that the "
  "regulations make every day a violation continues a separate offense, and differ on whether to "
  "impose fines now or wait to see the plan. Before that, the chair uses commissioner comments "
  "to propose two changes to the definition of a structure: that it be assembled at a "
  "continuously or permanently fixed location, and that it be made of man-made rather than "
  "naturally occurring materials. Both are aimed at coir terraces, fencing and temporary "
  "methods. The town&rsquo;s application for 2 Ames Avenue and Dionis Beach is opened, and an "
  "order issues for 34 Straight Wharf.",
 "uh-yOAHKYOI":
  "A third special meeting on the Sconset Bluff Preservation Trust at 41-119 Baxter Road, and "
  "the dispute is now about numbers and about who decides them. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney "
  "argues that once the order of conditions expired the matter became an enforcement question, "
  "which sits with the commission rather than with the town, and that officials who want to stop "
  "the project have other means of doing it. The town&rsquo;s representative reports being "
  "directed by the Select Board to have the missing sand placed, and that almost everyone "
  "consulted in town administration answered that the full volume owed under the previous permit "
  "should be pursued. The commission works through conditions. One exchange turns on whether a "
  "delivery condition should say must, on the grounds that a condition which does not is "
  "arbitrary. Another returns to a question the commission has not settled, whether geotubes or "
  "coir terraces amount to a coastal engineering structure. Here they are tied together with no "
  "gaps between them, which is why the commission has treated the whole as one. A redrafted "
  "order is to come back on March 20.",
 "bc9t4KDkAlg":
  "After four months of deliberation in open session, which the chair notes is unusual, the "
  "commission issues its orders for 41-119 Baxter Road. There are two, one under the state act "
  "and one under the town&rsquo;s own bylaw, and they differ: the state order carries no waiver, "
  "because there is nothing in the state act to waive, and it does not list the local resource "
  "areas. Both had been posted ahead of the meeting. Two commissioners do not sit. Enforcement "
  "at 14 Plover Lane takes much of the second half. The discussion is about security. One "
  "commissioner favors a bond, which would still hold if the property changed hands. Another "
  "argues for a conservation restriction on the land, which the wetlands bylaw allows and which "
  "would prevent a repeat. The violator&rsquo;s attorney says that financial security matters to "
  "the owners as well. The meeting ends by lifting the cease and desist order for one purpose "
  "only, so that plant material can be ordered, with no planting or other work on site. "
  "Certificates of compliance at 3 Cornish Street close out two orders, one of which lapsed with "
  "no work done under it.",
 "SU9EaPdIyxA":
  "Twenty-four minutes go to the restoration plan for 14 Plover Lane. A site visit has shown "
  "that the cutting ran onto the two neighboring properties, and a commissioner says it may have "
  "gone further than the survey records, with vines and greenbrier cut well beyond what the plan "
  "shows. The plan itself is detailed. A temporary access path is needed because the existing "
  "connection is too narrow to carry plant material, and it will be replanted once the work is "
  "done, with clusters of shrubs and three tupelo trees. The commission approves the restoration "
  "plan as submitted, then requires the violator to file a notice of intent within thirty days. "
  "Earlier it closes a hearing on a vote with one abstention and two recusals, and opens 8 Ames "
  "Avenue, which the engineer describes as a very small lot. Also before the commission: 76 Eel "
  "Point Road, 32 Dukes Road, 2 and 6 Easton Street, 15 Marsh Hawk Lane and 5 North Water Street.",
 "NwwabyFP4lQ":
  "Forty-five minutes go to 76 Eel Point Road, with the chair recused and another commissioner "
  "presiding. The applicant&rsquo;s representative opens by objecting on the record to a "
  "commissioner sitting, because that commissioner&rsquo;s employer has filed a comment letter "
  "on the project. Since the last hearing the commission has walked the site, and a revised plan "
  "adds infiltration for roof and surface runoff. Abutters, their attorney and a neighboring "
  "land trust raise drainage into connected wetlands, priority habitat, the cumulative effect of "
  "a possible later subdivision, and above all construction: the approach to the site is steep "
  "and presently a footpath, and they ask where vehicles, soil and materials would go. The "
  "commission asks for a construction mitigation and staging plan covering work within its "
  "jurisdiction, and the hearing is continued to May 8th. Also heard: a septic upgrade at 8 Ames "
  "Avenue, which the state has conditioned for plovers although the work area is lawn and flower "
  "beds, and raise-and-rebuild plans at 3 Pochick Avenue and 39 Cliff Road.",
 "ySkz9c5y3j8":
  "More than three hours, most of it enforcement. The commission amends the 14 Plover Lane "
  "enforcement order to require the unpermitted fence removed and the lighting brought into "
  "compliance by June 5th. Abutters say the plantings ordered on their own properties have not "
  "happened and describe repeated reversals over scheduling. The commission answers that an "
  "enforcement order requires full restoration, but that reaching the other parcels would mean "
  "amending the order to name those owners as violators too, which nobody wants. A long "
  "discussion of a fine follows. Town counsel explains that the commission can assess one but "
  "that a judge settles it if it is not resolved, and offers a memo on the options; "
  "commissioners weigh restoration against deterrence, and one says they have never supported "
  "fines against a cooperative violator. The meeting ends with an enforcement order for 59-83 "
  "Baxter Road permitting one delivery of sand to cover the existing geotubes while the orders "
  "are under appeal, conditioned on an off-site sand source, a compatibility analysis filed "
  "before placement, mean high water as the limit of work, and language keeping the delivery out "
  "of the failure criteria in both permits.",
 "bbK_FrGqMnU":
  "A special meeting on the commission&rsquo;s own regulations and procedures; no applications "
  "are heard. Much of it goes to the definition of a structure. The chair proposes narrowing it "
  "to material permanently assembled at a fixed location, and to material that is not naturally "
  "occurring, which would leave out dune guards, coastal grids and coir rolls while keeping "
  "geotextile fabric, plastic or steel sheeting and multiple rows of fencing as coastal "
  "engineering structures. Commissioners ask where geotubes would fall. The two-foot separation "
  "between a structure and high groundwater is questioned next: no one present can say whether "
  "the number is too large or too small, so the commission looks at hiring an outside "
  "hydrogeologist, noting that the expense has to be voted at a public hearing and that the "
  "fiscal year ends June 30th. It also takes up an enforcement coordinator, which the bylaw may "
  "not allow because it recognizes only a chair, a vice chair and a secretary; whether island "
  "streams should be a resource area of their own, with staff asked to check the mapping first; "
  "and voting the commissioners who make site visits. Two further meetings are to be scheduled, "
  "one with town counsel on enforcement and one to adopt the language.",
 "zXJWyL76NAc":
  "The longest hearing is 10 and 16 Ocean Avenue, where the filing covers removing part of a "
  "deck and moving a shed but the argument is about lunch service on the dune: staff of The "
  "Summer House of Nantucket Realty Trust carry ten tables and chairs onto open sand each day "
  "and take them in when service ends. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney argues that this is not an "
  "activity under either the state act or the town bylaw, that a coastal geologist&rsquo;s "
  "letter finds no impact on the dune, and that in any case the use predates both. The chair "
  "disagrees on each point, saying that compacting and shading sand is alteration, that the "
  "activity matches none of the listed minor activities, and that aerial photographs from 1998, "
  "2007 and 2014 show no tables while 2023 does, so a pre-existing use is not established. A "
  "land and water council speaker cautions against formalizing a use that may be degrading the "
  "dune. The commission also hears a deer fence at 62 Wanoma Way that a landscaper installed "
  "without permits, now to be moved back behind the fifty-foot buffer, and amends the 14 Plover "
  "Lane enforcement order to allow temporary fencing around the new plantings.",
 "mFKIKKWiPcQ":
  "Twenty minutes go to whether a conservation restriction should be required at 14 Plover Lane. "
  "The chair explains that a restriction is more durable than a regulation, because the holder, "
  "the select board and the Commonwealth all have to approve it and it is recorded on the deed, "
  "and that a conservation commission may hold one itself. Commissioners ask who would hold it, "
  "what it would cost, and whether requiring it would be punitive when the violator has done "
  "what was asked; one answers that a private deed restriction lapses after thirty years, so "
  "only a conservation restriction holds in perpetuity. On the enforcement order itself, the "
  "unpermitted fence has come down and the lighting has been disconnected until compliant "
  "fixtures arrive in July. Town counsel then takes the commission through a civil penalty: the "
  "Wetlands Protection Act allows up to $25,000, that language already appears in the "
  "commission&rsquo;s own enforcement order, and a 2017 Appeals Court decision confirms that a "
  "commission may impose one. Also heard: pruning a groundsel tree on Land Bank property at 74 "
  "Washington Street, and applications at 16 Walsh Street, 2 East Lincoln Avenue and 224 Polpis "
  "Road.",
 "fCNryGaGBRw":
  "Two long items. The first is a plan to remove buildings at 18 Hoicks Hollow Road as the bank "
  "erodes toward them, one of them now about nineteen feet from the top. The representative "
  "describes an asbestos survey that came back clean, removal of the septic system and the "
  "asphalt, and asks what should be planted in their place. Commissioners want the replacement "
  "soil to match the bank rather than the six inches of loam the local regulations call for, "
  "expect native grasses, and plan a condition requiring anything that later works out of the "
  "bank to be taken away. There is no state file number yet and the heritage program is still "
  "deliberating, so the hearing is continued to July 10th. The rest returns to 14 Plover Lane, "
  "where the commission settles on a conservation restriction rather than a deed restriction, "
  "one commissioner having explained that a private deed restriction lapses after thirty years. "
  "The chair notes that an applicant need not agree to a condition, since the commission writes "
  "the conditions and the permit holder may appeal, and proposes wording that leaves the form "
  "open while town counsel works out how to hold it in perpetuity. Monitoring runs through the "
  "2025, 2026 and 2027 growing seasons.",
 "4T5CX43eQVE":
  "A short meeting with one hearing. At 10 and 16 Ocean Avenue the applicant now argues that "
  "dining on the dune is grandfathered: two witnesses, one of them a former owner of the "
  "restaurant, say the practice goes back to 1970 and 1971, before the state act of 1972 and the "
  "town bylaw of the 1980s. A coastal geologist&rsquo;s supplemental report argues that the "
  "beach grass in the dining area is sparse enough for the effect on dune stability to be "
  "negligible, and takes issue with the land and water council&rsquo;s letter. The chair answers "
  "that showing the business existed is not the same as showing the use continued, and asks for "
  "whatever can be found for every year from 1972 on: affidavits, photographs, aerial imagery, "
  "receipts. The hearing is continued to August 7th at the applicant&rsquo;s request, with two "
  "commissioners recused. Certificates of compliance follow at 25 Willard Street, 14 Bassett "
  "Street and 29A Old North Wharf. The meeting closes on a question the regulations do not "
  "answer: how long a tent has to stand before it counts as a structure, which the commission "
  "means to take up in its next regulatory update.",
 "G88q18PNJFA":
  "The commission re-elects its chair and vice chair for another year. The deer fence at 62 "
  "Wanoma Way draws the longest discussion. The chair accepts that the adverse impact is minimal "
  "but says the alternatives are expansive, since fencing discourages the movement of deer and "
  "other animals and an existing hedge row could carry part of the run. Other commissioners "
  "point to three choke points where a small encroachment inside the fifty-foot buffer would "
  "make sense if the rest of the line moved back. The applicant asks to continue to August 7th "
  "and take a compromise to the owner. At 16 Codfish Park Road the commission hears an addition "
  "to a cottage and a Title 5 septic upgrade, sought under the waiver for a buffer zone already "
  "altered by a public way. A septic upgrade at 59 Starbuck Road receives a negative "
  "determination; an abutter raises a driveway said to cross the adjoining lot, which the chair "
  "rules outside the commission&rsquo;s authority, and then concerns for Long Pond. 10 and 16 "
  "Ocean Avenue is continued to August 7th on the attorney&rsquo;s written authorization.",
 "FK2lgV--FyQ":
  "The 14 Plover Lane violation is resolved. Town counsel has advised that the deed restriction "
  "drafted for the violator will run in perpetuity, rather than lapsing after thirty years, "
  "provided the commission holds it and signs its acceptance, and the commission takes a "
  "separate vote to accept the document so that one instrument can be recorded. Commissioners "
  "argue the alternative first. A conservation restriction would carry a right to walk the "
  "property once a year, which a deed restriction does not, and counsel confirms that the "
  "commission has no inspection rights and would learn of a new violation the way it learned of "
  "this one, from a public way or from an abutter. One commissioner objects to the phrase clear "
  "cutting, reading two dictionary definitions and noting that at least seventy-five mature "
  "trees are still standing; another answers that the destruction is real whatever it is called. "
  "A civil penalty of $11,100 goes into the order of conditions so that it can be enforced, and "
  "counsel says it has to go to the general fund rather than to a conservation purpose. Also "
  "heard: 18 Hoicks Hollow Road, 16 Walsh Street and 2 East Lincoln Avenue, and 72 and 74 "
  "Washington Street.",
 "SqYuPenhqUI":
  "With the chair joining by Zoom, the vice chair presides. The main hearing is 16 Hoicks Hollow "
  "Road, a coastal stabilization project on the east shore: solid core coir rolls in netting, "
  "anchored and cabled into the bank, with sand-filled coir logs set deeper in the beach at the "
  "toe because the beach there sits higher than at other sites, and sand added each year to keep "
  "the array covered. The chair asks whether two techniques that would not be engineering "
  "structures separately become one when combined, as the commission has previously found for "
  "coir rolls used with sand drift fencing, and says the hearing should not close before that is "
  "settled. The waiver sought is also questioned, because it rests on long-term net benefit "
  "rather than on there being no reasonable alternative, and the applicant&rsquo;s "
  "representative has proposed alternatives for the same stretch of shoreline in other cases. "
  "The applicant takes the comments under advisement and the hearing is continued. The "
  "commission also takes up a filing for Coatue and an amended order for 16 Walsh Street and 2 "
  "East Lincoln Avenue.",
 "z6zxl4IHMKQ":
  "Most of the meeting is invasive species management, on Conservation Foundation land at 220 "
  "Milestone Road and on four Land Bank parcels at 50 through 56 Madaket Road. The Madaket site "
  "holds a stand of bamboo inside a rhizome barrier, known as the bamboo forest, which has begun "
  "to come up in the meadow to the south and around the edges of the site, along with Japanese "
  "knotweed. The plan is cutting, mowing and wetland-approved herbicide, in phases and beginning "
  "this fall while the new growth is still small. A commissioner asks which bamboo it is and is "
  "told the species has not been identified. The commission then drafts its standard conditions "
  "for this kind of work on the screen: named herbicides applied by a licensed applicator, the "
  "quantity and type reported in writing each year, photographs before and after each treatment, "
  "proper disposal, and a new filing if less than half the area has returned to native species "
  "within two and a half years. Reports run until a certificate of compliance is issued or the "
  "permit expires, the second clause added because hundreds of orders never come back for a "
  "certificate. 18 Hoicks Hollow Road is continued while the state heritage program reviews it.",
 "oOV7fM9Lixw":
  "The deed restriction for 14 Plover Lane is signed on paper at the table, closing out the "
  "enforcement order and the notice of intent that followed it; the monitoring runs in "
  "perpetuity. The longest hearing is 5 and 7 Arbour Way, a developed lot permitted in the early "
  "2000s, where the owners want to repair the swimming pool, reconfigure the patio, take out "
  "invasives and dead trees, turn lawn into meadow and put a small addition on the house. Three "
  "waivers are sought: work within two feet of the water table, structures within fifty feet of "
  "the coastal bank, and a small amount of work inside the twenty-five-foot vegetated buffer. "
  "Repairing the pool means temporary dewatering to a storm drain in the street, under a "
  "separate permit and outside the growing season. 16 Hoicks Hollow Road is continued to October "
  "2nd, with a commissioner asking for a better set of drawings. Certificates of compliance "
  "close a 2019 garage permit at 30 Eel Point Road and a much older order at 3 Sherburne Way, "
  "where the fire department burned down a beach shack in the dune. Several carry conditions "
  "that survive the permit, on the undisturbed buffer, on pesticides and fertilizer, and on "
  "exterior lighting.",
 "jem-7fLTB2E":
  "A new house at 92 Washington Street takes the longest hearing. The site lies within land "
  "subject to coastal storm flowage, so the fifty-foot structure-free buffer cannot be met. The "
  "representative argues that the plan satisfies the local performance standards all the same: "
  "living space raised above base flood elevation plus two feet on a pile foundation, the garage "
  "on a slab with its second floor above the flood zone, and a new driveway of cobbles and "
  "crushed shell. The commission is told the design would not stand in the way of the town "
  "raising Washington Street Extension by about three feet, which the coastal resiliency plan "
  "contemplates. Other items include an amended order at 3 Pops Lane and two sets of minutes, "
  "one of which recorded a commissioner as voting while not sitting. The meeting closes on the "
  "previous meeting&rsquo;s disagreement between the commission and the natural resources "
  "department. The chair reminds commissioners that neither reports to the other, that staff and "
  "town counsel serve in an advisory role, that there is no call to threaten corrective action, "
  "and that a commissioner acting within the regulations and the oath of office should not be "
  "talked out of doing so.",
 "rpCowd7NqLw":
  "The commission opens the applications for 86 and 88 Pocomo Road, heard together: identical "
  "coir roll arrays reaching twelve to fifteen feet above the beach, anchored, covered with sand "
  "and planted with beach grass, the sand delivered by conveyor over the bank. The chair opens "
  "them because each property already has a wooden sand drift fence, and the commission has long "
  "treated that combination as a coastal engineering structure; either the fence comes out, the "
  "chair says, or the project is waived as one. The designs copy a neighbor&rsquo;s permit from "
  "earlier in the year, and the commission remarks on how varied the installations in Pocomo "
  "have become. The rest turns on 10 and 16 Ocean Avenue, with one commissioner recused. The "
  "unpermitted structures that brought the enforcement order, among them decking, storage and "
  "lighting near the pool, are still there, and the filing meant to resolve them has been "
  "continued for months. An applicant may continue indefinitely, the chair observes, but a "
  "violation may not, and lists what else is available: amending the order, issuing a new one, "
  "financial penalties, criminal proceedings, the state strike team. Staff are asked to look "
  "into the options.",
 "T-rk_xBSSTA":
  "Two halves. The first returns to 92 Washington Street, where the owner describes designing "
  "the house around two daughters with autism and the caregivers the family needs. The chair "
  "thanks the owner but says those considerations fall outside the commission&rsquo;s "
  "jurisdiction, which reaches only wetland resources. The architect then shows the existing "
  "plan, with the fifty-foot setback running through the middle of a house that has flooded "
  "repeatedly, and the proposal, which moves the house toward the street, lifts it on piers and "
  "gives the ground level breakaway walls so that flood water can pass through. The second half "
  "is the regulations. The commission tries to draw a line inside the definition of a structure: "
  "coir terraces, straw wattles and sand are natural materials that people have assembled, while "
  "steel, rock, brick and concrete are not, and a definition wide enough to catch everything "
  "would also catch landscape fabric, netting and trash hutches. Wood sits awkwardly in between. "
  "The companion problem is what fixed location means, with seasonal snow fencing on one side "
  "and something standing year round on the other, and permanent perhaps meaning for the life of "
  "the permit. Asked where another town has settled this, the chair answers that none has.",
 "i9fL7_XahJM":
  "The grandfathering question at 10 and 16 Ocean Avenue turns practical: if dining on the dune "
  "predates the act, how much of it is grandfathered? The applicant&rsquo;s attorney says the "
  "affidavits are the best evidence there will ever be, since a receipt for a table does not "
  "record which part of a property it stands on, and that the aerial photographs show the area "
  "shrinking as vegetation encroaches rather than growing. A plan drawn a few years ago for the "
  "restaurant&rsquo;s liquor license shows ten six-seat tables, and the owner confirms a "
  "seasonal license running from about June 15th to mid-September, with the furniture carried in "
  "after each service. The commission asks for that plan and the season dates so that any "
  "grandfathered use can be fixed at the 2023 photograph or less. A land and water council "
  "speaker asks whether the number has changed over the years, and is told no record exists. An "
  "attorney for 86 and 88 Pocomo Road argues that the commission answered the same question two "
  "doors away a few months ago and should follow its own precedent. Also heard: an in-kind "
  "rebuild of a barn predating 1929 at 38 Wauwinet Road, on a new concrete slab.",
 "Wze3tx0dDjc":
  "The commission writes the order for 10 and 16 Ocean Avenue on the screen. The dining area is "
  "to be clearly demarcated and held to eight tables and forty-eight chairs, and monitoring "
  "reports with photographs of the dune are due a month before the season opens and a month "
  "after it closes, so that beach grass can be replanted on a set timeline if the photographs "
  "show it receding. Asked what follows if a report never arrives, the chair describes the "
  "sequence: a letter, then an enforcement order and a cease and desist that would stop the "
  "season, and no certificate of compliance at the end. The hearing on 92 Washington Street "
  "continues, with questions about how roof and surface water will be collected and where it "
  "goes. The last discussion is the geotubes at 59-83 Baxter Road. Photographs sent in by "
  "residents show two or three of the four layers exposed, which leaves the system behaving as a "
  "hard sea wall, and with the order expired and under appeal the commission weighs a new or "
  "amended enforcement order for the sole purpose of compelling maintenance. Staff will raise it "
  "with the applicant before it is posted as an agenda item.",
 "YZfkTN0yXvI":
  "The main hearing is 54 Washington Street, where a house that predates the wetlands act would "
  "be lifted about five feet and given flood vents so that flood water can pass beneath it, "
  "jacked up on cribbing and lowered back onto its foundation with the work staged from the "
  "paved street. A member of the public asks whether the project has been coordinated with the "
  "resiliency corridor the town and the Land Bank are pursuing along Washington Street. The "
  "representative answers that both the owner and the engineer have met on site with the natural "
  "resources department, and that the discussion there was about a berm rather than about this "
  "house. A commissioner asks whether the coastal resilience advisory committee should comment, "
  "and the commission votes to refer it, since the regulations that took effect in January "
  "require projects in land subject to coastal storm flowage to meet the spirit of the Nantucket "
  "coastal resiliency plan. The representative asks how a referral sits with an "
  "applicant&rsquo;s right to a hearing and how long it will take. Later a commissioner notes "
  "that this is only the second such referral and that it caught the representative off guard, "
  "and asks how applicants might be told to expect it.",
 "9oZhRY6i0uE":
  "Both long items are the Sconset bluff. The first is the notice of intent for 59-83 Baxter "
  "Road, an after-the-fact filing required by an emergency certification. A speaker submits "
  "sixty-eight pages asking that any order require full compliance as a coastal engineering "
  "structure, a walkable public beach, construction standards, monitoring, accountability across "
  "many owners for a single applicant, prompt cleanup and the consent of the affected owners, "
  "and asks for a benefit to the public in return for building on public beach, pointing to two "
  "unpermitted staircases crossing a resource area. The chair answers that a project on a public "
  "beach also needs a town meeting vote under the town code, which is the select board&rsquo;s "
  "business and not the commission&rsquo;s, and that the staircases belong in enforcement rather "
  "than in this permit. The second is a partial certificate of compliance for 59-77 Baxter Road, "
  "closing a twenty-year-old expired order on the parcels it no longer touches while keeping "
  "enforceable conditions where the coir terraces remain, as a bridge until the new permit "
  "clears its appeal and a town meeting vote. The chair would rather use enforcement orders and "
  "emergency certifications, and says so while agreeing the aim is sound.",
 "K9GUuRGQED4":
  "Three applications and an appeal. The board takes up 11 North Water Street first, where a "
  "guest house seeks a variance to raise ground cover to 49.6 percent against a 40 percent "
  "maximum, together with a special permit to extend the building. Abutters object to the bulk "
  "accumulating beside the commercial district and to the loss of three to five parking spaces "
  "on a street where there is none to spare; the owner answers that guests and staff rarely "
  "bring cars. Polled informally, two members say they are against, and the attorney withdraws "
  "the application without prejudice. The board then grants a special permit at 42 Shimmo Pond "
  "Road to demolish two accessory structures and move the historic dwelling further from the "
  "wetland, reducing ground cover from 4,101 to 4,065 square feet, on condition that nothing "
  "larger than a zoning shed follows and that the road is regraded afterward. The rest of the "
  "afternoon is an appeal over 59 South Shore Road, where neighbors ask the board to overturn "
  "the Building Commissioner and stop the property being used as an event space. Attorneys argue "
  "standing and whether the bylaw defines an event at all, and the hearing is continued to "
  "February for want of time.",
 "qERJmoHmMCg":
  "The first of six special meetings on the Surfside Crossing comprehensive permit: 156 "
  "condominium units in eighteen buildings on about 13.6 acres at 3, 5, 7 and 9 South Shore "
  "Road, before the board on remand with instructions to measure the modified plan against the "
  "one originally filed. Department heads go first. The fire chief wants access roads twenty "
  "feet wide and unobstructed, hydrants within 500 feet, and a second entrance well away from "
  "the first; the plan has one entrance off South Shore Road and an emergency access close "
  "beside it. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney answers that nothing further apart is possible on a "
  "parcel ringed by private land, that the design meets the fire code, and that the modified "
  "plan is the safer of the two, since the sixty single-family houses originally proposed had no "
  "sprinklers. The chief agrees that sprinklering every unit is an improvement, but raises water "
  "pressure if wells are out of service. The board later hears that the town&rsquo;s stormwater "
  "reviewer has not seen the plan, and the Land and Water Council argues it meets neither the "
  "no-degradation standard nor ninety percent recharge. Continued to January 24th.",
 "He3F_E9Onac":
  "The opponents answer the applicant. Counsel for Nantucket Tipping Point asks the board to "
  "deny the Surfside Crossing comprehensive permit on the stormwater standards, on fire safety, "
  "and on whether there is water enough to fight a fire at the site. A resident explains why the "
  "water department has taken wells out of service, drawing harder on the upper aquifer pulls "
  "the contamination plume toward the deeper wells, and a board member turns the fire question "
  "around to evacuation: eighteen residential buildings and a clubhouse, one way in, and an "
  "emergency lane fifteen feet wide for more than three hundred people. Opponents return "
  "repeatedly to the clear-cutting of the site, which they say makes the applicant&rsquo;s "
  "before-and-after stormwater comparison worthless and leaves the board free to review the "
  "drainage afresh. On architecture the board asks about the space between window heads and "
  "roof, whether the massing can be broken up, and how the buildings address the street. Town "
  "counsel advises against narrowing the scope of review, which is broad and carries a "
  "catch-all, and sets out the three outcomes: deny, approve as filed, or approve with "
  "conditions.",
 "Thu6CKNKabs":
  "Access, neighborhood composition and recreation. A board member asks the applicant to study a "
  "second access road even if it costs a building or two, saying that getting in and out of a "
  "development this size matters more. The developer sets out the amenity package and where it "
  "came from: an all-condominium plan aimed at 75 percent year-round occupancy, a clubhouse "
  "designed to read from outside as two buildings, two community rooms, one always open and the "
  "other bookable through a full-time property manager, a gym for classes, a game room and "
  "bowling alley for the winter, offices and study rooms upstairs, and a basketball court, pool, "
  "playground and park outside, the court prompted years earlier by a police chief who pointed "
  "out that children in a nearby development play in the street for want of anywhere else. Board "
  "members ask whether residents will pay to use any of it; that is left to the condominium "
  "documents. A consultant then corrects a statement from the last meeting that runoff from a "
  "fire would be cleaner than the municipal supply, and the opposing attorneys close by asking "
  "for outright denial rather than conditions, which the board tried once already.",
 "TxkG96MG_RA":
  "Waivers, parking and the calendar. The board works through the parking count, 283 spaces at "
  "one per bedroom plus ten for the community building against 299 shown, eight of them the Land "
  "Bank&rsquo;s, so 291 would be built, and through the bylaw&rsquo;s screening requirements for "
  "lots of twenty spaces or more. It votes to ask Bristol Engineers for an updated report. On "
  "waivers, town counsel explains that refusing one can force the whole plan to be reconfigured, "
  "that some can be written conditionally, and that the board cannot waive what the Historic "
  "District Commission has no power to waive itself. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney says there "
  "is no point in further iterative changes to the design without some assurance of where they "
  "lead, and expects either a denial or an approval so laden with conditions that it returns to "
  "the Housing Appeals Committee. The hearing has to close by February 21st and the board then "
  "has forty days to decide, but it will lack a quorum for much of March, so it asks town "
  "counsel to seek more time, noting that a motion the developer joins would fare better than "
  "one it opposes.",
 "vP5amdonI4k":
  "Town counsel opens with the extension: the Housing Appeals Committee granted it only in part, "
  "moving the deadline to close the public hearing to March 4th. The town has authorized Bristol "
  "Engineers, which wrote the 2018 hydrogeology report, to update it, though counsel warns the "
  "work may not be finished before the hearing closes, in which case the board cannot weigh it. "
  "Most of the afternoon is the opponents&rsquo; closing. Their attorney numbers the grounds for "
  "denial: the stormwater standards, protection of drinking water, no real second entrance "
  "beyond a reinforced turf emergency lane, too little water to fight a fire at the site, "
  "roughly a thousand new vehicle trips, the well setbacks, and the Nantucket Historic Districts "
  "Act. A physician describes treating firefighters at the Veranda House fire, exhausted and "
  "short of breath after five hours. Another speaker calls six and a half acres of new "
  "impervious surface in the wellhead recharge district unprecedented on the island outside the "
  "airport. The applicant&rsquo;s attorney answers that most of what the board is weighing falls "
  "outside the remand. Continued to March 4th.",
 "aRGeN03J-v4":
  "The public hearing closes. First the board decides what to do about the Bristol Engineers "
  "update, which the developer has declined to fund: the select board votes on paying for it the "
  "next day, and the results would not arrive before the end of March, too late for "
  "deliberations. The board keeps the request alive anyway, and town counsel suggests a motion "
  "to reopen the hearing for the limited purpose of taking the report in. One member objects "
  "that the town&rsquo;s own comprehensive permit rules put peer review fees on the applicant "
  "and that the board should not be departing from them; counsel agrees in principle but calls "
  "denial over a refusal to fund one report a risky position to defend. The fire chief has "
  "offered to send material for review and the board votes to accept it. The opponents add a "
  "tenth ground to the nine they filed in February, and the applicant&rsquo;s attorney raises a "
  "safe harbor argument that counsel does not think would support a denial. The hearing is "
  "closed and deliberations are set for March 19th.",
 "iO1k7haYW9M":
  "The appeal brought by the South Siasconset Neighborhood Association over 1 Magnolia Avenue "
  "and the Ocean, Grand and Cottage Avenue properties is continued three months. Counsel on both "
  "sides explain that the owner will voluntarily apply to the Planning Board for a major "
  "commercial development permit, with neither party conceding whether one is required, and that "
  "the conditions being negotiated there should settle the neighbors&rsquo; concerns and allow "
  "the appeal to be withdrawn. The board then finishes the 59 South Shore Road appeal. After the "
  "owners describe how they use the house, and the board discusses a video that presents it as a "
  "wedding venue, the motion to overturn the Building Commissioner and order the use stopped "
  "fails three votes to two: four were needed, so the determination stands. A member voting "
  "against warns that reading accessory use more narrowly than the board has for short-term "
  "rentals would expose houses across the island. A special permit is granted at 9 Towaddy Lane "
  "to lift a pre-existing dwelling, square it to the road and set it on a foundation, which "
  "widens one side yard. 32 Monomoy Road and 14 Tautemo Way are continued to March 13th.",
 "hIWRFwqqP7I":
  "Two pools and a long appeal. The board grants a special permit at 4 Macy Road, then another "
  "at 14 Tautemo Way for a twelve by twenty-two foot plunge pool only a few feet deep, since the "
  "Conservation Commission will not allow it within two feet of high groundwater; because a "
  "fence cannot stand inside the hundred-foot buffer, safety rests on an automatic cover, and "
  "the applicant is to file the Historic District Commission approval with staff. 43 Union "
  "Street is continued to April. The rest of the afternoon is the appeal over 32 Monomoy Road, "
  "where neighbors ask the board to overturn the Building Commissioner&rsquo;s December 2024 "
  "refusal to enforce against short-term renting. Counsel for the owner says the house is no "
  "longer rented or advertised, which makes the question moot; others want the board to settle "
  "whether short-term renting is a customary accessory use at all, and members say they are "
  "reluctant to keep deciding that one house at a time. The motion to overturn fails three to "
  "two, four votes being required, and the board then votes to record mootness as its ground and "
  "to leave accessory use undecided.",
 "6o4Si1JrgmE":
  "Deliberation on Surfside Crossing, with a decision due by April 13th. Members say at once "
  "where they stand: no path to agreement with the applicant, approval with conditions a waste "
  "of everyone&rsquo;s time, denial the only outcome they can see. Town counsel sets out what "
  "that means. The board would be denying a change to a project it once approved with "
  "conditions, and denying the change amounts to denying the project; if the Housing Appeals "
  "Committee upholds it there is no permit at all, not even the earlier sixty-unit one, and the "
  "applicant cannot build as of right in the meantime. After the committee the route is judicial "
  "review. The board turns to what the written decision should say, and a member cautions that "
  "whatever else may be true of the application, the grounds have to rest on standards the board "
  "is entitled to apply, the stormwater standards above all. Counsel is asked to begin drafting "
  "a denial, and the board sets aside two further sessions to work through it, with an executive "
  "session on what the document should contain. Continued to April 2nd.",
 "5bpZRC6CbpI":
  "Four and a half minutes. The only business is the Surfside Crossing decision, and there is "
  "nothing yet to decide: town counsel expects the draft by Friday at noon, the board will see "
  "it first under attorney-client privilege, and it will go into the packet for the April 10th "
  "meeting. The board continues the matter to April 10th, then adjourns into executive session, "
  "not to return to open session, to discuss litigation strategy on the appeal concerning 32 "
  "Monomoy Road now before the Land Court.",
 "nThv1kYJiuw":
  "Two hearings and the Surfside Crossing decision. At 43 Union Street the question is an elm on "
  "the adjoining lot. The builder describes how an upward addition would be managed around it, "
  "with construction fencing, materials fetched daily and no dumpster, but the chair of the tree "
  "advisory committee points out that removing the side steps, and the parking space the new "
  "layout would allow, puts weight over a surface-rooted tree whose canopy reaches the roof. "
  "Told plainly how the board would vote today, the applicant asks to continue to May 8th, and "
  "is asked for drainage figures that do not assume the steps are moved. At 46 West Miacomet "
  "Road the board hears a plan to move an existing house about 175 feet north across the road, "
  "within the Moorlands Management District, over which town meeting transferred jurisdiction to "
  "this board last year; it wants the abandoned side revegetated in native species, a smaller "
  "driveway and the shed relocated, and continues that too. The afternoon ends with town counsel "
  "on the draft denial, which should rest on the grounds that actually compelled it, since the "
  "applicant intends to go straight to the Housing Appeals Committee.",
 "5V-9BvAssdU":
  "The board denies the Surfside Crossing comprehensive permit, five votes to none. The hour "
  "before the vote goes to the written decision: which grounds belong in it, how the need for "
  "affordable housing is weighed against them, and what will stand up when the applicant takes "
  "the matter to the Housing Appeals Committee. Members agree the document should address "
  "housing need squarely rather than in passing, and conclude that the need is real but that "
  "this is not the right project for this site. The Veranda House fire stays in, the risk having "
  "been put before the board during the hearing, while the safe harbor argument raised in March "
  "is left out as a ground counsel does not think viable. The motion is to deny the project as "
  "proposed in the notice of project change and to authorize the chair to sign and issue the "
  "written decision on the board&rsquo;s behalf. The roll call is unanimous, closing a hearing "
  "that has occupied the board since January.",
 "TzMQpwC4Sis":
  "Two Moorlands Management District applications are withdrawn without prejudice, at 46 West "
  "Miacomet Road and 10 Smooth Hummocks Road, after the Natural Heritage program identifies "
  "three protected plant species whose life cycle puts the required survey in late summer; the "
  "board waives the fee for both to reapply, and staff call that the more neighborly course, "
  "since abutters are notified afresh. 40 Low Beach Road and 8 Sandwich Road are continued to "
  "June 12th. The upward addition at 43 Union Street is approved four votes to one, with "
  "conditions built around the town tree at the lot line: construction fencing around it, "
  "methods worked out with the tree warden and the tree committee, no dumpster, no outdoor "
  "storage of materials or debris, no exterior work between Memorial Day and Labor Day, and a "
  "project manager abutters can telephone. The South Siasconset Neighborhood Association appeal "
  "over the Summer House properties, filed in April 2024 and dormant since, is denied after no "
  "one appears for it again. At 6 Marcus Way a motion to lift a 1,816-square-foot ground cover "
  "cap by 149 feet for a garage fails two votes to three, members saying the lot&rsquo;s "
  "variance history has already granted it several bites at the apple.",
 "9fOIfOYfJGg":
  "Four items in under fifty minutes. 40 Low Beach Road is continued to July 10th and the 16 "
  "Codfish Park Road application is withdrawn without prejudice, both at the applicants&rsquo; "
  "request. The board then grants a special permit for a 450-square-foot pool and 36-square-foot "
  "spa at 8 Sandwich Road, in the Village Residential district, where the lot runs 32,720 square "
  "feet against a 20,000-square-foot minimum and the pool and its equipment sit more than twenty "
  "feet from every lot line. A neighbor questions whether a lot of that history should qualify; "
  "staff answer that town meeting rezoned the area by vote and that the bylaw allows a pool "
  "there on a special permit. Conditions put the pool equipment in the crawl space of the pool "
  "house beyond the setback, attach the stamped Historic District Commission plan showing "
  "landscaping, and bar chemical treatment between November 1st and May 1st. At 4 Spruce Street "
  "the board approves a second story on a dwelling 2.2 feet from the side line, built straight "
  "up with no new foundation to make room for the owners&rsquo; son and four teenage children. "
  "Members note that adding height adds density, which they may weigh as a detriment even though "
  "they cannot regulate interiors.",
 "wyHC1TfCR_o":
  "Six minutes in open session, with the substance reserved for behind closed doors. The board "
  "approves the February 19th and March 4th minutes, takes no public comment, and continues the "
  "40 Low Beach Road groundcover variance to August 14th at the applicant&rsquo;s request. The "
  "election of officers listed on the agenda, for chair, vice chair and secretary, is put off to "
  "August 14th as well. The board then votes to adjourn, not to return to open session, and "
  "moves into executive session to discuss litigation strategy on two matters: Ward v. Town of "
  "Nantucket, in the Land Court, and Surfside Crossing, LLC v. Nantucket Zoning Board of Appeals "
  "before the Housing Appeals Committee, the appeal that followed the board&rsquo;s April denial.",
 "tcmZJl89rsI":
  "The longest matter of the day ends without a decision. The variance sought at 40 Low Beach "
  "Road would count the ground cover of three lots on the one that holds the house, raising it "
  "to about 4,500 square feet, with the two seaward lots and the paper roads the town took "
  "between them passing to the town. Counsel for the applicant argues soil, shape and topography "
  "as the grounds and presents the exchange as completing the town&rsquo;s open land from the "
  "bluff to the water; abutters answer that it concentrates development on a stretch of road "
  "that has none. Reading the board, counsel asks to withdraw without prejudice rather than be "
  "denied, and the board allows it. 9 Hinsdale Road is continued to October 16th. The variance "
  "at 6 Sheep Pond Road, to move a dwelling back from an eroding shoreline, is granted with two "
  "conforming parking spaces added to the plan. At 72 Baxter Road members say they will not undo "
  "an earlier condition barring further structural improvement, and the applicant takes a "
  "continuance. Staff propose raising the application fee from $450 to $750 and bringing it to a "
  "public hearing in November. The chair, vice chair and secretary are each re-elected.",
 "Imk3sTEPY0g":
  "A meeting the chair calls a record: well under ten minutes from call to adjournment. The June "
  "12th and September 11th minutes are approved without correction. The appeal at 9 Hinsdale "
  "Road, where the Building Commissioner&rsquo;s July order requires storage containers removed, "
  "is continued to November 13th: the owner is working toward compliance, the Building "
  "Commissioner raises no objection, and the November 21st action deadline still allows it. The "
  "request to withdraw the 72 Baxter Road application without prejudice is granted, one member "
  "recusing. Staff then report on the fee schedule raised last month, noting that the "
  "application fee was last changed more than a decade ago and that any increase has to be "
  "advertised, so a public hearing will be held in November.",
 "z6YEZCQmWz8":
  "A special permit passes twice. At 54R Union Street the applicant asks to add a second floor "
  "and a deck to a pre-existing garage and turn it into a garage apartment, a conforming use in "
  "the Commercial Neighborhood district, on a lot of about 2,611 square feet where 7,500 are "
  "required and with the flood plain ruling out ground-floor living space. Neighbors divide over "
  "density and traffic; the first vote is three to two, and when the board recalls that a "
  "special permit needs four, it votes to reconsider and the motion carries four to one. 9 "
  "Hinsdale Road and 25 Monohansett Road are continued to December 11th, and 18 Madequecham "
  "Valley Road is continued as well. The raised roof at 72 Baxter Road is approved four to one, "
  "with no exterior construction between Memorial Day and Labor Day. A pool is permitted at 6 "
  "Tautemo Way, prompting a question whether such applications might go to the zoning "
  "administrator instead, which would take a bylaw amendment. After a public hearing the board "
  "raises the application fee from $450 to $1,000, and the zoning administrator fee from $300 to "
  "$750, with abutter mailing billed separately.",
 "uKdRx5f8E2w":
  "Most of the agenda clears in twelve minutes. The storage containers at 9 Hinsdale Road have "
  "been craned onto registered trailers, but the Building Commissioner does not treat a trailer "
  "as a motor vehicle, so the owner will seek a planning board special permit for storage and "
  "warehousing instead, and the appeal is continued to February 12th on a signed extension. 25 "
  "Monohansett Road and 7 Pleasant Street are withdrawn without prejudice; 2 Green Lane and 18 "
  "Madequecham Valley Road are continued to January 8th. The hour then goes to 46 West Miacomet "
  "Road, back before the board with the Natural Heritage approval it withdrew to obtain in May. "
  "The plan moves the house north across the road away from the eroding shore, drops a viewing "
  "deck to cut disturbance, revegetates the old footprint, lawn and driveway, and leaves close "
  "to an acre of the north lot untouched. Members press for a smaller driveway, parking and "
  "walkway so that the new disturbed area comes nearer the old, and for the Historic District "
  "Commission approval to go in the record to show grade. Continued to January. The 2026 meeting "
  "schedule is approved.",
}


