# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# NANTUCKET BIODIVERSITY INITIATIVE -- the second Initiative Fund to move off
# the Community Foundation's page. Two videos, both NCTV recordings of NBI's
# biennial research conference, so one group and no year buttons. Running
# orders, affiliations and the spelling of every name come from NBI's own
# conference programs under nantucketbiodiversity.org/past-research-conferences,
# cross-checked against the recordings; where a paper had several authors the
# person who gave the talk is the one named.

NBI_MISSION = (
    '<span class="dl-mission__lead">About the Nantucket Biodiversity Initiative:</span>'
    "<br><br>The mission of the Nantucket Biodiversity Initiative (NBI) is to support and convey "
    "a deeper understanding of the biodiversity of Nantucket, Tuckernuck, Muskeget, and their "
    "associated waters by fostering collaboration, funding and facilitating research, and "
    "sharing scientific knowledge.")

NBI_CONFERENCES = [
 ("6gukKq7K7bc", "Nantucket Biodiversity Research Conference 2023",
   "The tenth biennial conference, a full day on 3 November 2023, opened by NBI chair Seth "
   "Engelbourg and run in four sessions with poster sessions in the breaks. Marine biodiversity "
   "first: Andrew Davinack of Wheaton College on DNA barcoding of invertebrates taken from "
   "island marinas and docks, where close to half the species he could barcode turned out to be "
   "introduced and a large share were cryptogenic, native or not by no clear evidence; Ryan "
   "Munnelly of the Coonamessett Farm Foundation on ancient peat deposits out on the continental "
   "shelf and whether they feed fisheries production, with two NBI-funded multibeam surveys "
   "either side of a storm season; and Cooper Kimball-Rhines of UMass Boston on sequencing the "
   "genetics of pickleweed for marsh conservation. Habitat management follows: Sarah Bois of the "
   "Linda Loring Nature Foundation on managing coastal sandplain grasslands as regional "
   "biodiversity hotspots; Karen Beattie of the Nantucket Conservation Foundation on designing "
   "the Windswept bog wetland restoration around its ecological data; Ian Ives of Mass Audubon "
   "on twelve years of rebuilding habitat for the eastern spadefoot toad; and Danielle "
   "O&rsquo;Dell on the southern pine beetle outbreak and what it taught about forest "
   "management. Then plants: Patrick Farrar of the Woodwell Climate Research Center on two "
   "decades of change in Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard plant communities, Alice Palmer on pickleweed "
   "and its microbiome in a city marsh against a country one, and Yingying Xie of Northern "
   "Kentucky University on how flowering times shape plant invasion here. Animals close the day: "
   "Richard Veit on vagrancy and dispersal in lesser black-backed gulls, Stephen St. Pierre of "
   "the Marine Mammal Alliance Nantucket on three stranded animals and what their necropsies "
   "showed, Caroline Collatos of the New England Aquarium on sandbar sharks in Nantucket Sound, "
   "and Allison Snow on tick surveys across Nantucket, Tuckernuck and Chappaquiddick."),
 ("3STnT-Dx4mY", "Nantucket Biodiversity Research Conference 2021",
   "Fourteen researchers over a full day, 5 November 2021, at the ninth biennial conference "
   "&mdash; the hybrid year, with some speakers in the room and some on screen, opened by NBI "
   "chair Jen Karberg and closed by Karen Beattie. The morning starts in the water and the "
   "marsh: Alyssa Novak of Boston University on an eelgrass health assessment and restoration in "
   "island waters, and Russ Hopping of the Trustees of Reservations on nature-based salt marsh "
   "work &mdash; filling ditches, cutting micro-runnels &mdash; meant to let a marsh keep pace "
   "with sea level rise. Then the land: Anastasia Pulak of the Woodwell Climate Research Center "
   "on what grows back on cranberry farms retired from agriculture, and Sarah Bois of the Linda "
   "Loring Nature Foundation on naturalized Scotch broom and the point on the invasion curve "
   "where a species is still worth catching. The second session is small animals: Greg Whitmore "
   "on the macroinvertebrate fauna of the island&rsquo;s perennial and ephemeral streams, a "
   "decade of collecting in which beetles made up half the species and amphipods turned up at "
   "only one site; Allison Snow on the prevalence of ticks and tick-borne disease agents at "
   "sentinel sites on Nantucket and Tuckernuck; and Andrew David of Clarkson University on "
   "genetic barcoding that found the parasitic worm Polydora neocaeca in wild bay scallops here. "
   "After lunch, Kate Ruskin on monitoring tidal marsh birds, Danielle O&rsquo;Dell on northern "
   "long-eared bats and white-nose syndrome and why coastal islands matter to them, and Amanda "
   "Tokash-Peters on microbial diversity in the gray seals at Great Point. The last session "
   "turns to the coast: Cynthia Dittbrenner and Brittany Hoffnagle on projected climate impacts "
   "to beaches and salt marshes across Nantucket, Martha&rsquo;s Vineyard and the Elizabeth "
   "Islands, Ben Bray of MIT Sea Grant on faster workflows for water quality and habitat data, "
   "and Shea Fee on extending the life of barrier beaches with nature-based solutions."),
]

