# NANTUCKET POND COALITION -- the third Initiative Fund to move off the
# Community Foundation's page. Eleven videos in three groups, all of them
# NCTV's own work; the five on Vimeo predate the YouTube channel and carry
# local thumbnails because Vimeo has no predictable thumbnail URL.
#
# Titles follow the Coalition's own video archive at
# nantucketpondcoalition.com/videos, which names these better than the
# upload titles do -- the Vimeo files are still named for the edit bay
# ("NPC_HummockPond_PhragmitesEradication_v3"), and "NPC" is expanded
# since the page is already headed by the Coalition's mark.
#
# Two of the thirteen videos Frank supplied are retired as duplicates, the
# way the 2018 Jim Lentowski upload is on the Conservation Foundation page:
#   3MlsoFcREiY "Update on the Land Bank and Pond Coalition's Work" is the
#     same film as by2r_di2zcc, word for word but for one segment either
#     way; the cut carried here is the Coalition's own and the later of the
#     two, and closes with the season's harvest totals rather than with the
#     hope of them.
#   d1pXwnMSkac "Phragmite Pilot Program 2015" is the first half of the
#     Vimeo cut carried here, which runs on to the October return visit.
#
# Names come from the videos' own lower thirds and credit cards, not from
# the caption tracks: Keith Gazaille, Rick Atherton, Phil Albertson and the
# rest are spelled as the titles spell them. Speakers the captions name but
# the picture does not are given by role instead -- the Land Bank chairman,
# the Land Council's executive director -- for the reason recorded against
# AGENDA_2026_09_15 above: captions get island surnames wrong. The two
# exceptions are Rita Higgins and John Johnson, from the transcript Frank
# supplied for the harvester celebration; the people given there by first
# name only keep their roles.

NPC_MISSION = (
    '<span class="dl-mission__lead">About the Nantucket Pond Coalition:</span>'
    "<br><br>We have created a charitable fund with the Community Foundation for Nantucket to "
    "receive donations for this initiative dedicated to restoring and protecting the ponds on "
    "Nantucket so they can be enjoyed by generations to come. We support finding scientific "
    "solutions to mitigate the accelerated eutrophication of the ponds due to excess nitrogen "
    "and phosphorus from fertilizer, septic systems and household products."
    "<br><br>Increased population on Nantucket has created poor water quality conditions such as "
    "harmful algae, excessive nuisance aquatic vegetation and low dissolved oxygen. Invasive "
    "plant species, like phragmites, create long term challenges for a natural pond habitat. We "
    "propose to balance the impact of human activity with the protection of Nantucket&rsquo;s "
    "pond and estuary resources through planning, public involvement, education, research and "
    "sound resource management."
    "<br><br>We work with all the not-for-profit organizations and the Town to continually move "
    "forward with action plans to improve the pond experience on Nantucket.")

NPC_ABOUT = [
 ("tyhl4IWrSXM", "Pond Coalition &amp; Egan Maritime Partnership",
   "Made with the Egan Maritime Institute for an oceanography unit in the island&rsquo;s middle "
   "school, and built around the question the students were set to debate: should Nantucket "
   "work to restore its ponds and wetlands to their original state. It opens on the geology "
   "&mdash; the eighty-odd named ponds were cut by the Laurentide glacier twelve thousand years "
   "ago and took their present shape about six thousand years ago, when the rising sea made "
   "Cape Cod and the islands &mdash; and on why restoration cannot wait: explosive population "
   "growth has pushed eutrophication forward by decades, and there is enough phosphorus and "
   "nitrogen on the pond bottoms now that sewering and fertilizer management alone will not "
   "undo it. The team then introduce themselves and their part in the work. The teacher and "
   "Egan Maritime&rsquo;s education manager explain why water quality is the students&rsquo; "
   "problem to inherit. The Land Bank&rsquo;s environmental and agricultural coordinator "
   "describes the Consue Springs marsh restoration, where phragmites more than fourteen feet "
   "tall will be sprayed from an amphibious Marsh Master and the dead stems cut each November "
   "for three to five years. The Land Council&rsquo;s executive director covers ten years of "
   "sampling the smaller ponds for harmful algal blooms and the phosphorus that feeds them. The "
   "town&rsquo;s water resource specialist covers the monitoring of streams, harbors and great "
   "ponds. Bob Williams closes on the heavy equipment the Coalition brings in: the aquatic "
   "harvester that took more than thirty thousand pounds of vegetation out of Hummock Pond, and "
   "the Marsh Master that treats and mulches the shorelines so a pond edge has a chance to "
   "heal."),
 ("by2r_di2zcc", "Pond Coalition &amp; Land Bank Partnership",
   "The Land Bank&rsquo;s chairman opens on six years of joint work on the great ponds, and Bob "
   "Williams takes it from there, at a celebration of the town&rsquo;s and the Land "
   "Bank&rsquo;s sponsorship of the aquatic harvester: a 4,500-pound paddle wheeler, twenty-six "
   "feet long, with a reciprocating blade that cuts below the surface and lifts about 130 cubic "
   "feet of vegetation a load &mdash; weed that would otherwise die and decompose on the "
   "bottom, drawing down oxygen, returning nutrients to the water and turning a pond slowly "
   "into a swamp. The Land Bank&rsquo;s director runs through the current projects: a third "
   "year of foliar spraying on Long Pond, with the hope of extending it to all the Land "
   "Bank&rsquo;s frontage there, and purple loosestrife and pioneer phragmites stands at "
   "Miacomet Pond. The season closes at eight acres harvested and seventy-seven cubic yards "
   "&mdash; over 22,000 pounds &mdash; of excess vegetation. Then the shorelines: the Long Pond "
   "pilot that tested the safety of foliar spray, the Marsh Master working north of the "
   "Massasoit Bridge, and the cut-and-mulch pass in late November once the plants have died "
   "back on their own. Before-and-after footage of the same stretch of Hummock Pond&rsquo;s "
   "southeastern shore, a year apart, shows a stand fourteen feet high and thirty feet deep "
   "give way to open shallows where fish, turtles, herons and cormorants feed. It ends on the "
   "2021 job at Consue Springs, where clearing the phragmites and knotweed brings the harbor "
   "back into view from Orange Street and the bike path."),
 ("https://vimeo.com/229182934", "Overview of the Nantucket Pond Coalition",
   "Narrated by Robert Williams and cut around his presentation to the Board of Selectmen on 19 "
   "July 2017. Between passages of the ponds themselves &mdash; shorebirds working a shallow "
   "edge, the cut at the beach where a pond is let out to the sea, aerials over the moors to the "
   "south shore &mdash; it steps through the Coalition&rsquo;s programs: the federal clean water "
   "grant, a working meeting around a kitchen table, sample jugs lined up on a porch, the "
   "aquatic harvester at work, and the September 2015 phragmites pilot, with Keith Gazaille "
   "spraying from a skiff and the Marsh Master out in the reeds. It closes on a Canada goose "
   "leading a line of goslings across the water, then the Coalition&rsquo;s mark and web "
   "address.",
   "npc-overview.webp"),
]

