NHA_WOMEN = [
 ("LuPkZ1Ov6WA", "Spirits Within Us: Extraordinary Women from 17th-19th Century Nantucket",
  "In celebration of the 2026 Women&rsquo;s History Month, the Nantucket Historical Association "
  "is releasing this video documenting the recently closed holographic experience, "
  "&ldquo;Spirits Within Us&rdquo;, at the Nantucket Whaling Museum (2021-2025). Life on remote, "
  "windswept Nantucket has always fostered extraordinary women. Intelligent, educated, "
  "passionate, and brave, they took on difficult issues involving anti-slavery, women&rsquo;s "
  "rights, suffrage, and times of war. Despite obstacles, they each assumed roles and attitudes "
  "that were not common for women of their time. The exhibit and now this video has brought just "
  "a few representatives of Nantucket&rsquo;s extraordinary women from the 17th to 19th century "
  "back to life to tell you in their own words about their own remarkable lives and the people "
  "they knew who inspired them. This production for the NHA was a collaboration of Novation "
  "Media, Theater Workshop of Nantucket, and Nantucket Community Television. Special thanks to "
  "the following whose writings and research inspired this production: Susan Boardman, Jascin "
  "Leonardo Finger, Elizabeth Gilbert, Peggi Godwin, Kate Hamilton, Michael Harrison, Karen "
  "MacNab, Nathaniel Philbrick, Linda Steelman, Betsy Tyler, Barbara Ann White, and the "
  "Manuscript Collections of the Nantucket Historical Association. Music by Sheldon Mirowitz "
  "0:00 Introduction 1:16 Wonoma 5:38 Mary Coffin Starbuck 10:37 Kezia Folger Coffin 17:25 Eliza "
  "Starbuck Barney 24:21 Susan Austin Veeder 29:54 Anna Gardner"),
 ("TgfJhcnBgd0", "A Walk with Nantucket&rsquo;s Notable Women",
  "At the Nantucket Whaling Museum, we&rsquo;ve always been intrigued with Nantucket&rsquo;s "
  "long and proud history of empowering women and we love to tell their stories. From the early "
  "settlers and their descendants to later arrivals from many diverse ethnic and racial "
  "backgrounds, Nantucket women have been remarkably industrious and independent. Some led "
  "social movements in abolition, suffrage and women&rsquo;s rights. Others were notable in "
  "science, medicine, and education reform. In this video, our museum guides tell the stories of "
  "some of the lesser known but very notable Nantucket women who ran businesses, raised large "
  "families, and helped build our diverse island community. Here we visit the places where they "
  "lived and worked. Today, you&rsquo;ll find that the tradition of extraordinary women "
  "continues. Women continue to inspire us in leadership positions as business owners, "
  "government officials, nonprofit directors, and in many other community endeavors. Come visit "
  "us at the Whaling Museum and our historic properties to hear our continuing stories about the "
  "notable women of Nantucket. https://nha.org/ Hosted by Peggi Godwin and Miranda Dale, Visitor "
  "Services, Nantucket Historical Association (NHA) Filmed and produced by Dan Driscoll, "
  "September Productions, for the NHA Made possible through a grant by the Massachusetts Office "
  "of Travel and Tourism"),
 ("7KqRYdWzfk0", "Extraordinary Nantucket Women, at the Nantucket Whaling Museum",
  "This 2018 performance at the Nantucket Whaling Museum highlights the lives of fifteen "
  "extraordinary women from the 17th through the 20th century on Nantucket. From its earliest "
  "days, dating back to the mid-1600s, Nantucketers have made their own way in the world. This "
  "is particularly true of the women who made their homes here. From the outset, men and women "
  "worked equally hard to make the fledgling settlement survive. When whaling became the "
  "dominant industry, whaling husbands would leave the island for years at a time, often, 3-5 "
  "years. At any given time, the women probably outnumbered the men four to one. Women had to "
  "fill the breach and did so in remarkable ways, raising their large families, managing "
  "households and running businesses in town, and found the time to become involved in the "
  "social movements of the day. William Oliver Stevens, an American author and professor, wrote, "
  "&ldquo;Indeed, it is probable that no other community in America of the size of Nantucket has "
  "ever given the country so many extraordinary women.&rdquo; The notable women include: Mary "
  "Coffin Starbuck (1645-1717) Abiah Folger Franklin (1667-1752) Kezia Folger Coffin (1772-1798) "
  "Lucretia Coffin Mott (1793-1880) Eliza Ann Chase McCleave (1811-1895) Susan Veeder "
  "(1816-1897) Maria Mitchell (1818-1889) Rebecca Ann Johnson (1829-??) Mary Ellen Pleasant (ca. "
  "1812-1904) Eliza Starbuck Barney (1802-1889) Anna Gardner (1816-1901) Lydia Folger Fowler, "
  "M.D. (1822-1879) Margaret Getchell (1841-1880) Hanna Monaghan (1889-1972) Mildred Jewitt "
  "&ldquo;Madaket Millie&rdquo; (1907-1990) Also featuring the &ldquo;Nantucket Girl&rsquo;s "
  "Song&rdquo; based on a witty poem found in the journal kept by Eliza Brock, wife of Peter C. "
  "Brock, master of the Nantucket ship Lexington on a whaling voyage from May 1853 to June 1856. "
  "It sums up how many women felt about their husbands being off on whaling voyages for years at "
  "a time. Songwriter Susan J. Berman set this poem to music and added a verse of her own."),
 ("GjGwlJWOYZ4", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Wonoma, Wampanoag Healer and Legendary Hero",
  "Wonoma (mid-1600s), the daughter of Wauwinet (and granddaughter of Nickanoose), was a young "
  "Wampanoag woman with knowledge of medicinal plants and their healing powers. Island legend, "
  "memorialized in 1876 in a poem by Nantucket poet Charlotte P. Baxter, tells of the maiden "
  "Wonoma, whose skills as a healer were known even among the western people, who lived beyond "
  "the tribal dividing line of Madequecham Valley. This is a love story that tells of "
  "Wonoma&rsquo;s heroism in preventing war between two Nantucket sachems. Produced for the "
  "Nantucket Historical Association for the 2010 exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes think of me: "
  "Notable Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; at the Nantucket Whaling Museum."),
 ("Axp1Uikk5jQ", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Phebe Folger Coleman, Artist, Needleworker, Poet",
  "Born to Walter Folger and Elizabeth Starbuck on Nantucket, Phebe Folger Coleman "
  "(1771&ndash;1857) was one of eight children in a remarkably talented and well-educated "
  "family. A close companion to her brother, island genius Walter Folger Jr., she was his "
  "intellectual partner, studying mathematics, history, French, and other subjects. In 1797, she "
  "became schoolmistress of a Quaker school on the island where she inspired many young "
  "girls&rsquo; needlework samplers. Examples of Phebe&rsquo;s artwork, needlework, and poetry "
  "have been preserved, showcasing her multiple talents. Phebe married Samuel Coleman in 1798 "
  "and together they had three children. She turned all of her skills to her domestic situation "
  "&ndash; and to the benefit of her husband&rsquo;s business enterprises. Samuel&rsquo;s "
  "success as a mariner and trader was in large part due to his wife, who taught him navigation. "
  "It seems there was nothing Phebe could not do. And if her circumstances had been different "
  "&ndash; without a husband and children to care for &ndash; she might have made history as a "
  "scientist, an inventor, a poet, an artist. In 1809, Phebe and Samuel sold their Nantucket "
  "house and moved to a farm near Ghent, New York. This video features a poem she wrote later "
  "that year, lamenting leaving her elderly parents, her brother, Walter, and her sisters. "
  "Produced for the Nantucket Historical Association for the 2010 exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes "
  "think of me: Notable Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; at the Nantucket Whaling "
  "Museum."),
 ("DY7aKGz3Oeo", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Eliza Spencer Brock, Whaling Journalist, Poet",
  "Eliza Spencer Brock (1810-1899), wife of Captain Peter C. Brock (1805−78), kept a "
  "journal aboard the whaleship Lexington during a voyage from 1853 to 1856. The journey took "
  "her to the Azores, Cape Verde Islands, New Zealand, Hawaii, and throughout the South Pacific. "
  "In her journal, Eliza expressed the isolation she felt at sea, noting poignantly, &ldquo;. . "
  ". if it was not for hope the heart would break&rdquo; noting often of her dreams of her home "
  "on Nantucket. Eliza included some eighty-five poems and snippets of verse in her journal, "
  "some of which she wrote. But the particular poem that has created a stir among scholars of "
  "women&rsquo;s history is the &ldquo;Nantucket Girls Song,&rdquo; dated February 1855 that "
  "Eliza attributes to Mrs. Martha Ford, Bay of Islands, New Zealand after having tea with her "
  "there. Produced for the Nantucket Historical Association for the 2010 exhibition "
  "&ldquo;Sometimes think of me: Notable Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; at the "
  "Nantucket Whaling Museum."),
 ("BJril-hSzTs", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Elizabeth Morse Morey, Whaling Journalist, Illustrator",
  "Elizabeth Morse Morey (1810-1893), wife of whaling Captain Israel Morey, kept a journal "
  "aboard the whaleship Phoenix during a voyage from 1853 to 1855. Phoenix crossed the Atlantic "
  "Ocean, rounded Cape Horn, and hunted for whales in the Pacific Ocean and later the Sea of "
  "Okhotsk. A kind, curious woman of a poetic nature, Betsey, as she was known to all, displayed "
  "a fascination with the diverse flora and fauna encountered during the voyage and the changing "
  "beauties of the sea. Known for a fondness for animals, children, and all living creatures, "
  "she felt a special sympathy for the whales captured by the crew and wrote tributes and "
  "invented colorful names for many of them. During the course of the voyage, Betsey named all "
  "thirty whales taken. In this video, we hear her tributes to the animals she encountered, "
  "especially to &ldquo;Mr. Hogg,&rdquo; a hog brought on board from Pitt&rsquo;s Island, and "
  "later slaughtered by the crew Produced for the Nantucket Historical Association for the 2010 "
  "exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes think of me: Notable Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; "
  "at the Nantucket Whaling Museum."),
 ("cbPy8vA8JFc", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Susan Austin Veeder, Whaling Voyage Journalist",
  "From September 1848 to March 1853, Susan Austin Veeder (1816-1897) sailed with her "
  "husband, Captain Charles A. Veeder, on a whaling voyage aboard the ship Nauticon. The journey "
  "took her around Cape Horn to ports in Chile and then to Oahu, Tahiti, and as far north as the "
  "Fox Islands in the Arctic. Susan was the first of the Nantucket whaling women to keep a "
  "journal. She filled it with gentle watercolors creating a chronicle of her experiences with "
  "panoramic views of ports as seen from the ship&rsquo;s deck. In this stunning journal, Veeder "
  "records the full range of shipboard events. Whale chases&mdash;shipboard accidents&mdash;crew "
  "mutinies&mdash;and the exchange of supplies and gossip (called a gam by the sailors) with "
  "passing ships and local peoples. In this video, we listen to her record of the birth of her "
  "own child, &ldquo;a fine daughter weighing 9 lbs.&rdquo; and her child&rsquo;s tragic death. "
  "Produced for the Nantucket Historical Association for the 2010 exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes "
  "think of me: Notable Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; at the Nantucket Whaling "
  "Museum."),
 ("yDFnxSufBQU", "Extraordinary Nantucket Woman: Eunice Ross, African American Student Activist for Education Rights",
  "Communities across America in the 1840s saw the bitter divisions of slavery and racial "
  "prejudice dominate public debate. On Nantucket in 1840, Eunice Ross (1824-1895), a "
  "seventeen-year-old student of Anna Gardner at the African School, by vote of a town meeting "
  "was denied admission to the Nantucket High School because of her race. Five years of hot "
  "debate followed the ban, bringing to light ugly prejudices and deeply ingrained fears within "
  "the community. Finally, in 1845, frustrated by the delays of the Nantucket School Committee, "
  "Edward J. Pompey and 104 other black citizens, including members of the Ross family, "
  "submitted a petition to the Massachusetts State House requesting that admission to public "
  "schools be extended to all children. In this video, we hear Eunice Ross&rsquo;s own petition "
  "to the legislature requesting equal educational opportunity. Such legislation eventually "
  "passed in 1845, but it was not until 1847 that children from the African School, including "
  "Eunice Ross and Phoebe Ann Boston, daughter of African American whaling caption, Absalom "
  "Boston, were admitted to Nantucket High School. Produced for the Nantucket Historical "
  "Association for the 2010 exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes think of me: Notable Nantucket Women "
  "through the Centuries&rdquo; at the Nantucket Whaling Museum."),
 ("qd8VobqXBys", "Extraordinary Nantucket Women: Gertrude and Hanna Monaghan, Quakers, Artists, Designers",
  "Gertrude (1887-1962) and Hanna Monaghan (1889-1972), natives of Philadelphia, "
  "came to Nantucket in the summers to immerse themselves in art beginning in 1923, when they "
  "rented a small cottage-studio joining Nantucket&rsquo;s thriving art colony in the 1920s and "
  "&rsquo;30s. They attracted some attention when they drove around town in their "
  "chauffeur-driven car, accompanied by their white greyhound named Angel Gabriel, and when they "
  "began to remodel an ancient livestock barn and pig sty on Howard Street into a home, it "
  "aroused the curiosity of the townspeople, who found them &ldquo;peculiar.&rdquo; The sisters "
  "wanted to make it perfectly clear that what they accomplished was for the sake of art, and "
  "was ultimately an expression of their faith in their inner light. They named their retreat "
  "Greater Light. It came to be recognized as a major work of design and decoration. Hanna "
  "Monaghan bequeathed Greater Light and its furnishings to the Nantucket Historical Association "
  "in 1972. Closed to the public in 2009 for restoration, it was reopened in 2011 as a monument "
  "to Nantucket&rsquo;s Art Colony, and to the era between the two world wars when artists "
  "flocked to the island for summers of intense work and instruction. Produced for the Nantucket "
  "Historical Association for the 2010 exhibition &ldquo;Sometimes think of me: Notable "
  "Nantucket Women through the Centuries&rdquo; at the Nantucket Whaling Museum."),
 ("-mk4EEKLYpc", "Nantucket Girl&rsquo;s Song",
  "The Nantucket Girl&rsquo;s Song is a witty poem found in the journal kept by Eliza Brock, "
  "wife of Peter C. Brock, master of the Nantucket ship Lexington on a whaling voyage from May "
  "1853 to June 1856. It sums up how many women felt about their husbands being off on whaling "
  "voyages for years at a time. Susan J. Berman, songwriter and interpreter at the Nantucket "
  "Historical Association, has set this poem to music and added a verse of her own Susan J. "
  "Berman - Guitar &amp; Vocals Ray K. Saunders - Lead Guitar Cadence Carroll - Backing Vocals "
  "Mark Dann - Bass Victor Ferrantella - Percussion Written by Susan J. Berman. Published by "
  "Gray Lady Music. Recorded at Garden Rock Studios, Nantucket, MA &amp; Mark Dann Studios, "
  "Woodstock, NY. Mixed and engineered by Mark Dann Studios, Woodstock, NY. Video by Novation "
  "Media Illustrations from the Collections of the Nantucket Historical Association Music "
  "available from iTunes, Amazon, CDBaby, and others."),
 ("RObT3CcL0rA", "Phebe Ann Coffin Hanaford: A Force from Nantucket",
  "Phebe Hanaford (1829-1921), born on Nantucket, was a friend of Lucretia Mott and Anna "
  "Gardner. She was a passionate crusader for equal rights, which she understood to mean both "
  "emancipation, education and integration for people of African heritage, and also securing "
  "voting rights for women. Her collection of women&rsquo;s organization pins shows the breadth "
  "of her engagement, leading to the passage of the 19th Amendment to the Constitution of the "
  "United States."),
 ("oE7FzUdFTZQ", "&ldquo;Miss Parker&rdquo;: Clara Parker, Nantucket Atheneum Head Librarian, &ldquo;New Voter&rdquo;",
  "&ldquo;Miss Parker&rdquo; is a biography of former Nantucket Atheneum head librarian Clara "
  "Parker (1877-1970) who held this position for fifty years, serving three generations of "
  "Nantucket readers. It also explores Parker&rsquo;s role as one of the first women on "
  "Nantucket to vote in the landmark national election of 1920. The film features a voiceover "
  "from acclaimed author Nathaniel Philbrick. The film by Nantucket High School freshman Anna "
  "Popnikolova was produced as part of the 2021 Teen View Labs, an educational filmmaking "
  "program run by the Nantucket Film Festival in partnership with Nantucket Community Television "
  "(NCTV)."),
]

