CHAMBER_MISSION = (
    '<span class="dl-mission__lead">About the Nantucket Island Chamber of Commerce:</span>'
    "<br><br>The Nantucket Chamber is the local Chamber of Commerce as well as the Regional "
    "Tourism Council for the Commonwealth of Massachusetts serving Nantucket Island. We are "
    "the central point of contact for national, local, and international press."
    "<br><br>Nantucket Chamber members reap the benefits of a wide variety of programs and "
    "services specifically tailored to serve our unique community. As a local, regional, and "
    "international destination marketing hub, we attract visitors from across the globe and "
    "drive business for all of our members. In addition, the Nantucket Chamber provides "
    "legislative liaison, professional development programs, networking events, business "
    "consultations, year round visitor information and referrals, and top quality collateral, "
    "such as publications, a destination website, social media marketing, and more."
)

# Four of the videos are NCTV's own uploads rather than the Chamber channel's,
# supplied by Frank: the Daffodil history, the 2020 breakfast (both of which
# the Chamber's channel duplicates), and the 2023 and 2026 breakfasts.
#
# Descriptions are written from each video's own captions (en-orig). The awards
# and Chamber Chats are short enough to read whole; the eleven panels and
# workshops run 35 minutes to 2:15, so those were digested first -- the opening,
# ten sampled windows and a regex pass for speaker introductions -- rather than
# read end to end. Same approach as the WPI spine, generalised.
#
# Two have no caption track at all and no publisher description. "Talk of the
# Town: Wendy Hudson" is written from a transcript Frank supplied on
# 2026-10-24 -- a human one, with timecodes and speaker labels, so it is more
# reliable than the auto-captions everything else here comes from. It still
# mishears a few island words ("Antigua Chamber of Commerce", "an antique" for
# "on Nantucket", "Mitchells Woodworks"), so it was read, not lifted. The 2019
# Volunteer of the Year video has a two-line description taken from the
# Chamber's own text, which is the only source for it.
#
# Three Chamber Walks are silent tours set to music -- their captions are
# "[Music]" and nothing else. Their descriptions name the storefronts the camera
# visits, which the Chamber lists in its own video description.
#
# Speakers are given by role rather than name, the usual rule, except where the
# spelling is confirmed: a name in one of the Chamber's own video titles or
# descriptions (Elin Hilderbrand, Emily Molden, Wendy Hudson, Denice Kronau,
# Marsha Egan, Rocky Fox), one already confirmed for this site (Libby Gibson,
# Cormac Collier, Dr. Margot Hartmann, Bruce Percelay), or a public figure the
# captions cannot plausibly be garbling (Fred Rogers, Ken Burns, Maura Healey).
# John Shea and Jamie Siminoff come from the Chamber's own event listing for the
# pitch competition. The five women in the 2026 breakfast -- Denice Kronau,
# Megan Calabrese, Sarah Bois, Liz Cordero and Stephanie Serra -- were spelled
# by Frank; the captions garble all but Cordero ("Calib", "Boyce", "Stephanie
# Sarah", "Denise"). Jean MacAusland was confirmed against outside coverage;
# the captions give "MacAuslan".

# Year buttons follow the year of the event, not the upload date: this channel
# posts some events more than a year late. The 12 February 2024 batch is the
# clearest case -- it carries the pitch competition held 8 October 2022
# (confirmed against the Chamber's own event listing, which names the same
# Dreamland venue and the same three judges as the video description), so the
# upload date would have mis-filed it by two years.
#
# The titles carry the year for the 2019, 2022 and 2024 awards. The remaining
# six are titled bare ("Achievement of Merit Award" and so on) and went up in
# that same February 2024 batch alongside the "2023 Year In Review". They are
# filed under 2023 by elimination: the celebration runs every September or
# October, the other three seasons already have their own titled sets, and 2023
# is the only one left between the October 2022 ceremony and the September 2024
# one. No dated outside source for that season's winners was found, so this is
# an inference rather than a confirmed date.
#
# Recipients come from the Chamber's own video descriptions, which name them
# where the titles do not -- two 2023 videos are both titled "Achievement of
# Merit Award", and two 2022 videos are both "Co-Volunteer of the Year".
